-- Chemistry part 1 of 2: longer explanations for CHE-0001 to CHE-1011 (1,011 live questions).
-- Longer explanations: each question's "why" now says in two or three sentences why the key is right, and each wrong
-- option gets a sentence or two on the mistake behind it. Facts checked against the source textbook named in each
-- question's source. Stems, options and answer keys are unchanged: every UPDATE also matches on the current key, so a
-- question whose key was edited since is left alone. Safe to run twice.

UPDATE questions SET why = 'Döbereiner grouped similar elements in threes, called triads, and noticed that the atomic mass of the middle element is roughly the average of the other two. For chlorine and iodine this gives (35.5 + 127) ÷ 2 = 162.5 ÷ 2 ≈ 81.3. Bromine''s real atomic mass, 79.90, is close to this prediction.', wrong = '{"0": "63.5 is half of iodine''s mass alone (127 ÷ 2). The rule averages the first and third members, so chlorine''s mass must be added first.", "2": "91.5 is the difference between the two masses (127 − 35.5). The rule needs their average, not their difference.", "3": "162.5 is the sum of the two masses (35.5 + 127). The sum still has to be halved to give the average, 81.3."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0001' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1869 Mendeleev arranged 63 elements by increasing atomic mass and grouped elements with similar properties together. His real success was that he left gaps for elements not yet discovered and predicted their atomic masses and properties. These predictions proved accurate when the missing elements were later found.', wrong = '{"0": "Mendeleev ordered the elements by atomic mass. Ordering by atomic number came later, after Moseley''s work in 1913.", "1": "Finding atomic numbers from X-ray emission was Moseley''s work in 1913, long after Mendeleev''s table.", "3": "Mendeleev''s success is credited to his gaps and correct predictions. The noble gases had not even been discovered when he made his table."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0002' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1913 Moseley used X-ray emission from different elements to find their exact atomic numbers. He then arranged the elements by atomic number instead of atomic mass, which removed the discrepancies in Mendeleev''s table. This led to the modern periodic law.', wrong = '{"1": "Alpha-particle scattering was Rutherford''s experiment, which revealed the nucleus. It was not used to measure atomic numbers.", "2": "Mass spectrometry measures the masses of atoms and isotopes and their relative abundance, not atomic numbers.", "3": "Cathode-ray deflection was used to study the electron and its charge-to-mass ratio, not to find atomic numbers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0003' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The modern periodic law says that the properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers, not their atomic masses. Argon has atomic number 18 and potassium has 19, so argon comes first even though it is slightly heavier. This order also keeps argon with the noble gases and potassium with the alkali metals.', wrong = '{"0": "There is no rule that gases come before metals. Hydrogen, a gas, sits at the top of Group 1 above the alkali metals.", "1": "Potassium''s average atomic mass of 39.10 is correct. The order is decided by atomic number, not by atomic mass.", "2": "The modern table orders every element by atomic number. Atomic mass is not used to order elements anywhere, even within a block."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0004' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chemical properties depend mainly on the valence electrons, the electrons in the outermost shell, because these take part in bonding. Elements in the same group have the same number of valence electrons, so they react in similar ways. Their physical properties still change gradually from top to bottom.', wrong = '{"0": "The number of shells increases by one at each step down a group. Elements in the same period, not group, share the same number of shells.", "2": "The number of neutrons differs between elements of a group, and even between isotopes of one element. It does not decide chemical behaviour.", "3": "Atomic mass increases down a group, so members do not share it. It does not decide chemical properties."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0005' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Metalloids show some properties of metals and some of non-metals. They lie along the stair-step line that runs from boron down to polonium, and include Si, Ge, As, Sb and Te. Germanium is one of them.', wrong = '{"0": "Aluminium lies to the left of the stair-step line, so it is a metal. It conducts electricity well and forms Al³⁺ ions.", "1": "Phosphorus lies to the right of the stair-step line, so it is a non-metal. It tends to gain electrons rather than lose them.", "3": "Tin lies to the left of the stair-step line, next to antimony, so it is a metal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0006' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Elements to the left of the stair-step line are generally metals, but hydrogen is the exception. It sits at the top of the far-left column (Group 1) because it has one valence electron, yet it is a non-metal gas.', wrong = '{"0": "Lithium is an alkali metal at the top of Group 1 below hydrogen, so it follows the rule.", "1": "Helium is at the far right of the table with the noble gases, so it is not to the left of the line at all.", "2": "Boron is where the stair-step line begins and is a metalloid. It is not one of the far-left elements the rule describes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0007' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An element is placed in a block according to the subshell that receives its last electrons. In 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ 4s² the 3d subshell is being filled, so the element is a d-block transition element. With 26 electrons, it is iron.', wrong = '{"0": "The 4s subshell is full, but the last electrons go into 3d. The s-block contains only Groups 1 and 2, whose last electron enters an s subshell.", "1": "The 2p and 3p subshells here are completely filled inner subshells. The p-block is for elements whose last electron enters a p subshell.", "3": "This configuration has no f electrons at all. The f-block holds the lanthanides and actinides, which are filling 4f and 5f."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0008' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The two separate rows at the bottom of the periodic table, the lanthanides and the actinides, form the f-block. In these elements the f subshells (4f and 5f) are being filled. Uranium belongs to the actinide series (Z = 89 to 103), so it is an f-block element.', wrong = '{"1": "The d-block holds the transition elements of Groups 3 to 12, where d subshells are being filled, not the actinides.", "2": "The p-block is Groups 13 to 18, from boron''s group to the noble gases.", "3": "The s-block is only Groups 1 and 2, the alkali and alkaline earth metals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0009' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Group 1 metals (Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr) have one valence electron, which they lose very easily. This makes them the most reactive metals. They are called alkali metals because they react with water to form alkalis, such as NaOH.', wrong = '{"0": "Two valence electrons and lower reactivity describe the alkaline earth metals of Group 2, not the alkali metals.", "2": "Metal oxides are basic, not acidic. Na₂O, for example, dissolves in water to give the alkali NaOH.", "3": "Being found mainly in the earth explains the name of the alkaline earth metals of Group 2. Alkali metals are named for forming alkalis with water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0010' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Transition metals such as Cr, Fe, Co and Cu show variable oxidation states. Iron, for example, forms both Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ compounds. So having only one fixed oxidation state is not a property of transition metals.', wrong = '{"0": "Variable oxidation states are a typical property of transition metals, so this is true, as with Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ or Cu⁺ and Cu²⁺.", "1": "Transition metals mostly form coloured compounds, such as blue copper(II) sulfate, so this is true.", "2": "The transition metals make up the largest family and sit in the middle of the periodic table, so this is true."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0011' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Group 16 elements (O, S, Se, Te, Po, Lv) are called chalcogens. The name comes from the Greek word chalkos, meaning copper, because most ores of copper are oxides or sulfides.', wrong = '{"1": "Forming salts with metals is the meaning of halogen (salt-former), the name used for Group 17.", "2": "Group 16 is not all non-metals. Only oxygen and sulfur are non-metals, and livermorium is a metal.", "3": "The name has nothing to do with chalk. It comes from the Greek word for copper."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0012' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Group 17 elements (F, Cl, Br, I, At, Ts) react easily with alkali metals and alkaline earth metals to form salts, such as NaCl. For this reason they are called halogens, which means salt-former.', wrong = '{"1": "Halogens are found in sea salt, but the name means salt-former, not sea-element.", "2": "Many halogens are coloured, such as yellow-green chlorine, but that is not what the name means.", "3": "Halogens do form acids such as HCl, but the name means salt-former, not acid-former."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0013' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Group 18 elements have completely filled outer shells and are almost unreactive. However, xenon was found to form compounds such as xenon hexafluoroplatinate, XePtF₆. Since these gases are not completely inert, the old name inert gases was replaced with noble gases.', wrong = '{"0": "Xenon stays in Group 18 because of its complete outer shell. Group 17 is the halogen group.", "1": "Xenon is a noble gas. Forming a few compounds does not make it a metalloid.", "3": "Group 18 elements are still almost unreactive under normal conditions and rarely form compounds. Only a few xenon compounds were made."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0014' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The period number equals the number of the valence shell, so a period 3 element has n = 3. A group 15 element has 5 valence electrons (subtract 10 from the group number for the p-block), and these fill as 3s² 3p³. The element is phosphorus.', wrong = '{"0": "2s² 2p³ has 5 valence electrons in shell 2, so it is in period 2. That element is nitrogen.", "1": "3s² 3p⁵ has 7 valence electrons, so it belongs to group 17. That element is chlorine.", "2": "3s² 3p¹ has only 3 valence electrons, so it belongs to group 13. That element is aluminium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0015' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The outermost shell is n = 3, so the element is in period 3. It has 7 valence electrons (3s² 3p⁵) and its last electron enters a p subshell, so it is in group 17 of the modern 18-group table. The element is chlorine.', wrong = '{"1": "Group 7 is the old A-group numbering, VIIA. In the modern 18-group table this group is numbered 17.", "2": "Period 2 elements have their valence electrons in shell 2. Here the valence electrons are in shell 3.", "3": "5 is the number of 3p electrons, not the number of shells. The highest shell here is 3."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0016' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Period 4 means the valence electrons are in shell 4. An s-block element with two valence electrons has the configuration 4s², which puts it in group 2. The group 2 element of period 4 is calcium.', wrong = '{"0": "Magnesium has its valence electrons in 3s², so it is in period 3, not period 4.", "2": "Strontium has its valence electrons in 5s², so it is in period 5.", "3": "Titanium is in period 4, but it is a d-block transition element (3d² 4s²), not an s-block element."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0017' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The atomic (covalent) radius is half the distance between the nuclei of two identical atoms bonded together. In Cl₂ the internuclear distance is 198 pm, so the radius of chlorine is 198 ÷ 2 = 99 pm.', wrong = '{"0": "49.5 pm is a quarter of the distance (198 ÷ 4). The radius is half of it.", "2": "198 pm is the whole distance between the two nuclei, which covers two radii, not one.", "3": "396 pm comes from doubling the distance (198 × 2) instead of halving it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0018' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Going down a group, each element has one more electron shell than the one above. The extra inner shells shield the outer electrons from the nucleus. This effect outweighs the larger nuclear charge, so the outer electrons are farther away and the atom gets bigger.', wrong = '{"0": "Elements in the same group have the same number of valence electrons, so this does not change down the group.", "1": "A sharp rise in effective nuclear charge would pull the electrons closer and make atoms smaller, not larger.", "3": "The type of bond an atom forms does not explain the trend in atomic size. Size depends on shells and shielding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0019' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Across a period the number of shells stays the same, but the nuclear charge increases by one at each step. The stronger pull draws the electron cloud closer, so atomic radius decreases from left to right. Sodium is at the left end of period 3, so it has the largest radius of these atoms.', wrong = '{"1": "Magnesium has one more proton than sodium but the same number of shells, so its electrons are pulled in more and it is smaller.", "2": "Phosphorus is further right in period 3 with a larger nuclear charge, so it is smaller than sodium.", "3": "Chlorine is near the right end of period 3, so it is one of the smallest atoms in the period."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0020' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sodium is 2, 8, 1, and forming Na⁺ removes its single 3s electron, so the whole third shell is lost. The same nuclear charge of +11 now acts on only 10 electrons and pulls them closer. So Na⁺ is much smaller than the Na atom.', wrong = '{"0": "Forming an ion changes only the number of electrons. The number of protons stays 11.", "2": "Removing an electron lowers the repulsion between the remaining electrons. Increased repulsion is the reason anions are larger.", "3": "Na and Na⁺ have the same nuclear charge, +11, because the number of protons does not change."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0021' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When chlorine gains an electron to form Cl⁻, the nuclear charge stays +17 but there are now 18 electrons. The extra electron increases the repulsion between electrons, so the electron cloud spreads out. This makes the anion larger than the atom.', wrong = '{"1": "The extra electron goes into the existing 3p subshell (3p⁵ becomes 3p⁶). No new shell is started.", "2": "The nuclear charge stays +17. Only the number of electrons changes when an ion forms.", "3": "Ions form by gaining or losing electrons. The number of protons never changes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0022' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All four are Group 2 ions with the same 2+ charge. Ionic radius increases down a group because each step adds another electron shell. Strontium is the lowest of the four in Group 2, so Sr²⁺ has the largest radius.', wrong = '{"0": "Be²⁺ is at the top of Group 2 and has only one shell of electrons, so it is the smallest of these ions.", "1": "Mg²⁺ has fewer shells than Ca²⁺ and Sr²⁺, so it is smaller than both.", "2": "Ca²⁺ is above strontium in Group 2, so it has one shell fewer than Sr²⁺ and is smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0023' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'N³⁻, O²⁻ and F⁻ all have 10 electrons, the same arrangement as neon. Their nuclear charges are +7, +8 and +9, so the pull on the same 10 electrons increases from N³⁻ to F⁻. The ion with the fewest protons is largest, giving N³⁻ > O²⁻ > F⁻.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse order. F⁻ has the most protons (9) for the same 10 electrons, so it is the smallest, not the largest.", "1": "N³⁻ has fewer protons than O²⁻ for the same number of electrons, so N³⁻ is larger than O²⁻.", "3": "F⁻ has more protons than O²⁻ for the same 10 electrons, so F⁻ is smaller than O²⁻, not larger."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0024' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First ionization energy is the energy needed to remove one electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms, giving one mole of gaseous 1+ ions. For magnesium this is Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻. Both the atom and the ion must be in the gas phase.', wrong = '{"0": "The atoms must start in the gaseous state. Starting from solid Mg would also include the energy needed to vaporise the metal.", "1": "This removes two electrons at once. Its energy is the sum of the first and second ionization energies.", "2": "Adding an electron to a gaseous atom describes electron affinity, not ionization energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0025' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each electron in an atom can be removed in turn, and each removal has its own ionization energy. So an element has as many successive ionization energies as it has electrons, which equals its atomic number. Calcium has 20 electrons, so it has 20 ionization energies.', wrong = '{"0": "2 is the number of calcium''s valence electrons. The inner electrons can also be removed, each with its own ionization energy.", "1": "4 is the number of shells calcium has (2, 8, 8, 2), not the number of electrons.", "3": "40 is calcium''s approximate atomic mass, which counts protons and neutrons, not electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0026' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first ionization energy is the energy for one mole of gaseous atoms. So the energy needed is proportional to the amount: 0.5 mol × 494 kJ mol⁻¹ = 247 kJ.', wrong = '{"1": "494 kJ is the energy for a whole mole of sodium atoms, not for half a mole.", "2": "988 kJ is the energy for 2 mol. It comes from doubling 494 instead of halving it.", "3": "124 kJ is the energy for about 0.25 mol (494 ÷ 4 ≈ 124). It halves the answer one time too many."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0027' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Calcium is in group 2, so it has two valence electrons in 4s². Once both are removed, Ca²⁺ has a stable, completely filled noble-gas (argon) arrangement. The third electron must come from the inner 3p subshell, which is closer to the nucleus and less shielded, so much more energy is needed.', wrong = '{"0": "Ca²⁺ has completely filled subshells (3s² 3p⁶). It has no half-filled subshell.", "1": "Calcium has only two valence electrons. If it had three, the big jump would come after the third ionization, not before it.", "2": "Calcium has no electrons in 4p. The third electron is removed from the filled 3p subshell."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0028' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrogen is 2s² 2p³, with one electron in each of the three 2p orbitals. This half-filled subshell is especially stable. Oxygen is 2s² 2p⁴, so one 2p orbital holds a pair whose repulsion makes one electron easier to remove, giving oxygen the lower first ionization energy.', wrong = '{"1": "Nitrogen and oxygen are both period 2 elements, so both have two shells.", "2": "Oxygen is actually the smaller atom, because radius decreases across a period. Size alone would give oxygen the higher value.", "3": "Oxygen''s valence electrons are in 2s and 2p, just like nitrogen''s. Period 2 elements do not use the third shell."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0029' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ionization energy decreases down Group 1 (Li > Na > K > Rb > Cs). Potassium''s outer electron is in shell 4, farther from the nucleus than sodium''s 3s electron, and more inner shells shield it. So it is held less tightly and is easier to remove.', wrong = '{"0": "Potassium''s nuclear charge (+19) is larger than sodium''s (+11). The greater size and shielding outweigh this.", "2": "Both are Group 1 metals with exactly one valence electron.", "3": "Both have a single s valence electron (3s¹ and 4s¹), which is not a half-filled subshell effect. Since it is the same for both, it cannot explain the difference."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0030' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Across a period electrons are added to the same shell, so no new shell is added and shielding hardly changes. Meanwhile the nuclear charge rises by one at each step, so the effective nuclear charge felt by the outer electrons increases. The electrons are held more tightly and are harder to remove.', wrong = '{"0": "The number of shells stays the same across a period. It increases only down a group.", "1": "The inner shells are the same across a period, so shielding changes very little. More shielding would lower ionization energy anyway.", "3": "Atomic radius decreases across a period. A larger radius would make electrons easier to remove and lower ionization energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0031' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First electron affinity is the enthalpy change when one mole of electrons is added to one mole of gaseous atoms to form gaseous 1− ions. A negative ΔH means the process is exothermic. So energy is released when a chlorine atom gains an electron, because the nucleus attracts the incoming electron.', wrong = '{"0": "Absorbed energy would give a positive ΔH, as happens for oxygen''s second electron affinity.", "2": "The equation shows chlorine gaining an electron to become Cl⁻, not losing one.", "3": "Electron affinity is defined for gaseous atoms, so this change does take place in the gas phase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0032' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The second electron must be added to O⁻, an ion that is already negatively charged. The ion repels the incoming electron, so energy has to be supplied to force it on. That makes the second electron affinity endothermic, with a positive value.', wrong = '{"1": "Oxygen is 2s² 2p⁴, which is not half-filled. Nitrogen, 2p³, is the element with a half-filled 2p subshell.", "2": "The second electron goes into the same 2p subshell (2p⁵ becomes 2p⁶). No new shell is started.", "3": "O and O⁻ have the same nuclear charge, +8. Only the number of electrons is different."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0033' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first electron affinities are F −328, Cl −349, Br −324 and I −295 kJ mol⁻¹, so chlorine releases the most energy when it gains an electron. The halogen order is At < I < Br < F < Cl. Fluorine is smaller, but its small, crowded 2p subshell repels the incoming electron, so chlorine comes out on top.', wrong = '{"0": "Fluorine''s value (−328) is slightly less negative than chlorine''s. Electron repulsion in its small 2p subshell lowers the energy released.", "1": "Bromine''s value (−324) is less negative than chlorine''s because the bromine atom is larger.", "3": "Iodine''s value (−295) is even less negative, because its large atom attracts the incoming electron more weakly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0034' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrogen is 2s² 2p³, so its 2p subshell is exactly half-filled, with one electron in each orbital. This arrangement is very stable, and an extra electron would have to pair up in an orbital already holding one. So nitrogen has a very low electron affinity, and phosphorus behaves the same way.', wrong = '{"0": "Carbon is 2s² 2p², which is not half-filled. Gaining an electron gives it the stable half-filled 2p³, so it accepts one readily.", "2": "Oxygen''s first electron affinity is negative (−141 kJ mol⁻¹), so it releases energy on gaining one electron.", "3": "Fluorine has a strongly negative electron affinity (−328 kJ mol⁻¹). It needs only one electron to complete its octet."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0035' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first electron affinities are Li −60, Na −53, K −48, Rb −47 and Cs −46 kJ mol⁻¹. Electron affinity becomes less negative down the group, because the larger atoms attract an incoming electron less strongly. Lithium, the smallest atom, has the most negative value and releases the most energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Caesium has the least negative value (−46 kJ mol⁻¹), because it is the largest atom in the list.", "1": "Potassium''s value (−48 kJ mol⁻¹) is less negative than lithium''s, so less energy is released.", "2": "Sodium''s value (−53 kJ mol⁻¹) is less negative than lithium''s, because sodium is the larger atom."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0036' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Across a period the nuclear charge increases, so the nucleus attracts an added electron more strongly. At the same time the atomic radius decreases, so the added electron comes closer to the nucleus. Both effects make electron affinity more negative from left to right.', wrong = '{"1": "Across a period radius decreases and the inner shells stay the same. More shielding and a larger radius would make electron affinity less negative.", "2": "The number of shells stays constant across a period, and nuclear charge increases rather than decreases.", "3": "Across a period the number of valence electrons increases and the radius decreases, the opposite of this option."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0037' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electronegativity is the power of an atom in a molecule to attract the shared pair of electrons in a bond towards itself. It describes a bonded atom, which is the key difference from electron affinity. Fluorine is the most electronegative element.', wrong = '{"0": "Electron affinity is the energy change when an electron is added to an isolated gaseous atom, not the pull on a shared pair in a bond.", "1": "Ionization energy is the energy needed to remove an electron from a gaseous atom, not to attract a shared pair.", "3": "Metallic character is the tendency of an element to lose electrons and form positive ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0038' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Linus Pauling made a scale of relative electronegativity values that compares atoms with one another. Because the values are relative, they have no units. Fluorine is given the highest value, 4.0.', wrong = '{"0": "kJ mol⁻¹ is the unit for ionization energy and electron affinity, which are energies. Pauling values are not energies.", "1": "Electron volts measure energy. Pauling electronegativity values are pure numbers with no units.", "2": "Picometres measure lengths such as atomic and ionic radii, not electronegativity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0039' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The polarity of a bond increases with the difference in electronegativity between the two atoms. Each bond here includes hydrogen (2.1), so the most polar bond is the one with the most electronegative partner. F–H has a difference of 4.0 − 2.1 = 1.9, the largest.', wrong = '{"0": "C–H has a difference of only 2.5 − 2.1 = 0.4, so it is the least polar of these bonds.", "1": "N–H has a difference of 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9, which is less than half that of F–H.", "2": "O–H has a difference of 3.5 − 2.1 = 1.4, which is polar but less than F–H at 1.9."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0040' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electronegativity decreases down a group. Each step adds a new shell, so the atom becomes larger and the inner electrons shield the bonding pair from the nucleus. In iodine the shared electrons are farther away and attracted less strongly than in fluorine.', wrong = '{"0": "Nuclear charge increases down a group, from +9 in F to +53 in I. The larger size and shielding outweigh it.", "2": "All halogens have seven valence electrons, so this does not change down the group.", "3": "Elements become more metallic down a group, not more non-metallic. Iodine is still a non-metal but less so than fluorine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0041' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Metallic character is the tendency of an atom to lose its valence electrons and form positive ions. From Na to Cl the nuclear charge increases while the atomic size decreases, so the valence electrons are held more strongly. They become harder to lose, so metallic character falls across the period.', wrong = '{"0": "Atomic size decreases across a period. Besides, easier loss of electrons would mean more metallic character, not less.", "2": "The number of shells stays the same (three) for every element in period 3.", "3": "The inner shells are the same across the period, so shielding barely changes. More shielding would increase metallic character."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0042' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Metallic character, the ease of losing an electron, increases down a group because atomic size and shielding increase. The single outer electron of caesium is the farthest from the nucleus and the easiest to remove. So caesium is the most reactive and most electropositive of these metals.', wrong = '{"0": "Lithium is at the top of Group 1 and holds its outer electron most tightly, so it is the least reactive alkali metal.", "1": "Sodium is above potassium and caesium in the group, so it loses its electron less easily than they do.", "2": "Potassium is more reactive than sodium but less than caesium, which is lower in the group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0043' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Magnesium reacts only slowly with cold water, but with steam it reacts more vigorously. Steam gives magnesium oxide and hydrogen gas: Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + H₂(g).', wrong = '{"0": "Mg(OH)₂ and H₂ form in the slow reaction of magnesium with cold liquid water, not with steam.", "1": "The metal takes oxygen from water and releases hydrogen. No oxygen gas is given off.", "3": "No magnesium hydride forms, and the gas released is hydrogen, not oxygen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0044' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sodium is very reactive and reacts quickly with oxygen and moisture in the air. Kerosene oil does not react with sodium and keeps air away from the metal surface. So sodium is stored under kerosene to prevent its reaction with air.', wrong = '{"1": "Sodium is a solid at room temperature (it melts at about 98 °C). Kerosene does not keep it liquid.", "2": "Sodium does not dissolve in air. The oil is there to keep air and moisture away from it.", "3": "Kerosene protects sodium from reacting. It does not make the metal more reactive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0045' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sodium burns in oxygen with a golden-yellow flame, forming a white solid. Magnesium burns with an intense white flame, which is why magnesium powder is used in fireworks and S.O.S. flares.', wrong = '{"0": "The colours are swapped. Golden yellow belongs to sodium and intense white to magnesium.", "2": "Lilac is the flame colour of potassium and brick red is the flame colour of calcium.", "3": "Neither sodium nor magnesium burns with a blue or green flame. Green is typical of barium or copper compounds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0046' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sodium normally burns in air to give a mixture of oxides, and in plenty of oxygen it forms the peroxide. Under special conditions, such as a limited supply of oxygen or a high temperature, sodium oxide is formed: 4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O.', wrong = '{"0": "Sodium peroxide, Na₂O₂, forms when sodium reacts vigorously with plenty of oxygen in open air.", "1": "NaOH forms when sodium reacts with water, not with oxygen alone.", "2": "NaO₂ is a superoxide. It is not the product sodium forms in a limited supply of oxygen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0047' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Non-metal oxides such as SO₂ are covalent and exist as separate small molecules. The forces between these molecules are weak, so SO₂ is a gas at room temperature. Across period 3 the oxides change from ionic to covalent as the electronegativity of the element increases.', wrong = '{"0": "Na₂O is an ionic oxide. It forms a giant ionic lattice held by strong electrostatic forces.", "1": "MgO is an ionic metal oxide with a giant ionic lattice, so it has a very high melting point.", "2": "CaO is an ionic metal oxide with a giant ionic lattice, not separate molecules."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0048' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Metal oxides are basic. Na₂O reacts with water to form the alkali sodium hydroxide: Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH. So the solution is alkaline. Non-metal oxides such as SO₃, P₂O₅ and SO₂ do the opposite and give acids.', wrong = '{"1": "SO₃ is an acidic non-metal oxide. With water it gives sulfuric acid, H₂SO₄.", "2": "P₂O₅ is an acidic non-metal oxide. With water it gives phosphoric acid, an acidic solution.", "3": "SO₂ is an acidic oxide. With water it gives sulfurous acid, H₂SO₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0049' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SO₂ is an acidic oxide, so it reacts with water to give an acid: SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃, sulfurous acid. Sulfur is in the +4 oxidation state in both SO₂ and H₂SO₃.', wrong = '{"0": "H₂SO₄ has sulfur in the +6 state. It comes from SO₃ and water, not from SO₂.", "1": "H₂S contains no oxygen, so it cannot form when an oxide simply dissolves in water.", "3": "H₂S₂O₇ (oleum) forms when SO₃ is absorbed in sulfuric acid, not from SO₂ and water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0050' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An amphoteric oxide reacts with both acids and bases. Al₂O₃ reacts with acid, Al₂O₃ + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂O, and with base, Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH → 2NaAlO₂ + H₂O. So the product with NaOH is sodium aluminate, and Al₂O₃ is amphoteric.', wrong = '{"0": "AlCl₃ is the product with HCl, not with NaOH. An oxide that also reacts with a base is not simply basic.", "2": "The product with NaOH is sodium aluminate, not Al(OH)₃. An oxide that also reacts with acids is not simply acidic.", "3": "Na₂SiO₃ is formed from SiO₂ and NaOH. It contains no aluminium, so it cannot come from Al₂O₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0051' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An acidic oxide is one that reacts with a base to form a salt and water. SiO₂ reacts with sodium hydroxide: SiO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SiO₃ + H₂O, giving sodium silicate. So SiO₂ is an acidic oxide.', wrong = '{"1": "A basic oxide reacts with acids, not with bases. The product with NaOH is sodium silicate, not Si(OH)₄.", "2": "SiO₂ does react with NaOH, so it is not a neutral oxide.", "3": "No chloride can form in a reaction with NaOH, and SiO₂ is classed as an acidic oxide, not amphoteric."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0052' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SiCl₄ is a covalent chloride that is hydrolysed by water: SiCl₄ + 2H₂O → SiO₂ + 4HCl. The hydrochloric acid formed makes the solution strongly acidic.', wrong = '{"0": "NaCl dissolves by hydration only, without reacting with water. The solution is neutral, pH 7.", "1": "KCl is a Group 1 chloride. Like NaCl it simply dissolves and gives a neutral solution.", "2": "MgCl₂ dissolves without being hydrolysed and gives a nearly neutral solution, pH about 6.5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0053' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PCl₃ is an acidic chloride that is hydrolysed by water: PCl₃ + 3H₂O → H₃PO₃ + 3HCl. Phosphorus keeps its +3 oxidation state, so the acid formed is H₃PO₃, phosphorous acid.', wrong = '{"0": "H₃PO₄ has phosphorus in the +5 state. It comes from hydrolysis of PCl₅, not PCl₃, where phosphorus is +3.", "2": "P₂O₅ is an oxide that itself reacts with water. It is not a product of hydrolysing PCl₃.", "3": "PH₃ (phosphine) would need phosphorus to be reduced to −3. Hydrolysis does not change its oxidation state."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0054' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When NaCl dissolves, its Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions become surrounded by water molecules, forming hydrated ions. This is called hydration. No reaction with water takes place, so the solution stays neutral.', wrong = '{"0": "Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction with water, as with AlCl₃ or SiCl₄, and it makes the solution acidic.", "1": "Neutralisation is the reaction of an acid with a base to form a salt and water.", "3": "Dehydration is the removal of water, which is the opposite of what happens here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0055' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The oxidation numbers in a neutral compound add up to zero, and oxygen is −2. For P₄O₆: 4x + 6(−2) = 0, so 4x = 12 and x = +3. The same method gives +5 for phosphorus in P₄O₁₀, where 4x = 20.', wrong = '{"0": "+6 would give 4 × 6 = 24 positive charges to balance only 12 negative ones from oxygen.", "1": "+5 is the oxidation number of phosphorus in P₄O₁₀, not in P₄O₆.", "2": "+4 would give 4 × 4 = 16 positive charges to balance only 12 negative ones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0056' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a chloride each chlorine is −1, so the oxidation number of the other element equals the number of chlorines in a simple chloride. Phosphorus is +5 in PCl₅, the highest of these. In period 3 chlorides the oxidation number rises from +1 in NaCl to +5 in PCl₅.', wrong = '{"0": "Aluminium is +3 in AlCl₃, which is lower than +5.", "1": "Silicon is +4 in SiCl₄, which is lower than +5.", "3": "Sulfur is only +2 in SCl₂, because just two of its electrons are used in bonding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0057' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Phosphorus and sulfur are in period 3 and have empty 3d orbitals. Sulfur can excite electrons into these orbitals and so use different numbers of valence electrons for bonding. It uses four in SO₂, giving +4, and all six in SO₃, giving +6.', wrong = '{"0": "Sulfur is 3s² 3p⁴, which is not a half-filled subshell. Phosphorus, 3p³, is the half-filled one.", "2": "Sulfur is less electronegative than oxygen. That is why sulfur has a positive oxidation number in its oxides.", "3": "Only valence electrons are used in bonding. Inner-shell electrons are not involved in variable oxidation states here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0058' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The oxidation number of a period 3 element in its oxide or chloride matches the number of its electrons used in bonding. Oxygen and chlorine are more electronegative than any of these elements, so they pull the bonding electrons towards themselves. That leaves the period 3 element with a positive oxidation number every time.', wrong = '{"1": "Period 3 is not all metals. It also contains non-metals such as phosphorus, sulfur and chlorine.", "2": "They do not always use all their valence electrons. Sulfur is +4 in SO₂ and phosphorus is +3 in PCl₃.", "3": "This is the reverse. If oxygen and chlorine were less electronegative, the period 3 element would have a negative oxidation number."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0059' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of protons equals the atomic number, Z = 17. Neutrons = A − Z = 37 − 17 = 20. The 1− charge means one extra electron has been gained, so electrons = 17 + 1 = 18.', wrong = '{"1": "16 electrons would give a 1+ ion. A negative ion has gained an electron, so it has 18.", "2": "The number of protons is fixed by the element. Chlorine always has 17 protons, not 18.", "3": "37 is the mass number (protons + neutrons). The number of neutrons is 37 − 17 = 20."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0060' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A neutral iron atom has as many electrons as protons, 26 (equal to Z). The 3+ charge means three electrons have been lost, so Fe³⁺ has 26 − 3 = 23 electrons. The mass number 56 affects only the neutron count.', wrong = '{"0": "26 is the number of protons, or the electrons in a neutral Fe atom. Fe³⁺ has lost three of them.", "1": "29 comes from adding three electrons (26 + 3). A positive ion has lost electrons, not gained them.", "3": "30 is the number of neutrons (56 − 26), not electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0061' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an ion, electrons = Z − charge. Sulfur has Z = 16, and the 2− charge means two extra electrons, so S²⁻ has 16 + 2 = 18 electrons. This is the same number as an argon atom.', wrong = '{"0": "O²⁻ has 8 + 2 = 10 electrons, the same as neon.", "1": "Na⁺ has 11 − 1 = 10 electrons, the same as neon.", "2": "A neutral chlorine atom has 17 electrons. Only Cl⁻ would have 18."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0062' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moseley bombarded elements with cathode rays and measured the frequency of the characteristic X-rays they gave off. He found that the square root of the frequency is directly proportional to the atomic number: √ν ∝ Z. From this he concluded that Z is a fundamental property of an element.', wrong = '{"1": "This puts the square root on the wrong side. It is √ν that is proportional to Z, so ν itself rises as Z².", "2": "Frequency increases with atomic number, so it cannot be inversely proportional to Z.", "3": "Wavelength becomes shorter as frequency rises, so λ decreases with Z rather than increasing in proportion to it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0063' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Deflection in an electric field depends on a particle''s charge and mass. Electrons are negative, so they bend towards the positive plate, and they are about 1836 times lighter than protons, so they are deflected much more. Protons bend slightly towards the negative plate, and neutrons are not deflected at all.', wrong = '{"0": "Neutrons have no charge, so the field does not act on them. They pass straight through without deflection.", "1": "Equal speed is not enough. The particles have different charges and masses, and the neutron is not deflected at all.", "2": "Protons are positive, so they are attracted towards the negative plate, not the positive one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0064' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If a deflected particle is imagined moving on a circular path, its radius of deflection is proportional to mass ÷ charge. A heavier particle curves less (larger radius), and a more highly charged one curves more (smaller radius). This is the reciprocal of the angle of deflection, which is proportional to charge ÷ mass.', wrong = '{"0": "The radius does not grow with charge. More charge bends the particle more strongly, giving a smaller radius.", "1": "Charge ÷ mass gives the angle of deflection. The radius depends on the reciprocal, mass ÷ charge.", "2": "The radius increases with mass, so mass cannot sit in the denominator."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0065' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An electron has a mass of 9.1095 × 10⁻³¹ kg and a proton 1.6726 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. Dividing gives a ratio of about 1 : 1836. So an electron is about 1/1836 of the mass of a proton.', wrong = '{"0": "1/18 is about a hundred times too large. The electron is much lighter than that.", "2": "1/2 would make the electron comparable in mass to the proton, which it is not.", "3": "1/18360 is ten times too small. It adds an extra zero to the correct factor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0066' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The neutron''s mass is 1.0087 amu (1.6750 × 10⁻²⁷ kg) and the proton''s is 1.0073 amu (1.6726 × 10⁻²⁷ kg). The values are very close, but the neutron''s is slightly larger. So the neutron is slightly heavier than the proton.', wrong = '{"1": "The masses are very close but not equal: 1.0087 amu for the neutron and 1.0073 amu for the proton.", "2": "It is the other way round. The neutron, at 1.0087 amu, is the heavier particle.", "3": "The factor 1836 compares the proton with the electron. The proton and neutron have almost the same mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0067' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both spectra come from electrons moving between the same fixed energy levels. In emission an electron falls and gives out light of a certain wavelength, and in absorption it takes in exactly that wavelength to jump up. So the dark absorption lines sit at exactly the same wavelengths as the coloured emission lines.', wrong = '{"1": "The lines are not shifted to longer wavelengths. The same energy gaps give exactly the same wavelengths.", "2": "The lines are not shifted to shorter wavelengths. The same energy gaps give exactly the same wavelengths.", "3": "Both spectra come from the same energy levels of the same atom, so their lines match exactly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0068' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each element has its own arrangement of electrons and therefore its own set of energy levels. The wavelengths it emits or absorbs are fixed by the gaps between these levels, so no two elements give the same line pattern. Like a fingerprint, the spectrum can be used to identify the element.', wrong = '{"0": "If all elements gave the same spectrum, spectra could not be used to tell elements apart.", "1": "Spectral lines come from electrons changing energy levels outside the nucleus, not from the neutrons.", "2": "Any element in the gaseous state gives a spectrum when heated or excited by an electric discharge, not only metals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0069' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A very large jump in successive ionization energies shows that the next electron comes from an inner shell, much closer to the nucleus. Here the big jump comes between the first (496) and second (4562) values, so there is only one electron in the outer shell. One valence electron means Group 1 (this element is sodium).', wrong = '{"1": "A Group 2 element has two valence electrons, so its big jump would come between the second and third ionization energies.", "2": "A Group 13 element has three valence electrons, so its big jump would come between the third and fourth values.", "3": "A Group 14 element has four valence electrons, so no big jump would appear among the first four values."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0070' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Magnesium has 12 electrons. The first large jump comes when the third electron is removed, because it comes from the second shell after the two outer electrons have gone. An even bigger jump comes at the eleventh electron, which comes from the first shell. Two jumps separate the electrons into three groups, which is evidence for three shells.', wrong = '{"1": "Magnesium does have two valence electrons, but two large jumps separate three groups of electrons, so there are three shells.", "2": "Twelve is the number of electrons, not shells. Within one shell the values rise only gradually.", "3": "If all electrons were in one shell, the values would rise gradually with no large jumps."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0071' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An electron is attracted to the nucleus, so energy must always be supplied to pull it away. For this reason every ionization energy is strongly endothermic, with a positive value.', wrong = '{"1": "Alkali metals have the lowest first ionization energies in each period, because their single outer electron is easy to remove.", "2": "First ionization energies decrease down a group, because the atom gets larger and the outer electron is held less tightly.", "3": "Noble gases have the highest first ionization energies in each period, because of their stable filled shells."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0072' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The maximum number of electrons a shell can hold is 2n², where n is the principal quantum number. For n = 4 this gives 2 × 4² = 2 × 16 = 32 electrons. The first four shells therefore hold 2, 8, 18 and 32 electrons.', wrong = '{"0": "8 is the capacity of the n = 2 shell (2 × 2²).", "1": "16 is n², the number of orbitals in the shell. Each orbital holds two electrons, giving 32.", "2": "18 is the capacity of the n = 3 shell (2 × 3²)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0073' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The azimuthal quantum number ℓ can take whole-number values from 0 up to (n − 1). For n = 3 the allowed values are 0, 1 and 2, which give the 3s, 3p and 3d subshells. ℓ = 3 would be an f subshell, which first appears in shell 4.', wrong = '{"0": "ℓ = 0 is the 3s subshell, which is allowed for n = 3.", "1": "ℓ = 1 is the 3p subshell, which is allowed for n = 3.", "2": "ℓ = 2 is the 3d subshell. It is allowed because n − 1 = 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0074' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A subshell has (2ℓ + 1) orbitals, and each orbital holds two electrons, so its capacity is 2(2ℓ + 1). For an f subshell, ℓ = 3, so it has 7 orbitals and holds 2 × 7 = 14 electrons.', wrong = '{"0": "6 is the capacity of a p subshell (ℓ = 1, three orbitals).", "1": "7 is the number of f orbitals. Each orbital holds two electrons, so the subshell holds 14.", "2": "10 is the capacity of a d subshell (ℓ = 2, five orbitals)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0075' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The azimuthal quantum number ℓ fixes the shape of an orbital. ℓ = 2 means a d orbital, which has a cloverleaf, or double dumb-bell, shape with four lobes in most cases.', wrong = '{"0": "A spherical shape belongs to s orbitals, which have ℓ = 0.", "1": "A dumb-bell shape belongs to p orbitals, which have ℓ = 1.", "3": "No atomic orbital is described as linear. Orbitals are three-dimensional regions of space."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0076' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of subshells in a shell equals its principal quantum number n. For n = 4 there are four subshells, 4s, 4p, 4d and 4f, with ℓ = 0, 1, 2 and 3. Each shell adds one new type of subshell: shell 4 is the first to have an f subshell.', wrong = '{"0": "Two subshells belong to the n = 2 shell, namely 2s and 2p.", "1": "Three subshells belong to the n = 3 shell, namely 3s, 3p and 3d.", "3": "16 is the number of orbitals (n²) in the fourth shell, not the number of subshells."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0077' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic quantum number m can take values from −ℓ to +ℓ. A p orbital has ℓ = 1, so m can only be −1, 0 or +1, giving three orientations. m = +2 requires ℓ to be at least 2, a d orbital.', wrong = '{"0": "m = −1 is one of the three allowed values for a p orbital (ℓ = 1).", "1": "m = 0 is allowed for every value of ℓ, including p orbitals.", "2": "m = +1 is one of the three allowed values for a p orbital (ℓ = 1)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0078' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Orbitals of the same subshell have the same energy and are called degenerate orbitals. The three 2p orbitals, for example, are degenerate. They differ only in orientation and can be told apart in a magnetic field.', wrong = '{"0": "Orbitals in different shells have different energies, so they are not degenerate.", "1": "Orbitals of different shapes in one shell, such as 3s and 3p, have different energies, so they are not degenerate.", "2": "Degeneracy is about having equal energy. It has nothing to do with whether the orbital holds electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0079' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first three quantum numbers, n, ℓ and m, describe an orbital, but one orbital can hold two electrons. The spin quantum number, +½ or −½, tells these two electrons apart. Without it, both electrons in an orbital would have the same description.', wrong = '{"0": "The size and energy of an orbital are given by the principal quantum number, n.", "1": "The shape of an orbital is given by the azimuthal quantum number, ℓ.", "3": "The orientation of an orbital in space is given by the magnetic quantum number, m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0080' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A shell with principal quantum number n contains n² orbitals. For n = 3 this is 3² = 9: one 3s, three 3p and five 3d orbitals (1 + 3 + 5 = 9). A quick check: 9 orbitals × 2 electrons = 18, the capacity of the third shell.', wrong = '{"0": "3 is the number of subshells in the third shell (3s, 3p, 3d), not the number of orbitals.", "1": "6 does not match the count. Adding the orbitals of 3s, 3p and 3d gives 1 + 3 + 5 = 9.", "3": "18 is the maximum number of electrons in the shell (2n²). Each of the 9 orbitals holds two."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0081' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the modern wave-mechanical model an electron''s exact path cannot be known. An atomic orbital is the three-dimensional region in space around the nucleus where the probability of finding the electron is maximum.', wrong = '{"1": "A fixed circular path is a Bohr orbit. The orbital model replaced fixed paths with regions of probability.", "2": "Orbitals describe electrons, and electrons are found outside the nucleus, not inside it with the protons.", "3": "An orbital is a region of space where an electron is likely to be, not a path taken between energy levels."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0082' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All s orbitals are spherical, with the electron density spread evenly in every direction around the nucleus. As the principal quantum number increases, the s orbital becomes larger. So 1s and 2s are both spherical, and 2s is larger.', wrong = '{"1": "The dumb-bell shape belongs to p orbitals. Every s orbital, including 2s, is spherical.", "2": "The size of an s orbital grows with n, so 2s is larger than 1s.", "3": "s orbitals are spherical, not dumb-bell shaped. Dumb-bells are p orbitals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0083' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Four of the five d orbitals have a cloverleaf shape. In dx²−y² the four lobes lie directly along the x and y axes. In dxy the lobes lie in the same plane but point between the axes.', wrong = '{"0": "dxy lies in the xy plane, but its lobes point between the x and y axes, not along them.", "1": "dxz lies in the xz plane, with its lobes between the x and z axes.", "2": "dz² has two lobes along the z axis and a doughnut-shaped ring in the xy plane."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0084' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the (n + ℓ) rule, the subshell with the lower (n + ℓ) value has lower energy and fills first. 6s: 6 + 0 = 6; 4f: 4 + 3 = 7; 5d: 5 + 2 = 7; 6p: 6 + 1 = 7. So 6s, with the lowest value, fills first.', wrong = '{"0": "4f has n + ℓ = 4 + 3 = 7, which is higher than 6 for 6s.", "1": "5d has n + ℓ = 5 + 2 = 7, which is higher than 6 for 6s.", "3": "6p has n + ℓ = 6 + 1 = 7, which is higher than 6 for 6s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0085' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '4d has n + ℓ = 4 + 2 = 6 and 5p has 5 + 1 = 6, so the values are equal. When (n + ℓ) is equal, the subshell with the lower n has lower energy and fills first. So 4d fills before 5p.', wrong = '{"0": "The tie-breaking rule is the reverse. The subshell with the lower n value fills first.", "1": "The tie is broken by the n value, so one subshell fills before the other.", "2": "p subshells do not always fill before d subshells. For example, 3d fills before 4p."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0086' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The filling order around here is 5s < 4d < 5p < 6s < 4f < 5d. After 5p (n + ℓ = 6) the next lowest is 6s, which also has n + ℓ = 6 but comes after 4d and 5p because of its higher n. All the remaining subshells have n + ℓ = 7 or more, so the next electron enters 6s.', wrong = '{"0": "4f has n + ℓ = 7, so it fills after 6s.", "1": "5d has n + ℓ = 7 and a higher n than 4f, so it fills after both 6s and 4f.", "3": "4d is already filled before 5p, since it has the same n + ℓ value with a lower n."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0087' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Copper is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹. A completely filled d subshell is especially stable, so one 4s electron moves into 3d. This gives a full 3d¹⁰ instead of the 3d⁹ 4s² the simple filling order predicts.', wrong = '{"0": "3d⁹ 4s² is what the simple filling order predicts, but copper takes the more stable full 3d¹⁰ arrangement.", "2": "4p fills only after 4s and 3d. In copper the 4s subshell still holds an electron and 4p is empty.", "3": "3d fills before 4p, so 4p⁶ cannot be filled while 3d holds only one electron."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0088' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pauli''s exclusion principle says that no two electrons in an atom can have the same set of all four quantum numbers. In this pair n, ℓ, m and s are all identical (2, 0, 0, +½). Two electrons in the same 2s orbital must have opposite spins.', wrong = '{"0": "These two electrons share the 1s orbital but have opposite spins, which is allowed, as in helium.", "1": "The m values differ (0 and 1), so the electrons are in different 2p orbitals and can have the same spin.", "3": "The n values differ (2 and 3), so the electrons are in different shells."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0089' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Oxygen is 1s² 2s² 2p⁴. By Hund''s rule the first three 2p electrons go singly into the three 2p orbitals, and the fourth must pair with one of them. That leaves two orbitals with a single electron each, so there are two unpaired electrons.', wrong = '{"0": "Oxygen''s 2p subshell is not full (it holds 4 of 6), so some electrons must be unpaired.", "1": "One unpaired electron fits fluorine, 2p⁵, where only one 2p orbital is left half-filled.", "3": "Three 2p orbitals cannot hold four unpaired electrons. With four electrons one pair must form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0090' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hund''s rule says that electrons entering a set of degenerate orbitals occupy separate orbitals first, with parallel (same) spins, before any pairing begins. So carbon''s two 2p electrons sit in two different 2p orbitals with the same spin. This arrangement keeps the electrons apart and has lower energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Pairing in one orbital happens only after each degenerate orbital already has one electron.", "2": "Electrons in separate degenerate orbitals keep parallel (same) spins, not opposite spins.", "3": "3s is higher in energy than 2p. By the Aufbau principle the 2p orbitals fill first."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0091' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Iron is [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s². By Hund''s rule the first five 3d electrons go singly into the five 3d orbitals, and the sixth pairs with one of them. This leaves four unpaired electrons, while the 4s² electrons are paired.', wrong = '{"0": "Two unpaired electrons would need eight d electrons in five orbitals, as in nickel (3d⁸).", "2": "Five unpaired electrons means a half-filled 3d⁵, as in manganese.", "3": "Five d orbitals can hold at most five unpaired electrons, so six is impossible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0092' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Groups 1 and 2 make up the s-block, where the last electrons enter the outer s subshell. Group 2 elements, such as Be, Mg and Ca, have two valence electrons, so their outer configuration is ns².', wrong = '{"0": "ns¹ is the outer configuration of Group 1, the alkali metals.", "2": "ns² np¹ is the outer configuration of Group 13, such as boron and aluminium.", "3": "ns² np² is the outer configuration of Group 14, such as carbon and silicon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0093' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The width of each block matches the number of electrons its subshell can hold: s holds 2, p holds 6, d holds 10 and f holds 14. A d subshell has five orbitals with two electrons each, so the d-block is ten columns wide.', wrong = '{"0": "This only restates the width. The reason there are ten is the capacity of the d subshell.", "2": "A d orbital has four lobes, or two lobes and a ring for dz². The lobes do not set the block''s width.", "3": "The d-block runs from Group 3 to Group 12. It does not start at Group 10."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0094' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In main-group (representative) elements the electrons are being added to the outer s or p subshell. So their valence-shell configurations have the form nsᵃ npᵇ, for example ns¹ for Group 1 or ns² np⁵ for Group 17.', wrong = '{"0": "Filling an inner (n−1)d subshell describes the d-block transition elements.", "2": "Filling an (n−2)f subshell describes the inner-transition (f-block) elements.", "3": "Main-group elements fill ns and np only. An inner d subshell is not being filled."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0095' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Subshells fill in order of increasing energy, judged by the (n + ℓ) rule. In the fourth period 4s (n + ℓ = 4) fills first, then 3d (n + ℓ = 5, lower n than 4p), which gives the ten transition metals, and finally 4p (n + ℓ = 5).', wrong = '{"0": "3d fills before 4p. They have the same n + ℓ value (5), and 3d has the lower n.", "1": "4s fills before 3d, because 4s has the lower (n + ℓ) value, 4 compared with 5.", "3": "4d belongs to the fifth period. In the fourth period it is 3d that is filled."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0096' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The highest shell occupied is n = 4, so the element is in period 4. The last electrons enter 4p, so it is a p-block element. It has four outer electrons (4s² 4p²), which places it in group 14, and with 32 electrons the element is germanium.', wrong = '{"1": "The last electrons go into 4p, not 4s, so it is not an s-block group 2 element.", "2": "The outermost shell is n = 4, not n = 3, so the element is in period 4.", "3": "The 3d subshell is already complete. The last electrons are in 4p, so it is not in the d-block."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0097' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Argon has 18 electrons, arranged 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶. S²⁻ has 16 + 2 = 18 electrons, so it has exactly the same configuration as argon. Species with the same electron configuration are called isoelectronic.', wrong = '{"0": "Na⁺ has 11 − 1 = 10 electrons, so it matches neon, not argon.", "1": "Mg²⁺ has 12 − 2 = 10 electrons, so it matches neon, not argon.", "2": "F⁻ has 9 + 1 = 10 electrons, so it matches neon, not argon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0098' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Titanium is [Ar] 3d² 4s². Although 4s fills before 3d, when a d-block atom forms a positive ion the 4s electrons are lost first. Removing both 4s electrons gives Ti²⁺ as [Ar] 3d².', wrong = '{"0": "[Ar] 3d² 4s² is the neutral titanium atom. The ion has lost two electrons.", "2": "This removes the two 3d electrons instead. In d-block atoms the 4s electrons are lost first.", "3": "This removes one 3d and one 4s electron. Both electrons removed come from 4s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0099' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Manganese is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s². When it forms a positive ion, the 4s electrons are lost before the 3d electrons. Removing both 4s electrons gives Mn²⁺ as [Ar] 3d⁵, a stable half-filled d subshell.', wrong = '{"1": "This removes two 3d electrons first. d-block atoms lose their 4s electrons before any 3d electrons.", "2": "This removes one 4s and one 3d electron. Both electrons removed come from 4s.", "3": "3d⁶ would mean an extra electron added to 3d. Mn²⁺ has lost electrons, so it keeps 3d⁵."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0100' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A free radical is an atom or group of atoms with one or more unpaired electrons, which makes it very reactive. In the methyl radical, •CH₃, carbon forms three C–H bonds and keeps one unpaired electron, shown by the dot.', wrong = '{"0": "In CH₄ carbon uses all four valence electrons in four C–H bonds, so every electron is paired.", "1": "In H₂O all electrons are paired, in two O–H bonds and two lone pairs.", "3": "Na⁺ has the neon configuration (2, 8), in which all electrons are paired."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0101' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Trivalent atoms such as aluminium have only three valence electrons. When one replaces a silicon atom, one of the four bonds to neighbouring atoms is short of an electron, leaving a hole. These holes act as positive charge carriers, so the material is a P-type (positive-type) semiconductor.', wrong = '{"1": "Phosphorus, with five valence electrons, supplies extra free electrons. That gives an N-type semiconductor, not P-type.", "2": "Aluminium has fewer valence electrons than silicon (3 compared with 4), so it creates holes, not extra electrons.", "3": "Phosphorus has more valence electrons than silicon, so it gives extra electrons, not holes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0102' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Phosphorus has five valence electrons. When it replaces a silicon atom, four of them form bonds with the neighbouring silicon atoms and the fifth is left over. This extra electron is free to move and carries negative charge, giving an N-type (negative-type) semiconductor.', wrong = '{"0": "Holes are created by trivalent atoms such as aluminium, which have one electron too few.", "2": "Phosphorus is a non-metal. It improves conduction here because of its extra fifth electron.", "3": "The silicon atoms keep all four of their bonds. The free electrons come from the phosphorus atoms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0103' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Silicon (2, 8, 4) has four valence electrons. In the pure crystal each silicon atom bonds covalently to four other silicon atoms, using all four of these electrons. No free electrons are left to carry an electric current, so pure silicon conducts poorly.', wrong = '{"0": "Silicon has four valence electrons, as its configuration 2, 8, 4 shows.", "2": "Pure silicon is made of neutral atoms joined by covalent bonds. It contains no ions.", "3": "An isolated silicon atom has four outer electrons, not eight. It reaches eight only by sharing in bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0104' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'ℓ = 1 describes p electrons. Argon is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶, so it has two full p subshells. That gives 6 (2p) + 6 (3p) = 12 electrons with ℓ = 1. The 1s, 2s and 3s electrons have ℓ = 0 and are not counted.', wrong = '{"0": "6 counts only one p subshell. Argon has both 2p and 3p completely filled.", "1": "8 is the number of electrons in argon''s outer shell (3s² 3p⁶), which includes two s electrons.", "3": "18 is the total number of electrons, including the six s electrons with ℓ = 0."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0105' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Schrödinger''s wave equation treats the electron in three-dimensional space, so three quantum numbers come out of it. These are the principal (n), azimuthal (ℓ) and magnetic (m) quantum numbers, which describe the size, shape and orientation of an orbital. The spin quantum number was added separately to describe the electron''s spin.', wrong = '{"0": "n alone was enough for Bohr''s simple model of circular orbits, but not for three-dimensional orbitals.", "2": "The spin quantum number does not come from the wave equation and does not describe an orbital. It describes the electron''s spin.", "3": "n and m are both needed to describe an orbital, and s does not describe an orbital at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0106' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The subshell letters come from old names for lines in atomic spectra: s for sharp, p for principal, d for diffused and f for fundamental. So p stands for principal.', wrong = '{"0": "Polar is not one of the spectral-line names. The letter p stands for principal.", "2": "Primary is not one of the spectral-line names. The four names are sharp, principal, diffused and fundamental.", "3": "Paired refers to electron spins in an orbital. It is not what the letter p stands for."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0107' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Lewis concept says atoms combine to complete their outermost shell and reach the stable electronic configuration of a noble gas. For most atoms this means eight electrons in the valence shell, an octet. They do this by losing, gaining or sharing electrons.', wrong = '{"0": "Bonding does not change the number of protons or neutrons, so atomic mass stays the same.", "1": "Bonding usually pairs up electrons, so it reduces the number of unpaired electrons.", "2": "Atoms need not lose all their valence electrons. Many gain or share electrons to complete their shell."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0108' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chlorine (2, 8, 7) gains one electron from sodium to form Cl⁻ (2, 8, 8). This is the electronic configuration of argon, the noble gas that follows chlorine. Sodium, by losing its electron, reaches the configuration of neon.', wrong = '{"0": "Neon''s configuration (2, 8) is reached by Na⁺, not by Cl⁻.", "1": "Helium''s configuration has only two electrons, but Cl⁻ has 18.", "2": "2, 8, 1 is the configuration of a neutral sodium atom, not of any ion here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0109' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A bond is non-polar when the shared electron pair is shared equally. In Br₂ both atoms are bromine, so they have exactly the same electronegativity. The shared pair sits midway between them and neither atom carries a partial charge.', wrong = '{"0": "Chlorine is more electronegative than hydrogen, so the shared pair is pulled towards Cl and the bond is polar.", "1": "Bromine is more electronegative than hydrogen, so H–Br is a polar bond.", "3": "Oxygen is much more electronegative than hydrogen, so both O–H bonds in water are polar."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0110' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A dative (coordinate) bond forms when both electrons of the shared pair come from the same atom. The oxygen atom of water has two lone pairs. It donates one of them to the proton, H⁺, which has no electrons, forming H₃O⁺.', wrong = '{"1": "A proton has no electrons at all, so it cannot donate a pair. It is the acceptor.", "2": "Each atom giving one electron describes an ordinary covalent bond, not a dative bond.", "3": "Complete transfer of an electron describes an ionic bond. In H₃O⁺ the pair is shared."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0111' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In CO, carbon and oxygen first form a normal double bond, but carbon then has only six electrons around it. Oxygen donates one of its lone pairs to carbon, forming a dative bond. This completes carbon''s octet and gives CO a triple bond, one of which is dative.', wrong = '{"1": "The donor is oxygen. Carbon is the atom whose octet is incomplete, so it accepts the pair.", "2": "Each atom sharing one more electron would make an ordinary covalent bond, not a dative bond.", "3": "CO is a covalent molecule. No electrons are transferred from one atom to the other."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0112' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ozone forms when an O₂ molecule combines with a free oxygen atom. The central oxygen keeps a double bond to one oxygen and donates a lone pair to the other, forming a coordinate bond. Having given away a share of its pair, the central (donor) oxygen carries a positive charge, and the acceptor oxygen carries a negative charge.', wrong = '{"1": "The coordinate bond leaves the donor and acceptor atoms charged, so not all three oxygens are neutral.", "2": "The central oxygen donates the electron pair, so it becomes positive, not negative.", "3": "Only one end oxygen, the acceptor, is charged, and its charge is negative. The other end oxygen is neutral."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0113' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the usual Lewis structure of SO₄²⁻ with an expanded octet, sulfur forms two S=O double bonds and two S–O single bonds. That puts 2 × 4 + 2 × 2 = 12 electrons around sulfur. This is four more than an octet, an example of an expanded octet.', wrong = '{"0": "8 would be a normal octet, but sulfate is given as an example of an expanded octet.", "1": "10 is the count around the central iodine in I₃⁻, not sulfur in sulfate.", "3": "14 electrons would need more bonds or lone pairs on sulfur than this structure has."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0114' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The central iodine in I₃⁻ forms two single bonds and carries three lone pairs. Counting electrons around it: 2 bonds × 2 + 3 lone pairs × 2 = 4 + 6 = 10. That is two more than an octet.', wrong = '{"1": "Exceeding the octet by 4 is the case for sulfur in SO₄²⁻, which has 12 electrons.", "2": "Exceeding by 6 would mean 14 electrons around iodine, more than two bonds and three lone pairs give.", "3": "I₃⁻ is an example of an expanded octet, so the central iodine does exceed eight electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0115' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An octet can expand only when d orbitals take part in bonding. Sulfur is in period 3 and has empty 3d orbitals. It can unpair electrons into these orbitals and so hold more than eight electrons in its valence shell, as in SO₄²⁻.', wrong = '{"0": "Sulfur is a non-metal, so being a metal cannot be the reason.", "1": "Lone pairs are not what allows the octet to expand. The vacant 3d orbitals are.", "3": "Sulfur is in period 3. Period 2 elements have no 2d orbitals, so they can never expand their octet."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0116' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electronegativity difference between two bonded atoms indicates the bond type. A difference below 0.4 gives a pure covalent bond, 0.4 to 1.8 a polar covalent bond, and above 1.8 an ionic bond. A difference of 1.2 lies in the polar covalent range.', wrong = '{"0": "A pure (non-polar) covalent bond needs a difference below 0.4. 1.2 is much larger.", "1": "Metallic bonding is not one of the bond types classified by electronegativity difference in this scheme.", "2": "An ionic bond needs a difference above 1.8. 1.2 is below that."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0117' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dipole moment measures how polar a bond or molecule is, from the size of the partial charges and the distance between them. It is measured in Debye units, D. A higher dipole moment means a more polar bond or molecule.', wrong = '{"1": "The pascal (Pa) is the SI unit of pressure.", "2": "The joule (J) is the SI unit of energy.", "3": "The picometre (pm) is used for bond lengths and atomic radii."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0118' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each O–H bond in water is polar. If the molecule were linear, H–O–H at 180°, the two equal bond moments would point in opposite directions and cancel, giving zero. A dipole moment of 1.85 D shows they do not cancel, so water must be non-linear, and it is in fact V-shaped.', wrong = '{"1": "In a linear molecule the two equal O–H bond moments would cancel, giving a dipole moment of zero.", "2": "Water has only three atoms, so it cannot form a tetrahedral molecule.", "3": "The O–H bonds are polar. Without polar bonds the molecule could not have a dipole moment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0119' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chlorine is more electronegative than carbon, so each C–Cl bond is polar. CCl₄ is perfectly tetrahedral, and its four equal bond moments point symmetrically outwards and cancel. So the molecule has zero net dipole moment and is non-polar.', wrong = '{"0": "The C–Cl bonds are polar. The molecule is non-polar only because its symmetry cancels the bond moments.", "1": "Polar bonds alone do not make a polar molecule. The symmetric tetrahedral shape cancels the bond moments.", "3": "Carbon in CCl₄ has no lone pairs. All four of its electron pairs are used in bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0120' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A molecule has a net dipole moment when its bond moments do not cancel. H₂S is bent (V-shaped) like water, so its two polar S–H bond moments add up instead of cancelling. The other three molecules are symmetrical.', wrong = '{"0": "BeCl₂ is linear (180°), so its two equal Be–Cl bond moments point in opposite directions and cancel.", "1": "CCl₄ is perfectly tetrahedral, so its four equal bond moments cancel.", "2": "BF₃ is trigonal planar and symmetrical (120°), so its three bond moments cancel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0121' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The three van der Waals forces named are London dispersion (instantaneous dipole–induced dipole) forces, permanent dipole–permanent dipole forces and hydrogen bonding. These act between molecules. Ionic bonding is a strong chemical bond between ions, not an intermolecular force.', wrong = '{"0": "London dispersion forces are one of the three intermolecular forces named.", "1": "Permanent dipole–permanent dipole forces are one of the three intermolecular forces named.", "2": "Hydrogen bonding is one of the three intermolecular forces named, and the strongest of them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0122' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bond energy is the average amount of energy needed to break all the bonds of a particular type in one mole of a substance. It is measured in kJ mol⁻¹ and found from the heat involved in reactions. The higher the bond energy, the stronger the bond.', wrong = '{"0": "Bond energy refers to breaking a mole of bonds, measured as an average, not to forming one bond in one molecule.", "1": "The energy needed to remove an electron from a gaseous atom is the ionization energy.", "2": "The distance between two bonded nuclei is the bond length, not an energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0123' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bond energy increases with the electronegativity difference between the bonded atoms. Fluorine is far more electronegative than iodine, so H–F is much more polar than H–I. The partial charges add an extra electrostatic attraction, so H–F needs more energy to break.', wrong = '{"1": "Fluorine is the most electronegative element, far more electronegative than iodine.", "2": "H–F is a single bond. Hydrogen can form only one bond.", "3": "H–I is a polar covalent bond with only a small electronegativity difference, not an ionic bond."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0124' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A triple bond shares three electron pairs between two atoms, a double bond two and a single bond one. More shared pairs hold the atoms together more strongly. So multiple bonds have greater bond energies, giving C≡C > C=C > C–C.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse order. More shared electron pairs make a stronger bond, so the triple bond is strongest.", "1": "The triple bond, with three shared pairs, is stronger than the double bond.", "3": "The energies differ because the number of shared electron pairs differs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0125' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bond length depends on the size of the bonded atoms: larger atoms give longer bonds. Chlorine has an extra electron shell compared with fluorine, so it is much larger. So the C–Cl bond (about 180 pm) is longer than the C–F bond (135 pm).', wrong = '{"0": "Fluorine is more electronegative than chlorine. A larger electronegativity difference would shorten a bond, not lengthen it.", "1": "Both C–Cl and C–F are single bonds here.", "3": "C–F is a strongly polar covalent bond, not an ionic bond."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0126' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Si (electronegativity about 1.8) and F (4.0) differ greatly in electronegativity, so the Si–F bond has partial ionic character. The partial charges add an extra attraction that pulls the atoms closer. So the bond, about 155 pm, is shorter than the sum of the covalent radii, 117 + 64 = 181 pm.', wrong = '{"0": "Si–F in SiF₄ is a single bond. Silicon forms four single bonds to four fluorine atoms.", "1": "The radii are not wrong. The shortening is explained by the ionic character of the bond.", "2": "Fluorine (64 pm) is much smaller than silicon (117 pm), and size cannot explain a shorter bond here anyway."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0127' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bond lengths are measured with physical techniques that probe the arrangement of atoms in molecules or crystals. The methods used are X-ray diffraction, electron diffraction and spectral studies. Acid–base titration measures the concentration of a solution, not distances between atoms.', wrong = '{"0": "X-ray diffraction is a standard method for finding bond lengths, especially in crystals.", "1": "Electron diffraction is used to find bond lengths, especially in gas molecules.", "2": "Spectral studies, which measure how molecules absorb radiation, are used to find bond lengths."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0128' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Typical strengths are: ionic bond in NaCl 760, covalent O–H bond 464, hydrogen bond 20 to 25 and London forces 1 to 20 kJ mol⁻¹. Chemical bonds are much stronger than intermolecular forces, and London forces are the weakest. So the order is ionic > covalent > hydrogen bond > London forces.', wrong = '{"1": "Hydrogen bonds (20 to 25 kJ mol⁻¹) are far weaker than ionic (760) and covalent (464) bonds.", "2": "This is the complete reverse of the correct order. London forces are the weakest, not the strongest.", "3": "London forces (1 to 20 kJ mol⁻¹) are the weakest here, far weaker than ionic bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0129' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The metallic bond has an average energy of only 100 to 150 kJ mol⁻¹. It is explained by the extensive delocalization of the valence electrons throughout the metallic crystal. The electrons are spread over many atoms, so the attraction between any two atoms is weaker than in an ionic or covalent bond.', wrong = '{"1": "Metals contain a sea of delocalized electrons between the metal ions, so electrons are not absent.", "2": "Hydrogen bonds need H bonded to N, O or F. They do not hold metal atoms together.", "3": "Metals have low electronegativities, which is why they lose electrons easily."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0130' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Valence bond theory says a covalent bond forms when a half-filled valence orbital of one atom overlaps with a half-filled valence orbital of another atom of similar energy. The two unpaired electrons pair up with opposite spins in the overlap region. The greater the overlap, the stronger the bond.', wrong = '{"0": "Completely filled orbitals have no unpaired electrons left to pair up, so their overlap does not form a bond.", "1": "Transfer of an electron from one atom to another forms an ionic bond, not a covalent bond.", "3": "Empty orbitals contain no electrons, so there is nothing to share between the atoms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0131' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sigma (σ) bond forms by head-on, or linear, overlap of two half-filled orbitals that approach each other along the line joining the two nuclei. The electron density is greatest on this nuclear axis. s orbitals, p orbitals and hybrid orbitals can all form σ bonds.', wrong = '{"0": "Sideways overlap of parallel p orbitals forms a pi (π) bond, not a σ bond.", "1": "Electron density above and below the nuclear axis, with none on the axis, describes a π bond.", "2": "Transfer of electrons between atoms forms an ionic bond, not a σ bond."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0132' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hydrogen has one electron in a half-filled 1s orbital, and chlorine has one unpaired electron in a half-filled 3p orbital. These two orbitals overlap head-on to form a σ bond by s–p overlap. Chlorine is more electronegative, so the electron density lies closer to Cl and the molecule is polar.', wrong = '{"1": "s–s overlap forms the H₂ molecule, where both atoms use 1s orbitals.", "2": "p–p overlap forms the Cl₂ molecule, where both atoms use 3p orbitals.", "3": "sp³–s overlap forms the C–H bonds of methane. Chlorine in HCl is described using a pure 3p orbital."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0133' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sulfur (3s² 3p⁴) has two half-filled 3p orbitals, for example 3py and 3pz. Each overlaps with the 1s orbital of a hydrogen atom. Since p orbitals are at right angles to each other, the H–S–H angle stays close to 90°, at about 92°.', wrong = '{"0": "sp hybridization would give a linear molecule with a 180° angle, far from 92°.", "1": "Sulfur in H₂S does have two lone pairs. The near-90° angle is explained by its use of pure p orbitals.", "3": "Strong repulsion between the H atoms would widen the angle, not keep it close to 90°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0134' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each oxygen atom has two half-filled 2p orbitals. The px orbitals overlap end-to-end along the nuclear axis to form a σ bond. The py orbitals, which lie parallel, overlap sideways above and below the axis to form a π bond, so O=O is one σ and one π bond.', wrong = '{"0": "Only one σ bond can form between two atoms. Any second bond between them is a π bond.", "1": "No lone pair is donated in O₂. Both bonds are ordinary covalent bonds, each atom giving one electron.", "2": "One of the two bonds forms by head-on overlap along the axis, which is a σ bond, not a π bond."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0135' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In hybridization, atomic orbitals of the same atom mix to give new hybrid orbitals of equal energy. The number of hybrid orbitals always equals the number of atomic orbitals mixed. One s and two p orbitals give 1 + 2 = 3 sp² hybrid orbitals.', wrong = '{"0": "Two hybrid orbitals come from mixing one s and one p orbital, which is sp hybridization.", "2": "Four hybrid orbitals come from one s and three p orbitals, which is sp³ hybridization.", "3": "Hybridization conserves the number of orbitals. Three atomic orbitals cannot give six hybrids."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0136' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In BeCl₂, beryllium mixes its 2s orbital with one 2p orbital to form two sp hybrid orbitals. These two orbitals point in opposite directions to keep as far apart as possible, so the angle between them is 180°. This gives the linear BeCl₂ molecule.', wrong = '{"0": "90° is the angle found in octahedral arrangements and between axial and equatorial bonds in trigonal bipyramids.", "1": "109.5° is the angle between sp³ hybrid orbitals, as in methane.", "2": "120° is the angle between sp² hybrid orbitals, as in BF₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0137' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Boron (2s² 2p¹) excites one 2s electron into a 2p orbital, then mixes its 2s orbital with two 2p orbitals to form three sp² hybrid orbitals. These lie in one plane at 120° to each other. Each overlaps with a half-filled 2p orbital of a fluorine atom, giving trigonal planar BF₃.', wrong = '{"1": "sp hybridization gives two orbitals in a straight line at 180°, not three at 120°.", "2": "sp³ hybridization gives four orbitals in a tetrahedral arrangement at 109.5°.", "3": "Ground-state boron has only one unpaired electron, so it must be excited and hybridized to form three equal bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0138' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In methane, carbon mixes its 2s orbital with all three 2p orbitals to give four equal sp³ hybrid orbitals. These point to the corners of a regular tetrahedron, at 109.5° to each other. Each overlaps head-on with a hydrogen 1s orbital, forming four σ bonds of the sp³–s type.', wrong = '{"0": "sp² orbitals lie in one plane at 120°, which would give a planar molecule, not tetrahedral methane.", "1": "Unhybridized p orbitals are at 90° to each other and could not give four equal bonds at 109.5°.", "3": "sp orbitals form only two bonds in a straight line, so they cannot give four C–H bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0139' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The valence shell electron pair repulsion (VSEPR) model was presented by Sidgwick and Powell in 1940. It predicts molecular shapes from the repulsion between electron pairs around a central atom.', wrong = '{"0": "Lewis and Langmuir are linked with the electron-pair and octet ideas of bonding, not with VSEPR.", "1": "Mulliken and Hund are linked with molecular orbital theory.", "2": "Heitler and London are linked with valence bond theory."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0140' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A lone pair is held by only one nucleus, so it spreads out and takes up more space than a bond pair. As a result lone pairs repel other pairs more strongly. The order of repulsion is lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair > bond pair–bond pair.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse order. Bond pairs, held between two nuclei, repel each other least.", "2": "Lone pair–lone pair repulsion is the greatest, because both pairs take up extra space.", "3": "Lone pairs repel more strongly than bond pairs, so the repulsions are not equal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0141' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the VSEPR model, the two electron pairs of a double bond (or three of a triple bond) are treated as one region of electron density. SO₃ therefore has three regions around sulfur and no lone pair. Three regions spread out to 120° in a plane, giving a trigonal planar shape.', wrong = '{"0": "Double bonds are not ignored. Each one is counted as a single region of electron density.", "2": "If sulfur had a lone pair, SO₃ could not be planar with 120° angles. SO₂, which has one, is bent.", "3": "Multiple bonds hold more electron density, not less. They are simply counted as one region each."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0142' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Tin has four valence electrons. Two are used to bond with the two chlorine atoms and two remain as a lone pair, so there are three electron regions arranged trigonally. Counting only the atoms, SnCl₂ is V-shaped, and the lone pair squeezes the Cl–Sn–Cl angle to less than 120°.', wrong = '{"0": "A linear shape needs two regions and no lone pair, as in BeCl₂. Tin also has a lone pair.", "2": "A trigonal planar shape needs three bond pairs. SnCl₂ has two bond pairs and one lone pair.", "3": "A tetrahedral shape needs four electron pairs around the central atom. Tin has only three regions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0143' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrogen in NH₃ has four electron pairs, three bond pairs and one lone pair, so the pairs are arranged tetrahedrally. The lone pair repels the bond pairs more strongly than they repel each other, squeezing the H–N–H angle from 109.5° to 107°. Counting only the atoms, the molecule is triangular pyramidal.', wrong = '{"0": "Trigonal planar would need only three electron pairs and no lone pair, as in BF₃.", "1": "109.5° is the angle with no lone pair, as in CH₄. The lone pair in NH₃ reduces it.", "3": "V-shaped with 104.5° describes water, which has two lone pairs on oxygen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0144' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fluorine is very electronegative, so it pulls the N–F bonding pairs away from nitrogen and towards itself. Near the nitrogen these bond pairs then repel each other less and can come closer together. The lone pair''s repulsion now dominates, so the angle closes to 102.5°, smaller than 107° in NH₃.', wrong = '{"0": "NF₃, like NH₃, has just one lone pair on nitrogen. Nitrogen uses three of its five electrons in bonds.", "2": "Fluorine atoms are larger than hydrogen atoms, and size is not the reason for the smaller angle.", "3": "NF₃ has four electron pairs around nitrogen, just like NH₃, so it is not sp² hybridized."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0145' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'CH₄, NH₃ and H₂O all have four electron pairs around the central atom. CH₄ has no lone pair (109.5°), NH₃ has one (107°) and H₂O has two (104.5°). Each extra lone pair pushes the bond pairs closer, so the order is CH₄ > NH₃ > H₂O.', wrong = '{"1": "This is the reverse order. More lone pairs reduce the bond angle, so water has the smallest.", "2": "NH₃ (107°) has a smaller angle than CH₄ (109.5°) because of its lone pair.", "3": "Only CH₄ has the ideal 109.5°. Lone pairs reduce the angles in NH₃ and H₂O."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0146' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In NH₂⁻ nitrogen has its own 5 valence electrons, 2 shared from the two H atoms and 1 extra for the negative charge, making 8 electrons, or four pairs. Two are bond pairs and two are lone pairs, arranged tetrahedrally. Counting only the atoms, the ion is V-shaped, just like water.', wrong = '{"0": "Two bond pairs with two lone pairs do not give a straight line. The lone pairs bend the ion.", "2": "Trigonal pyramidal needs three bond pairs and one lone pair, as in NH₃ or H₃O⁺.", "3": "Tetrahedral needs four bond pairs and no lone pair, as in NH₄⁺."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0147' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PCl₅ has five bond pairs around phosphorus, arranged as a trigonal bipyramid. The three equatorial (planar) bonds are at 120° to each other, and each of the two axial bonds is at 90° to the plane. The two axial bonds are at 180° to each other.', wrong = '{"0": "109.5° is the tetrahedral angle, found in molecules with four bond pairs such as CH₄.", "2": "All angles of 90° describe an octahedral molecule such as SF₆, which has six bond pairs.", "3": "Only the two axial bonds are at 180° to each other. The other angles are 120° and 90°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0148' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The central iodine in I₃⁻ has five electron pairs: two bond pairs and three lone pairs, arranged as a trigonal bipyramid. The three lone pairs take the equatorial positions in the plane, where they have more room. The two iodine atoms sit in the axial positions on opposite sides, so the ion is linear (180°).', wrong = '{"0": "Trigonal bipyramidal is the arrangement of the electron pairs. The shape describes only the atoms, which are in a line.", "1": "A T-shape needs three bond pairs and two lone pairs, as in ClF₃.", "3": "The lone pairs occupy the equatorial positions, so the two bonded atoms end up opposite each other, not bent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0149' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Xenon has eight valence electrons. In XeF₄ four of them form bonds with four fluorine atoms, and the remaining four make two lone pairs. With six electron pairs in an octahedral arrangement, the two lone pairs sit 180° apart, leaving a square planar molecule.', wrong = '{"0": "With no lone pairs, xenon would have only four electron pairs and XeF₄ would be tetrahedral.", "1": "Xenon has eight valence electrons. Four are used in bonds, which leaves four electrons, or two pairs.", "3": "Three lone pairs plus four bonds would be seven pairs, needing 14 electrons, more than xenon has."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0150' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A drug works by fitting into the active site of its biological target, such as an enzyme, much like a key fits a lock. Only a molecule of suitable shape can fit and bind. VSEPR predicts molecular shapes, so it helps in designing drugs. For example, aspirin blocks the enzyme COX by binding to its active site through its acetyl group.', wrong = '{"0": "VSEPR predicts the shapes of molecules. It says nothing about their colour.", "2": "VSEPR does not measure mass. Molar mass is found from the formula or by mass spectrometry.", "3": "VSEPR deals with the shapes of covalent molecules, not with whether a compound is ionic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0151' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In molecular orbital theory, two atomic orbitals combine to form two molecular orbitals. Overlap of lobes with the same sign gives a bonding MO, lower in energy than the original atomic orbitals. Overlap with opposite signs gives an antibonding MO, higher in energy.', wrong = '{"0": "The number of molecular orbitals always equals the number of atomic orbitals combined, so two form.", "2": "Only one of the pair is bonding. The other is an antibonding MO of higher energy.", "3": "Only one of the pair is antibonding. The other is a bonding MO of lower energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0152' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two helium atoms would bring four electrons. Two fill the bonding σ1s orbital and two fill the antibonding σ*1s orbital. Bond order = (bonding − antibonding electrons) ÷ 2 = (2 − 2) ÷ 2 = 0, so no stable bond forms.', wrong = '{"0": "Each helium atom has two electrons, 1s².", "2": "A bond order of 2 would need four more bonding than antibonding electrons. For He₂ it works out to zero.", "3": "A helium atom is 1s², with both electrons paired, so it has no unpaired electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0153' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the MO diagram of O₂, the last two electrons go into the two degenerate antibonding orbitals π*2py and π*2pz. By Hund''s rule they occupy these orbitals singly with parallel spins. These two unpaired electrons make O₂ paramagnetic, something simple Lewis structures cannot explain.', wrong = '{"0": "If all electrons were paired, O₂ would be diamagnetic, not paramagnetic.", "2": "The bond order of O₂ is (10 − 6) ÷ 2 = 2, a double bond, not zero.", "3": "O₂ has a double bond (bond order 2). The triple bond belongs to N₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0154' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A paramagnetic substance has one or more unpaired electrons, and these make it attracted into a magnetic field. The more unpaired electrons, the stronger the attraction. Liquid oxygen, for example, is held between the poles of a strong magnet.', wrong = '{"0": "Being weakly repelled by a magnetic field with all electrons paired describes diamagnetism.", "2": "Conduction of electricity by free ions is not what paramagnetism means.", "3": "Dipole moment is about separation of electric charge, not about magnetic behaviour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0155' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The mole is defined using carbon-12 as the standard. One mole is the amount of substance that contains as many elementary entities as there are atoms in exactly 12 g (0.012 kg) of carbon-12. This number is Avogadro''s number, 6.02 × 10²³.', wrong = '{"1": "The standard used to define the mole is carbon-12, not hydrogen-1.", "2": "The standard used to define the mole is carbon-12, not oxygen-16.", "3": "22.4 dm³ is the molar volume of a gas at STP, not of a liquid, and it is not the basis of the definition."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0156' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The molar mass of water is 2(1) + 16 = 18 g mol⁻¹, so 9.0 g is 9.0 ÷ 18 = 0.5 mol. Number of molecules = n × Nᴀ = 0.5 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.01 × 10²³. A quick check: 9 g is half of 18 g, so it holds half of Avogadro''s number of molecules.', wrong = '{"0": "6.02 × 10²³ is the number of molecules in one mole, 18 g of water, not 9 g.", "1": "1.204 × 10²⁴ is the number of molecules in two moles, 36 g of water.", "3": "9.0 × 10²³ treats the mass in grams as if it were a count of molecules. The mass must first be turned into moles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0157' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The molar mass of CO₂ is 12 + 2(16) = 44 g mol⁻¹. Number of moles n = mass ÷ molar mass = 11 ÷ 44 = 0.25 mol. A quick check: 0.25 mol × 44 g mol⁻¹ = 11 g.', wrong = '{"1": "0.5 mol of CO₂ would have a mass of 0.5 × 44 = 22 g, not 11 g.", "2": "4 mol comes from dividing 44 by 11, the wrong way round.", "3": "11 is the mass in grams. It must be divided by the molar mass to give moles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0158' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The molar mass of CaCO₃ is 40 + 12 + 3(16) = 100 g mol⁻¹. Mass = n × M = 0.25 mol × 100 g mol⁻¹ = 25 g. A quick check: a quarter of a mole has a quarter of the molar mass, 100 ÷ 4 = 25 g.', wrong = '{"1": "100 g is the mass of one whole mole of CaCO₃, not 0.25 mol.", "2": "400 g comes from dividing 100 by 0.25 instead of multiplying.", "3": "10 g would be only 0.1 mol of CaCO₃ (10 ÷ 100)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0159' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Molar mass is the sum of the atomic masses of all atoms in the formula, in g mol⁻¹. For H₂SO₄: 2(1) + 32 + 4(16) = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98 g mol⁻¹. Each oxygen contributes 16, so the four oxygens alone make up 64 of the 98.', wrong = '{"0": "49 is half the molar mass (98 ÷ 2). It is the equivalent mass of the diprotic acid, not its molar mass.", "1": "82 counts only three oxygen atoms: 2 + 32 + 48. H₂SO₄ has four.", "3": "112 counts five oxygen atoms: 2 + 32 + 80. H₂SO₄ has four."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0160' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One mole of Na₂SO₄ contains 6.02 × 10²³ formula units, and each formula unit has two Na⁺ ions. So the number of sodium ions is 2 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴.', wrong = '{"0": "6.02 × 10²³ is the number of formula units, which equals the number of sulfate ions, not sodium ions.", "1": "1.806 × 10²⁴ is the total number of ions (2 Na⁺ + 1 SO₄²⁻ = 3 per unit), not just sodium ions.", "2": "3.01 × 10²³ is half of Avogadro''s number. It would be the formula units in only 0.5 mol."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0161' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '0.5 mol of NH₃ contains 0.5 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.01 × 10²³ molecules. Each NH₃ molecule has 4 atoms (1 N + 3 H). So total atoms = 4 × 3.01 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²⁴.', wrong = '{"0": "3.01 × 10²³ is the number of molecules. Each molecule contains four atoms.", "1": "6.02 × 10²³ counts only two atoms per molecule, as for 0.5 mol of a diatomic gas.", "3": "2.408 × 10²⁴ is the number of atoms in one whole mole of NH₃, not 0.5 mol."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0162' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Number of moles = number of particles ÷ Avogadro''s number. Here n = (1.204 × 10²⁴) ÷ (6.02 × 10²³) = 2 mol. A quick check: 1.204 × 10²⁴ is exactly twice 6.02 × 10²³.', wrong = '{"0": "0.5 mol comes from dividing the wrong way round, Nᴀ ÷ number of molecules. 0.5 mol holds only 3.01 × 10²³ molecules.", "1": "1 mol would contain 6.02 × 10²³ molecules, half the number given.", "3": "12 does not come from dividing by Nᴀ. It is close to the digits 1.204 read without the powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0163' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Equal numbers of atoms means equal moles of atoms. 6 g of carbon is 6 ÷ 12 = 0.5 mol of atoms. Helium is monatomic, so 2 g of He is 2 ÷ 4 = 0.5 mol of atoms, the same number.', wrong = '{"1": "23 g of sodium is 23 ÷ 23 = 1 mol of atoms, twice as many as in 6 g of carbon.", "2": "16 g of O₂ is 0.5 mol of molecules, but each molecule has two atoms, so it holds 1 mol of atoms.", "3": "1 g of H₂ is 0.5 mol of molecules, but each has two atoms, so it holds 1 mol of atoms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0164' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The molar mass of O₂ is 2 × 16 = 32 g mol⁻¹, so 8.0 g is 8.0 ÷ 32 = 0.25 mol. At STP one mole of any gas occupies 22.4 dm³, so the volume is 0.25 × 22.4 = 5.6 dm³.', wrong = '{"1": "2.8 dm³ is the volume of 0.125 mol. It comes from using 64 g mol⁻¹ as the molar mass instead of 32.", "2": "11.2 dm³ comes from using 16 g mol⁻¹, the mass of O atoms, which gives 0.5 mol instead of 0.25 mol.", "3": "22.4 dm³ is the volume of one whole mole of O₂, which would have a mass of 32 g."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0165' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At STP one mole of any gas occupies 22.4 dm³, so 44.8 dm³ is 44.8 ÷ 22.4 = 2 mol of methane. The molar mass of CH₄ is 12 + 4(1) = 16 g mol⁻¹. Mass = 2 × 16 = 32 g.', wrong = '{"0": "8 g is only half a mole of methane, which occupies 11.2 dm³ at STP.", "1": "16 g is one mole of methane, which occupies only 22.4 dm³, half the given volume.", "3": "44.8 is a volume in dm³. It cannot be used as a mass in grams without converting to moles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0166' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Avogadro''s law states that equal volumes of all ideal gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules. It follows that one mole of any ideal gas occupies the same volume, 22.4 dm³ at STP, and contains 6.02 × 10²³ molecules.', wrong = '{"0": "Equal masses of different gases contain different numbers of moles, so they occupy different volumes.", "1": "Gases have different densities at STP, because the same volume holds the same moles but different molar masses.", "2": "Volume being inversely proportional to pressure at constant temperature is Boyle''s law, not Avogadro''s law."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0167' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Avogadro''s law, equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules. 11.2 dm³ at STP is 0.5 mol of each gas. Their masses differ because their molar masses differ: 0.5 × 2 = 1 g of H₂ and 0.5 × 44 = 22 g of CO₂.', wrong = '{"0": "In a gas the molecules are far apart, so their size does not change how many fit in a given volume.", "1": "Equal volumes contain equal numbers of molecules. The masses are different (1 g and 22 g).", "3": "They contain the same number of molecules, and CO₂ is 22 times denser than H₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0168' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One mole of gas occupies 22.4 dm³ at STP, so the molar mass equals the mass of 22.4 dm³. M = density × molar volume = 2.86 g dm⁻³ × 22.4 dm³ mol⁻¹ ≈ 64 g mol⁻¹. Sulfur dioxide, SO₂, is a gas with this molar mass.', wrong = '{"1": "44 g mol⁻¹ would give a density of 44 ÷ 22.4 ≈ 1.96 g dm⁻³, as for CO₂.", "2": "32 g mol⁻¹ would give a density of 32 ÷ 22.4 ≈ 1.43 g dm⁻³, as for O₂.", "3": "128 g mol⁻¹ is double the correct value and would need a density of about 5.7 g dm⁻³."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0169' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At STP one mole of every gas occupies the same volume, 22.4 dm³. So density = molar mass ÷ 22.4, and the gas with the highest molar mass is the densest. CO₂ has M = 12 + 2(16) = 44 g mol⁻¹, the largest here.', wrong = '{"0": "CH₄ has M = 16 g mol⁻¹, the smallest here, so it is the least dense.", "1": "N₂ has M = 28 g mol⁻¹, which is less than CO₂ at 44 g mol⁻¹.", "2": "O₂ has M = 32 g mol⁻¹, which is less than CO₂ at 44 g mol⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0170' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The molar mass of NaCl is 23 + 35.5 = 58.5 g mol⁻¹, so 5.85 g is 5.85 ÷ 58.5 = 0.1 mol. The volume is 500 cm³ = 0.5 dm³. Molar concentration C = n ÷ V = 0.1 ÷ 0.5 = 0.2 mol dm⁻³.', wrong = '{"0": "0.1 is the number of moles. It must still be divided by the volume, 0.5 dm³.", "1": "0.05 comes from multiplying the moles by the volume (0.1 × 0.5) instead of dividing.", "3": "11.7 comes from dividing the mass, not the moles, by the volume (5.85 ÷ 0.5)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0171' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The volume must first be in dm³: 250 cm³ = 0.250 dm³. Number of moles n = C × V = 0.4 mol dm⁻³ × 0.250 dm³ = 0.1 mol. A quick check: 250 cm³ is a quarter of 1 dm³, so it holds a quarter of 0.4 mol.', wrong = '{"0": "1.6 comes from dividing 0.4 by 0.25 instead of multiplying.", "2": "100 comes from multiplying by 250 without converting cm³ to dm³.", "3": "0.4 mol is the amount in a full 1 dm³, but only a quarter of that volume is present."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0172' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles of NaOH needed: n = C × V = 0.5 mol dm⁻³ × 2.0 dm³ = 1.0 mol. The molar mass of NaOH is 23 + 16 + 1 = 40 g mol⁻¹. Mass = 1.0 × 40 = 40 g. A quick check: 1 dm³ of this solution would need 20 g, so 2 dm³ needs 40 g.', wrong = '{"1": "20 g is 0.5 mol of NaOH, enough for only 1 dm³ of this solution.", "2": "10 g is only 0.25 mol of NaOH, a quarter of what is needed.", "3": "80 g is 2 mol of NaOH. It comes from multiplying by the volume twice or ignoring the concentration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0173' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the same temperature and pressure, gas volumes react in the same ratio as their moles. The equation shows 1 volume of N₂ giving 2 volumes of NH₃. So 10 dm³ of N₂ gives 2 × 10 = 20 dm³ of NH₃.', wrong = '{"0": "The ratio N₂ : NH₃ is 1 : 2, not 1 : 1, so the volume of ammonia is double.", "1": "30 dm³ is the volume of hydrogen used up (3 × 10), not the ammonia formed.", "3": "40 dm³ is the total volume of reactants (10 + 30), not the ammonia formed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0174' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The coefficients give mole ratios: 1 mol N₂ reacts with 3 mol H₂ to give 2 mol NH₃. In grams this is 28 g N₂ + 3 × 2 = 6 g H₂ → 2 × 17 = 34 g NH₃. Mass is conserved, since 28 + 6 = 34.', wrong = '{"0": "The coefficients are mole ratios, not gram ratios. 1 g, 3 g and 2 g do not match the molar masses.", "2": "3 mol of H₂ has a mass of 3 × 2 = 6 g, not 3 g.", "3": "The equation shows 3 mol of H₂ for each mole of N₂, not 1 mol."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0175' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stoichiometric calculations assume that all reactants are completely converted into products, that the laws of conservation of mass and definite proportions are obeyed, and that no side reaction occurs. A reversible reaction that reaches equilibrium never converts the reactants completely, so it goes against these assumptions.', wrong = '{"0": "Complete conversion of reactants into products is one of the stated assumptions.", "1": "That no side reaction occurs is one of the stated assumptions.", "2": "That the law of conservation of mass is obeyed is one of the stated assumptions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0176' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The law of definite proportions says that a pure compound always contains the same elements combined in the same fixed ratio by mass, however it is made. Pure water, for example, always contains hydrogen and oxygen in the mass ratio 1 : 8.', wrong = '{"0": "Matter being neither created nor destroyed is the law of conservation of mass.", "1": "Gases reacting in simple whole-number volume ratios is Gay-Lussac''s law of combining volumes.", "2": "Equal volumes of gases containing equal numbers of molecules is Avogadro''s law."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0177' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The balanced equation shows that 2 mol of H₂ react with 1 mol of O₂, a mole ratio of 2 : 1. So 5 mol of H₂ needs 5 × (1 ÷ 2) = 2.5 mol of O₂. A quick check: 5 mol is 2.5 times the 2 mol of H₂ in the equation, so the O₂ is 2.5 × 1 mol.', wrong = '{"1": "1.25 mol comes from dividing 5 by 4 instead of by 2.", "2": "5 mol assumes a 1 : 1 ratio, but only one O₂ is needed for every two H₂.", "3": "10 mol inverts the ratio, multiplying by 2 instead of dividing by 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0178' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The balanced equation shows that 1 mol of C₃H₈ gives 3 mol of CO₂, since each propane has three carbon atoms. So 2 mol of propane gives 2 × 3 = 6 mol of CO₂.', wrong = '{"0": "2 mol assumes a 1 : 1 ratio, but each propane molecule gives three CO₂ molecules.", "2": "3 mol is the CO₂ from only one mole of propane, not two.", "3": "10 mol is the oxygen needed for 2 mol of propane (2 × 5), not the CO₂ formed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0179' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles of Mg = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.5 mol. The equation gives Mg : MgO as 2 : 2, which is 1 : 1, so 0.5 mol of MgO forms. The molar mass of MgO is 24 + 16 = 40 g mol⁻¹, so the mass is 0.5 × 40 = 20 g.', wrong = '{"0": "10 g would be only 0.25 mol of MgO. It halves the mole ratio, which is really 1 : 1.", "1": "24 g is the molar mass of magnesium, not the mass of oxide formed.", "3": "40 g is one whole mole of MgO, which would need 24 g of magnesium, not 12 g."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0180' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles of Zn = 6.5 ÷ 65 = 0.1 mol. The equation shows Zn : H₂ as 1 : 1, so 0.1 mol of H₂ forms. At STP this occupies 0.1 × 22.4 = 2.24 dm³. The coefficient 2 belongs to HCl, so it does not change the H₂ amount.', wrong = '{"0": "1.12 dm³ is 0.05 mol of H₂. It halves the amount, but the Zn : H₂ ratio is 1 : 1.", "2": "4.48 dm³ is 0.2 mol. The coefficient 2 belongs to HCl, not to H₂.", "3": "22.4 dm³ is the volume of a whole mole of H₂, which would need 65 g of zinc."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0181' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The equation shows CaCO₃ : CaO as 1 : 1, so 0.2 mol of CaCO₃ gives 0.2 mol of CaO. The molar mass of CaO is 40 + 16 = 56 g mol⁻¹. Mass = 0.2 × 56 = 11.2 g.', wrong = '{"0": "5.6 g is only 0.1 mol of CaO, half the amount that forms.", "2": "20 g is the mass of 0.2 mol of CaCO₃ (0.2 × 100), not of CaO.", "3": "56 g is one whole mole of CaO, which would need 1 mol of CaCO₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0182' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the same temperature and pressure, gas volumes react in the same ratio as their moles. The equation gives CH₄ : O₂ = 1 : 2. So 5 dm³ of methane needs 2 × 5 = 10 dm³ of oxygen.', wrong = '{"0": "2.5 dm³ inverts the ratio, dividing by 2 instead of multiplying.", "2": "5 dm³ assumes a 1 : 1 ratio, but each CH₄ needs two O₂.", "3": "15 dm³ adds the methane volume to the oxygen needed (5 + 10)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0183' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The limiting reactant is the one that is used up first. Once it runs out the reaction stops, even though other reactants are left. So the maximum amount of product depends on the limiting reactant.', wrong = '{"0": "The largest mass does not decide which reactant runs out. The moles and the mole ratio in the equation decide it.", "1": "A high molar mass alone does not make a reactant limiting. What matters is moles available compared with moles needed.", "2": "The reactant left over at the end is the excess reactant, the opposite of the limiting one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0184' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In industry a large amount of an inexpensive reactant is often supplied. This makes sure that all of the expensive reactant is converted into the desired product, so the maximum amount of product is obtained. A larger amount of reactant also increases the rate of reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "Supplying one reactant in excess does not make a reaction reversible.", "1": "The aim of using an excess is to get the maximum amount of product, not less.", "3": "Using an excess of one reactant increases the rate of reaction rather than lowering it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0185' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The equation needs H₂ and O₂ in a 2 : 1 ratio. 4 mol of H₂ needs only 4 ÷ 2 = 2 mol of O₂, and 3 mol is available. So H₂ runs out first and is limiting. It gives 4 mol of H₂O (H₂ : H₂O = 1 : 1), and 3 − 2 = 1 mol of O₂ is left over.', wrong = '{"0": "3 mol of O₂ would need 6 mol of H₂, but only 4 mol is available, so H₂ is limiting.", "1": "The reactants are not in the 2 : 1 ratio of the equation, so one must be in excess.", "2": "H₂ is limiting, but water formed equals the moles of H₂ (2 : 2 ratio), so 4 mol forms, not 3."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0186' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles: N₂ = 28 ÷ 28 = 1 mol and H₂ = 9 ÷ 2 = 4.5 mol. 1 mol of N₂ needs only 3 mol of H₂, so N₂ is limiting and H₂ is in excess. 1 mol of N₂ gives 2 mol of NH₃, which is 2 × 17 = 34 g.', wrong = '{"1": "17 g is one mole of NH₃, but 1 mol of N₂ gives 2 mol of NH₃.", "2": "51 g assumes all 4.5 mol of H₂ reacts to give 3 mol of NH₃, but that would need 1.5 mol of N₂.", "3": "37 g just adds the reactant masses (28 + 9). 3 g of H₂ is left unreacted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0187' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles: CO = 56 ÷ 28 = 2 mol and O₂ = 48 ÷ 32 = 1.5 mol. The equation needs CO : O₂ = 2 : 1, so 2 mol of CO uses only 1 mol of O₂. That leaves 1.5 − 1 = 0.5 mol of O₂, which is 0.5 × 32 = 16 g.', wrong = '{"0": "O₂ is in excess, since only 1 of the 1.5 mol reacts, so some oxygen remains.", "2": "8 g is 0.25 mol of O₂. The amount left over is 0.5 mol.", "3": "32 g is the 1 mol of oxygen that reacts, not the amount left over."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0188' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A fire needs fuel and oxygen, and the oxygen of the air is the excess reactant in burning. Smothering with foam, inert gas or CO₂ cuts off the air and lowers the oxygen supply below the level needed for combustion. So the fire goes out.', wrong = '{"0": "Adding more fuel would feed the fire rather than put it out.", "1": "A catalyst speeds up a reaction. Foam and CO₂ do not speed anything up, and they work by stopping combustion.", "2": "Raising the temperature would help the fire burn. Smothering works by removing oxygen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0189' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles = C × V. Ag⁺ = 0.2 × 0.100 = 0.02 mol and Cl⁻ = 0.2 × 0.050 = 0.01 mol. They react 1 : 1, so Cl⁻ runs out first and is limiting. Only 0.01 mol of AgCl can precipitate.', wrong = '{"0": "0.03 mol adds the two amounts together. Each AgCl needs one Ag⁺ and one Cl⁻.", "1": "0.02 mol is the silver present, but there is only 0.01 mol of chloride to combine with it.", "3": "0.2 is the concentration in mol dm⁻³, not the amount of AgCl formed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0190' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The theoretical yield is the maximum amount of product that could be obtained from a given amount of reactant. It is calculated from the balanced chemical equation, assuming complete reaction. The amount really collected, the actual yield, is usually less.', wrong = '{"0": "The mass of product actually obtained is the actual yield, not the theoretical yield.", "1": "Reactant left over at the end is the excess reactant, not a yield.", "2": "It is the other way round. The actual yield is usually less than the theoretical yield."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0191' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The actual yield is often lower than the theoretical yield because of losses during filtration, washing or crystallization, competing side reactions, and reversible reactions that do not go to completion. Conservation of mass does not lower the yield. It is one of the laws assumed when the theoretical yield is calculated.', wrong = '{"0": "Losses of product when it is filtered, washed or crystallized do reduce the actual yield.", "1": "A competing side reaction uses up some reactant to make other products, so it lowers the yield.", "2": "A reversible reaction does not go to completion, so it gives less product than calculated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0192' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Percentage yield = (actual yield ÷ theoretical yield) × 100. Here it is (15 ÷ 20) × 100 = 0.75 × 100 = 75%. The other 25% of the expected product was lost during the process.', wrong = '{"1": "25% is the percentage of product lost (5 g out of 20 g), not the yield.", "2": "133% comes from dividing the theoretical yield by the actual yield (20 ÷ 15), the wrong way round.", "3": "5 is the mass lost in grams (20 − 15), not a percentage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0193' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles of Mg = 4.8 ÷ 24 = 0.2 mol. Mg : MgO is 1 : 1, so the theoretical yield is 0.2 mol × 40 g mol⁻¹ = 8.0 g of MgO. Percentage yield = (6.0 ÷ 8.0) × 100 = 75%.', wrong = '{"0": "60% would need a theoretical yield of 10 g, but 0.2 mol of MgO is 8.0 g.", "2": "80% comes from comparing the mass of Mg with the MgO collected (4.8 ÷ 6.0) instead of using the theoretical yield.", "3": "125% divides the MgO collected by the mass of magnesium (6.0 ÷ 4.8). A yield cannot exceed 100%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0194' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Percentage yield compares the product actually obtained with the maximum possible. It is used as a measure of the efficiency of a reaction: the greater the percentage yield, the more efficient the reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "A higher percentage yield means less product is wasted, so the reaction is more efficient, not less.", "2": "Percentage yield measures how much product is obtained, not how quickly it forms.", "3": "Percentage yield does not show whether a reactant is limiting. Any reaction not mixed in the exact ratio still has one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0195' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stoichiometry is used to work out the exact amount of active ingredient in each dose of a medicine. Too little of a drug has no useful effect, while too much can be toxic. A paracetamol overdose, for example, can cause severe liver damage.', wrong = '{"0": "A paracetamol overdose can be very harmful, causing organ damage and severe liver damage.", "1": "Medicines do not work equally well at any dose. The amount of active ingredient must be exact to be safe and effective.", "3": "Stoichiometry deals with amounts of substances, not with the colour of a tablet."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0196' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Molar mass is the mass of one mole of a substance expressed in grams, with the unit g mol⁻¹. It equals the atomic, molecular or formula mass written in grams. For example, the molar mass of water is 18 g mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "The mass of one atom is tiny, equal to the molar mass divided by Avogadro''s number.", "1": "The number of particles in one gram is not the molar mass. Molar mass is a mass per mole.", "2": "The volume of one mole of a gas is the molar volume, 22.4 dm³ at STP."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0197' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles = number of molecules ÷ Nᴀ = 3.01 × 10²³ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = 0.5 mol. At STP one mole of any ideal gas occupies 22.4 dm³, so 0.5 mol occupies 0.5 × 22.4 = 11.2 dm³, whatever the gas.', wrong = '{"1": "5.6 dm³ is the volume of 0.25 mol, which would be 1.505 × 10²³ molecules.", "2": "22.4 dm³ is the volume of one whole mole, 6.02 × 10²³ molecules.", "3": "44.8 dm³ is the volume of two moles, 1.204 × 10²⁴ molecules."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0198' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One mole of water, 6.02 × 10²³ molecules, has a mass of 18 g. So one molecule has a mass of 18 ÷ (6.02 × 10²³) ≈ 2.99 × 10⁻²³ g. This tiny mass shows why chemists count molecules in moles rather than one by one.', wrong = '{"0": "18 g is the mass of a whole mole of water molecules, not of one molecule.", "2": "1.08 × 10²⁵ comes from multiplying 18 by Avogadro''s number instead of dividing.", "3": "3.34 comes from Nᴀ ÷ 18 = 3.34 × 10²², the number of molecules in 1 g. With the sign of the power changed it is about 11 times too large."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0199' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The word stoichiometry comes from the Greek stoicheion, meaning element, and metron, meaning measure. It is the part of chemistry that deals with the quantitative relationships between reactants and products in a balanced chemical equation.', wrong = '{"0": "Mole and mass are quantities used in stoichiometry, but they are not the meaning of the word.", "2": "Gas and volume are used in stoichiometric calculations, but they are not the meaning of the word.", "3": "Reaction and rate belong to reaction kinetics. They are not the meaning of stoichiometry."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0200' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles of NaHCO₃ = mass ÷ molar mass = 4.2 ÷ 84 = 0.05 mol. The balanced equation shows NaHCO₃ and HCl reacting in a 1 : 1 ratio. So 0.05 mol of HCl is neutralized.', wrong = '{"1": "0.025 mol would be neutralized by only 2.1 g of NaHCO₃. It halves the 1 : 1 ratio.", "2": "0.1 mol would need 0.1 × 84 = 8.4 g of NaHCO₃, twice the mass given.", "3": "0.5 mol is ten times too large. It would need 42 g of NaHCO₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0201' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every substance is liquid only between its melting point and its boiling point, and this range is fairly small compared with the conditions on Earth. So the liquid state of any substance exists only within a relatively narrow range of temperature and pressure. That is why liquids are less common than solids and gases.', wrong = '{"0": "Liquids do have reasonably strong intermolecular forces. These forces are what keep them from becoming gases.", "1": "Liquids are hard to compress, but that property has nothing to do with how common they are.", "3": "Liquids are not unstable. They are simply stable over only a limited range of conditions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0202' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a gas the molecules are very far apart compared with their own size. Nearly 99.9% of the volume of a gas is empty space. Pressure can easily push the molecules closer together into this space, so gases are highly compressible.', wrong = '{"0": "Gas molecules take up only a tiny part of the gas volume. Their size is not why gases compress.", "1": "Gases have no definite volume. They fill whatever container they are in.", "2": "Intermolecular forces in gases are negligible. Strong attraction is not what makes them compressible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0203' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a gas expands suddenly, the molecules do work against the attractions between them, and the gas cools down. This cooling on sudden expansion is called the Joule–Thomson effect. It is used in refrigeration and in liquefying gases.', wrong = '{"1": "The Tyndall effect is the scattering of a beam of light by colloidal particles.", "2": "Brownian motion is the random, zig-zag movement of tiny particles suspended in a fluid.", "3": "The greenhouse effect is the trapping of heat in the atmosphere by gases such as CO₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0204' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gas molecules move randomly at high speed and keep hitting the walls of their container. Each collision pushes on the wall, and the total force of these collisions per unit area is the gas pressure. More collisions per second, or harder collisions, give a higher pressure.', wrong = '{"0": "The weight of gas molecules is negligible compared with the force of their collisions with the walls.", "1": "Intermolecular forces in gases are negligible. Attraction between molecules would actually lower the pressure.", "3": "The molecules take up almost none of the volume. In an ideal gas their volume is treated as zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0205' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ideal gas is a model in which the gas particles themselves have zero volume and there are no (negligible) forces between them. Real gases behave close to this at low pressure and high temperature.', wrong = '{"1": "This is the opposite of an ideal gas. Real gases deviate from ideal behaviour because of molecular volume and attractions.", "2": "No gas has a definite shape. A gas takes the shape of its container.", "3": "Gases are easily compressed because most of their volume is empty space."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0206' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Charles'' law says that the volume of a gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature, V ∝ T. This holds only if the amount of gas (n) and the pressure (P) are kept constant.', wrong = '{"0": "Keeping n and T constant is the condition for Boyle''s law, V ∝ 1/P.", "1": "V is one of the two variables in Charles'' law, so it cannot be held constant.", "2": "Keeping P and T constant is the condition for Avogadro''s law, V ∝ n."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0207' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the ideal gas equation PV = nRT, R is the general gas constant. With pressure in atmospheres and volume in dm³, R = 0.0821 atm dm³ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. In SI units the same constant is 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "8.21 has the decimal point in the wrong place. It is 100 times the correct value.", "2": "22.4 dm³ is the molar volume of a gas at STP, not the gas constant.", "3": "6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ is Avogadro''s number, not the gas constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0208' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rearranging PV = nRT gives P = nRT ÷ V. Substituting: P = (2.0 × 0.0821 × 300) ÷ 24.63 = 49.26 ÷ 24.63 = 2.0 atm. A quick check: doubling n at fixed V and T doubles P, and 1 mol here would give 1.0 atm.', wrong = '{"0": "0.5 atm comes from dividing by n instead of multiplying by it.", "1": "1.0 atm is the pressure of only 1 mol of gas under these conditions.", "3": "4.0 atm is double the correct value, as if n were counted twice."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0209' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Since n = m ÷ M, the ideal gas equation becomes PV = (m ÷ M)RT, so M = mRT ÷ PV. Substituting: M = (3.2 × 0.0821 × 300) ÷ (2.0 × 1.231) = 78.8 ÷ 2.462 ≈ 32. The relative molecular mass is 32, as for O₂.', wrong = '{"1": "16 is half the correct value, as if an extra factor of 2 were divided out.", "2": "64 comes from leaving out the pressure of 2.0 atm, dividing 78.8 by 1.231 only.", "3": "44 is the molar mass of CO₂. It does not come from these data."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0210' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Liquid molecules are in constant random motion, so two miscible liquids slowly mix by diffusion. Liquid molecules are much closer together than gas molecules and keep colliding, so they diffuse much more slowly than gases.', wrong = '{"0": "Liquids do diffuse. Miscible liquids mix on their own, which shows that their molecules move.", "1": "Liquids are about 10⁵ times less compressible than gases, because their molecules are already close together.", "3": "Liquid molecules are always moving randomly, which is why liquids can flow and diffuse."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0211' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electrons are always moving, so at any instant the electron cloud of a molecule may be uneven, making it a temporary dipole. This instantaneous dipole disturbs the electrons of a neighbouring molecule and induces a dipole in it. The two dipoles attract each other for a moment, and this is the London dispersion force.', wrong = '{"0": "Attraction between the permanent positive and negative ends of polar molecules is a dipole–dipole force.", "1": "Complete transfer of electrons forms an ionic bond, not an intermolecular force.", "2": "Hydrogen bonded to F, O or N is the requirement for hydrogen bonding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0212' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Halogen molecules are non-polar, so only London forces act between them. Down the group the molecules become larger with bigger electron clouds, which are more easily polarized. London forces become stronger, so boiling points rise and the state changes from gas (F₂, Cl₂) to liquid (Br₂) to solid (I₂).', wrong = '{"0": "Halogen molecules contain no hydrogen, so they cannot form hydrogen bonds.", "2": "Diatomic halogen molecules have two identical atoms, so they are non-polar, not more polar down the group.", "3": "Halogen molecules are held together by weak intermolecular forces, not ionic bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0213' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Noble gases exist as single non-polar atoms, held together only by London forces. These forces grow stronger as the atoms get larger and more polarizable. So boiling points rise down the group, from helium (4.22 K) to xenon (165 K), the highest here.', wrong = '{"0": "Helium has the smallest atoms and the weakest London forces, so it has the lowest boiling point, 4.22 K.", "1": "Neon boils at 27.1 K, far below xenon, because its atoms are smaller.", "2": "Argon boils at 87.3 K, below krypton and xenon, which have larger atoms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0214' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Molecules of the same mass can still differ in London forces because of their shape. A long, straight chain has a larger surface area, giving more contact between neighbouring molecules and stronger London forces. So n-pentane has the highest boiling point, and the compact, nearly spherical 2,2-dimethylpropane has the lowest.', wrong = '{"0": "Being compact gives 2,2-dimethylpropane the smallest surface area, the weakest London forces and the lowest boiling point.", "1": "Branching reduces surface area, so 2-methylbutane has a lower boiling point than straight-chain n-pentane.", "3": "The isomers have different shapes and surface areas, so their London forces and boiling points differ."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0215' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chlorine is more electronegative than carbon, so CHCl₃ is a polar molecule with a partial negative end at the chlorine atoms and a partial positive end near hydrogen. The positive end of one molecule attracts the negative end of a neighbour. This is a permanent dipole–permanent dipole force.', wrong = '{"0": "The hydrogen in CHCl₃ is bonded to carbon, not to F, O or N, so it cannot form hydrogen bonds.", "1": "Metallic bonding is found only in metals, between metal ions and delocalized electrons.", "2": "Chloroform is made of neutral molecules. It contains no ions, so there is no ionic bonding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0216' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A hydrogen bond needs a hydrogen atom bonded to a highly electronegative atom (F, O or N) that also carries a lone pair. In CH₄ every hydrogen is bonded to carbon, which is not electronegative enough and has no lone pair. So methane molecules cannot hydrogen bond with each other.', wrong = '{"0": "In water hydrogen is bonded to oxygen, which is highly electronegative and has two lone pairs.", "1": "In ammonia hydrogen is bonded to nitrogen, which is highly electronegative and has a lone pair.", "2": "In HF hydrogen is bonded to fluorine, the most electronegative element, which has three lone pairs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0217' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The hydrogen bond is the strongest of the intermolecular forces, but it is still much weaker than chemical bonds such as ionic, metallic and covalent bonds. Its strength is roughly one tenth of an ordinary covalent bond.', wrong = '{"1": "Hydrogen bonds are much weaker than covalent bonds, roughly a tenth as strong.", "2": "Ionic bonds are far stronger than hydrogen bonds.", "3": "Hydrogen bonds have real strength. They give water its unusually high boiling point."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0218' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of hydrogen bonds a molecule can form on average depends on both its number of H atoms on F, O or N and its number of lone pairs on those atoms. A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms and two lone pairs on oxygen, which match up evenly. So each water molecule forms two hydrogen bonds on average.', wrong = '{"1": "One hydrogen bond per molecule is the case for NH₃ (one lone pair) and HF (one hydrogen atom).", "2": "Three would need more matching hydrogen atoms and lone pairs than water has.", "3": "Each hydrogen bond links two molecules, so counted per molecule the average is two, not four."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0219' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of hydrogen bonds a molecule forms depends on both its H atoms and its lone pairs. NH₃ has three hydrogen atoms but only one lone pair on nitrogen, so the lone pair limits it to one hydrogen bond per molecule on average. Its hydrogen bonding is weaker than water''s, so it boils at a much lower temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "Nitrogen is highly electronegative, which is why NH₃ can form hydrogen bonds at all.", "1": "Ammonia is a polar molecule, because of its polar N–H bonds and pyramidal shape.", "3": "Ammonia molecules are small. Size is not what limits the number of hydrogen bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0220' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'HF has three lone pairs but only one hydrogen atom, so it forms one hydrogen bond per molecule on average, just like NH₃. However, fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, so the H–F bond is more polar and each hydrogen bond is stronger. This gives HF the higher boiling point.', wrong = '{"0": "HF has only one hydrogen atom, while NH₃ has three.", "1": "HF is strongly polar, because fluorine is the most electronegative element.", "2": "NH₃ does have a lone pair on nitrogen. That single lone pair limits it to one hydrogen bond per molecule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0221' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When water freezes, hydrogen bonds hold the molecules in an open, cage-like structure with empty spaces. Ice therefore occupies about 9% more space than the same mass of liquid water, so it is less dense and floats. The floating ice layer insulates the water below, which stays liquid, so fish survive.', wrong = '{"0": "Ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why it floats instead of sinking.", "2": "Water has a high heat capacity, not a low one, and that is not the main reason here.", "3": "The floating ice layer is a poor conductor. It acts as an insulating blanket over the water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0222' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Water has a high specific heat capacity, 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹. Much of the heat supplied goes into breaking its strong hydrogen bonds rather than raising its temperature. So water can absorb or release a lot of heat with only a small temperature change.', wrong = '{"1": "Water''s boiling point is unusually high for its size, not low, also because of hydrogen bonding.", "2": "Water is a polar molecule, with polar O–H bonds and a bent shape.", "3": "Water molecules are held mainly by hydrogen bonds, which are much stronger than London forces alone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0223' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From H₂S to H₂Te, boiling points rise because the molecules get larger and London forces get stronger. Water breaks this trend: it has extensive hydrogen bonding, which needs much more energy to overcome. So water has the highest boiling point of the group 16 hydrides.', wrong = '{"0": "H₂S has the lowest boiling point here. Sulfur is not electronegative enough for strong hydrogen bonding.", "1": "H₂Se boils lower than H₂Te and far lower than water, since it has only weaker forces.", "2": "H₂Te has the highest boiling point among the heavier hydrides, but water''s hydrogen bonding puts it higher still."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0224' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A molecule inside a liquid is pulled equally in all directions, but a molecule at the surface feels a net inward pull. This surface tension makes a liquid shrink its surface to the smallest area possible. For a given volume, a sphere has the smallest surface area, so drops are spherical.', wrong = '{"0": "Viscosity is a liquid''s resistance to flow. It does not make drops round.", "1": "Vapour pressure is about evaporation from the surface, not about the shape of drops.", "3": "Gravity tends to flatten drops. Surface tension is what pulls them into spheres."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0225' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Surface tension comes from the attraction between liquid molecules. As temperature rises, the molecules gain kinetic energy and the effect of these attractions weakens. So surface tension decreases as temperature increases.', wrong = '{"0": "Higher temperature weakens the effect of intermolecular forces, so surface tension falls rather than rises.", "1": "Surface tension falls steadily as temperature rises. It does not dip and then rise again.", "2": "Surface tension depends on temperature, because temperature changes how strongly molecules hold together."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0226' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Liquids with stronger intermolecular forces have higher surface tension. Water''s strong hydrogen bonding gives it 72.75 × 10⁻³ N m⁻¹ at 20 °C. This is far higher than ethanol (22.75), acetone (23.70) and benzene (28.85), in the same units.', wrong = '{"1": "Ethanol''s surface tension, 22.75 × 10⁻³ N m⁻¹, is only about a third of water''s.", "2": "Acetone''s surface tension, 23.70 × 10⁻³ N m⁻¹, is much lower than water''s.", "3": "Benzene''s surface tension, 28.85 × 10⁻³ N m⁻¹, is much lower than water''s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0227' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Viscosity is a liquid''s resistance to flow, caused by attractions between its molecules. Raising the temperature increases the average kinetic energy of the molecules, so they overcome these attractions more easily and flow more freely. So viscosity decreases.', wrong = '{"1": "Faster-moving molecules overcome attractions more easily, so they flow more readily and viscosity falls.", "2": "Viscosity depends on temperature. Honey, for example, flows much more easily when warmed.", "3": "A warm liquid still resists flow. Its viscosity falls but does not become zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0228' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Viscosity rises with the strength of attraction between molecules. Water molecules are held by strong and extensive hydrogen bonds. Ethanol forms hydrogen bonds to a much smaller extent, and hexane, a hydrocarbon, cannot form them at all. So water is the most viscous of the three.', wrong = '{"0": "Water molecules are actually smaller than hexane and ethanol molecules.", "1": "Hexane is a hydrocarbon with no H on F, O or N, so it cannot form hydrogen bonds.", "3": "Ethanol can form hydrogen bonds through its O–H group, but fewer than water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0229' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Water seeps through the tiny pores of the clay pot and evaporates from its outer surface. Only molecules with high kinetic energy escape, and the energy they need is taken from the remaining water. So the average kinetic energy, and the temperature, of the water inside fall.', wrong = '{"0": "No chemical reaction takes place between clay and water. The cooling comes from evaporation.", "1": "Evaporation lowers the temperature of the remaining liquid, because the most energetic molecules leave.", "2": "Clay is a poor conductor of heat, and conduction is not what cools the water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0230' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a closed container, molecules evaporate from the liquid and some vapour molecules condense back. Eventually the rates of evaporation and condensation become equal, a dynamic equilibrium. The pressure exerted by the vapour at this point, at a given temperature, is the vapour pressure.', wrong = '{"1": "In an open container the vapour escapes and equilibrium is never reached. Vapour pressure is defined for a closed system.", "2": "Vapour pressure is exerted by the vapour above the liquid, not by the liquid pressing on the walls.", "3": "A gas at STP is a set of standard conditions. It does not define vapour pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0231' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Vapour pressure depends on how easily molecules escape from the liquid, which depends on the liquid''s nature and its temperature. Raising the temperature gives more molecules enough energy to escape, so the vapour pressure rises. It does not depend on the amount of liquid, the volume of the container or the surface area.', wrong = '{"0": "Vapour pressure does not depend on the amount of liquid, as long as some liquid is present at equilibrium.", "1": "Vapour pressure does not depend on the volume of the container. A larger space just holds more vapour at the same pressure.", "2": "A larger surface speeds up both evaporation and condensation equally, so the vapour pressure is unchanged."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0232' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Vapour pressure is higher when the intermolecular forces are weaker, because molecules escape more easily. Diethyl ether has no O–H group, so its molecules cannot hydrogen bond with each other. Its vapour pressure is therefore far higher than that of water, ethylene glycol or glycerine, which are all hydrogen bonded.', wrong = '{"0": "Water''s hydrogen bonding holds its molecules more strongly, so its vapour pressure is lower than ether''s.", "1": "Ethylene glycol has two O–H groups and strong hydrogen bonding, so it has a low vapour pressure.", "3": "Glycerine has three O–H groups and very extensive hydrogen bonding, so its vapour pressure is very low."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0233' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A liquid boils when its vapour pressure becomes equal to the external pressure. At Murree the air pressure is about 700 torr, less than 760 torr at sea level. Water''s vapour pressure reaches 700 torr at a lower temperature, so it boils at about 98 °C.', wrong = '{"1": "A higher external pressure would raise the boiling point above 100 °C, not lower it.", "2": "Hydrogen bonding in water is the same everywhere. It does not change with location.", "3": "Purity is not the reason. Dissolved impurities would raise the boiling point, not lower it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0234' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pressure cooker is a closed container, so the steam cannot escape and the pressure on the water surface increases. Water must then reach a higher temperature before its vapour pressure equals this pressure. So the boiling point rises above 100 °C, the water is hotter and food cooks faster.', wrong = '{"0": "Higher pressure raises the boiling point. Lower pressure, as at high altitude, lowers it.", "2": "The steam is kept inside the cooker. That is how the pressure builds up.", "3": "The food absorbs heat faster from the hotter water, not less."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0235' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At 1 atm the boiling points are acetone 56.00 °C, ethanol 78.30 °C, water 100.00 °C and acetic acid 118.50 °C. Acetic acid has the highest value. Its molecules are heavier than water molecules and are strongly hydrogen bonded, often in pairs.', wrong = '{"0": "Acetone boils at only 56.00 °C. It cannot form hydrogen bonds between its own molecules.", "1": "Ethanol boils at 78.30 °C, lower than water and acetic acid.", "2": "Water boils at 100.00 °C, which is below acetic acid at 118.50 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0236' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A liquid boils when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure. Once it is boiling, any further heat supplied does not raise the temperature. Instead it is used to overcome the intermolecular forces and turn liquid into vapour, so the temperature stays constant at the boiling point.', wrong = '{"1": "The boiling liquid keeps absorbing heat. That heat is used for vaporization, not to raise the temperature.", "2": "At the boiling point the vapour pressure equals the external pressure and stays there. It does not fall.", "3": "Heating an open liquid does not change the external (atmospheric) pressure above it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0237' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The molar heat of vaporization is the amount of heat absorbed by one mole of a liquid to change it into vapour at its boiling point, at 1 atmosphere pressure. The temperature does not change during this process.', wrong = '{"0": "The definition is per mole of liquid and at its boiling point, not per gram at 25 °C.", "2": "The heat needed to melt one mole of a solid is the molar heat of fusion.", "3": "Condensation releases heat. Vaporization is the reverse process, which absorbs heat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0238' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The molar heat of vaporization is the heat for one mole, so the heat needed is proportional to the amount. Heat = n × ΔHv = 0.5 mol × 40.6 kJ mol⁻¹ = 20.3 kJ.', wrong = '{"0": "10.15 kJ is the heat for only 0.25 mol (40.6 ÷ 4). It halves the answer one time too many.", "2": "40.6 kJ is the heat for one whole mole of water, not 0.5 mol.", "3": "81.2 kJ is the heat for 2 mol. It doubles instead of halving."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0239' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Water has a high heat of fusion, so a large amount of energy is needed to melt ice. This keeps glaciers, icebergs and polar ice caps stable, because they do not melt easily when they absorb some heat. In this way the ice helps regulate Earth''s temperature and sea levels.', wrong = '{"0": "A high heat of fusion means ice needs a lot of energy to melt, so it melts with difficulty, not easily.", "2": "Ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why it floats. Heat of fusion does not change density.", "3": "The importance of a high heat of fusion is the stability of ice, not cooling of the oceans."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0240' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a solid, the particles are held in fixed positions by strong attractive forces. They cannot move from place to place or rotate freely. They can only vibrate about their mean positions, so their kinetic energy is vibrational, and the vibration grows as the temperature rises.', wrong = '{"0": "Particles in a solid cannot move from place to place or rotate freely. Only vibration is possible.", "2": "Translational motion, moving from place to place, is typical of liquids and gases, not solids.", "3": "Solid particles are never completely still. They vibrate about fixed positions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0241' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Spheres cannot fill space completely, because gaps are always left between them. Even in the closest arrangements, hexagonal and cubic close packing, where each sphere touches its neighbours, only 74% of the space is filled. The other 26% is empty space.', wrong = '{"0": "50% is well below what close packing achieves. Spheres can fill up to 74% of space.", "1": "68% is the figure for a less efficient arrangement, not the closest packing. The maximum is 74%.", "3": "Spheres always leave gaps between them, so they can never fill all the space."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0242' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Anisotropic means that a property, such as refractive index or electrical conductivity, has different values when measured in different directions. Crystalline solids are anisotropic because their particles are arranged in a regular pattern that differs along different directions. Amorphous solids are isotropic.', wrong = '{"0": "Melting over a range of temperature describes amorphous solids. Crystalline solids have sharp melting points.", "2": "Crystalline solids have a regular, long-range arrangement of particles. It is amorphous solids that lack this.", "3": "Having the same properties in all directions is called isotropy, a feature of amorphous solids."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0243' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An amorphous solid has no regular, long-range arrangement of particles and no definite geometric shape. Glass is amorphous and softens over a wide range of temperature instead of melting sharply. Other examples are wood, plastic sulfur, charcoal, coal and coke.', wrong = '{"0": "Sodium chloride is a crystalline solid, forming regular cubic crystals.", "1": "Diamond is a crystalline solid with a regular three-dimensional network of carbon atoms.", "3": "Ice is a crystalline solid with an ordered, hydrogen-bonded arrangement of water molecules."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0244' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Crystalline solids have their particles arranged in regular planes. When a crystal is broken, it splits along these definite planes, called cleavage planes. The angle between cleavage planes is characteristic of each crystalline solid.', wrong = '{"0": "Habit is the shape in which a crystal usually grows. It is not the name for the planes along which it breaks.", "2": "Lattice points are the positions of particles in a crystal lattice, not planes of breaking.", "3": "Crystallites are small ordered regions found inside amorphous solids."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0245' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The habit of a crystal is the shape in which it usually grows. The habit stays the same under the same conditions, but it can change when the growth conditions change. Sodium chloride grows as cubes from pure water but as needles when 10% urea is present.', wrong = '{"0": "The needles are still crystals with a regular structure. Only their outer shape has changed.", "2": "Urea changes the growth conditions as an impurity. It does not react with sodium chloride.", "3": "Cleavage planes are a property of the crystal structure, and they are still present in the needles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0246' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A supersaturated solution holds more dissolved solute than it can at equilibrium, so it is not in equilibrium with the solid. It can stay like this until something starts crystallization. A small seed crystal gives the particles a surface to build on, so crystallization happens rapidly.', wrong = '{"0": "A saturated solution at equilibrium would not suddenly crystallize. This solution holds more solute than at equilibrium.", "2": "Sodium thiosulfate forms well-defined crystals, so it is a crystalline substance, not amorphous.", "3": "Its solubility falls on cooling, from 231 g per 100 cm³ of water at 100 °C to 50 g at room temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0247' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Amorphous solids have no long-range order in the arrangement of their particles. They may still contain tiny ordered regions called crystallites. Because they have no regular structure, they soften and melt over a range of temperature and have no definite heat of fusion.', wrong = '{"0": "Sharp melting points are typical of crystalline solids, not amorphous ones.", "1": "Long-range order is exactly what amorphous solids lack. Only small crystallites may be ordered.", "3": "Amorphous solids are isotropic, with the same properties in all directions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0248' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In most liquid crystals the molecules are rigid and rod-like, about four to eight times longer than they are wide. They line up with their long axes roughly parallel. They can move past one another and spin about their long axis, but they cannot tumble end over end.', wrong = '{"1": "Liquid crystal molecules are long and partly ordered in direction. Spherical, unoriented molecules would not form a liquid crystal.", "2": "Ions held in a rigid lattice describe an ionic crystal such as NaCl, not a liquid crystal.", "3": "Liquid crystals are a condensed phase with molecules close together, not a gas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0249' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some liquid crystals, such as cholesteric ones, change colour with temperature. A tumour is often warmer than the tissue around it, so a liquid crystal film on the skin shows its position. The same temperature sensitivity is used in thermometers and to find faulty connections on circuit boards.', wrong = '{"0": "Glass is an amorphous solid. Making it does not depend on liquid crystals.", "2": "Purifying water does not use the temperature sensitivity of liquid crystals.", "3": "Producing fertilizers is an industrial chemical process unrelated to liquid crystals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0250' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The enthalpy change of a reaction is ΔH = Hₚ − Hᵣ. When the products have less enthalpy than the reactants, ΔH is negative. The extra energy is released to the surroundings as heat, so the reaction is exothermic.', wrong = '{"0": "A positive ΔH means heat is absorbed, not evolved. Here Hₚ < Hᵣ, so ΔH is negative.", "1": "A positive ΔH with heat absorbed needs Hₚ > Hᵣ. That describes an endothermic reaction.", "2": "Energy conservation does not make ΔH zero. The enthalpy difference is passed to the surroundings as heat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0251' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dissolving NH₄Cl in water is endothermic, with ΔH = +16.2 kJ mol⁻¹. When the pack is kneaded, the inner bag breaks and the salt dissolves, absorbing heat from its surroundings. So the pack and anything touching it become cold.', wrong = '{"0": "The water does not freeze. The temperature falls because heat is absorbed as the salt dissolves.", "1": "An exothermic process releases heat and would make the pack warm, as in a hot pack.", "2": "No ammonia gas is released. The cooling comes from the endothermic dissolving of the salt."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0252' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a reaction to occur, colliding molecules must have enough energy to cross the energy barrier between reactants and products. The minimum energy needed just to cross this barrier is the activation energy, Ea. It is shown as the height of the peak above the reactants on an energy profile.', wrong = '{"0": "The enthalpy of reaction is the difference between the enthalpies of products and reactants, not the height of the barrier.", "1": "Bond dissociation energy is the energy needed to break one mole of a particular bond.", "2": "Lattice energy is the enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid forms from its gaseous ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0253' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an endothermic reaction the products lie above the reactants, so ΔH is positive. The top of the energy barrier lies higher still, above the products. So the climb from reactants to the peak (Ea) must be larger than the climb from reactants to products (ΔH).', wrong = '{"0": "For an endothermic reaction both Ea and ΔH are positive, so they have the same sign.", "1": "The peak of the barrier lies above the products, so Ea cannot be smaller than ΔH.", "2": "Every reaction shown on the energy profile has an energy barrier, so Ea is not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0254' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Enthalpy depends on temperature and pressure, so values are compared under agreed standard conditions. For standard enthalpy changes these are a temperature of 25 °C (298 K) and a pressure of 1 atm (101 kPa).', wrong = '{"0": "0 °C is 273 K, which is used for STP in gas calculations, not for standard enthalpy changes.", "2": "273 K and 101 kPa are the STP conditions used for gas volumes. Standard enthalpy data use 298 K.", "3": "The standard temperature is 25 °C, which is 298 K. 25 K would be extremely cold."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0255' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By definition, the standard enthalpy of formation of an element in its standard state is zero. The standard state is the most stable form of the element at 298 K and 1 atm. For carbon this is graphite, not diamond.', wrong = '{"0": "Diamond is not carbon''s most stable form at 298 K, so it is not the standard state and its ΔHf is not zero.", "1": "Water is a compound formed from hydrogen and oxygen, so it has a non-zero ΔHf, about −286 kJ mol⁻¹.", "2": "CO₂ is a compound formed from carbon and oxygen, so its ΔHf is non-zero, about −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0256' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The standard enthalpy change of combustion, ΔHc, is the enthalpy change when one mole of a substance is burnt completely in excess oxygen under standard conditions. Combustion always releases heat, so ΔHc is always negative (exothermic).', wrong = '{"0": "Complete combustion needs excess oxygen, not a limited supply, and ΔHc is always negative.", "1": "ΔHc is defined per mole of the substance burnt, not per mole of oxygen, and it is never endothermic.", "2": "ΔHc is defined per mole of substance burnt, and combustion always releases heat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0257' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The standard enthalpy of formation is the enthalpy change when one mole of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states. It can be exothermic or endothermic. Methane has ΔHf = −74.8 kJ mol⁻¹, while ethene has ΔHf = +52.2 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethene''s ΔHf is +52.2 kJ mol⁻¹, so formation enthalpies are not always negative.", "1": "Methane''s ΔHf is −74.8 kJ mol⁻¹, so formation enthalpies are not always positive.", "3": "Only elements in their standard states have zero ΔHf. Gaseous compounds such as CH₄ do not."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0258' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The standard enthalpy change of atomization is defined per mole of gaseous atoms formed, here ½H₂(g) → H(g). Breaking one mole of H–H bonds (436 kJ) gives two moles of H atoms. So the energy per mole of atoms is 436 ÷ 2 = +218 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "+436 kJ is the energy to break a mole of H–H bonds, which forms two moles of H atoms, not one.", "2": "+872 kJ would form four moles of H atoms. It doubles instead of halving.", "3": "Breaking bonds always absorbs energy, so atomization is positive, not negative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0259' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The standard enthalpy change of atomization is the enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous atoms is formed from the element in its standard state. For chlorine this is ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g). Its value, +121 kJ mol⁻¹, appears in the Born–Haber cycle of NaCl.', wrong = '{"0": "Cl₂(g) → 2Cl(g) forms two moles of atoms. Its enthalpy change is the Cl–Cl bond energy, twice ΔHat.", "1": "This combines atomization with gaining an electron, so it is not atomization alone.", "2": "Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g) represents the first electron affinity of chlorine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0260' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Strong acids and strong bases are fully ionized in dilute solution. When they are mixed, the only change is H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l), and ions such as Na⁺ and Cl⁻ remain unchanged as spectators. So the enthalpy of neutralization is nearly the same for all of them, about −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹ of water formed.', wrong = '{"0": "Molar mass has nothing to do with it. ΔHn is measured per mole of water formed.", "1": "Salts such as NaCl stay dissolved. No precipitate forms in these neutralizations.", "3": "Neutralization is always exothermic. Its ΔHn is negative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0261' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The enthalpy of neutralization is measured per mole of water formed. H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O, so 0.10 mol of H₂SO₄ gives 0.20 mol of water. ΔHn = −11.42 kJ ÷ 0.20 mol = −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "−114.2 comes from dividing by 0.10 mol of acid. That is the heat per mole of H₂SO₄, which forms two moles of water.", "2": "−11.42 kJ is the heat for the whole sample. It must be divided by the moles of water formed.", "3": "−28.6 comes from dividing by 0.40 mol, which counts the water formed twice."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0262' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first electron affinity is the enthalpy change when one mole of electrons is added to one mole of gaseous atoms to form one mole of gaseous 1− ions. A negative sign means the process releases energy. So when gaseous chlorine atoms gain electrons, 348.8 kJ mol⁻¹ of energy is given out.', wrong = '{"0": "Energy absorbed would give a positive value. The negative sign shows energy is released.", "1": "Electron affinity is about adding an electron. Removing an electron is ionization.", "2": "Chlorine forms Cl⁻ ions readily. The negative value shows that this process releases energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0263' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bonds between the same pair of atoms usually have slightly different bond energies in different compounds. This is because a bond''s energy is affected by the other atoms in the molecule. Each value given is the exact C–C bond energy in that particular molecule, and averaging many such values gives the average C–C bond energy.', wrong = '{"0": "These are exact bond energies, one for each molecule. The average bond energy comes from averaging values like these.", "1": "Ethane, propane and butane are alkanes, so all their C–C bonds are single bonds.", "3": "Here the bond energy falls as molar mass increases, and molar mass is not the cause anyway."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0264' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a reaction, breaking the old bonds absorbs energy and forming the new bonds releases energy. If the energy released in forming new bonds is greater than the energy absorbed in breaking old ones, the difference is given out as heat. The reaction is then exothermic, with a negative ΔH.', wrong = '{"0": "The number of bonds does not decide the sign of ΔH. What matters is the total energy of the bonds.", "1": "If more energy is absorbed in breaking bonds than released in forming them, the reaction is endothermic.", "2": "Every chemical reaction breaks some bonds before new ones can form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0265' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'ΔH = Σ(bond energies of bonds broken) − Σ(bond energies of bonds formed). Broken: C=C + H–H = 615 + 436 = 1051 kJ. Formed: C–C + 2 C–H = 348 + 2(413) = 1174 kJ, since the four C–H bonds of ethene appear on both sides and cancel. ΔH = 1051 − 1174 = −123 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "+123 comes from reversing the formula, taking bonds formed minus bonds broken.", "2": "−1051 counts only the energy of the bonds broken, with a negative sign added.", "3": "−413 is just one C–H bond energy with a negative sign. It ignores the other bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0266' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'ΔH = Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed). Broken: 4 C–H + 2 O=O = 4(413) + 2(498) = 1652 + 996 = 2648 kJ. Formed: 2 C=O + 4 O–H = 2(805) + 4(463) = 1610 + 1852 = 3462 kJ. ΔH = 2648 − 3462 = −814 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "+814 comes from taking bonds formed minus bonds broken, which gives the wrong sign.", "2": "−386 ignores the coefficient 2 on O₂ and H₂O: (1652 + 498) − (1610 + 926) = −386.", "3": "−2648 is the energy of the bonds broken only, with a negative sign added."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0267' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For H₂ + Br₂ → 2HBr, ΔH = (436 + 192) − 2(366) = 628 − 732 = −104 kJ. This is for 2 mol of HBr, but the enthalpy of formation is per mole of compound. So ΔHf = −104 ÷ 2 = −52 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "−104 kJ is the enthalpy change for forming 2 mol of HBr. ΔHf is per mole, so it must be halved.", "2": "The bonds formed (732 kJ) release more energy than the bonds broken (628 kJ) absorb, so the value is negative.", "3": "+104 comes from reversing the formula (formed minus broken) and not dividing by 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0268' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The specific heat capacity of water is the heat needed to raise the temperature of 1 g of water by 1 K. Its value is 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ (the same as J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹), often rounded to 4.2 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ in calorimetry calculations.', wrong = '{"1": "4.18 kJ g⁻¹ K⁻¹ is 1000 times too large. The value is in joules per gram, not kilojoules.", "2": "1.00 is the value in calories per gram per kelvin, not in joules.", "3": "41.8 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ is ten times too large."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0269' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A simple glass (coffee-cup) calorimeter is an open vessel used for reactions in solution, such as neutralization. If a reaction produces a gas, the gas escapes and carries energy and mass away, so the heat change cannot be measured properly. It is also unsuitable for reactions whose products reach high temperatures.', wrong = '{"0": "Reactions in dilute aqueous solution are exactly what this calorimeter is designed for.", "2": "It can measure heat evolved in exothermic reactions as well as heat absorbed in endothermic ones.", "3": "Measuring the heat of neutralization is a standard use of this calorimeter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0270' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A glass calorimeter must keep the heat of the reaction inside the solution so that the temperature change can be measured accurately. The outside of the beaker is wrapped in cotton wool, a poor conductor of heat. This insulation minimizes heat exchange with the surrounding air.', wrong = '{"0": "Mixing is done with a separate stirrer. Cotton wool around the outside cannot stir the solution.", "1": "The loosely fitting lid keeps the contents at atmospheric pressure. Cotton wool does not change the pressure.", "3": "Cotton wool is there as insulation. A glass calorimeter is not meant for reactions that give off gases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0271' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heat evolved q = mcΔT = 100 g × 4.2 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ × 6.8 K = 2856 J = 2.856 kJ. Moles of water formed = 1.0 × 50 ÷ 1000 = 0.050 mol, since HCl and NaOH react 1 : 1. ΔHn = −2.856 ÷ 0.050 = −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹, negative because heat is released.', wrong = '{"0": "−28.6 comes from dividing by 0.10 mol, wrongly adding the moles of acid and base. Only 0.050 mol of water forms.", "1": "−2.86 kJ is the heat evolved by this sample. It must be divided by the moles of water formed.", "3": "−114.2 comes from dividing by 0.025 mol, half the 0.050 mol of water actually formed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0272' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In these experiments the acid and base solutions are very dilute, so nearly all of the mass of the mixture is water. The dissolved solutes make up only a tiny part of the mass. So the specific heat capacity of water, 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, can be used for the whole mixture with little error.', wrong = '{"0": "The material of the beaker is not the reason. The heat capacity used is that of the solution, not the glass.", "1": "Solutes do have heat capacities. They are ignored only because they form a negligible part of the mass.", "3": "A glass calorimeter is open to the air and works at constant (atmospheric) pressure, not constant volume."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0273' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Calorie content is the energy released per gram, expressed in kilocalories, where 1 kcal = 4.184 kJ. So 16.7 kJ g⁻¹ ÷ 4.184 kJ kcal⁻¹ ≈ 4.0 kcal g⁻¹. This is typical of carbohydrates and proteins.', wrong = '{"1": "69.9 comes from multiplying 16.7 by 4.184 instead of dividing.", "2": "16.7 is the value in kJ g⁻¹. It has not been converted to kcal.", "3": "0.25 comes from inverting the conversion, 4.184 ÷ 16.7."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0274' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find the energy per gram: 1368 kJ mol⁻¹ ÷ 46 g mol⁻¹ = 29.7 kJ g⁻¹. Then convert to kilocalories, using 1 kcal = 4.184 kJ: 29.7 ÷ 4.184 ≈ 7.1 kcal g⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "29.7 is the energy content in kJ g⁻¹. It still has to be divided by 4.184 to give kcal.", "2": "327 comes from converting kJ mol⁻¹ to kcal mol⁻¹ (1368 ÷ 4.184) without dividing by the molar mass.", "3": "1.7 comes from dividing by 4.184 twice: 29.7 ÷ 4.184 ÷ 4.184."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0275' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hess''s law states that the total enthalpy change of a reaction is the same whichever route is taken, as long as the initial and final conditions are the same. So a reaction done in one step or in several steps has the same overall ΔH. This follows from the conservation of energy.', wrong = '{"0": "The route, including how many steps it has, does not change the total ΔH.", "2": "Activation energy depends on the pathway taken, but ΔH does not. The two are not equal.", "3": "The enthalpy changes of the steps add up to the overall ΔH. The total is not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0276' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some enthalpy changes cannot be measured directly in a calorimeter. Tetrachloromethane cannot be made simply by combining carbon and chlorine, so its enthalpy of formation cannot be measured directly. Hess''s law allows it to be calculated from the enthalpy changes of other reactions that can be measured.', wrong = '{"0": "CCl₄ is a compound of carbon and chlorine, not an element.", "2": "Forming CCl₄ does involve an enthalpy change. The difficulty is only that it cannot be measured directly.", "3": "Burning in oxygen is not the reason given. CCl₄ does not burn readily and was once used in fire extinguishers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0277' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adding the two given equations gives the overall reaction: S(s) + 1½O₂(g) → SO₃(g), since the SO₂ cancels. By Hess''s law, the enthalpy changes add in the same way: ΔH = (−297) + (−99) = −396 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "−198 comes from subtracting the second step instead of adding it: −297 − (−99).", "2": "+198 comes from subtracting the first step from the second. Both steps should be added.", "3": "−594 is twice the first step (2 × −297) and leaves out the second. Each step is used once."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0278' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'ΔHr = ΣΔHf(products) − ΣΔHf(reactants). H₂ is an element in its standard state, so its ΔHf is zero. ΔHr = (−84.7) − (+52.2 + 0) = −136.9 kJ mol⁻¹. The negative value shows that adding hydrogen to ethene is exothermic.', wrong = '{"0": "−32.5 comes from adding the two values (−84.7 + 52.2) instead of subtracting.", "1": "+136.9 comes from reactants minus products, which gives the right size but the wrong sign.", "3": "+32.5 comes from adding the two values and then changing the sign."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0279' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Burning the reactants and burning the product both end as CO₂ and H₂O, so by Hess''s law ΔHf = ΣΔHc(reactants) − ΣΔHc(products). ΔHf = [−394 + 2(−286)] − (−890) = −966 + 890 = −76 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "+76 comes from taking products minus reactants for combustion data, which reverses the sign.", "2": "+210 uses only one mole of H₂: (−394 − 286) + 890. The coefficient 2 must be included.", "3": "−1856 adds all three combustion enthalpies (−394 − 572 − 890) instead of subtracting methane''s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0280' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When an ionic solid dissolves, water molecules surround each ion, with their oppositely charged ends pointing towards it. These ion–dipole attractions are new interactions, and forming them releases energy. So hydration enthalpy is always negative (exothermic).', wrong = '{"0": "Breaking up the lattice absorbs energy. That is a separate step from hydration.", "1": "Ions keep their charges in water. They are simply surrounded by water molecules.", "2": "The water molecules stay intact around the ions. They do not split into H⁺ and OH⁻."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0281' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hydration enthalpy is more exothermic for ions with higher charge density, meaning a larger charge on a smaller ion. Such ions attract water molecules more strongly. Al³⁺ has the highest charge and the smallest radius of these ions, so its hydration releases the most energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Na⁺ has only a +1 charge, so it attracts water molecules less strongly than Al³⁺.", "1": "K⁺ has a +1 charge and a large radius, so it has the lowest charge density of these ions.", "2": "Mg²⁺ has a high charge density, but Al³⁺ has a larger charge and a smaller radius."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0282' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rearranging the cycle gives ΔHsol = ΔHhyd − ΔHlatt. The total hydration enthalpy is −406 + (−377) = −783 kJ mol⁻¹. So ΔHsol = −783 − (−787) = +4 kJ mol⁻¹. The small positive value shows that dissolving NaCl is slightly endothermic.', wrong = '{"1": "−4 comes from reversing the subtraction, ΔHlatt − ΔHhyd.", "2": "−1570 comes from adding the lattice energy to the hydration enthalpies (−787 − 783).", "3": "+1570 adds the sizes of the two quantities and gives a large positive value. They must be subtracted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0283' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lattice energy depends on how strongly the ions attract, which increases as the ions get smaller and come closer together. Among the alkali metal halides, lattice energy falls as either the cation or the anion gets larger. LiF has the smallest cation and the smallest anion, so it has the most negative lattice energy.', wrong = '{"0": "K⁺ and Br⁻ are both larger than Li⁺ and F⁻, so the lattice energy of KBr is smaller.", "1": "Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are both larger than Li⁺ and F⁻, so the lattice energy of NaCl is smaller.", "3": "Cs⁺ and I⁻ are the largest ions here, so CsI has the smallest lattice energy of the four."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0284' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lattice energy depends strongly on the charges of the ions. In MgO both ions carry double charges (Mg²⁺ and O²⁻), while in LiF they carry single charges (Li⁺ and F⁻). With similar sizes and the same arrangement, the doubly charged ions attract each other far more strongly, so MgO''s lattice energy is much larger.', wrong = '{"0": "The two compounds have the same arrangement of ions, so crystal structure does not explain the difference.", "1": "The ions are of similar size, and larger ions would lower the lattice energy anyway.", "2": "MgO is an ionic compound. The difference comes from the larger ionic charges."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0285' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Down group 2 the cations get larger, so both lattice energy and hydration energy decrease. For the hydroxides, the lattice energy falls more rapidly than the hydration energy. So ΔHsol becomes more exothermic down the group, and the solubility increases from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂.', wrong = '{"0": "Hydration energy decreases down the group as the cations get larger, and lattice energy also decreases.", "2": "This describes the sulfates, where hydration energy falls faster and solubility decreases down the group.", "3": "Both energies decrease down the group, because the cations get larger."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0286' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The SO₄²⁻ ion is much larger than the cations, so changing the cation from Mg²⁺ to Ba²⁺ changes the lattice energy only a little. The hydration energy of the cation, however, falls by a larger amount as the cation gets bigger. So ΔHsol becomes more endothermic down the group and the solubility decreases.', wrong = '{"1": "Lattice energy decreases slightly down the group as the cation gets larger. It does not increase.", "2": "Hydration energy of the cation decreases down the group, because the larger cations have lower charge density.", "3": "SO₄²⁻ is much larger than OH⁻. That large size is why the lattice energy changes so little."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0287' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lattice energy is the energy released when gaseous ions come together to form one mole of an ionic solid. This process cannot be carried out directly in a calorimeter. Instead it is calculated with the Born–Haber cycle, which applies Hess''s law to other measurable steps such as atomization, ionization energy and electron affinity.', wrong = '{"0": "Forming a crystal from separate gaseous ions cannot be carried out as a single experiment in a calorimeter.", "2": "The boiling point of a compound does not give its lattice energy.", "3": "The Gibbs free energy equation, ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, predicts spontaneity. It is not used to find lattice energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0288' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the Born–Haber cycle, ΔHf = ΔHat(K) + IE₁(K) + ΔHat(Cl) + EA₁(Cl) + ΔHlatt. The first four steps add up to 89 + 419 + 121 − 349 = +280 kJ mol⁻¹. So ΔHlatt = −437 − 280 = −717 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "−157 comes from adding the +280 to ΔHf (−437 + 280) instead of subtracting it.", "2": "−1415 comes from taking the electron affinity as +349, which makes the other steps total 978.", "3": "Lattice energy here means forming the solid from gaseous ions, which releases energy, so it is negative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0289' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Hess''s law the Born–Haber steps add up to the enthalpy of formation: ΔHf = ΔHat(Li) + IE₁ + ΔHat(Cl) + EA₁ + ΔHlatt. Substituting: 161 + 520 + 121 − 349 − 834 = −381 kJ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "+317 comes from using +349 for the electron affinity, which is really −349.", "2": "−834 is the lattice energy alone. All the other steps of the cycle must be added to it.", "3": "−542 leaves out the atomization of lithium (+161)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0290' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Entropy, S, is a measure of the number of ways energy can be distributed within a system. A system with more possible arrangements of its particles and energy is more random or disordered, so entropy can also be thought of as a measure of disorder.', wrong = '{"0": "The heat content of a system is its enthalpy, H, not its entropy.", "2": "The minimum energy needed to start a reaction is the activation energy.", "3": "The energy released when a bond forms is related to bond energy, not entropy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0291' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of ways of arranging particles is xʸ, where x is the number of places and y is the number of particles. Each of the 4 molecules can be in any of the 3 jars, so the number of arrangements is 3⁴ = 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 81.', wrong = '{"0": "12 comes from multiplying 3 × 4 instead of raising 3 to the power 4.", "1": "64 is 4³, with the number of places and particles swapped.", "3": "7 comes from adding 3 + 4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0292' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Among substances of similar chemical nature, harder substances have lower entropy. Diamond is much harder than graphite because its atoms are held by stronger forces in a rigid three-dimensional network. These forces restrict the vibrations of the atoms, so there are fewer ways to spread energy, and diamond has the lower entropy.', wrong = '{"1": "One mole of diamond and one mole of graphite contain the same number of carbon atoms.", "2": "Diamond is a solid at room temperature, and an extremely hard one.", "3": "Graphite is not a compound. Graphite and diamond are both forms of the element carbon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0293' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Particles in a solid are held in fixed positions, those in a liquid move more freely, and those in a gas move freely in all directions. So entropy increases from solid to liquid to gas. For water the standard entropies are ice 48.0, liquid 69.9 and vapour 188.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse order. Solids have the lowest entropy and gases the highest.", "1": "Water vapour has the highest entropy of the three states, not the middle value.", "3": "Water vapour has the highest entropy, not the lowest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0294' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gases have much higher entropy than solids or liquids. When solid KClO₃ decomposes, it produces 3 moles of O₂ gas from no gas at all. The large increase in the number of gas molecules gives a positive entropy change.', wrong = '{"0": "Three moles of gas (2SO₂ + O₂) become two moles (2SO₃), so entropy decreases.", "1": "Condensing water vapour to liquid makes the particles more ordered, so entropy decreases.", "2": "Two gases combine to form a solid, so the system becomes more ordered and entropy decreases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0295' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'ΔS° = ΣS°(products) − ΣS°(reactants). Products: 2(69.9) = 139.8. Reactants: 2(130.6) + 205.0 = 466.2. ΔS° = 139.8 − 466.2 = −326.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. The large decrease fits three moles of gas turning into liquid.', wrong = '{"1": "+326.4 comes from taking reactants minus products, which gives the wrong sign.", "2": "−265.7 ignores the coefficients: 69.9 − (130.6 + 205.0).", "3": "−121.4 leaves out O₂: 139.8 − 2(130.6) = 139.8 − 261.2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0296' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The entropy change of the surroundings is ΔSsurr = −ΔHr ÷ T, with ΔH in joules. ΔSsurr = −(−92 000 J mol⁻¹) ÷ 300 K = +306.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Heat released by an exothermic reaction increases the entropy of the surroundings.', wrong = '{"1": "−306.7 drops the minus sign in the formula ΔSsurr = −ΔH ÷ T.", "2": "+0.307 leaves ΔH in kJ. It must be converted to joules (−92 000 J) to give J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.", "3": "−27 600 comes from multiplying ΔH by T instead of dividing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0297' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Whether a reaction is spontaneous depends on the total entropy change, ΔStotal = ΔSsystem + ΔSsurroundings, which must be positive. Burning calcium is highly exothermic (ΔH = −1270.2 kJ mol⁻¹), so ΔSsurr = 1 270 200 ÷ 298 = +4262.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. This outweighs the decrease in the system, giving ΔStotal = +4054.0 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "The reaction is strongly exothermic (ΔH = −1270.2 kJ mol⁻¹), not endothermic.", "2": "ΔSsystem is never ignored. It is part of ΔStotal, but here it is outweighed.", "3": "Spontaneity needs a positive total entropy change. A negative ΔSsystem alone works against it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0298' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use ΔG = ΔH − TΔS with ΔS in kJ: 120 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ = 0.120 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. ΔG = 40 − 400 × 0.120 = 40 − 48 = −8 kJ mol⁻¹. ΔG is negative, so the reaction is spontaneous at 400 K.', wrong = '{"0": "+88 comes from adding TΔS to ΔH (40 + 48) instead of subtracting it.", "2": "+8 comes from reversing the subtraction, TΔS − ΔH, which gives the wrong sign.", "3": "−47 960 uses ΔS in J with ΔH in kJ (40 − 400 × 120). ΔS must first be converted to kJ."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0299' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The sign of ΔG tells whether a process can occur by itself: negative means spontaneous, positive means non-spontaneous. When ΔG = 0, neither the process nor its reverse has any tendency to happen. The system is at equilibrium.', wrong = '{"0": "A spontaneous process needs ΔG to be negative, not zero.", "1": "The reverse process is spontaneous only when ΔG for the forward process is positive.", "3": "ΔG = 0 describes equilibrium. It says nothing about a reaction being fast or explosive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0300' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, a negative ΔS makes the −TΔS term positive, and it grows as T rises. At low temperature the negative ΔH dominates, so ΔG is negative and the reaction is spontaneous. At high temperature the positive −TΔS term wins, ΔG becomes positive and the reaction is non-spontaneous.', wrong = '{"0": "Spontaneous at all temperatures applies when ΔH is negative and ΔS is positive.", "1": "Non-spontaneous at all temperatures applies when ΔH is positive and ΔS is negative.", "3": "Spontaneous only at high temperature applies when both ΔH and ΔS are positive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0301' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The decomposition becomes spontaneous once ΔG = ΔH − TΔS turns negative, which happens when T > ΔH ÷ ΔS. With ΔS in kJ, T = 178.0 ÷ 0.1604 ≈ 1110 K, about 837 °C. Above this temperature the reaction is spontaneous.', wrong = '{"0": "At 298 K, ΔG = 178.0 − 298 × 0.1604 = 178.0 − 47.8 = +130 kJ, so it is not spontaneous.", "1": "1.11 comes from dividing kJ by J K⁻¹ without converting units (178.0 ÷ 160.4).", "3": "At 1273 K (1000 °C) ΔG is already negative, but this is not the lowest temperature where that happens."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0302' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Collision theory says that reactant particles must collide to react, but not every collision works. An effective collision needs the particles to have at least a certain minimum energy, the activation energy, and to approach each other with the proper orientation. Otherwise they just bounce apart unchanged.', wrong = '{"0": "Equal masses are not a condition for an effective collision. Energy and orientation are what matter.", "1": "Collision theory applies to particles in any phase, and reactions in solids are actually slow.", "2": "Moving in the same direction does not make a collision effective. Particles need enough energy and the right orientation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0303' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Reactant particles collide extremely often, so if every collision gave products, almost all reactions would be over almost instantly. Most reactions are much slower than this. That shows only a small fraction of collisions are effective, those with enough energy and the correct orientation.', wrong = '{"0": "Particles collide very frequently. The problem is that most collisions do not succeed.", "1": "Products can form only as a result of effective collisions between reactants.", "2": "If activation energy were zero, nearly every collision would succeed and reactions would be very fast."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0304' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rate of reaction is the change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time. With concentration in mol dm⁻³ and time in seconds, rate = (mol dm⁻³) ÷ s. So its units are mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ are the units of a second-order rate constant, not of rate.", "1": "mol dm⁻³ are the units of concentration. Rate also needs a per-time unit.", "2": "s⁻¹ is the unit of a first-order rate constant, not of rate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0305' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As the reaction proceeds, the concentration of reactant A falls, so Δ[A] is negative. The minus sign in −Δ[A]/Δt shows that A is decreasing and also makes the rate a positive number. The product B increases, so its term has a plus sign.', wrong = '{"0": "Rate is always reported as a positive quantity. The minus sign is there to make it positive.", "1": "B is the product, and its concentration increases. That is why its term carries a plus sign.", "2": "The sign in a rate expression has nothing to do with whether heat is absorbed or released."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0306' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a reaction between gases, the pressure of a gas is proportional to its concentration at constant temperature. So pressure can be used in place of molar concentration. The rate is then expressed as a change in pressure per unit time, such as atm s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "Temperature change per unit time is not used as a measure of reaction rate.", "1": "Mass per mole is a molar mass, not a rate, and it has no time unit.", "2": "A rate needs a change per unit time. Volume alone includes no time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0307' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A reaction slows down as reactants are used up, so the instantaneous rate keeps falling. Over a chosen interval, the instantaneous rate starts above the average rate and ends below it. The two are equal at only one instant within the interval.', wrong = '{"0": "The instantaneous rate keeps changing, so it equals the average rate at only one instant.", "1": "This is the reverse. The reaction slows down, so the instantaneous rate is higher at the start than at the end.", "2": "At the start of the interval the instantaneous rate is higher than the average rate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0308' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Average rate = change in concentration ÷ time interval. Here Δc = 3.20 − 0.80 = 2.40 mol dm⁻³ and Δt = 8.0 − 2.0 = 6.0 min. Rate = 2.40 ÷ 6.0 = 0.40 mol dm⁻³ min⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "0.53 comes from dividing the final concentration by the time interval (3.20 ÷ 6.0) instead of using the change.", "2": "0.30 comes from dividing the change by the final time (2.40 ÷ 8.0) instead of by the interval.", "3": "1.6 comes from dividing the final concentration by the initial time (3.20 ÷ 2.0)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0309' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The average rate of disappearance is −Δ[HI]/Δt. Δ[HI] = 0.0716 − 0.100 = −0.0284 mol dm⁻³ over Δt = 50 s. Rate = 0.0284 ÷ 50 = 5.68 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "1.43 × 10⁻³ comes from dividing the concentration at 50 s by 50 (0.0716 ÷ 50) instead of using the change.", "2": "2.84 × 10⁻² is the change in concentration. It has not been divided by the 50 s.", "3": "2.00 × 10⁻³ comes from dividing the initial concentration by 50 (0.100 ÷ 50)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0310' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between 0 and 50 s, [HI] falls by 0.100 − 0.0716 = 0.0284 mol dm⁻³, more than in any later 50 s interval. The reaction is fastest at the start because the reactant concentration is highest then, so the curve is steepest there. Later the drops are much smaller, only 0.0040 mol dm⁻³ between 250 and 300 s.', wrong = '{"1": "Between 100 and 150 s, [HI] falls by only 0.0101 mol dm⁻³, about a third of the first interval.", "2": "Between 200 and 250 s, [HI] falls by only 0.0051 mol dm⁻³.", "3": "Between 250 and 300 s, [HI] falls by only 0.0040 mol dm⁻³, the slowest interval listed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0311' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On a concentration–time graph, the rate at a particular instant equals the slope of the curve at that point. To find it, a tangent is drawn to the curve at that time, for example at 100 s. The slope of this tangent, change in concentration ÷ change in time, gives the instantaneous rate.', wrong = '{"1": "The highest point of a reactant curve is simply the initial concentration, not a rate.", "2": "The area under a concentration–time graph does not give the rate of reaction.", "3": "The x-intercept marks a time, not the rate at a chosen moment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0312' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To follow the reaction by titration, the amount of acid present must be measured at known times. Running the sample into a large volume of ice-cold water dilutes and chills it, which practically stops the hydrolysis. The acid formed can then be titrated against standard NaOH using phenolphthalein without the reaction going on.', wrong = '{"0": "Cooling and diluting slow the reaction down almost to a stop. They do not speed it up.", "1": "No precipitate forms. The acid catalyst stays in solution.", "2": "Neutralization happens later, in the titration with NaOH, not when the sample is added to ice-cold water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0313' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Spectrophotometry measures how much radiation a sample absorbs. If a reactant or product absorbs ultraviolet, visible or infrared radiation, the amount absorbed changes as its concentration changes. So the rate can be followed by measuring the absorption at regular intervals.', wrong = '{"0": "If no species absorbs radiation, there is nothing for a spectrophotometer to measure.", "1": "A reaction that gives off a gas is best followed by measuring the volume of gas.", "3": "The method depends on a species absorbing radiation, not on the reaction being in the solid state."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0314' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electrical conductivity of a solution depends on the concentration of ions in it. If ions are used up or formed during a reaction, the conductivity changes as the reaction proceeds. Measuring the conductivity at intervals therefore follows the rate.', wrong = '{"0": "Every method needs a measurable change. With no change in concentration there is nothing to follow.", "1": "Non-polar molecules do not form ions, so the conductivity would not change.", "2": "Gas-phase reactions with a change in volume are followed by the volume change method."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0315' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This reaction gives off carbon dioxide gas. The volume of CO₂ collected at regular intervals, for example in a gas syringe, is proportional to how far the reaction has gone. So the volume change method is the most convenient way to follow its rate.', wrong = '{"0": "CaCO₃ is a solid reactant, so it cannot simply be titrated while the reaction is running.", "1": "None of the species here is coloured, so spectrophotometry would not be convenient.", "3": "The boiling point of the mixture is not used to follow the rate of a reaction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0316' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Very fast reactions are over too quickly for sampling and titration. In stopped-flow spectrophotometry, small volumes of reactant solutions are driven at high speed into a mixing chamber and then into an observation cell. The progress of the reaction is monitored there within milliseconds, usually by ultraviolet absorption.', wrong = '{"0": "Distillation is a method of separating liquids. It does not measure reaction rates.", "1": "Titration takes far too long for very fast reactions, which are finished before a sample can be taken.", "2": "Weighing once a day could follow only extremely slow reactions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0317' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At a higher concentration there are more reactant particles in the same volume. They collide more often, so there are more collisions per unit time, and more of them are effective. That increases the rate of reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "Concentration does not change the activation energy. A catalyst is what lowers it.", "1": "Concentration does not change the enthalpy change of a reaction.", "3": "Changing the concentration does not change the mass of the particles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0318' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Increasing the pressure of a gas squeezes the same number of molecules into a smaller volume, which raises its concentration. More collisions per unit time then speed up the reaction. Solids and liquids hardly compress, so pressure has no effect on their reaction rates.', wrong = '{"0": "Solids are almost incompressible, so pressure does not change their concentration or their rate of reaction.", "2": "Liquids are almost incompressible, so pressure does not change their concentration or their rate of reaction.", "3": "The pressure effect on gaseous reactants does not depend on whether a catalyst is present."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0319' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rise from 25 °C to 55 °C is 30 °C, which is three 10 °C steps. The rate doubles at each step, so it becomes 2 × 2 × 2 = 2³ = 8 times the original rate.', wrong = '{"0": "3 is the number of 10 °C steps. The rate doubles at each step, giving 2³, not 3.", "1": "6 comes from multiplying 2 × 3 instead of raising 2 to the power 3.", "3": "30 treats the rate as rising in proportion to the number of degrees, which it does not."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0320' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At a higher temperature the average kinetic energy of the molecules increases. The Boltzmann distribution curve becomes flatter and its peak moves to the right, towards higher energies. The total area stays the same, since the number of molecules is unchanged, but more molecules now have high energies.', wrong = '{"0": "A taller, narrower curve with the peak shifted left is what happens on cooling, not heating.", "1": "The spread of molecular energies depends on temperature, so the curve does change.", "3": "Some molecules still have low energies. Only the fraction with energy above Ea increases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0321' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The area under a Boltzmann distribution curve represents numbers of molecules. The shaded area to the right of Ea counts the molecules with energy equal to or greater than the activation energy, which can react when they collide. For a 10 °C rise this area roughly doubles, which is why the rate roughly doubles.', wrong = '{"0": "The total number of molecules is given by the area under the whole curve, not just the part beyond Ea.", "2": "The average kinetic energy is not shown by an area beyond Ea. It lies near the middle of the curve.", "3": "The enthalpy change of the reaction is not shown on a Boltzmann distribution curve at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0322' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A catalyst is a substance that changes the rate of a chemical reaction but is chemically unchanged at the end of it. Because it is not used up, it is often needed in only very small amounts. Most catalysts are used to speed reactions up.', wrong = '{"0": "A catalyst is not used up. It is chemically unchanged at the end, though it may take part in intermediate steps.", "2": "Catalysts usually speed reactions up. The definition says they alter the rate, and most are used to increase it.", "3": "A catalyst changes how fast products form, not which products form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0323' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Potassium chlorate decomposes on heating to give potassium chloride and oxygen. A small amount of manganese dioxide, MnO₂, makes this decomposition much faster. This is a common way of preparing oxygen in the laboratory.', wrong = '{"0": "Platinum catalyses reactions such as the oxidation of ammonia to NO, not the decomposition of KClO₃.", "2": "CuCl₂ is the catalyst for oxidizing HCl to chlorine.", "3": "Nickel is the catalyst for hydrogenating unsaturated compounds such as ethene."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0324' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'HCl can be oxidized by oxygen to give chlorine and water: 4HCl + O₂ → 2H₂O + 2Cl₂. This reaction is catalysed by copper(II) chloride, CuCl₂. Copper(II) chloride is not used up and can be recovered at the end of the reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "Iron is used as a catalyst in the Haber process for ammonia, not for oxidizing HCl.", "1": "MnO₂ is the catalyst for decomposing potassium chlorate.", "2": "V₂O₅ is the solid catalyst for oxidizing SO₂ to SO₃ in the Contact process."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0325' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A catalyst provides a different reaction pathway with a lower activation energy. On the Boltzmann distribution, more molecules now have energy equal to or greater than this lower Ea. So a larger fraction of collisions are effective and the rate increases.', wrong = '{"0": "Raising the temperature increases the average kinetic energy. A catalyst does not change it.", "1": "A catalyst does not change the enthalpies of reactants or products, so ΔH stays the same.", "3": "A catalyst does not change how much reactant is present, so the concentrations are unchanged."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0326' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In homogeneous catalysis the catalyst and the reactants are in the same phase. In the hydrolysis of ethyl ethanoate, the ester, the water and the dilute H₂SO₄ catalyst are all in one liquid solution. So it is homogeneous catalysis.', wrong = '{"0": "Solid nickel with gaseous ethene and hydrogen is in a different phase, so this is heterogeneous catalysis.", "2": "Solid platinum with gaseous ammonia and oxygen is in a different phase, so this is heterogeneous catalysis.", "3": "Solid V₂O₅ with gaseous SO₂ and O₂ is in a different phase, so this is heterogeneous catalysis."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0327' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the manufacture of nitric acid, ammonia is oxidized to nitrogen monoxide by passing it with air over platinum gauze at about 800 °C. The solid catalyst and gaseous reactants are in different phases, so this is heterogeneous catalysis. The NO is then converted into HNO₃.', wrong = '{"0": "Dilute H₂SO₄ at room temperature is the catalyst for hydrolysing esters, not oxidizing ammonia.", "1": "Nickel at about 150 °C is used for hydrogenating ethene to ethane.", "2": "MnO₂ is the catalyst for decomposing potassium chlorate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0328' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Enzymes are biological catalysts that speed up reactions in living cells. Most are proteins, and like other catalysts they work by lowering the activation energy and are not used up. Their activity depends on pH, temperature and substrate concentration.', wrong = '{"0": "Enzymes, like all catalysts, lower the activation energy. Raising it would slow the reaction.", "1": "Enzyme activity depends strongly on pH and temperature. Too much heat or the wrong pH stops them working.", "3": "Enzymes are catalysts, so they are not consumed in the reactions they speed up."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0329' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some vitamins help enzymes work, acting as coenzymes. Vitamin K is necessary for blood clotting. Low levels of vitamin K can cause bleeding diathesis, a tendency to bleed easily.', wrong = '{"0": "Vitamin D is mainly linked with calcium uptake and healthy bones, not blood clotting.", "2": "Vitamin C is mainly linked with healthy skin and gums. It is not the clotting vitamin.", "3": "Vitamin A is mainly linked with vision. It is not the clotting vitamin."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0330' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a rate equation, the exponents x and y give the order with respect to each reactant. They must be found from experimental data. They may or may not equal the coefficients a and b in the balanced equation, and they can be 0, whole numbers or fractions.', wrong = '{"0": "Exponents are not always 1. They can be 0, 1, 2 or even fractions, depending on the reaction.", "1": "The exponents need not match the coefficients. 2FeCl₃ + 6KI has eight reactant units but is only third order.", "3": "Order cannot be read from the balanced equation. It has to be measured experimentally."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0331' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In rate = k[A][B], setting [A] = [B] = 1 mol dm⁻³ gives rate = k. So the rate constant is the rate of reaction when all reactant concentrations are unity, and it is also called the specific rate constant. It is constant for a reaction at a given temperature but changes when the temperature changes.', wrong = '{"1": "The rate constant changes with temperature. It usually increases as the temperature rises.", "2": "The initial rate equals k only if every reactant concentration happens to be 1 mol dm⁻³.", "3": "The time for half of a reactant to be used up is the half-life, not the rate constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0332' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rate is first order in X and second order in Y. Halving [X] multiplies the rate by ½, and tripling [Y] multiplies it by 3² = 9. Together the rate changes by ½ × 9 = 4.5 times.', wrong = '{"0": "1.5 treats the reaction as first order in Y: ½ × 3. The square on [Y] makes the factor 9.", "2": "9 ignores the halving of [X]. The factor ½ must also be applied.", "3": "3 ignores the halving of [X] and treats Y as first order."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0333' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rate equation rate = k shows that the rate does not depend on the concentration of either reactant, because rate = k[H₂]⁰[Cl₂]⁰. The sum of the exponents is zero, so the reaction is zero order. Photochemical reactions are usually zero order, since their rate depends on the light absorbed.', wrong = '{"0": "A first-order reaction has a rate proportional to the concentration of one reactant.", "2": "A second-order reaction would have a rate equation such as rate = k[H₂][Cl₂].", "3": "Third order would need exponents adding to 3. Here the rate depends on no concentration at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0334' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The order of a reaction is the sum of the exponents in the experimental rate equation. For rate = k[N₂O₅], the exponent is 1, so the reaction is first order. The coefficient 2 in the balanced equation does not decide the order.', wrong = '{"1": "The rate depends on [N₂O₅], so the reaction cannot be zero order.", "2": "Second order would follow from the coefficient 2, but the experimental exponent is 1.", "3": "The rate equation has only one term raised to the first power, so the order is 1, not 3."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0335' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The balanced equation has 2 + 6 = 8 reactant formula units, but the experimental rate equation gives an order of 1 + 2 = 3. The order does not follow the coefficients. It depends on how the reaction actually takes place, so it must be found by experiment.', wrong = '{"1": "There is no rule that links the number of reactant units in the equation to the order.", "2": "FeCl₃ is a reactant that is used up, and it appears in the rate equation.", "3": "The rate equation contains [KI]², so the reaction is second order in KI, not zero order."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0336' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The overall order is the sum of the exponents: 1 + ½ = 1.5. A fractional order like this is typical of complex reactions that go through several steps. Reactions involving free radicals frequently show fractional orders.', wrong = '{"0": "The overall order is 1 + ½ = 1.5, not 2, and fractional orders are linked with free-radical reactions, not ionic ones.", "2": "0.5 counts only the exponent of Cl₂. Photochemical reactions are usually zero order.", "3": "The exponents add up to 1.5, not 3, so this is not a third-order (termolecular) case."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0337' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The units of a rate constant for an nth-order reaction follow kₙ = (mol dm⁻³)¹⁻ⁿ s⁻¹. For a third-order reaction n = 3, so k = (mol dm⁻³)⁻² s⁻¹ = dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹. A quick check: rate (mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹) ÷ concentration³ (mol³ dm⁻⁹) gives dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ is the unit of a second-order rate constant (n = 2), not a third-order one.", "2": "s⁻¹ is the unit of a first-order rate constant, where (mol dm⁻³)¹⁻¹ = 1 leaves only s⁻¹.", "3": "mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ is the unit of a zero-order rate constant, which has the same units as the rate itself."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0338' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using kₙ = (mol dm⁻³)¹⁻ⁿ s⁻¹, units of mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ need the exponent 1 − n to equal 1, so n = 0. A zero-order rate constant has the same units as the rate, because rate = k[A]⁰ = k.', wrong = '{"1": "A first-order rate constant has units of s⁻¹ only, with no concentration term.", "2": "A second-order rate constant has units of dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹, not mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.", "3": "A third-order rate constant has units of dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹, not mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0339' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rearranging the rate equation gives k = rate ÷ ([A][B]) = (4.0 × 10⁻⁵) ÷ (0.20 × 0.10) = (4.0 × 10⁻⁵) ÷ 0.020 = 2.0 × 10⁻³. The reaction is first order in A and first order in B, so it is second order overall and k has units dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "The number is right but s⁻¹ is the unit of a first-order constant. This reaction is second order overall, so the unit is dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹.", "1": "8.0 × 10⁻⁷ comes from multiplying the rate by the two concentrations (4.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.020) instead of dividing.", "3": "5.0 × 10² comes from inverting the division, 0.020 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁻⁵), so it is the reciprocal of the correct k."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0340' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rate equation contains only the species whose concentrations affect the rate, found by experiment. Changing [H⁺] does not change the rate, so the reaction is zero order with respect to H⁺. Since [H⁺]⁰ = 1, it simply drops out of the calculation of k.', wrong = '{"1": "H⁺ is a reactant: it appears on the left of the balanced equation and is used up to form water.", "2": "[H⁺] is not fixed at 1 mol dm⁻³. The experiments for this reaction use small values such as 0.0100 and 0.0200 mol dm⁻³.", "3": "H⁺ is consumed in the reaction, not produced. The products are H₂O and I₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0341' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a first-order reaction the half-life is t½ = 0.693/k. Substituting, t½ = 0.693 ÷ (1.155 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹) = 600 s. Converting to minutes, 600 ÷ 60 = 10 minutes. For a first-order reaction the half-life does not depend on the starting concentration.', wrong = '{"1": "600 is the half-life in seconds. Labelling it as minutes skips the conversion 600 s ÷ 60 = 10 min.", "2": "14.4 minutes comes from t½ = 1/k = 866 s, which leaves out the factor 0.693.", "3": "0.17 minutes comes from dividing 600 s by 60 twice instead of once."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0342' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a first-order reaction every half-life halves the concentration, whatever the starting value. Going 0.80 → 0.40 → 0.20 → 0.10 mol dm⁻³ is three halvings, so the time taken is 3 × 15 = 45 minutes.', wrong = '{"1": "30 minutes is two half-lives, which only brings the concentration down to 0.20 mol dm⁻³.", "2": "15 minutes is one half-life, which only brings the concentration down to 0.40 mol dm⁻³.", "3": "105 minutes assumes a fall of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ every 15 minutes (7 steps). A first-order reaction halves the concentration each half-life instead."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0343' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An elementary step happens in a single collision. Its molecularity is the number of reactant particles (molecules, atoms or ions) that take part in that step. It is always a whole number such as 1, 2 or 3, and is read directly from the step itself.', wrong = '{"0": "The sum of the exponents in the overall rate equation is the order of reaction, which is found by experiment.", "1": "Molecularity counts the reacting particles in a step. The number of products formed is not part of the definition.", "3": "Molecularity belongs to one elementary step. The overall order comes from experiment and need not match it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0344' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A termolecular step involves three particles colliding at the same moment, which is rare. In 2O₂ + O → O₃ + O₂, two O₂ molecules and one O atom react together, so three reactant particles take part and the step is termolecular.', wrong = '{"0": "Only one N₂O₅ molecule reacts, splitting into two fragments, so this step is unimolecular.", "2": "One NO molecule and one O₃ molecule collide, so two particles react and the step is bimolecular.", "3": "One CO molecule and one NO₃ molecule collide, so two particles react and the step is bimolecular."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0345' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An intermediate is formed in one step and used up in a later step, so it does not appear in the overall equation. The F atom is produced in step 1 and consumed in step 2. Adding the two steps cancels F, giving 2NO₂ + F₂ → 2NO₂F.', wrong = '{"0": "F₂ is a starting reactant in the overall equation. It is consumed in step 1 and is not formed in any step.", "2": "NO₂F is the final product. It is formed in both steps and never used up.", "3": "NO₂ is a starting reactant in the overall equation. It is consumed in both steps and never formed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0346' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The slow step is the rate-determining step, and the species reacting in it appear in the rate equation with their coefficients as powers. Step 1 uses 2NO and 1H₂, so rate = k[NO]²[H₂]. This fits experiment: doubling [NO] makes the rate four times faster and doubling [H₂] doubles it.', wrong = '{"0": "[NO]²[H₂]² takes the powers from the overall balanced equation. Orders must come from experiment or the slow step, not the overall equation.", "1": "k[H₂O₂][H₂] is the rate of the fast second step. A fast step after the slow one does not control the overall rate.", "3": "The slow step involves two NO molecules, so the rate depends on [NO]², not [NO]."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0347' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only species that take part in or before the rate-determining (slow) step appear in the rate equation. H⁺ reacts only in the fast steps that come after the slow step, so it does not affect the rate. The rate therefore depends only on [H₂O₂] and [I⁻].', wrong = '{"0": "A catalyst is regenerated, but H⁺ is used up overall. It also does not take part in the slow step at all.", "1": "An intermediate is formed and then used up within the mechanism. H⁺ is a starting reactant in the overall equation.", "3": "The amount of H⁺ present is not the reason. It is missing from the rate equation because it is absent from the slow step."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0348' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An irreversible reaction goes in one direction only, turning reactants into products until it is complete. It stops when the limiting reactant, the one present in the smallest required amount, is fully used up, since nothing is left to react.', wrong = '{"0": "Equal forward and reverse rates describe dynamic equilibrium, which belongs to reversible reactions, not irreversible ones.", "1": "Raising the temperature usually makes a reaction faster. It does not make it stop.", "2": "A catalyst only changes the rate of a reaction. It does not cause a reaction to stop."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0349' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Microscopic events happen at the level of atoms and molecules and cannot be seen with the naked eye. Examples are collisions between molecules, the breaking and forming of bonds, rearrangement of atoms and transfer of electrons. Bond breaking and forming is therefore a microscopic event.', wrong = '{"0": "A change in colour can be seen directly, so it is a macroscopic (observable) event.", "1": "A precipitate is a visible solid forming in a solution, so its formation is a macroscopic event.", "3": "Bubbles of gas can be seen, so the evolution of a gas is a macroscopic event."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0350' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chemical equilibrium is dynamic: molecules keep reacting in both directions, but the forward and reverse rates are equal. Since products form as fast as they break down, the concentrations of reactants and products stay constant and the composition of the mixture no longer changes.', wrong = '{"0": "At the molecular level the reactions never stop. Equilibrium is a dynamic state, not a static one.", "1": "The reverse reaction also continues. Both reactions run at the same rate at equilibrium.", "2": "Concentrations at equilibrium are constant, not necessarily equal. Reactant concentration may be greater than, less than or equal to product concentration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0351' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the start only H₂O and CO are present, so their collisions are most frequent and the forward rate is at its maximum. As they are used up, the forward rate falls. Meanwhile H₂ and CO₂ build up from zero, so the reverse rate rises. Equilibrium is reached when the two rates become equal.', wrong = '{"0": "The rates change as the reaction proceeds. They become constant only once equilibrium has been established.", "1": "The forward rate does not start at zero. It is highest at the start, when reactant concentrations are greatest.", "2": "This is backwards. The forward rate falls as reactants are used up, and the reverse rate rises as products form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0352' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A physical equilibrium involves a change of state, not a new substance. At 0 °C, ice melts into water and water freezes into ice at the same rate, so both phases coexist and their amounts stay constant. This is a dynamic equilibrium between solid and liquid.', wrong = '{"0": "Sodium reacts with water to give NaOH and H₂ gas. This is an irreversible chemical reaction, not a change of state.", "1": "Rusting forms a new substance (hydrated iron oxide). It is an irreversible chemical change, not a physical equilibrium.", "2": "Burning methane forms CO₂ and H₂O. It is an irreversible chemical reaction, not an equilibrium between states."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0353' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a closed container, liquid molecules keep evaporating while vapour molecules keep condensing. At first evaporation is faster, but the condensation rate rises as vapour builds up. When the two rates become equal, a dynamic equilibrium is reached and the vapour pressure stays constant at that temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "Molecules continue to escape from the liquid surface. The vapour pressure is steady only because just as many molecules return.", "1": "Vapour molecules keep returning to the liquid. Condensation goes on at the same rate as evaporation.", "3": "If all the liquid had evaporated, there would be no liquid left to be in equilibrium with its vapour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0354' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Raising the temperature gives the liquid molecules more average kinetic energy, so more of them escape and the rate of evaporation increases. The vapour concentration rises until condensation catches up, giving a new dynamic equilibrium with a higher vapour pressure.', wrong = '{"0": "At a higher temperature more molecules escape the liquid, so the vapour pressure rises, not falls.", "1": "Evaporation does not stop. Both evaporation and condensation continue, just at faster rates.", "2": "Vapour pressure is constant only at a fixed temperature. When the temperature changes, the vapour pressure changes too."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0355' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Equilibrium can only be set up in a closed vessel from which no reactant or product can escape. In an open container the CO₂ gas escapes into the air, so it cannot react back with CaO. The reverse reaction is prevented and the CaCO₃ simply keeps decomposing.', wrong = '{"0": "CaCO₃ does decompose on strong heating to give CaO and CO₂. The problem is that the CO₂ is lost.", "1": "CaO (quicklime) is a solid, as shown by (s) in the equation. Only CO₂ is a gas.", "3": "The reaction is reversible. In a closed vessel, CaCO₃, CaO and CO₂ do reach equilibrium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0356' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One feature of chemical equilibrium is that it can be approached from either side. Starting with only reactants or only products, the same equilibrium state is reached under the same conditions, because the forward and reverse reactions both occur.', wrong = '{"0": "Equilibrium can equally be reached by starting with the products, since the reverse reaction also takes place.", "1": "A catalyst speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally. It changes neither the equilibrium position nor Kc.", "2": "Kc does not depend on the starting concentrations. For a given reaction it depends only on temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0357' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A catalyst provides a new reaction path with a lower activation energy. It lowers the energy barrier for the forward and reverse reactions by the same amount, so both speed up equally. Equilibrium is reached sooner, but the position of equilibrium, the yield and Kc are unchanged.', wrong = '{"0": "A catalyst does not increase the equilibrium yield. It only helps the system reach the same equilibrium faster.", "1": "Kc changes only when the temperature changes. A catalyst leaves it unchanged.", "3": "Since both directions speed up equally, a catalyst does not shift the equilibrium towards reactants or products."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0358' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pure solids and pure liquids have a constant concentration, so they are left out of the equilibrium constant expression. Here C is a solid, while H₂O, CO and H₂ are gases. Leaving out C(s), Kc = [CO][H₂]/[H₂O].', wrong = '{"0": "This includes [C], but carbon is a pure solid. Its concentration is constant and is not written in Kc.", "1": "This is upside down. It is the Kc expression for the reverse reaction, CO + H₂ ⇌ C + H₂O.", "3": "H₂O here is a gas, not a pure liquid, so its concentration must appear in the denominator."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0359' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a heterogeneous equilibrium the reactants and products are in more than one phase. In 3Fe(s) + 4H₂O(g) ⇌ Fe₃O₄(s) + 4H₂(g), iron and Fe₃O₄ are solids while steam and hydrogen are gases, so two phases are present.', wrong = '{"0": "N₂, H₂ and NH₃ are all gases, so they form a single phase. This is a homogeneous equilibrium.", "1": "SO₂, O₂ and SO₃ are all gases in one phase, so this is a homogeneous equilibrium.", "3": "Acid, alcohol, ester and water are all in one liquid phase, so this esterification is a homogeneous equilibrium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0360' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Guldberg and Waage''s law of mass action states that the rate of a reaction is directly proportional to the active masses of the reactants, each raised to the power of its coefficient in the balanced equation. Active mass means molar concentration, in mol dm⁻³.', wrong = '{"0": "The law is about the reactants that are reacting, not the masses of the products formed.", "1": "Active mass means molar concentration (mol dm⁻³), not molar mass (g mol⁻¹).", "2": "The volume of the container does not appear in the law. It only affects rate through the concentrations."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0361' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At equilibrium the forward and reverse rates are equal: kf[A][B] = kr[C][D]. Rearranging, Kc = [C][D]/([A][B]) = kf/kr. Substituting, Kc = (4.0 × 10⁻²) ÷ (2.0 × 10⁻³) = 20.', wrong = '{"0": "8.0 × 10⁻⁵ comes from multiplying the two rate constants (4.0 × 10⁻² × 2.0 × 10⁻³) instead of dividing.", "1": "0.05 is kr/kf = (2.0 × 10⁻³) ÷ (4.0 × 10⁻²), the inverse ratio. Kc is the forward constant over the reverse one.", "3": "4.2 × 10⁻² comes from adding the two rate constants. Kc is their ratio, not their sum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0362' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kc is the product of the product concentrations divided by the product of the reactant concentrations, each raised to the power of its coefficient. For 2NO + O₂ ⇌ 2NO₂, Kc = [NO₂]²/([NO]²[O₂]).', wrong = '{"1": "This ignores the coefficients. Each coefficient of 2 must appear as a power of 2 on that concentration.", "2": "This puts reactants over products, so it is the Kc expression for the reverse reaction.", "3": "Coefficients are written as powers, not multipliers, and concentrations are multiplied together, not added."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0363' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First convert moles to concentrations by dividing by 2.0 dm³: acid 0.10, ethanol 0.050, ester 0.15 and water 0.15 mol dm⁻³. Kc = (0.15 × 0.15) ÷ (0.10 × 0.050) = 0.0225 ÷ 0.0050 = 4.5. Since moles are equal on both sides, the volume cancels and using moles directly gives the same answer.', wrong = '{"0": "1.1 comes from using concentrations for the products but moles for the reactants: 0.0225 ÷ (0.20 × 0.10) ≈ 1.1.", "1": "0.22 is 1 ÷ 4.5. It puts reactants over products, which is Kc for the reverse reaction.", "3": "15 comes from leaving water out of the expression: 0.30 ÷ (0.20 × 0.10). Water here is a product in the same liquid mixture, so it is included."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0364' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each mole of C formed uses one mole of A and one mole of B. So at equilibrium [A] = [B] = 0.100 − 0.060 = 0.040 mol dm⁻³. Kc = [C]/([A][B]) = 0.060 ÷ (0.040 × 0.040) = 0.060 ÷ 0.0016 = 37.5 dm³ mol⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "6.0 comes from using the starting concentrations of A and B, 0.060 ÷ (0.100 × 0.100). Kc needs equilibrium concentrations.", "2": "1.5 comes from dividing by only one reactant concentration, 0.060 ÷ 0.040. Both [A] and [B] belong in the denominator.", "3": "0.027 mol dm⁻³ is 1 ÷ 37.5, the inverse expression with matching units. It is Kc for the reverse reaction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0365' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kc = [PCl₃][Cl₂]/[PCl₅]. Putting in units: (mol dm⁻³)(mol dm⁻³)/(mol dm⁻³) = mol dm⁻³. There are 2 moles of gas on the right and 1 on the left, so one concentration unit is left over.', wrong = '{"0": "Kc has no units only when the total moles on both sides are equal. Here there are 2 on the right and 1 on the left.", "1": "dm⁶ mol⁻² fits a reaction with two more moles on the reactant side, such as N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃.", "2": "dm³ mol⁻¹ fits a reaction with one more mole on the reactant side. Here the product side has the extra mole."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0366' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kp, Kc, Kx and Kn are related through terms raised to the power Δn, the moles of gaseous products minus moles of gaseous reactants. When Δn = 0 those terms become 1 and all four constants are equal. For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, Δn = 2 − 2 = 0.', wrong = '{"1": "For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, Δn = 2 − 4 = −2, so the constants differ from each other.", "2": "For N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂, Δn = 2 − 1 = +1, so the constants differ from each other.", "3": "For PCl₅ ⇌ PCl₃ + Cl₂, Δn = 2 − 1 = +1, so the constants differ from each other."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0367' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kp and Kc are related by Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn, where Δn is moles of gaseous products minus moles of gaseous reactants. For N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂, Δn = 2 − 1 = +1, so Kp = Kc × RT. When RT is greater than 1, Kp is greater than Kc.', wrong = '{"0": "Kp equals Kc only when Δn = 0. Here one mole of gas gives two, so Δn = +1.", "1": "Kp = Kc/(RT) applies when Δn = −1, as in 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃. Here Δn is +1.", "3": "The power of RT is Δn, which is 2 − 1 = +1 here, not 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0368' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂, Kp = p(NO₂)²/p(N₂O₄), with the coefficient 2 appearing as a square. Substituting, Kp = (0.20)² ÷ 0.50 = 0.040 ÷ 0.50 = 0.080 atm. The unit is atm because there is one more mole of gas on the product side.', wrong = '{"0": "0.40 comes from forgetting to square p(NO₂): 0.20 ÷ 0.50 = 0.40.", "2": "12.5 atm⁻¹ is 1 ÷ 0.080. It puts the reactant over the product, which is Kp for the reverse reaction.", "3": "0.020 atm comes from multiplying by p(N₂O₄) instead of dividing: 0.040 × 0.50 = 0.020."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0369' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kp = p(HI)²/(p(H₂) × p(I₂)) = (1.4 × 10⁵)² ÷ (2.0 × 10⁴ × 2.0 × 10⁴) = 1.96 × 10¹⁰ ÷ 4.0 × 10⁸ = 49. There are 2 moles of gas on each side (Δn = 0), so the pressure units cancel and Kp has no units.', wrong = '{"1": "7.0 is the square root of 49, from p(HI) ÷ p(H₂) = 1.4 × 10⁵ ÷ 2.0 × 10⁴. It leaves out the square and one reactant pressure.", "2": "The number is right, but the units cancel because the moles of gas are equal on both sides (Δn = 0). Kp has no units here.", "3": "3.5 comes from dividing p(HI) by the sum of the reactant pressures, 1.4 × 10⁵ ÷ 4.0 × 10⁴. Pressures are multiplied, not added."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0370' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Let x mol dm⁻³ of H₂ react. At equilibrium [H₂] = [I₂] = 1.0 − x and [HI] = 2x. Then Kc = (2x)²/(1.0 − x)² = 49, so taking square roots, 2x/(1.0 − x) = 7. This gives 9x = 7, x = 0.778, and [HI] = 2x ≈ 1.56 mol dm⁻³.', wrong = '{"1": "0.78 mol dm⁻³ is x, the amount of H₂ that reacts. Each mole of H₂ gives 2 moles of HI, so [HI] = 2x.", "2": "0.22 mol dm⁻³ is 1.0 − x, the equilibrium concentration of H₂ (and of I₂), not of HI.", "3": "1.75 comes from solving x/(1 − x) = 7 (x = 0.875), dropping the 2 inside the expression, and then doubling x."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0371' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Le Chatelier''s principle says that when a system at equilibrium is disturbed by a change in concentration, pressure or temperature, it shifts in the direction that reduces or counteracts that disturbance. The system then settles into a new equilibrium.', wrong = '{"1": "The reactions do not stop. The system keeps reacting and moves to a new equilibrium position.", "2": "The direction of the shift depends on the disturbance. Adding a product, for example, shifts it towards the reactants.", "3": "Concentration and pressure changes only shift the position. Only a change in temperature alters the equilibrium constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0372' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'HCl is a product of this equilibrium. Adding concentrated HCl raises its concentration, so by Le Chatelier''s principle the system shifts in the reverse direction to use some of it up. BiOCl reacts with HCl to re-form BiCl₃ and water, so the white precipitate dissolves and the solution clears. Kc stays the same.', wrong = '{"0": "Adding a product shifts the equilibrium backwards, which removes BiOCl. The solution becomes clearer, not cloudier.", "2": "Changing the concentration of a product does disturb the equilibrium, so the position shifts.", "3": "Kc changes only with temperature. Adding HCl changes the position of equilibrium, not Kc."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0373' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adding a reactant (BiCl₃) disturbs the equilibrium. To use up the extra BiCl₃, the reaction moves forward, forming more BiOCl and HCl until equilibrium is re-established. The position shifts, but at constant temperature Kc does not change.', wrong = '{"0": "The position does shift forward, but Kc does not increase. Kc changes only when the temperature changes.", "1": "Adding a reactant shifts the equilibrium forward, not backward, and Kc remains the same.", "2": "The extra BiCl₃ upsets the balance of concentrations, so the equilibrium position must change."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0374' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only gases are counted when judging the effect of pressure. There is 1 mole of gas on the left (H₂O) and 2 on the right (CO + H₂). Increasing the pressure shifts the equilibrium towards the side with fewer moles of gas, which is the left.', wrong = '{"0": "The right side has more moles of gas (2 against 1), so higher pressure works against it, not in its favour.", "2": "Solid carbon is not counted, but the gaseous moles are still unequal (1 against 2), so pressure does have an effect.", "3": "A pressure change shifts the position of equilibrium. Kc changes only with temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0375' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Temperature is the only factor that changes the value of the equilibrium constant. Raising the temperature favours the endothermic direction, so Kc rises for an endothermic reaction and falls for an exothermic one. Other factors only shift the position or the speed of reaching equilibrium.', wrong = '{"0": "Changing a reactant''s concentration shifts the position of equilibrium, but Kc stays the same.", "2": "Pressure can shift the position for gas reactions with unequal moles, but Kc is unchanged.", "3": "A catalyst only helps equilibrium be reached faster. It changes neither the position nor Kc."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0376' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kc rises from 7.7 × 10⁻⁵ at 273 K to 0.4 at 373 K, so heating favours the product NO₂. By Le Chatelier''s principle, heating favours the endothermic direction. The forward reaction must therefore be endothermic (ΔH about +57 kJ), and heating shifts the equilibrium to the right.', wrong = '{"0": "If the forward reaction were exothermic, Kc would fall as the temperature rises. Here it increases.", "1": "Kc changes by more than a thousand times between 273 K and 373 K, so it clearly depends on temperature.", "3": "Heating increases Kc, so more NO₂ and less N₂O₄ is present. Formation of NO₂, not N₂O₄, is favoured."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0377' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an exothermic reaction, heat acts like a product. Lowering the temperature removes heat, so the system shifts forward to release more, forming more product. Since temperature changes Kc, Kc increases: for 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, Kc is about 1 × 10²⁶ at 298 K but only 2.8 × 10² at 1000 K.', wrong = '{"0": "Shifting left and decreasing Kc is the effect of raising the temperature of an exothermic reaction.", "2": "The shift is to the right, but temperature is the one factor that does change Kc, so Kc increases.", "3": "Temperature changes both the position of equilibrium and the value of Kc."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0378' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Haber reaction is exothermic, so a low temperature such as 200 °C gives a higher equilibrium yield. However, at that temperature the reaction is far too slow to be economical. A moderate temperature of about 400 °C with an iron catalyst gives a reasonable yield at a useful rate.', wrong = '{"0": "For an exothermic reaction Kc is smaller at the higher temperature, so 400 °C gives a smaller Kc, not a larger one.", "1": "Ammonia is more stable at lower temperatures. It does not decompose completely at 200 °C.", "3": "The iron catalyst is used at the moderate temperature of about 400 °C, not only at 200 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0379' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The industrial Haber process uses a pressure of 200 to 300 atmospheres, a temperature of about 673 K (400 °C), and a catalyst of iron crystals in a fused mixture of MgO, Al₂O₃ and SiO₂. High pressure helps the yield, while the temperature and catalyst give a practical rate.', wrong = '{"0": "About 1 atm with a V₂O₅ catalyst describes the contact process for making SO₃, not ammonia.", "2": "2000 K would greatly lower the yield of this exothermic reaction, and platinum is not the Haber catalyst.", "3": "The Haber process needs high pressure, not 1 atm, and its catalyst is iron, not nickel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0380' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A catalyst speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached sooner but the yield is unchanged. The other changes all shift the equilibrium to the right: removing NH₃ replaces lost product, higher pressure favours fewer gas moles (2 against 4), and cooling favours the exothermic forward reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "Removing ammonia lowers the product concentration, so the equilibrium shifts forward to make more. This does raise the yield.", "1": "Higher pressure favours the side with fewer moles of gas, 2NH₃ against 4 moles of reactants. This does raise the yield.", "2": "The forward reaction is exothermic, so cooling favours it. This does raise the yield."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0381' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the contact process for sulphuric acid, SO₂ and air at about 1 atmosphere are passed over a solid catalyst such as vanadium pentoxide, V₂O₅. This gives a good yield of SO₃ in a reasonable time without needing high pressure.', wrong = '{"1": "Iron at 200 to 300 atm is the catalyst and pressure of the Haber process for ammonia.", "2": "Nickel at around 150 °C is used for hydrogenation, such as making margarine from oils.", "3": "Platinum gauze at high temperature is used to oxidise ammonia to NO in making nitric acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0382' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between 200 °C and 700 °C at 1 atm, Kc falls from 5500 to 13 and the mole % of SO₃ falls from 98% to about 31%. This is expected because the forward reaction is exothermic, so heating favours the reverse reaction. Higher temperature speeds the reaction up but lowers the yield.', wrong = '{"0": "Both values fall as the temperature rises, because the forward reaction is exothermic.", "2": "Kc does not increase. For this exothermic reaction Kc falls steadily as the temperature rises.", "3": "Kc changes with temperature, dropping from 5500 at 200 °C to 13 at 700 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0383' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Macroscopic changes are those we can observe, such as a colour change or constant pressure. They are the combined result of a huge number of microscopic events, such as collisions, bond breaking and bond forming, happening at the same time. Understanding the microscopic events explains what is observed.', wrong = '{"1": "Microscopic events happen at the level of molecules and, by definition, cannot be seen with the naked eye.", "2": "The cause runs the other way. Collisions and bond changes produce the observable macroscopic changes.", "3": "The two are directly linked: what is observed is the sum of many molecular events."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0384' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pure solids have a constant concentration, so they are left out of the equilibrium constant expression. CaCO₃ and CaO are both solids, and only CO₂ is a gas. So Kc = [CO₂], which means the CO₂ concentration at equilibrium is fixed at a given temperature.', wrong = '{"1": "CaO and CaCO₃ are pure solids, so they are not written in the Kc expression.", "2": "This contains only the solids, which must be left out, and misses CO₂, the one species that belongs.", "3": "1/[CO₂] is the expression for the reverse reaction, CaO + CO₂ ⇌ CaCO₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0385' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The position of equilibrium describes the relative amounts of reactants and products present in the equilibrium mixture. If products are in excess it lies to the right; if reactants are in excess it lies to the left. Changes in conditions can shift this position.', wrong = '{"1": "The time taken to reach equilibrium depends on the reaction rate, not on the equilibrium position.", "2": "At equilibrium the forward rate equals the reverse rate. The position is about composition, not rate.", "3": "Temperature can change the position of equilibrium, but it is not what the position means."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0386' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn, where Δn = moles of gaseous products − moles of gaseous reactants. For 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, Δn = 2 − 3 = −1. So Kp = Kc(RT)⁻¹, which is the same as Kc/(RT).', wrong = '{"0": "This uses Δn = +1. There are fewer moles of gas on the product side, so Δn is −1.", "2": "The power 3 is the moles of gaseous reactants. Δn is the difference, 2 − 3 = −1, not a single side''s total.", "3": "Kp equals Kc only when Δn = 0, which is not the case here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0387' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Increasing the volume lowers the pressure. The system responds by shifting towards the side with more moles of gas, which occupies more volume. The reactants have 4 moles of gas and the product 2, so the equilibrium shifts left and some NH₃ decomposes.', wrong = '{"0": "More NH₃ forms when the pressure is increased. Lowering the pressure favours the side with more gas moles, the reactants.", "2": "Being all gases is exactly why pressure matters here, since the moles of gas on the two sides are unequal (4 against 2).", "3": "A change in volume or pressure shifts the position of equilibrium. Kc changes only with temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0388' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Haber reaction is exothermic and reduces 4 moles of gas to 2, so low temperature and high pressure both favour ammonia. The most complete conversion, about 98.3%, occurs at 473 K (200 °C) and 1000 atmospheres. Industry does not use these conditions because the rate is slow and very high pressure is costly.', wrong = '{"0": "673 K and about 250 atm are close to the industrial conditions, chosen for a reasonable rate, not maximum yield.", "2": "High temperature and low pressure both work against ammonia formation, so the yield would be low.", "3": "At 298 K the reaction is extremely slow, and 1 atm gives no help from pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0389' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The equilibrium constant gets a subscript that shows how amounts are expressed. Kc uses molar concentrations, Kp uses partial pressures, Kn uses numbers of moles, and Kx uses mole fractions. So with mole fractions the constant is Kx.', wrong = '{"1": "Kc is written when amounts are expressed as molar concentrations in mol dm⁻³.", "2": "Kp is written when amounts of gases are expressed as partial pressures.", "3": "Kn is written when amounts are expressed as numbers of moles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0390' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Arrhenius theory defined acids and bases only by the H⁺ and OH⁻ ions they give in water, so it applied only to aqueous solutions. Brønsted and Lowry defined an acid as a proton donor and a base as a proton acceptor, which also works in other solvents and with no solvent at all.', wrong = '{"0": "The Arrhenius theory did explain neutralization in water, as H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O.", "1": "Sour taste is a property of acids, but it was not the limitation the Brønsted–Lowry concept removed.", "2": "Arrhenius acids release H⁺ ions in water, so protons were already part of that theory."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0391' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A conjugate base is the species left after an acid donates a proton. Here H₂SO₄ gives one H⁺ to water and becomes HSO₄⁻. So H₂SO₄ and HSO₄⁻ form a conjugate acid–base pair, differing by one proton.', wrong = '{"0": "H₃O⁺ is formed when water accepts a proton, so it is the conjugate acid of water.", "1": "SO₄²⁻ would need the loss of two protons. In this reaction H₂SO₄ loses only one.", "2": "H₂O accepts the proton here, so it is the base in this reaction, not the conjugate base of H₂SO₄."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0392' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A Brønsted–Lowry acid donates a proton. In the forward reaction, H₂O gives an H⁺ to CO₃²⁻, forming HCO₃⁻ and leaving OH⁻. So water is the acid and carbonate is the base.', wrong = '{"0": "CO₃²⁻ accepts the proton to become HCO₃⁻, so it is the base, not the acid.", "1": "OH⁻ is what remains of water after it donates a proton, so it is the conjugate base of water.", "2": "HCO₃⁻ is formed in the forward reaction. It is the conjugate acid of CO₃²⁻ and acts as an acid only in the reverse direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0393' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An amphoteric species can act as either an acid or a base. Water accepts a proton from HCl to form H₃O⁺, so it is a base there. It donates a proton to NH₃ to form OH⁻ and NH₄⁺, so it is an acid there.', wrong = '{"0": "Being neutral means [H⁺] = [OH⁻]. It is not the same as being able to act both as an acid and as a base.", "1": "A high boiling point comes from hydrogen bonding and has nothing to do with acid–base behaviour.", "2": "Pure water is a very poor conductor, and conductivity is not what amphoteric means."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0394' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the Brønsted–Lowry concept a base is a proton acceptor, and no water or OH⁻ ions are needed. In this gas-phase reaction, NH₃ accepts a proton from HCl to form NH₄⁺, which ends up in solid NH₄Cl. So NH₃ is a base even though no OH⁻ is produced.', wrong = '{"0": "No OH⁻ ions are present in this gas-phase reaction. Releasing OH⁻ is the Arrhenius idea of a base.", "2": "HCl is the proton donor here. NH₃ receives the proton.", "3": "The reaction happens between two gases with no water present, so dissolving plays no part."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0395' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A Lewis acid is an electron-pair acceptor. In BF₃, boron has only six valence electrons around it, an incomplete octet. It can therefore accept a lone pair from a Lewis base such as NH₃ or F⁻, which makes BF₃ a good Lewis acid.', wrong = '{"0": "BF₃ contains no hydrogen, so it cannot donate a proton. It is an acid only in the Lewis sense.", "1": "BF₃ has no hydrogen atoms, so it cannot release H⁺ in water.", "2": "It is the electron-deficient boron atom that accepts the electron pair, not the fluorine atoms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0396' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each NH₃ molecule donates a lone pair to Ag⁺, forming the adduct [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺. NH₃ molecules are neutral, so the total charge on the left is just the +1 of Ag⁺. Charge is conserved, so the adduct also carries +1.', wrong = '{"0": "NH₃ is neutral and adds no charge, so the adduct keeps the +1 charge of Ag⁺ rather than becoming neutral.", "1": "Only one positive charge is present on the left, from Ag⁺. The NH₃ molecules add none.", "3": "Neither Ag⁺ nor NH₃ carries a negative charge, so the adduct cannot be negative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0397' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One dm³ of water has a mass of about 1000 g. Dividing by the molar mass, 1000 g ÷ 18 g mol⁻¹ ≈ 55.5 mol dm⁻³. Water is in such large excess that this stays constant, so it is combined with Kc to give Kw = Kc × 55.5 = [H⁺][OH⁻].', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 mol dm⁻³ is a standard concentration, not the concentration of pure water.", "2": "18 is the molar mass of water in g mol⁻¹, not its concentration.", "3": "1000 is the mass of water in g per dm³. It must be divided by 18 g mol⁻¹ to give moles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0398' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In pure water [H⁺] = [OH⁻], so [H⁺] = √Kw = √(3.0 × 10⁻¹⁴) = 1.73 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³. Then pH = −log(1.73 × 10⁻⁷) = 6.76. The pH is below 7, but since [H⁺] still equals [OH⁻], the water is neutral.', wrong = '{"0": "The pH value is right, but water is acidic only if [H⁺] exceeds [OH⁻]. Here they are equal, so it is neutral.", "1": "pH 7.00 is neutral only at 25 °C, where Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴. At 40 °C Kw is larger, so the pH is lower.", "3": "A larger Kw at 40 °C means a larger [H⁺] and a pH below 7, not above it, and the water is neutral, not basic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0399' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In any aqueous solution at 25 °C, [H⁺][OH⁻] = Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴. Adding acid raises [H⁺], so [OH⁻] must fall to keep the product equal to Kw. The solution becomes acidic, with [H⁺] greater than [OH⁻], while Kw itself is unchanged.', wrong = '{"0": "Kw depends only on temperature. Adding acid or base at 25 °C does not change it.", "1": "[OH⁻] becomes greater than [H⁺] when a base is added, not an acid.", "2": "Their product must stay equal to Kw, so if [H⁺] rises, [OH⁻] has to fall."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0400' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitric acid is a strong acid and ionizes completely, so [H⁺] equals the acid concentration, 0.020 mol dm⁻³. Then pH = −log(0.020) = −log(2.0 × 10⁻²) = 2 − 0.30 = 1.70.', wrong = '{"0": "12.30 is 14 − 1.70, which is the pOH of this solution, not the pH.", "1": "pH 2.00 matches [H⁺] = 0.010 mol dm⁻³. The acid here is twice as concentrated, so the pH is a little lower.", "3": "0.020 is [H⁺] in mol dm⁻³. The pH is its negative logarithm, not the concentration itself."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0401' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ba(OH)₂ is a strong base and each formula unit releases two OH⁻ ions. So [OH⁻] = 2 × 0.0050 = 0.010 mol dm⁻³, giving pOH = −log(0.010) = 2.0. At 25 °C pH + pOH = 14, so pH = 14 − 2.0 = 12.0.', wrong = '{"1": "11.7 comes from using [OH⁻] = 0.0050 (pOH 2.3), forgetting that each Ba(OH)₂ releases two OH⁻ ions.", "2": "2.0 is the pOH. The pH is 14 − 2.0 = 12.0.", "3": "2.3 is the pOH obtained when the factor of 2 is forgotten, so it is wrong twice over."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0402' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ka measures how far an acid ionizes in water: the larger Ka, the stronger the acid. Comparing the values, HF has Ka = 6.7 × 10⁻⁵, which is larger than all the others, so HF is the strongest acid of the four.', wrong = '{"1": "CH₃COOH has Ka = 1.85 × 10⁻⁵, which is smaller than HF''s 6.7 × 10⁻⁵, so it is weaker.", "2": "H₂S has Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁷, about 670 times smaller than HF, so it is much weaker.", "3": "HCO₃⁻ has the smallest Ka of the four, 4.7 × 10⁻¹¹, so it is the weakest acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0403' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acids can be classified by Ka: strong when Ka is greater than 1, moderately strong when Ka lies between 1 and 10⁻³, and weak when Ka is less than 10⁻³. Since 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ is smaller than 10⁻³, this acid is weak.', wrong = '{"0": "A strong acid has Ka greater than 1. This Ka is far smaller.", "1": "Moderately strong acids have Ka from 10⁻³ to 1. 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ is below this range.", "3": "A substance with a Ka value donates protons, so it is an acid, however weak, not neutral."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0404' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A weak acid ionizes only slightly, so [HA] stays close to C and Ka ≈ [H₃O⁺]²/C. This gives [H₃O⁺] ≈ √(Ka × C) = √(4.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.040) = √(1.6 × 10⁻⁷) = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³.', wrong = '{"0": "0.040 mol dm⁻³ would mean complete ionization. A weak acid ionizes only slightly, so [H₃O⁺] is much smaller.", "1": "1.6 × 10⁻⁷ is Ka × C. The square root has not been taken.", "3": "2.0 × 10⁻³ is √Ka alone, which ignores the acid concentration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0405' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'pH 4.0 means [H₃O⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³, and [A⁻] equals this. Since ionization is small, [HA] ≈ 0.010 mol dm⁻³. So Ka = [H₃O⁺][A⁻]/[HA] = (10⁻⁴)² ÷ 0.010 = 10⁻⁸ ÷ 10⁻² = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶.', wrong = '{"1": "1.0 × 10⁻⁴ is [H₃O⁺] read from the pH, not Ka.", "2": "1.0 × 10⁻² is the concentration of the acid, not Ka.", "3": "1.0 × 10⁻⁸ is [H₃O⁺]² before dividing by [HA] = 0.010."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0406' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Brine is a saturated solution with the equilibrium NaCl(s) ⇌ Na⁺ + Cl⁻. Passing in HCl gas adds extra Cl⁻, a common ion. To counteract this, the equilibrium shifts left and pure NaCl crystallizes out, while soluble impurities stay in solution. This is the common ion effect.', wrong = '{"0": "HCl supplies Cl⁻ ions. It does not react with Na⁺ to form any new precipitate.", "1": "No evaporation is involved. The crystals form because of the extra Cl⁻ ions.", "2": "The crystallization is caused by the common ion shifting the equilibrium, not by cooling."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0407' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'H₂S is a weak acid: H₂S ⇌ 2H⁺ + S²⁻. HCl is a strong acid that supplies a lot of H⁺, a common ion, which pushes this equilibrium to the left. The S²⁻ concentration stays low, which is just enough to precipitate the very insoluble group II sulphides but not the others.', wrong = '{"1": "HCl is not acting as an oxidizing agent here. Its role is to supply the common ion H⁺.", "2": "The common ion effect lowers [S²⁻], not raises it, by suppressing ionization of H₂S.", "3": "The aim is to precipitate the group II sulphides, not to dissolve them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0408' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ammonia ionizes slightly: NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻. NH₄Cl is a strong electrolyte that adds a large excess of NH₄⁺, a common ion. This pushes the equilibrium left and lowers [OH⁻], so only the group III hydroxides with very low solubility products precipitate.', wrong = '{"0": "The common ion NH₄⁺ shifts the equilibrium to the left, which lowers [OH⁻], not raises it.", "1": "Ammonia stays dissolved. NH₄Cl only reduces how much it ionizes.", "2": "The mixture stays basic. NH₃ with NH₄Cl actually forms a basic buffer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0409' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A basic buffer is made from a weak base and its salt with a strong acid, and its pH is above 7. NH₄OH is a weak base and NH₄Cl is its salt with HCl, so this pair forms a basic buffer.', wrong = '{"0": "A weak acid with its salt, such as CH₃COOH and CH₃COONa, forms an acidic buffer, not a basic one.", "1": "HCl is a strong acid. A strong acid and its salt cannot resist pH changes, so they do not form a buffer.", "3": "NaOH is a strong base. A strong base and its salt do not form a buffer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0410' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An acetic acid and sodium acetate buffer holds a reserve of CH₃COOH and CH₃COO⁻ ions. Added H₃O⁺ is taken up by CH₃COO⁻ to form acetic acid. Acetic acid is weak and stays mostly undissociated, so few extra H₃O⁺ ions remain and the pH barely changes.', wrong = '{"0": "Acetic acid is a weak acid. That is exactly why the CH₃COOH formed stays mostly undissociated.", "1": "The added acid does not evaporate. It reacts with acetate ions in the buffer.", "2": "Na⁺ is a spectator ion. It does not react with H₃O⁺."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0411' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acetate ion is a common ion for acetic acid, CH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻. Adding sodium acetate pushes this equilibrium left. As [CH₃COO⁻] rises from 0 to 0.15 mol dm⁻³, the percentage dissociation falls from 1.3% to 0.012% and the pH rises from 2.89 to 4.92.', wrong = '{"0": "The common ion suppresses dissociation, so fewer H⁺ ions form. The percentage dissociation falls and the pH rises.", "1": "The pH does change, rising from 2.89 to 4.92 as sodium acetate is added.", "3": "Adding acetate makes the solution less acidic, so the pH rises above 2.89 rather than falling below 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0412' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Henderson equation for an acidic buffer is pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]). With a ratio of 10, pH = 4.74 + log 10 = 4.74 + 1 = 5.74. More salt than acid makes the buffer less acidic than pKa.', wrong = '{"0": "pH = pKa = 4.74 only when [salt] = [acid], so that log 1 = 0.", "1": "3.74 comes from using the inverse ratio, log([acid]/[salt]) = log 0.1 = −1.", "3": "14.74 comes from adding the ratio 10 itself instead of its logarithm, log 10 = 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0413' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using the Henderson equation, pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]) = 4.20 + log(0.30/0.10) = 4.20 + log 3 = 4.20 + 0.48 = 4.68. The salt is in excess, so the pH is above the pKa.', wrong = '{"1": "3.72 comes from using log([acid]/[salt]) = log(1/3) = −0.48, the inverse ratio.", "2": "pH = pKa = 4.20 only when the salt and acid concentrations are equal.", "3": "4.50 comes from adding the salt concentration 0.30 directly instead of the log of the ratio."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0414' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Blood must stay close to pH 7.4. Its main buffering system involves bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻) together with carbonic acid (H₂CO₃). Added acid is taken up by HCO₃⁻ and added base by H₂CO₃, which keeps the blood pH stable.', wrong = '{"0": "Nitrates are not part of the main blood buffer system.", "1": "Sulphate is the anion of a strong acid, so it cannot act as the blood''s buffer.", "2": "Chloride is the anion of a strong acid (HCl), so it does not take up H⁺ and cannot buffer the blood."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0415' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Al(OH)₃ dissolves as Al(OH)₃(s) ⇌ Al³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq). The solubility product is the product of the ion concentrations in a saturated solution, each raised to the power of its coefficient. So Ksp = [Al³⁺][OH⁻]³.', wrong = '{"1": "The power 3 belongs to OH⁻, since three OH⁻ ions are released. Only one Al³⁺ ion forms.", "2": "The coefficient 3 of OH⁻ is missing. It must appear as a power on [OH⁻].", "3": "Coefficients are written as powers outside the brackets, not as multipliers inside them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0416' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The solubility product describes the equilibrium between a sparingly soluble salt and its ions in a saturated solution. Its value is usually very small. Like other equilibrium constants, Ksp changes with temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "Very soluble salts such as NaCl dissolve and dissociate fully. Ksp is used for slightly soluble salts.", "1": "Ksp is an equilibrium constant, so its value depends on temperature.", "3": "The undissolved solid has a constant concentration, so it is left out of the Ksp expression."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0417' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'AgBr dissolves as AgBr(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Br⁻. If S is the molar solubility, [Ag⁺] = [Br⁻] = S, so Ksp = S². Then S = √(5.0 × 10⁻¹³) = 7.1 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³. A quick check: (7.1 × 10⁻⁷)² ≈ 5.0 × 10⁻¹³.', wrong = '{"0": "2.5 × 10⁻²⁵ is Ksp squared. The solubility needs the square root of Ksp, not its square.", "2": "5.0 × 10⁻¹³ is the Ksp value itself, not the solubility. Ksp = S² for this salt.", "3": "3.5 × 10⁻⁷ comes from Ksp = 4S², which applies to a 1:2 salt pattern, not to AgBr where Ksp = S²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0418' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'CaF₂ dissolves as CaF₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺ + 2F⁻. If the solubility is S, then [Ca²⁺] = S and [F⁻] = 2S, so Ksp = S(2S)² = 4S³. S³ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ ÷ 4 = 8.0 × 10⁻¹², so S = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³.', wrong = '{"1": "5.7 × 10⁻⁶ is √Ksp, which treats CaF₂ like a 1:1 salt. CaF₂ releases two F⁻ ions.", "2": "3.2 × 10⁻⁴ is the cube root of Ksp without first dividing by 4.", "3": "8.0 × 10⁻¹² is S³. The cube root still has to be taken."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0419' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'BaCO₃ dissolves as BaCO₃ ⇌ Ba²⁺ + CO₃²⁻, so [Ba²⁺] = [CO₃²⁻] = S. Then Ksp = S² = (1.05 × 10⁻⁵)² = 1.1 × 10⁻¹⁰. This matches the tabulated Ksp for BaCO₃. For any 1:1 salt, Ksp is simply the square of the molar solubility.', wrong = '{"1": "1.05 × 10⁻⁵ is the molar solubility. Ksp is the product of the two ion concentrations, S × S.", "2": "2.1 × 10⁻⁵ comes from adding the two ion concentrations (S + S) instead of multiplying them.", "3": "4.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ comes from 4S³, which applies to a 1:2 salt such as CaF₂, not BaCO₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0420' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mixing equal volumes doubles the volume, so each concentration is halved to 2.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³. The ionic product is [Ca²⁺][SO₄²⁻] = (2.0 × 10⁻³)² = 4.0 × 10⁻⁶. This is less than Ksp (2 × 10⁻⁵), so the solution is unsaturated and no precipitate forms.', wrong = '{"1": "1.6 × 10⁻⁵ ignores the halving on mixing. Even this value is below 2 × 10⁻⁵, so it would not cause precipitation.", "2": "A precipitate forms only when the ionic product exceeds Ksp, not whenever the two ions meet.", "3": "The concentrations must be halved on mixing, and 1.6 × 10⁻⁵ is not equal to 2 × 10⁻⁵ anyway."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0421' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Br⁻ from NaBr is a common ion, so [Br⁻] ≈ 0.010 mol dm⁻³ and almost all of it comes from NaBr. The solubility S equals [Ag⁺] = Ksp ÷ [Br⁻] = 5.0 × 10⁻¹³ ÷ 0.010 = 5.0 × 10⁻¹¹ mol dm⁻³. This is far lower than in pure water.', wrong = '{"0": "7.1 × 10⁻⁷ is the solubility in pure water. The common ion Br⁻ lowers it greatly.", "2": "5.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ comes from multiplying Ksp by 0.010 instead of dividing.", "3": "7.1 × 10⁻⁵ is higher than in pure water, but a common ion always decreases solubility."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0422' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A saturated PbCrO₄ solution holds PbCrO₄(s) ⇌ Pb²⁺ + CrO₄²⁻. Na₂CrO₄ adds extra CrO₄²⁻, a common ion, so the ionic product now exceeds Ksp. The equilibrium shifts left and more PbCrO₄ precipitates. Ksp itself stays constant.', wrong = '{"1": "A common ion lowers solubility, so PbCrO₄ comes out of solution rather than dissolving.", "2": "Ksp depends only on temperature, so adding Na₂CrO₄ does not change it.", "3": "Na⁺ is a spectator, but the added CrO₄²⁻ ions are a common ion and do shift the equilibrium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0423' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'NH₄Cl is the salt of a strong acid (HCl) and a weak base (NH₃). The NH₄⁺ ion is a weak acid and reacts with water: NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺. The H₃O⁺ formed makes the solution acidic, while Cl⁻ has no effect on pH.', wrong = '{"1": "NaCl comes from a strong acid and a strong base. Neither ion reacts with water, so its solution is neutral.", "2": "Sodium acetate comes from a weak acid and a strong base. Acetate ions give OH⁻, so its solution is basic.", "3": "K₂CO₃ comes from a weak acid and a strong base. CO₃²⁻ ions give OH⁻, so its solution is basic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0424' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sodium acetate is a salt of a weak acid and a strong base. The acetate ion, the conjugate base of weak acetic acid, reacts with water: CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻. The OH⁻ produced makes the solution basic, while Na⁺ does not affect the pH.', wrong = '{"0": "Na⁺ comes from a strong base, so it does not react with water to any real extent.", "2": "Sodium acetate is a salt, not a strong base. Its basic nature comes from hydrolysis of the acetate ion.", "3": "Acetic acid is a weak acid. That is why its conjugate base, acetate, is strong enough to take H⁺ from water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0425' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ammonium acetate comes from a weak acid and a weak base, so both ions hydrolyse. NH₄⁺ produces H₃O⁺, and CH₃COO⁻ produces OH⁻. The final pH depends on which ion reacts more, so the solution may be slightly acidic, slightly basic or nearly neutral.', wrong = '{"0": "The acetate ion also hydrolyses, producing OH⁻, so NH₄⁺ alone does not decide the pH.", "1": "The NH₄⁺ ion also hydrolyses, producing H₃O⁺, so acetate alone does not decide the pH.", "3": "The pH is not fixed at 7. It depends on the balance of the two hydrolysis reactions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0426' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a strongly acidic solution there is a large excess of H⁺. By Le Chatelier''s principle, the equilibrium HIn ⇌ H⁺ + In⁻ shifts left, as In⁻ ions accept protons. So the indicator is present mainly as un-ionized HIn and shows its acid colour.', wrong = '{"0": "Added H⁺ is a product of the ionization, so it pushes the equilibrium towards HIn, not In⁻.", "2": "HIn and In⁻ are present in similar amounts only within the indicator''s colour-change range, not in strong acid.", "3": "This indicator model involves only two forms, HIn and In⁻. No H₂In⁺ form is considered."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0427' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The colour of an indicator depends on the ratio of HIn to In⁻ at a given pH. Methyl red is red in its HIn form and yellow in its In⁻ form, and it changes between pH 4.4 and 6.2. At pH 4.4 it is mostly HIn and looks red.', wrong = '{"0": "pH 1.2 to 2.8 is a much more acidic range than methyl red''s colour change.", "1": "pH 8.2 to 10.0 is the colour-change range of phenolphthalein, not methyl red.", "3": "pH 10.0 to 12.0 is far more basic than methyl red''s range of 4.4 to 6.2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0428' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each indicator changes colour gradually over a pH range called its indicator range, as the ratio of HIn to In⁻ shifts. For most indicators this range is about 2 pH units, for example 8.2 to 10.0 for phenolphthalein.', wrong = '{"0": "The colour change of a single indicator is gradual and spreads over more than 0.1 pH unit.", "2": "A range of 7 pH units would be far too broad to mark an end point.", "3": "14 pH units is the whole normal pH scale, not the range of one indicator."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0429' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The equivalence point is where the added titrant has exactly neutralized the analyte. The end point is where the indicator changes colour. An indicator is chosen so that its end point falls very close to the equivalence point.', wrong = '{"0": "The point where acid and base are in exact stoichiometric amounts is the equivalence point, not the end point.", "2": "The pH at equivalence is 7 only for strong acid and strong base titrations. Other titrations end above or below 7.", "3": "The amount left in the burette has nothing to do with the end point."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0430' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the equivalence point all the ammonia has become NH₄Cl. NH₄⁺, the conjugate acid of a weak base, releases H⁺ ions, so the pH is below 7, about 5.3. Methyl orange changes colour in the acidic region, so it is used for strong acid and weak base titrations.', wrong = '{"0": "The pH at equivalence is not 7, because NH₄⁺ makes the solution acidic. Phenolphthalein changes colour only in basic solution.", "2": "A pH above 7 at equivalence is typical of a weak acid and strong base titration, not this one.", "3": "The equivalence pH is acidic, about 5.3, not 12."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0431' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a strong acid and strong base titration the pH at equivalence is 7.0. Near this point, a single drop of base makes the pH jump steeply from about 4 to about 10. Any indicator that changes colour within this range, such as phenolphthalein, can be used.', wrong = '{"0": "pH 1 to 2 is the flat, low-pH region at the start of the titration, not the steep jump.", "2": "The steep part is much larger than one pH unit. It spans roughly 4 to 10.", "3": "pH 11 to 13 is the flat region reached after excess NaOH has been added."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0432' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Oxidation is the loss of electrons. When an atom loses electrons, its oxidation number rises, for example Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ goes from +2 to +3. So in terms of oxidation number, oxidation is an increase in oxidation number.', wrong = '{"0": "A decrease in oxidation number happens when an atom gains electrons, which is reduction.", "1": "Gain of electrons is reduction, and it lowers the oxidation number.", "2": "If the oxidation number does not change, that atom has been neither oxidized nor reduced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0433' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a neutral compound the oxidation numbers add up to zero. Na is +1 and O is −2, so 2(+1) + 2S + 3(−2) = 0. This gives 2S − 4 = 0, so 2S = +4 and S = +2 on average for each sulphur atom.', wrong = '{"1": "+4 is the oxidation number of sulphur in SO₂ and sulphites, not in thiosulphate.", "2": "+6 is the oxidation number of sulphur in sulphates such as SO₄²⁻ and in H₂SO₄.", "3": "−2 is the oxidation number of sulphur in sulphides such as Na₂S."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0434' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a polyatomic ion the oxidation numbers add up to the charge on the ion. Hydrogen with a non-metal is +1, so N + 4(+1) = +1. Solving, N = +1 − 4 = −3. This is the same value nitrogen has in NH₃, since adding H⁺ does not change its oxidation number.', wrong = '{"1": "The sign is wrong. Nitrogen is more electronegative than hydrogen, so it takes the negative value, −3.", "2": "+4 ignores the fact that the four hydrogens contribute +4 and the ion''s total charge is only +1.", "3": "+1 is the overall charge on the NH₄⁺ ion, not the oxidation number of nitrogen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0435' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Potassium, a group 1 metal, is always +1. In KO₂ the total must be zero, so +1 + 2(O) = 0, giving 2(O) = −1 and O = −½. Oxygen is −2 in normal oxides like H₂O, −1 in peroxides like Na₂O₂, and −½ in superoxides like KO₂.', wrong = '{"0": "−2 is oxygen''s usual oxidation number in ordinary oxides such as H₂O.", "1": "−1 is oxygen''s oxidation number in peroxides such as Na₂O₂ and H₂O₂.", "3": "+2 is oxygen''s oxidation number in F₂O, where it is bonded to the more electronegative fluorine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0436' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hydrogen is +1 when combined with non-metals, which are more electronegative. In metal hydrides such as NaH, hydrogen is bonded to a less electronegative metal. Sodium is +1, so hydrogen must be −1 to make the compound neutral.', wrong = '{"0": "Chlorine is more electronegative than hydrogen, so hydrogen is +1 in HCl.", "1": "Oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, so hydrogen is +1 in water.", "3": "Nitrogen is more electronegative than hydrogen, so hydrogen is +1 in ammonia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0437' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Oxygen is negative in almost all its compounds. The exception is with fluorine, the only element more electronegative than oxygen. In F₂O each F is −1, so O + 2(−1) = 0, giving O = +2.', wrong = '{"0": "H₂O₂ is a peroxide, where oxygen is −1.", "1": "KO₂ is a superoxide, where oxygen is −½.", "2": "Na₂O is a normal oxide, where oxygen has its usual value of −2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0438' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a disproportionation reaction the same element is both oxidized and reduced. In H₂O₂ oxygen is −1. Some of it is reduced to −2 in H₂O and some is oxidized to 0 in O₂, so oxygen changes in both directions.', wrong = '{"0": "Two different elements change here: sodium is oxidized (0 to +1) and chlorine is reduced (0 to −1).", "1": "Two different elements change here: zinc is oxidized (0 to +2) and copper is reduced (+2 to 0).", "3": "No oxidation numbers change in the decomposition of CaCO₃, so it is not a redox reaction at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0439' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An oxidizing agent takes electrons from another substance and is itself reduced. Here Cu²⁺ gains two electrons to become Cu, and in doing so it oxidizes Zn to Zn²⁺. So Cu²⁺ is the oxidizing agent and Zn is the reducing agent.', wrong = '{"0": "Zn loses two electrons and is oxidized, so it is the reducing agent.", "1": "Cu is the product formed when Cu²⁺ is reduced. It does not oxidize anything in this reaction.", "2": "Zn²⁺ is the product formed when zinc is oxidized. It does not act as the oxidizing agent here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0440' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cu goes from 0 to +2, losing 2e⁻, while N goes from +5 in HNO₃ to +4 in NO₂, gaining 1e⁻. So 2NO₂ are needed per Cu, using 2HNO₃. Two more HNO₃ supply the nitrate ions that stay unchanged in Cu(NO₃)₂. The balanced equation is Cu + 4HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO₂ + 2H₂O.', wrong = '{"0": "Two HNO₃ only supply the nitrogen that is reduced to NO₂. Two more are needed as nitrate in Cu(NO₃)₂.", "2": "With 6HNO₃ the nitrogen, hydrogen and oxygen atoms cannot all balance for one Cu.", "3": "8HNO₃ belongs to dilute nitric acid, 3Cu + 8HNO₃ → 3Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO + 4H₂O, which gives NO, not NO₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0441' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An electrolytic cell uses electrical energy from a direct current supply to drive a chemical change. The cathode is connected to the negative terminal: cations move to it and gain electrons, so reduction happens there. The anode is positive, and oxidation takes place there.', wrong = '{"0": "Converting chemical energy into electrical energy is what a galvanic (voltaic) cell does, not an electrolytic cell.", "1": "In an electrolytic cell the anode is the positive electrode. Oxidation does happen there, but it is not negative.", "3": "Electrolysis needs direct current, so each electrode keeps a fixed charge. Alternating current would keep reversing them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0442' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In molten ZnCl₂ the ions are Zn²⁺ and Cl⁻. Anions move to the positive anode and lose electrons, which is oxidation: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻. Chlorine gas is given off at the anode, while zinc metal is deposited at the cathode.', wrong = '{"0": "Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn is a reduction. It happens at the cathode, not the anode.", "1": "Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻ is a reduction, the reverse of what happens. The anode is the site of oxidation.", "2": "Molten ZnCl₂ with inert electrodes has no zinc metal at the anode to be oxidized. Zinc forms at the cathode."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0443' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Charge is current multiplied by time, Q = I × t, with time in seconds. Ten minutes is 10 × 60 = 600 s, so Q = 2.0 A × 600 s = 1200 C. One coulomb is the charge carried by a current of one ampere flowing for one second.', wrong = '{"0": "20 C comes from using the time in minutes (2.0 × 10). Time must be in seconds.", "2": "120 C comes from using 60 s, which is only one minute, instead of 600 s.", "3": "300 C comes from dividing 600 s by 2.0 A instead of multiplying."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0444' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles of electrons = charge ÷ F = 1930 ÷ 96 500 = 0.0200 mol. Each Cu²⁺ needs 2 electrons (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu), so moles of Cu = 0.0200 ÷ 2 = 0.0100 mol. Mass = 0.0100 × 63.5 = 0.635 g.', wrong = '{"0": "1.27 g ignores the 2 electrons needed per Cu²⁺ ion, taking 0.0200 mol of copper instead of 0.0100 mol.", "2": "0.318 g comes from dividing the moles of electrons by 4 instead of 2.", "3": "63.5 g is a full mole of copper, which needs 2 × 96 500 = 193 000 C, a hundred times more charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0445' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles of Al = 9.0 ÷ 27 = ⅓ mol. Each Al³⁺ needs 3 electrons (Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al), so ⅓ mol of Al needs 3 × ⅓ = 1 mol of electrons. One mole of electrons is 1 faraday, which is 96 500 C.', wrong = '{"1": "32 167 C is 96 500 ÷ 3, which forgets that each Al³⁺ ion needs 3 electrons.", "2": "289 500 C is 3 × 96 500, the charge to deposit a whole mole (27 g) of aluminium, not 9.0 g.", "3": "48 250 C is half a faraday, as if each Al³⁺ needed only 1.5 electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0446' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moles of electrons = 9650 ÷ 96 500 = 0.100 mol. At the cathode 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂, so 2 moles of electrons give 1 mole of H₂. Moles of H₂ = 0.0500 mol, and the volume at STP = 0.0500 × 22.4 = 1.12 dm³.', wrong = '{"0": "2.24 dm³ assumes one mole of H₂ per mole of electrons. Each H₂ molecule needs two electrons.", "2": "0.56 dm³ assumes four electrons per molecule, which applies to O₂ at the anode, not H₂.", "3": "11.2 dm³ is the volume of 0.500 mol of H₂, ten times too much. It comes from a power-of-ten slip."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0447' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The charge on one mole of electrons equals the Avogadro constant times the charge on one electron. So NA = 95 250 C mol⁻¹ ÷ (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) = 5.95 × 10²³ mol⁻¹. This is close to, but not the same as, the accepted value because of experimental error.', wrong = '{"1": "1.52 × 10⁻¹⁴ comes from multiplying the two charges instead of dividing.", "2": "6.02 × 10²³ is the accepted value. These experimental data give 5.95 × 10²³ instead.", "3": "1.68 × 10⁻²⁴ comes from dividing the electron''s charge by 95 250, the inverse of the correct division."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0448' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With copper electrodes in CuSO₄, the anode dissolves (Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻) and loses mass, while copper is deposited on the cathode. The deposited copper does not always stick firmly to the cathode, so some may fall off. Weighing the anode''s loss in mass gives a more reliable result.', wrong = '{"0": "The anode does change in mass. Copper atoms leave it as Cu²⁺ ions, so it gets lighter.", "1": "Copper is deposited on the cathode, not the anode. The anode dissolves.", "3": "It is the anode that dissolves. The cathode gains copper."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0449' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a metal is placed in a solution of its ions, charges separate at the surface and form an electrical double layer. The potential of this single layer cannot be measured directly, since any meter needs a second electrode. Only the difference from a reference, the standard hydrogen electrode, can be measured.', wrong = '{"0": "The metal does set up an equilibrium with its ions. That equilibrium is what creates the potential.", "1": "Ions in solution do carry electric current. That is how cells work.", "2": "The metal surface does become charged. Zinc, for example, gains excess electrons as Zn²⁺ ions leave it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0450' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Platinum black is finely divided platinum with a very large surface area. It gives close contact between H₂ gas and H⁺ ions in solution, so the equilibrium 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ H₂ is set up quickly. The platinum itself is inert and only provides a surface and electrical contact.', wrong = '{"0": "Platinum is inert and does not form a compound with hydrogen here.", "1": "The standard hydrogen electrode is assigned a potential of 0.00 V, not +1.00 V.", "3": "Platinum is inert. It is not an oxidizing agent and takes no part in the reaction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0451' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electrode potentials depend on concentration, temperature and pressure, so a standard set of conditions is fixed. Standard electrode potentials use ion concentrations of 1.00 mol dm⁻³, a temperature of 298 K (25 °C), and gases at 1 atm, measured against the standard hydrogen electrode.', wrong = '{"0": "Neither 0.10 mol dm⁻³ nor 273 K is a standard condition for electrode potentials.", "1": "Electrode potential changes with concentration, so the concentration must be fixed at 1.00 mol dm⁻³.", "2": "The concentration is right, but the standard temperature is 298 K, not 373 K."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0452' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A more positive E° means a greater tendency to be reduced. Cu²⁺/Cu (+0.34 V) is more positive than H⁺/H₂ (0.00 V), so Cu²⁺ ions are reduced more easily than H⁺. In a cell with the SHE, Cu²⁺ accepts electrons from the H₂/H⁺ half-cell.', wrong = '{"0": "A more positive E° means a species is easier to reduce, so Cu²⁺ is easier, not harder, to reduce than H⁺.", "1": "H₂ gives electrons to Cu²⁺, so hydrogen is the stronger reducing agent, not copper.", "3": "Copper lies below hydrogen in the series and does not release hydrogen from dilute acids."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0453' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Zinc has E° = −0.76 V, more negative than the SHE. So zinc is oxidized (Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻) at the negative terminal, and electrons flow to the hydrogen electrode, where 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂. In a galvanic cell, reduction always occurs at the positive terminal.', wrong = '{"0": "The zinc electrode is the negative terminal, but oxidation takes place there, not reduction.", "1": "One electrode is the site of oxidation and the other is the site of reduction. They are not equal.", "2": "The salt bridge only lets ions move between the half-cells. No electron transfer happens in it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0454' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A salt bridge connects the two half-cells and lets ions move between them, keeping each solution electrically neutral, but it must not carry electrons. A strip of filter paper (or other porous material) soaked in saturated potassium nitrate solution does this well.', wrong = '{"0": "A copper wire carries electrons, not ions. A salt bridge must conduct by ions only.", "1": "A glass rod does not conduct ions or electrons, so the circuit would stay broken.", "3": "A zinc strip carries electrons and would also react with the solutions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0455' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Magnesium has the more negative E°, so it is oxidized at the anode, while Cu²⁺ is reduced at the cathode. E°cell = E°(cathode) − E°(anode) = +0.34 − (−2.37) = +2.71 V. A positive value shows the cell works spontaneously.', wrong = '{"1": "2.03 V comes from adding the two values as written, −2.37 + 0.34 = −2.03, and dropping the sign.", "2": "A working galvanic cell always has a positive E°cell. The negative sign comes from subtracting the wrong way round.", "3": "1.10 V is the standard potential of the Zn–Cu (Daniell) cell, not the Mg–Cu cell."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0456' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Zinc has the more negative E°, so it is the anode and Ag⁺ is reduced at the cathode. E°cell = E°(cathode) − E°(anode) = 0.80 − (−0.76) = 1.56 V. E° values are not multiplied by 2 even though the equation needs 2Ag⁺.', wrong = '{"1": "0.04 V comes from adding −0.76 and +0.80 instead of subtracting the anode value from the cathode value.", "2": "0.84 V comes from doubling E°(Ag⁺/Ag) to 1.60 V and then adding −0.76 V.", "3": "2.36 V comes from doubling E°(Ag⁺/Ag) because of 2Ag⁺. Electrode potentials do not change with coefficients."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0457' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Zinc has the more negative E°, so zinc atoms lose electrons (Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻) and the Zn electrode is the negative pole. The electrons travel through the external wire to the Ag electrode, the positive pole, where Ag⁺ is reduced. Electrons never pass through the solutions.', wrong = '{"0": "Electrons never travel through the electrolyte solution, and the direction is also reversed.", "1": "The salt bridge carries ions, not electrons, to keep the half-cells neutral.", "2": "The wire is right but the direction is reversed. Zinc is the source of electrons, so they flow from Zn to Ag."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0458' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To reduce Zn²⁺ to Zn, a metal must give up electrons more readily than zinc, so its E° must be more negative than −0.76 V. Magnesium has E° = −2.37 V, so it is a stronger reducing agent than zinc and can reduce Zn²⁺ ions.', wrong = '{"1": "Silver''s E° (+0.80 V) is far more positive than −0.76 V. It is a very weak reducing agent.", "2": "Copper''s E° (+0.34 V) is more positive than −0.76 V, which is why it cannot reduce Zn²⁺.", "3": "Lead''s E° (−0.13 V) is less negative than −0.76 V, so lead cannot reduce Zn²⁺ either."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0459' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The half-cell with the more positive E° is reduced. Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ (+0.77 V) is more positive than I₂/I⁻ (+0.54 V), so Fe³⁺ is the stronger oxidizing agent and oxidizes I⁻ to I₂: 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. E°cell = 0.77 − 0.54 = +0.23 V, so the reaction is feasible.', wrong = '{"0": "Fe²⁺ reducing I₂ is the reverse reaction. Its E°cell is −0.23 V, so it does not happen under standard conditions.", "1": "I₂ oxidizing Fe²⁺ would give E°cell = 0.54 − 0.77 = −0.23 V, so it is not feasible.", "3": "The reaction of Fe³⁺ with I⁻ has a positive E°cell of +0.23 V, so a reaction does occur."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0460' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, z = 1 and silver is a solid, so only [Ag⁺] appears. E = E° + 0.059 log[Ag⁺] = 0.80 + 0.059 × log(0.010) = 0.80 + 0.059 × (−2) = 0.80 − 0.118 = +0.68 V.', wrong = '{"0": "+0.74 V comes from using z = 2. Each Ag⁺ ion gains only one electron, so z = 1.", "2": "+0.92 V comes from adding 0.118 V. Since log(0.010) = −2, the potential falls, not rises.", "3": "E equals E° (+0.80 V) only when [Ag⁺] = 1.00 mol dm⁻³. Here it is 0.010 mol dm⁻³."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0461' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For Mⁿ⁺ + ne⁻ ⇌ M, lowering [Mⁿ⁺] shifts the equilibrium to the left by Le Chatelier''s principle, so the metal releases electrons more readily. The electrode potential becomes less positive. For example, zinc in 0.1 mol dm⁻³ Zn²⁺ has E more negative than −0.76 V.', wrong = '{"1": "E becomes more positive when the concentration of the metal ion is increased, not decreased.", "2": "Electrode potential depends on ion concentration, as the Nernst equation shows, so it does change.", "3": "There is no reason for the potential to become exactly zero. It just shifts to a less positive value."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0462' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the activity series, the most reactive metals, from lithium down to sodium, react with cold water and release hydrogen. Sodium reacts vigorously: 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂. Metals lower in the series, such as zinc and iron, need steam or acids.', wrong = '{"0": "Copper lies below hydrogen in the series. It reacts with neither water nor dilute acids.", "1": "Zinc reacts with steam or dilute acids, but not with cold water.", "2": "Iron reacts with steam or dilute acids, but not with cold water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0463' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A metal higher in the activity series, with a more negative E°, can displace a metal lower in the series from its ions. Iron (E° = −0.44 V) is above silver (E° = +0.80 V), so iron displaces silver. E°cell = 0.80 − (−0.44) = +1.24 V, which is positive, so the reaction occurs.', wrong = '{"0": "Copper (+0.34 V) is below zinc (−0.76 V) in the series, so copper cannot displace zinc from its ions.", "2": "Silver is below iron in the series. This is the reverse of the feasible reaction, so it does not occur.", "3": "Copper (+0.34 V) is far below magnesium (−2.37 V), so it cannot displace magnesium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0464' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a photovoltaic (solar) cell, photons of light give energy to electrons in a semiconductor, lifting them from the valence band to the conduction band. This leaves positive holes behind. The PN junction separates the electrons and holes, which creates a voltage and drives a current.', wrong = '{"0": "A photovoltaic cell turns light directly into electrical energy. It does not store it as chemical energy.", "2": "Converting electricity into light is what a light-emitting diode does, the reverse of a solar cell.", "3": "The photovoltaic effect produces a voltage and current, not heat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0465' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Photovoltaic cells turn sunlight directly into electricity. Sunlight is renewable and will not run out, so PV systems are a sustainable source of energy that can keep working as long as light falls on them. They do not depend on fossil fuels and produce no exhaust gases while running.', wrong = '{"1": "PV cells run on sunlight, so they are independent of fossil fuels. That independence is one of their main merits.", "2": "The photovoltaic effect needs light to excite electrons. Without light no current is produced.", "3": "PV cells convert light energy into electrical energy. They have nothing to do with nuclear energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0466' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) is the amount of oxygen used by microorganisms as they oxidize the dissolved organic matter in a water sample. It is measured over five days in a sealed sample kept in the dark at a fixed temperature. A high BOD means a lot of organic pollution.', wrong = '{"0": "The oxygen dissolved in water at a given moment is called dissolved oxygen (DO), not BOD.", "2": "Plants add oxygen to water. BOD measures the oxygen used up by microorganisms, not oxygen released.", "3": "BOD measures biological oxidation by microorganisms, not burning of the sample."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0467' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Clean water normally has a dissolved oxygen level of 5 to 8 mg dm⁻³, and 5 mg dm⁻³ is the minimum acceptable level. A DO of 3 mg dm⁻³ is below this, so the water is regarded as polluted. Low DO usually goes with high BOD, since microorganisms breaking down organic matter use up the oxygen.', wrong = '{"0": "The minimum acceptable DO is 5 mg dm⁻³, not 2 mg dm⁻³, so 3 mg dm⁻³ indicates polluted water.", "2": "DO and BOD are inversely related. Low DO points to a high BOD, not a low one.", "3": "3 mg dm⁻³ is below the normal range of 5 to 8 mg dm⁻³, so the water has less oxygen than normal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0468' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the Winkler method, the dissolved oxygen is fixed chemically and then made to oxidize iodide ions to iodine. The amount of iodine formed matches the amount of oxygen. This iodine is titrated with sodium thiosulphate, using starch, which gives a dark blue colour with iodine, to detect the end point.', wrong = '{"1": "Potassium permanganate is not used in the Winkler method. The iodine is titrated with thiosulphate.", "2": "NaOH with phenolphthalein is an acid–base titration, not a method for titrating iodine.", "3": "Silver nitrate with potassium chromate is used to find chloride ions, not iodine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0469' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Aromatic hydrocarbons are based on the benzene ring, C₆H₆, and have a high carbon-to-hydrogen ratio. Methylbenzene (toluene), C₆H₅CH₃, is benzene with one hydrogen replaced by a methyl group, so it is aromatic.', wrong = '{"1": "Hexane is an open-chain saturated hydrocarbon, an alkane, so it is aliphatic, not aromatic.", "2": "Hex-1-ene is an open-chain hydrocarbon with one C=C bond, an alkene, so it is aliphatic.", "3": "Cyclohexane has a ring, but it is saturated with no benzene ring. It is a cycloalkane, an alicyclic compound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0470' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Open-chain alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. For 12 carbon atoms, the number of hydrogens is 2 × 12 + 2 = 26, so the formula is C₁₂H₂₆ (dodecane). Each extra CH₂ unit adds one carbon and two hydrogens to the chain.', wrong = '{"0": "C₁₂H₂₄ fits CₙH₂ₙ, the formula of alkenes and cycloalkanes, not of open-chain alkanes.", "1": "C₁₂H₂₈ has more than 2n + 2 hydrogens. No alkane can hold that many, since carbon forms only four bonds.", "2": "C₁₂H₂₂ fits CₙH₂ₙ₋₂, the formula of alkynes, not alkanes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0471' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Joining the two ends of an alkane chain into a ring removes two hydrogen atoms, so cycloalkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ. Cyclopentane has five carbons, so its formula is C₅H₁₀.', wrong = '{"0": "C₅H₁₂ is pentane, the open-chain alkane, which follows CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.", "1": "C₆H₁₂ is cyclohexane, which has six carbons in its ring, not five.", "2": "C₅H₈ has two fewer hydrogens than cyclopentane. It fits CₙH₂ₙ₋₂, such as a pentyne or a cyclopentene."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0472' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The isobutyl group, (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–, is attached through its CH₂ carbon. Numbering from that attachment point, the longest chain is three carbons (propyl), and a methyl branch sits on carbon 2. So its IUPAC name is 2-methylpropyl.', wrong = '{"0": "1-methylpropyl is the sec-butyl group, CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)–, attached through a CH carbon.", "1": "Butyl is the straight-chain n-butyl group, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂–, with no branch.", "2": "1,1-dimethylethyl is the tert-butyl group, (CH₃)₃C–, attached through a carbon with three methyls."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0473' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The longest continuous chain has six carbons, so the parent is hexane. Numbering from either end puts the two methyl groups on carbons 3 and 4. The prefix di- shows two identical groups, so the name is 3,4-dimethylhexane.', wrong = '{"0": "Numbering must give the substituents the lowest possible numbers. 3,4 is lower than 4,5.", "1": "This picks a five-carbon chain, but the longest chain has six carbons, so the ethyl is part of the main chain.", "2": "This picks a four-carbon chain. The ethyl groups are really part of the longest six-carbon chain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0474' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The longest chain has four carbons, so the parent is butane. Numbering from the end nearer the branches puts both methyl groups on carbon 2. The number is written once for each group, giving 2,2-dimethylbutane.', wrong = '{"1": "Hexane is the unbranched C₆H₁₄ isomer. This compound has a branched four-carbon chain.", "2": "This picks a three-carbon chain, but the longest chain is four carbons, so ethyl becomes part of it.", "3": "Numbering must start from the end nearer the substituents, giving 2,2 rather than 3,3."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0475' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each carbon in an alkane forms four σ bonds using four sp³ hybrid orbitals. These point to the corners of a tetrahedron, giving bond angles of about 109.5°. The C–C single bond length is 1.54 Å.', wrong = '{"0": "sp hybridization with 180° bond angles is found in alkynes such as ethyne, not alkanes.", "1": "sp² hybridization with 120° bond angles is found in alkenes such as ethene.", "2": "Alkane carbons do hybridize, forming four equivalent sp³ orbitals for their four σ bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0476' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each carbon in ethene is sp² hybridized and keeps one unhybridized p orbital at right angles to the plane of the molecule. These two parallel p orbitals overlap sideways, above and below the C–C axis, to form the π bond.', wrong = '{"0": "Head-on overlap of two sp² orbitals forms the C–C σ bond, not the π bond.", "1": "The carbons in ethene are sp² hybridized, not sp³, and head-on overlap gives σ bonds.", "3": "Overlap of a carbon sp² orbital with a hydrogen 1s orbital forms a C–H σ bond."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0477' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The physical state of straight-chain alkanes depends on chain length. Those with 1 to 4 carbons are gases, those with 5 to 17 carbons are liquids, and those with 18 or more carbons are waxy solids. C₂₀H₄₂ has 20 carbons, so it is a waxy solid.', wrong = '{"0": "Only alkanes with up to four carbons (methane to butane) are gases at room temperature.", "1": "Liquid alkanes have 5 to 17 carbons. C₂₀H₄₂ is beyond this range.", "2": "Plasma is an ionized state reached only at very high temperatures, not at room temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0478' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Branching makes an alkane molecule more compact, reducing its surface area and the van der Waals forces between molecules. So branched isomers boil lower than straight-chain ones; for example butane boils at −0.5 °C and isobutane at −11.7 °C. 2,2-Dimethylpropane is the most branched C₅H₁₂ isomer, so it boils lowest.', wrong = '{"0": "Pentane is the straight-chain isomer, with the largest surface area, so it has the highest boiling point.", "1": "2-Methylbutane has one branch and is less compact than 2,2-dimethylpropane, so it boils higher.", "3": "Isomers have the same formula, but branching changes intermolecular forces and therefore boiling points."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0479' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alkanes are non-polar because C–H and C–C bonds have almost no polarity. Following the rule that like dissolves like, they do not dissolve in polar water but dissolve easily in non-polar solvents such as benzene, ether and hexane.', wrong = '{"0": "Alkanes are non-polar, not polar, and that is exactly why they do not dissolve in water.", "1": "Alkanes dissolve readily in non-polar solvents such as benzene and ether.", "3": "Alkanes do not dissolve in water, although they do dissolve in benzene."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0480' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In homolytic fission a covalent bond breaks evenly, with each atom keeping one of the two shared electrons. This produces free radicals, species with an unpaired electron, such as Cl• from Cl₂. Free radicals are very reactive because of that unpaired electron.', wrong = '{"0": "Hydrolysis is the splitting of a compound by reaction with water, not even bond breaking.", "1": "In heterolytic fission one atom takes both electrons, giving a positive and a negative ion, not radicals.", "2": "Ionization forms charged ions. Even bond breaking gives neutral radicals, not ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0481' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carbon (2.5) and hydrogen (2.1) have similar electronegativities, so C–H and C–C bonds are almost non-polar and offer no charged site for polar reagents to attack. Their σ bonds also hold electrons tightly between the nuclei and are strong, so a lot of energy is needed to break them.', wrong = '{"1": "Each carbon in an alkane uses all four valence electrons in bonds, so there are no lone pairs on carbon.", "2": "Alkanes are covalent compounds, not ionic ones.", "3": "Alkanes contain only σ bonds. π bonds are found in alkenes and would make them more reactive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0482' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Free radical chlorination begins with initiation, in which UV light supplies the energy to break the Cl–Cl bond evenly. Homolytic fission gives two chlorine free radicals: Cl₂ → 2Cl•. These radicals then start the chain of propagation steps.', wrong = '{"0": "A Cl• radical taking an H from methane to form CH₃• and HCl is a propagation step, not initiation.", "1": "This reaction goes by free radicals. Methane does not split into ions such as CH₃⁺ and H⁻.", "2": "Two radicals joining to form CH₃Cl is a termination step, which removes radicals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0483' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a termination step two free radicals join together. When two methyl radicals meet, they form ethane: CH₃• + CH₃• → C₂H₆. This happens only rarely, but finding traces of ethane supports the idea that methyl radicals are formed, so the reaction is a free radical one.', wrong = '{"1": "Chlorine is a reactant. It is used up to form chloromethane and HCl, so it is not a catalyst.", "2": "Methane is saturated and undergoes substitution, not addition.", "3": "Ethane forms from two methyl radicals joining, which points to a radical mechanism, not an ionic one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0484' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The reactivity of halogens with alkanes falls as F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂. Iodine is the least reactive, and its substitution reaction is too slow and also reversible, since the HI formed can reduce the iodoalkane back. So iodine does not substitute directly into alkanes.', wrong = '{"1": "Iodine is a solid at room temperature, not a gas, and its state is not the reason.", "2": "Iodine is the least reactive of these halogens, not the most reactive.", "3": "Alkanes also react with chlorine and bromine in light, not only with fluorine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0485' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With excess chlorine, the hydrogens of methane are replaced one after another, giving CH₃Cl, CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and finally CCl₄. Trichloromethane, CHCl₃, is commonly called chloroform, once used as an anaesthetic.', wrong = '{"0": "Carbon tetrachloride is CCl₄ (tetrachloromethane), with all four hydrogens replaced.", "1": "Vinyl chloride is chloroethene, CH₂=CHCl, the monomer of PVC. It is not formed from methane.", "3": "Methylene chloride is CH₂Cl₂ (dichloromethane), with only two hydrogens replaced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0486' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heating ethanol with excess concentrated sulphuric acid at about 180 °C removes a molecule of water (dehydration). The reaction goes through ethyl hydrogen sulphate as an intermediate, which then breaks down to give ethene, CH₂=CH₂.', wrong = '{"0": "At room temperature (25 °C) no dehydration takes place.", "1": "80 °C is too low for ethene to form from ethanol by this method.", "2": "500 °C is far higher than needed. The method uses about 180 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0487' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alcoholic KOH removes an H atom and a Br atom from neighbouring carbon atoms of bromoethane, forming a C=C double bond: CH₃CH₂Br → CH₂=CH₂ + HBr. Removing a small molecule this way is an elimination, and when the molecule lost is a hydrogen halide it is called dehydrohalogenation.', wrong = '{"0": "No free radicals are involved, and a double bond is formed rather than one atom replacing another.", "1": "In addition, atoms are added across a double bond. Here atoms are removed to create one.", "3": "Hydrogenation adds H₂ across a double bond. Here a double bond is formed, not removed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0488' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A π bond''s electron cloud lies above and below the line joining the two carbon nuclei. These electrons are held less firmly than σ electrons and are exposed, so they attract electrophiles (electron-seeking species). The π bond breaks easily, so alkenes readily undergo electrophilic addition.', wrong = '{"1": "Alkenes have fewer hydrogens than the alkanes with the same number of carbons, not more.", "2": "This is reversed. A π bond is weaker than a σ bond, which is why alkenes are more reactive.", "3": "Alkenes are covalent compounds, not ionic ones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0489' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The inductive effect is the pull or push of bonding electrons along a chain. A chlorine atom is more electronegative than carbon, so it pulls electrons towards itself and leaves the carbon slightly positive. This electron-withdrawing pull is a −I effect. Alkyl groups instead push electrons (+I).', wrong = '{"0": "A methyl group is an alkyl group that pushes electrons towards the chain, a +I effect.", "1": "An ethyl group is an alkyl group with a +I (electron-donating) effect.", "3": "A tert-butyl group is an alkyl group with a strong +I (electron-donating) effect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0490' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carbocations are classified by how many carbon atoms are bonded to the positively charged carbon. In (CH₃)₂CH⁺, the positive carbon carries two methyl groups and one hydrogen, so it is bonded to two carbons and is a secondary (2°) carbocation.', wrong = '{"1": "A primary carbocation has only one alkyl group on the positive carbon, as in CH₃CH₂⁺.", "2": "A methyl carbocation, CH₃⁺, has only hydrogen atoms on the positive carbon.", "3": "A tertiary carbocation has three alkyl groups on the positive carbon, as in (CH₃)₃C⁺."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0491' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When bromine solution is added to an alkene, bromine adds across the C=C double bond and its red-brown colour disappears at once. For example, ethene gives colourless 1,2-dibromoethane. This quick colour change is used to detect carbon–carbon unsaturation.', wrong = '{"0": "Alkanes have no double bonds and do not decolourize bromine solution in the dark.", "1": "Alcohols have no C=C bond and do not decolourize bromine. This is not a test for alcohols.", "3": "Carboxylic acids are identified by tests such as fizzing with NaHCO₃, not by bromine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0492' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As a Br₂ molecule approaches the C=C bond, the dense π electron cloud repels the Br–Br bonding electrons towards the farther bromine atom. The nearer bromine becomes slightly positive (δ+), an induced dipole. The π electrons then attack this δ+ bromine, so Br₂ behaves as an electrophile.', wrong = '{"0": "Br₂ is a covalent molecule made of two identical atoms, not an ionic compound.", "1": "Bromine addition to an alkene is an electrophilic addition and does not need light to form radicals.", "3": "Ethene is a neutral molecule. It is its electron-rich π bond that polarizes the bromine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0493' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All hydrogen halides add across a C=C bond in the same way, but their rates differ. Going from HF to HI the H–X bond gets longer and weaker, so it breaks more easily and gives up H⁺ more readily. The rate therefore increases in the order HF < HCl < HBr < HI.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse order. HI, with the weakest H–X bond, reacts fastest, not slowest.", "1": "The rates are not equal. They increase from HF to HI as the H–X bond weakens.", "2": "This order is mixed up. The rate rises steadily down the group, HF < HCl < HBr < HI."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0494' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Markovnikov''s rule, the H of HCl adds to the double-bonded carbon that already has more hydrogens, the =CH₂ carbon. This forms the more stable tertiary carbocation (CH₃)₃C⁺. Cl⁻ then attacks it, giving (CH₃)₃CCl, which is 2-chloro-2-methylpropane.', wrong = '{"0": "1-chloro-2-methylpropane would come from a less stable primary carbocation, so it is only a minor product.", "2": "1-chlorobutane has a straight carbon chain. Adding HCl cannot straighten the branched skeleton of 2-methylpropene.", "3": "2-chlorobutane also has a straight four-carbon chain, but 2-methylpropene has a branched skeleton."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0495' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hydrogen adds across the C=C double bonds of unsaturated vegetable oils when heated with a finely divided nickel catalyst. This raises their melting point and turns liquid oils into solid fats such as margarine (banaspati ghee).', wrong = '{"0": "PVC is made by polymerizing chloroethene (vinyl chloride), not by hydrogenation.", "2": "Ethanol is made industrially by adding steam to ethene with an acid catalyst, which is hydration.", "3": "Chloroform is made by chlorinating methane, a free radical substitution."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0496' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acid-catalysed hydration of an alkene goes through a carbocation, like the addition of HX. H⁺ adds to the CH₂ end of propene, forming the more stable secondary carbocation CH₃CH⁺CH₃. Water attacks this carbon and loses H⁺, giving CH₃CH(OH)CH₃, propan-2-ol.', wrong = '{"0": "Propan-1-ol would come from the less stable primary carbocation, so it is only a minor product.", "2": "Adding water to an alkene gives an alcohol, not an aldehyde such as propanal.", "3": "Propane is formed by adding hydrogen (hydrogenation), not steam."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0497' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Passing ethene and oxygen over a silver catalyst adds one oxygen atom across the double bond, giving ethylene oxide (epoxyethane). This is an epoxide, a three-membered ring of two carbons and one oxygen. Acid hydrolysis opens the strained ring to give ethane-1,2-diol, HOCH₂CH₂OH.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanol is made by adding steam to ethene. It is not hydrolysed to a diol.", "2": "Ethanal is a carbonyl compound obtained by other reactions such as ozonolysis or oxidation of ethanol, not by epoxidation.", "3": "Ethanoic acid is made by oxidizing ethanol with acidified dichromate, not from ethene and O₂ over silver."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0498' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ozonolysis followed by Zn/H₂O cuts the C=C bond and turns each of the two double-bonded carbons into a C=O group. In but-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃, the two halves are identical, so each gives CH₃CHO. The products are two molecules of ethanal.', wrong = '{"0": "Methanal and propanal come from but-1-ene, CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃, where the double bond is at the end of the chain.", "2": "Propanone and methanal come from 2-methylpropene, (CH₃)₂C=CH₂, which has a branched skeleton.", "3": "Ozonolysis splits the molecule at the double bond into two carbonyl compounds. It cannot give one four-carbon product."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0499' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In addition polymerization the C=C bond of each monomer opens to a single bond, and the monomers join into a long chain. The repeating unit is the monomer with its double bond made single. Chloroethene, CH₂=CHCl, therefore gives the repeating unit –CH₂–CHCl–.', wrong = '{"0": "–CH₂–CH₂– is the repeating unit of poly(ethene), made from ethene, which has no chlorine.", "2": "–CHCl–CHCl– would need a monomer with two chlorine atoms, CHCl=CHCl.", "3": "The C=C bond opens up during polymerization, so the repeating unit has only single bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0500' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A conjugated diene has two C=C bonds separated by exactly one single bond, so the π electrons can spread over all four carbons. Buta-1,3-diene, CH₂=CH–CH=CH₂, fits this pattern. It is used to make synthetic rubber.', wrong = '{"1": "Here the double bonds are separated by a CH₂ group, two single bonds apart, so they are isolated, not conjugated.", "2": "Here both double bonds share one carbon atom with no single bond between them. This is a cumulated diene (an allene).", "3": "But-2-ene has only one C=C bond, so it is not a diene at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0501' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Five carbon atoms can be arranged in three different skeletons. These give pentane (straight chain), 2-methylbutane or isopentane (one branch), and 2,2-dimethylpropane or neopentane (two branches on one carbon). So C₅H₁₂ has three structural isomers.', wrong = '{"0": "Two isomers is the number for butane, C₄H₁₀ (butane and 2-methylpropane).", "2": "Only three distinct carbon skeletons are possible for five carbons. Any fourth drawing repeats one of them.", "3": "Five isomers is the number for hexane, C₆H₁₄. Pentane has only three."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0502' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chain isomers have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of the carbon chain. Butane (straight chain) and 2-methylpropane (branched) are both C₄H₁₀, so they are chain isomers.', wrong = '{"0": "Position isomers have the same carbon chain with a group or bond in a different position. Here the chains themselves differ.", "2": "Neither butane nor 2-methylpropane has a functional group, so they cannot be functional group isomers.", "3": "Metamers differ in the alkyl groups on either side of a functional group such as –O– or C=O. Alkanes have no such group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0503' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Position isomers have the same carbon chain and the same functional group, but the group sits in a different position. But-1-ene and but-2-ene both have an unbranched four-carbon chain with one C=C bond, located between carbons 1 and 2 in one and carbons 2 and 3 in the other.', wrong = '{"1": "Both compounds have the same unbranched four-carbon chain, so this is not chain isomerism.", "2": "Both contain the same functional group, a C=C bond, so this is not functional group isomerism.", "3": "Metamerism involves different alkyl groups on either side of a functional group such as –O– or C=O, not alkenes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0504' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Functional group isomers share a molecular formula but contain different functional groups. Ethanol and methoxymethane are both C₂H₆O, but ethanol is an alcohol with an –OH group, while methoxymethane is an ether with a C–O–C link.', wrong = '{"0": "The carbon chains are not the key difference. The two compounds belong to different families.", "2": "Position isomers have the same functional group in different places. Here the functional groups are different.", "3": "Tautomers interconvert by a proton moving within the molecule. Ethanol and methoxymethane do not do this."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0505' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Metamers belong to the same family but have different alkyl groups on either side of the functional group. Both compounds are ketones, C₅H₁₀O. Pentan-2-one has CH₃ and C₃H₇ around the C=O, while pentan-3-one has C₂H₅ on both sides, so they are metamers.', wrong = '{"0": "Both have the same unbranched five-carbon chain, so they are not chain isomers.", "2": "Both contain the same ketone group, C=O, so they are not functional group isomers.", "3": "Geometrical isomers differ only in the arrangement in space around a C=C bond. These compounds differ in how the atoms are connected."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0506' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In organic chemistry, reduction means adding hydrogen to a molecule or removing oxygen from it. Adding H₂ across the C=C bond of ethene, with a nickel catalyst, gives ethane. Hydrogen is added, so this is an organic reduction.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanol gains oxygen and loses hydrogen to form ethanoic acid, so this is an oxidation.", "1": "Combustion adds oxygen to carbon and hydrogen, forming CO₂ and H₂O, so it is an oxidation.", "3": "Adding ozone to an alkene adds oxygen. Ozonolysis is an oxidative reaction, not a reduction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0507' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acidified potassium dichromate, K₂Cr₂O₇ with H₂SO₄, is an oxidizing agent. It oxidizes a primary alcohol first to an aldehyde and then to a carboxylic acid, for example ethanol to ethanoic acid. The orange dichromate turns green as it is reduced to Cr³⁺.', wrong = '{"0": "Alcoholic KOH removes HX from haloalkanes (dehydrohalogenation). It does not oxidize alcohols.", "1": "H₂ over Ni adds hydrogen, so it reduces compounds. It cannot oxidize an alcohol.", "3": "Br₂ in CCl₄ adds across C=C bonds of alkenes. It does not convert an alcohol to an acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0508' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Besides the very strong N≡N triple bond, N₂ is unreactive because its bond is non-polar. Both atoms are nitrogen with the same electronegativity, so the bonding electrons are shared equally. With no partial charges, there is no site for polar reagents to attack.', wrong = '{"0": "N₂ is a covalent molecule held by a triple bond. It has no ions.", "2": "Nitrogen has five valence electrons (2s² 2p³), three of which form the triple bond.", "3": "Nitrogen is a gas at room temperature. It boils at about −196 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0509' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the laboratory, nitrogen can be made by gently heating a solution of ammonium nitrite. It decomposes as NH₄NO₂(aq) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(l). On an industrial scale, nitrogen is obtained instead by fractional distillation of liquid air.', wrong = '{"0": "Heating potassium chlorate with MnO₂ as a catalyst produces oxygen, not nitrogen.", "1": "Electrolysis of water produces hydrogen and oxygen, not nitrogen.", "2": "Zinc reacting with dilute HCl produces hydrogen gas, not nitrogen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0510' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrogen is very unreactive, so it can be used as an inert blanket. Filling the space above the cargo with nitrogen keeps out oxygen and moisture, which reduces the risk of fire with hydrocarbons and stops edible oils from going rancid.', wrong = '{"0": "Nitrogen is very unreactive, not a strong oxidizing agent. An oxidizing gas would spoil the cargo.", "1": "Nitrogen (M = 28 g mol⁻¹) is much heavier than hydrogen (M = 2 g mol⁻¹).", "3": "Nitrogen is useful precisely because it does not react with the oil."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0511' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ammonia accepts a proton from water using the lone pair on nitrogen: NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻. This happens only to a small extent. Its basicity constant Kb is only 1.8 × 10⁻⁵, so the equilibrium lies far to the left and few OH⁻ ions are formed.', wrong = '{"0": "Ammonia does have a lone pair on nitrogen. That lone pair is what accepts a proton.", "1": "If ammonia reacted completely with water, it would be a strong base, not a weak one.", "2": "Ammonia accepts protons from water. It does not donate them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0512' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A Brønsted–Lowry acid donates a proton. In this reaction NH₄⁺ gives an H⁺ to OH⁻ from Ca(OH)₂, forming water and releasing NH₃ gas. So NH₄⁺ is the acid and OH⁻ is the base.', wrong = '{"0": "Cl⁻ does not take part in the proton transfer. It is a spectator ion that ends up in CaCl₂.", "1": "OH⁻ accepts the proton to form water, so it is the base, not the acid.", "3": "Ca²⁺ does not take part in the proton transfer. It is a spectator ion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0513' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The ammonia gas is dried by passing it through calcium oxide (quicklime). CaO is a basic drying agent, so it removes water vapour without reacting with ammonia, which is itself a base.', wrong = '{"0": "Absorbing the ammonia would defeat the purpose of the preparation, which is to collect it.", "1": "Concentrated H₂SO₄ is acidic and would react with ammonia to form ammonium sulphate.", "2": "Ammonia is extremely soluble in water, so passing it through water would dissolve most of it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0514' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Oxygen is −2 in these oxides. In N₂O, 2N + (−2) = 0, so N = +1. For comparison, nitrogen is +2 in NO, +4 in NO₂ and +5 in N₂O₅. Nitrogen shows a wide range of oxidation states in its oxides.', wrong = '{"1": "In NO₂, N + 2(−2) = 0, so N = +4.", "2": "In NO, N + (−2) = 0, so N = +2.", "3": "In N₂O₅, 2N + 5(−2) = 0, so N = +5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0515' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrous oxide, N₂O, is a colourless, sweet-smelling gas known as laughing gas. It is used as an anaesthetic in dentistry and as the propellant in cans of whipped cream. It is also used in making sodium azide, NaN₃.', wrong = '{"0": "N₂O is not used as a bleaching agent.", "1": "Rocket propellant use belongs to N₂O₄, the dimer of NO₂, not to N₂O.", "3": "N₂O is not used as a fertilizer. Nitrogen fertilizers supply ammonium or nitrate ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0516' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitric oxide, NO, has an odd number of electrons (15), so it has an unpaired electron and is paramagnetic. It is a colourless gas. In the body it acts as a biochemical messenger, for example relaxing blood vessels and so lowering blood pressure.', wrong = '{"1": "The colourless liquid used as a rocket propellant is N₂O₄, not NO.", "2": "Laughing gas is nitrous oxide, N₂O.", "3": "NO is colourless and paramagnetic. The reddish-brown gas is NO₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0517' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'NO₂ is a reddish-brown gas and N₂O₄ is colourless. The dimerization 2NO₂ → N₂O₄ is exothermic, so cooling favours N₂O₄ by Le Chatelier''s principle. As more colourless N₂O₄ forms, the mixture becomes paler.', wrong = '{"0": "Heating, not cooling, favours brown NO₂, because the breakdown of N₂O₄ is endothermic.", "1": "NO₂ and N₂O₄ have different colours, so any shift in the equilibrium changes the colour.", "2": "Neither NO₂ nor N₂O₄ is green. The colour changes only between brown and colourless."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0518' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrogen oxides come from both natural and man-made sources. In lightning, the very high temperature makes nitrogen and oxygen in the air combine: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO. Other natural sources include volcanic eruptions, forest fires and soil bacteria.', wrong = '{"0": "Motor vehicles are a man-made (anthropogenic) source, in fact the main one.", "1": "Power plants burning fossil fuels are a man-made source of nitrogen oxides.", "3": "Welding is a man-made source of nitrogen oxides, not a natural one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0519' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Photochemical smog forms when nitrogen oxides (NOx) and volatile organic compounds (VOCs) react in strong sunlight. It is oxidizing in nature and contains photochemical oxidants such as NO₂, ozone and peroxyacyl nitrates (PANs). It is typical of sunny cities with heavy traffic, like Los Angeles.', wrong = '{"1": "The key ingredients are NOx and VOCs, not sulphur dioxide. SO₂ is linked to classical (London) smog.", "2": "Ground-level ozone is one of the main oxidants in photochemical smog.", "3": "A reducing smog forming in cold, foggy weather describes classical smog. Photochemical smog is oxidizing and needs sunlight."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0520' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sunlight splits nitrogen dioxide into nitric oxide and a free oxygen atom: NO₂ + sunlight → NO + O. This very reactive oxygen atom then joins an O₂ molecule to form ozone: O + O₂ → O₃. This is how ground-level ozone builds up in smog.', wrong = '{"0": "CO reacting with O₂ to release an oxygen atom is not one of the steps that form ozone in smog.", "1": "N₂ and O₃ do not combine to form N₂O₃ in smog formation.", "2": "SO₂ + O₂ → SO₃ is the oxidation of sulphur dioxide, linked to acid rain, not ozone formation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0521' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PANs have the general formula RC(O)OONO₂. In peroxyacetyl nitrate the R group is CH₃, which comes from the acetyl group, CH₃CO–. Other hydrocarbon groups give other members of the family; for example R = C₆H₅ gives peroxybenzoyl nitrate (PBN).', wrong = '{"1": "R = C₆H₅ gives peroxybenzoyl nitrate (PBN), not peroxyacetyl nitrate.", "2": "R = H would give a formyl compound, not an acetyl compound. Acetyl needs a CH₃ group.", "3": "R in PANs is a hydrocarbon group, not an OH group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0522' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PAN forms in several steps. Ozone oxidizes a hydrocarbon to an aldehyde, which reacts with a hydroxyl radical to give an acyl radical. The acyl radical adds O₂ to form a peroxyacyl radical. In the final step, this peroxyacyl radical combines with NO₂ to give PAN.', wrong = '{"0": "Ozone oxidizing a hydrocarbon to an aldehyde is an early step, not the final one.", "1": "The acyl radical reacts with oxygen, not water, to form a peroxyacyl radical.", "3": "N₂ does not take part in forming PAN. The nitrogen comes from NO₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0523' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A three-way catalytic converter has a ceramic honeycomb coated with a layer of alumina, which gives a large surface area. The noble metals platinum, palladium and rhodium are spread thinly over this alumina, where they catalyse removal of CO, hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides.', wrong = '{"0": "Iron, copper and zinc are not the catalysts used in catalytic converters.", "1": "Vanadium (as V₂O₅) is the catalyst of the contact process, not of catalytic converters.", "3": "Nickel, cobalt and manganese are not the metals used in three-way converters."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0524' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a catalytic converter, nitrogen oxides are removed by reduction. Over a Pt/Rh catalyst NO reacts with CO: 2NO + 2CO → N₂ + 2CO₂. NO is reduced to harmless nitrogen and CO is oxidized to CO₂, so two pollutants are removed at once.', wrong = '{"0": "4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O is the oxidation of ammonia used in making nitric acid. It produces NO.", "1": "N₂ + O₂ → 2NO forms NO, as in lightning or hot engines. The converter removes NO.", "2": "2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂ forms NO₂, another pollutant, rather than removing nitrogen oxides."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0525' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrification is carried out by nitrifying bacteria that oxidize NH₄⁺ to NO₂⁻ and then NO₃⁻; they need oxygen, so it is aerobic. Denitrification is carried out by bacteria that reduce NO₃⁻ and NO₂⁻ back to N₂ gas in the absence of oxygen, so it is anaerobic.', wrong = '{"1": "Nitrification needs oxygen (aerobic). Only denitrification happens under anaerobic conditions.", "2": "Converting NO₃⁻ to N₂ is denitrification. Nitrification converts NH₄⁺ to nitrate.", "3": "Converting NH₄⁺ to NO₃⁻ is nitrification. Denitrification turns nitrate back into N₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0526' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Anammox stands for anaerobic ammonium oxidation. Bacteria oxidize ammonium ions using nitrite ions, without oxygen: NH₄⁺ + NO₂⁻ → N₂ + 2H₂O. It is used in wastewater treatment to remove nitrogen compounds as harmless N₂ gas.', wrong = '{"1": "Converting N₂ into NH₃ is nitrogen fixation, done by certain bacteria or by the Haber process.", "2": "Reducing NO₃⁻ to NH₄⁺ is not the Anammox reaction, which produces N₂ gas.", "3": "Oxidizing ammonia to nitrate is nitrification, an aerobic process."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0527' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sulphur is a larger atom than oxygen, so its p orbitals overlap poorly sideways and S=S double bonds are weak. Instead, each sulphur atom forms two single bonds to other sulphur atoms. This catenation builds rings and chains, such as the crown-shaped S₈ molecule.', wrong = '{"1": "Sulphur is a non-metal, not a metal.", "2": "Each sulphur atom in S₈ has two lone pairs, so sulphur does have lone pairs.", "3": "Sulphur atoms are larger than oxygen atoms. Their size causes poor π overlap; it does not stop bonding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0528' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The +6 state in SO₃ is the most stable thermodynamically, but SO₃ forms very slowly under ordinary conditions. Because of this kinetic barrier, burning sulphur in air gives mainly SO₂, with sulphur in the +4 state. A catalyst such as V₂O₅ in the contact process is needed to form SO₃ at a useful rate.', wrong = '{"0": "SO₃ (+6) is thermodynamically more stable than SO₂. The problem is the slow rate, not stability.", "1": "Burning sulphur in oxygen gives SO₂, where sulphur is already +4, so oxygen clearly oxidizes it beyond +2.", "3": "Sulphur does reach +6, as in SO₃, SO₄²⁻ and H₂SO₄."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0529' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sulphur burns in air with a pale blue flame, forming sulphur dioxide: S + O₂ → SO₂. Turning SO₂ into SO₃ is slow and needs a higher temperature and a catalyst, such as V₂O₅ in the contact process, so simple burning gives SO₂.', wrong = '{"0": "SO₃ does not form directly on burning, because that step needs a catalyst. Sulphur gives SO₂ with a blue flame, not yellow.", "1": "H₂S needs a source of hydrogen. Burning in air supplies only oxygen.", "2": "Sulphur does react with oxygen in air, burning readily once lit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0530' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With less electronegative elements such as metals, sulphur acts as an oxidizing agent and forms sulphides. Silver reacts with sulphur compounds in the air: 2Ag + S → Ag₂S. The thin black layer of silver sulphide is the tarnish seen on silverware.', wrong = '{"0": "Turning Ag into Ag⁺ is oxidation, not reduction. Sulphur is the oxidizing agent that causes it.", "1": "The coating is silver sulphide, Ag₂S. Silver sulphate would need oxygen as well.", "3": "Only a thin surface coating forms. The silver does not dissolve."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0531' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When sulphur is heated with potassium cyanide, it adds to the cyanide ion: KCN + S → KSCN. The product is potassium thiocyanate. The SCN⁻ ion behaves like a halide ion in many reactions, so it is called a pseudohalide.', wrong = '{"0": "Potassium sulphide (K₂S) would lose the cyanide group. In fact the CN group is kept and sulphur joins it.", "1": "Potassium sulphate needs oxygen, which is not present in this reaction.", "2": "No hydrogen is present, so HCN cannot form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0532' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sulphur reacts directly with fluorine to form sulphur hexafluoride, SF₆. In SF₆ the sulphur atom is fully surrounded by six fluorine atoms, so the gas is very unreactive and stable. It does not conduct electricity, so it is used as an insulating gas in high-voltage electrical equipment.', wrong = '{"0": "SF₆ is useful because it is very unreactive. A reactive gas would attack the equipment.", "1": "SF₆ is colourless, and colour has nothing to do with insulation.", "3": "An insulating gas must not conduct electricity. SF₆ is valued as a non-conductor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0533' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Natural rubber is soft and weak because its long chains slide past each other. In vulcanization, rubber is heated with sulphur, which forms disulphide (S–S) bridges between neighbouring chains. These cross-links stop the chains sliding, making the rubber harder, stronger and more elastic.', wrong = '{"0": "Sulphur adds bridges between chains. It does not remove carbon from the rubber.", "1": "Sulphur does not dissolve rubber. It links the chains together.", "2": "Cross-linking makes rubber stronger and less sticky, not softer and stickier."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0534' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In gunpowder, charcoal (carbon) is the main fuel and sulphur is an extra fuel that makes it burn faster. Potassium nitrate supplies the oxygen needed for the fuels to burn very quickly without air, so it is the oxidizer.', wrong = '{"1": "KNO₃ is used up in the reaction, so it is not a catalyst.", "2": "Charcoal, at 15%, is the main fuel. KNO₃ provides the oxygen for it to burn.", "3": "KNO₃ reacts as the source of oxygen. It is not an inert filler."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0535' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Natural gas (mainly methane) has no smell, so a leak could go unnoticed and cause an explosion. Mercaptans (thiols) are sulphur compounds with a strong, unpleasant smell. Tiny amounts are added to the gas so that any leak can be detected by its odour.', wrong = '{"0": "Mercaptans are added in tiny amounts for their smell. They do not noticeably change the heat of combustion.", "1": "Mercaptans are not drying agents and do not remove water from the gas.", "3": "Methane already burns with a blue flame. Mercaptans are added for smell, not flame colour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0536' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When SO₂ is made by roasting pyrite, it contains impurities, including arsenic oxide, As₂O₃. Arsenic poisons the V₂O₅ catalyst and reduces its efficiency. The gases are passed through an arsenic purifier containing gelatinous Fe(OH)₃, which absorbs the arsenic oxide.', wrong = '{"0": "Drying is done separately with concentrated H₂SO₄. Fe(OH)₃ is used to remove arsenic.", "1": "SO₃ is absorbed in concentrated sulphuric acid to form oleum, not in Fe(OH)₃.", "2": "SO₂ is converted to SO₃ over the V₂O₅ catalyst in the converter, not by Fe(OH)₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0537' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'V₂O₅ works through a redox cycle. First it oxidizes SO₂ to SO₃ and is itself reduced, V⁵⁺ becoming V⁴⁺: SO₂ + V₂O₅ → SO₃ + V₂O₄. Then oxygen reoxidizes it: V₂O₄ + ½O₂ → V₂O₅. The catalyst is regenerated and can be used again.', wrong = '{"0": "A catalyst is regenerated at the end. V₂O₅ is reduced only to V₂O₄ and then reoxidized, never to vanadium metal.", "2": "SO₃ passes out of the converter. It is not trapped by the catalyst as vanadium sulphate.", "3": "The catalysis is a cycle of vanadium oxides changing oxidation state. No hydrogen ions are involved."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0538' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After purification, SO₂ and air are preheated to 420 to 450 °C and fed into the first converter stage at a pressure of 1 to 2 atm. Here they pass over the V₂O₅ catalyst. This temperature gives a good compromise between rate and yield for the exothermic reaction.', wrong = '{"1": "At 25 °C the reaction is far too slow, and a pressure of 200 atm is not used in the contact process.", "2": "At 1000 °C the equilibrium yield of SO₃ would be very low, because the reaction is exothermic.", "3": "200 °C and 300 atm are not used. Such high pressure is unnecessary because the yield is already high at 1 to 2 atm."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0539' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SO₃ is not dissolved directly in water, because it forms a fine mist of acid. Instead it is absorbed in concentrated sulphuric acid: H₂SO₄ + SO₃ → H₂S₂O₇. This product, oleum or fuming sulphuric acid, is then diluted with water to give concentrated H₂SO₄.', wrong = '{"1": "H₂SO₃ is sulphurous acid, formed when SO₂ dissolves in water.", "2": "H₂S₂O₃ is thiosulphuric acid, the parent acid of thiosulphates.", "3": "SO₃·H₂O is just another way of writing H₂SO₄, not oleum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0540' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In H₂SO₄ the sulphur atom is surrounded by four oxygen atoms arranged tetrahedrally. Two of them are bonded as S–OH single bonds and two as S=O double bonds. So the molecule is tetrahedral around sulphur.', wrong = '{"0": "Trigonal planar with three S=O bonds describes SO₃, not H₂SO₄.", "2": "Four groups around sulphur cannot lie in a straight line. A linear shape needs only two.", "3": "Sulphur here has four bonded groups and no lone pairs, which gives a tetrahedral, not square planar, shape."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0541' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In self-ionization (autoprotolysis), one H₂SO₄ molecule gives a proton to another: 2H₂SO₄ ⇌ H₃SO₄⁺ + HSO₄⁻. Its constant, 2.7 × 10⁻⁴, is much larger than that of water. So sulphuric acid contains many ions of its own and can be used as a non-aqueous protic solvent.', wrong = '{"0": "Its strong ionizing power makes it a useful solvent, able to dissolve many substances.", "2": "H₂SO₄ has pKa₁ = −2, so it is a strong acid, not a weak one.", "3": "Sulphuric acid is a strong acid, not a base."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0542' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The lower the pKa, the stronger the acid. A negative pKa₁ of −2 means the first proton is lost almost completely, so H₂SO₄ is a strong acid. The positive pKa₂ of 1.92 shows that HSO₄⁻ loses its proton much less readily, so it is a far weaker acid.', wrong = '{"0": "A higher pKa means a weaker acid. HSO₄⁻ (pKa 1.92) is weaker than H₂SO₄ (pKa −2), not stronger.", "2": "The two pKa values differ by about 4 units, so the second proton is lost far less easily than the first.", "3": "A pKa of −2 shows that H₂SO₄ is a strong acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0543' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Concentrated sulphuric acid has a strong attraction for water. It removes the hydrogen and oxygen atoms from sugar in the ratio of water: C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ → 12C + 11H₂O. Black carbon is left behind and swells up with steam. This shows the acid acting as a dehydrating agent.', wrong = '{"0": "The acid removes the elements of water from sugar. It is not giving electrons, so it is not acting as a reducing agent.", "2": "The acid takes up the water itself, so it does more than just speed up the reaction.", "3": "Sulphuric acid is an acid, not a base. Its role here is dehydration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0544' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Concentrated sulphuric acid is a non-volatile acid. It displaces the more volatile acid HCl from its salt: NaCl(s) + H₂SO₄(l) → NaHSO₄(s) + HCl(g). This reaction is used to make hydrogen chloride gas in the laboratory.', wrong = '{"0": "Chlorine gas is not formed, because H₂SO₄ cannot oxidize chloride ions to Cl₂ under these conditions.", "2": "SO₂ forms when the acid acts as an oxidizing agent and is reduced, as with copper. With NaCl it acts only as an acid.", "3": "Sodium stays as Na⁺ in NaHSO₄. No sodium metal is produced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0545' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Copper lies below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so it cannot release H₂ from dilute acids. Hot concentrated H₂SO₄, however, is an oxidizing agent. It oxidizes copper to CuSO₄ and is itself reduced to SO₂: Cu + 2H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + SO₂ + 2H₂O.', wrong = '{"0": "Copper is below hydrogen in the series, so it does not release hydrogen gas from acids.", "2": "The acid is reduced to SO₂, not to H₂S, and the copper product is CuSO₄, not CuO.", "3": "Cu₂S and O₂ are not formed. Oxygen gas is never released in this reaction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0546' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'About three-quarters of all sulphuric acid produced goes into making fertilizers. Most of it is used to digest phosphate rock into soluble superphosphate, and some is used to make ammonium sulphate. Other uses, such as batteries, are much smaller.', wrong = '{"0": "Lead storage batteries use sulphuric acid, but this is a small share of total use.", "2": "Drying gases is a minor laboratory and industrial use.", "3": "The food industry uses only a small amount of sulphuric acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0547' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The word halogen comes from two Greek words: halos, meaning salt, and gen, meaning to produce or make. So halogens are the salt makers. They react directly with metals to form salts such as NaCl.', wrong = '{"1": "The halogens are coloured, but their name refers to salts, not colour.", "2": "Halogens do form acids such as HCl, but that is not the meaning of the name.", "3": "Light bearer is the meaning of phosphorus, not halogen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0548' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chlorine was the first of the halogens to be isolated and recognized as an element. It was first prepared by Scheele in 1774 and shown to be an element by Davy in 1810. The others were isolated later, and fluorine, the most reactive, was the last.', wrong = '{"1": "Bromine was isolated in 1826, after chlorine.", "2": "Fluorine is the most reactive halogen and was very hard to isolate. It was the last of these four, obtained in 1886.", "3": "Iodine was discovered in 1811, after chlorine had been recognized as an element."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0549' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bromine has proton number 35 and the shell structure 2, 8, 18, 7. It is in period 4, so its seven outer electrons are in the fourth shell, arranged as 4s² 4p⁵. Like all halogens, it has ns² np⁵ in its outer shell.', wrong = '{"0": "3s² 3p⁵ is the outer configuration of chlorine, in period 3.", "1": "4s² 4p⁶ is a full outer shell, the configuration of the noble gas krypton.", "3": "5s² 5p⁵ is the outer configuration of iodine, in period 5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0550' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adding up the shells, 2 + 8 + 18 + 18 + 7 = 53 electrons. A neutral atom with 53 electrons has proton number 53, which is iodine. The 7 electrons in the fifth shell show it is a halogen in period 5.', wrong = '{"0": "Chlorine has 17 electrons, arranged 2, 8, 7.", "2": "Bromine has 35 electrons, arranged 2, 8, 18, 7.", "3": "Fluorine has 9 electrons, arranged 2, 7."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0551' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A substance is a liquid at 20 °C if its melting point is below 20 °C and its boiling point is above it. Bromine melts at −7 °C and boils at 59 °C, so it is a liquid at 20 °C. It is the only non-metal that is liquid at room temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "Fluorine boils at −188 °C, so it is a gas at 20 °C.", "1": "Chlorine boils at −35 °C, so it is a gas at 20 °C.", "3": "Iodine melts at 113 °C, so it is a solid at 20 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0552' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The boiling points are F₂ −188 °C, Cl₂ −35 °C, Br₂ 59 °C and I₂ 183 °C. Down the group the molecules get bigger and have more electrons, so London dispersion forces become stronger and boiling points rise. The correct order is I₂ > Br₂ > Cl₂ > F₂.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse order. Fluorine has the lowest boiling point, not the highest.", "1": "Iodine (183 °C) boils at a higher temperature than bromine (59 °C), so it must come first.", "2": "Fluorine (−188 °C) has the lowest boiling point, so it cannot come before bromine or chlorine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0553' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Halogen molecules are non-polar, so only London dispersion forces (instantaneous dipole–induced dipole forces) hold them together. Down the group the molecules have more electrons and larger electron clouds, which are more easily polarized. The forces become stronger, so more energy is needed to vaporise iodine than fluorine.', wrong = '{"0": "X₂ molecules are made of two identical atoms, so they are non-polar all the way down the group.", "1": "Halogen molecules contain no hydrogen, so they cannot hydrogen bond with each other.", "3": "Vaporisation separates molecules without breaking the X–X bond. That bond actually gets weaker from chlorine to iodine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0554' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A small molecule with low polarizability has weak London dispersion forces between its molecules. Little energy is needed to separate them, so the boiling point is low. A low boiling point means the substance evaporates easily, so it is highly volatile.', wrong = '{"0": "Weak London forces give a low boiling point, not a high one. A high boiling point would also mean low volatility.", "1": "Strong forces, a high boiling point and low volatility describe a large, easily polarized molecule such as I₂.", "3": "Strong London forces would raise the boiling point, not lower it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0555' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Volatility is how easily a substance turns into vapour. A substance with weak intermolecular forces evaporates easily and also boils at a low temperature. So the more volatile a substance is, the lower its boiling point: volatility is inversely related to boiling point.', wrong = '{"1": "Volatility and boiling point both depend on the strength of intermolecular forces, so they are closely linked.", "2": "This is the reverse relationship. A higher boiling point means the substance is less volatile.", "3": "The link holds for all kinds of substances, including the non-polar halogen molecules."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0556' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At 20 °C iodine dissolves only about 0.018 g per 100 g of water. This is far less than chlorine (0.59 g) and bromine (3.6 g). Iodine is a large non-polar molecule, so it mixes poorly with polar water.', wrong = '{"0": "Fluorine reacts vigorously with water, oxidizing it, instead of simply dissolving. It does not have the lowest solubility.", "1": "Chlorine dissolves about 0.59 g per 100 g of water, much more than iodine.", "2": "Bromine dissolves about 3.6 g per 100 g of water, the highest of the values given."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0557' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fluorine atoms are very small, so in F₂ the two atoms are very close together. The lone pairs on each atom are then close to each other and repel strongly. This repulsion weakens the F–F bond, making it weaker (156 kJ mol⁻¹) than Cl–Cl (243 kJ mol⁻¹).', wrong = '{"0": "Fluorine is the most electronegative element, more electronegative than chlorine, not less.", "1": "Fluorine and chlorine atoms both have seven valence electrons.", "2": "Fluorine atoms are smaller, so the F–F bond is shorter than the Cl–Cl bond, not longer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0558' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The X–X bond energies are Cl₂ 243, Br₂ 193, F₂ 156 and I₂ 151 kJ mol⁻¹. Bond energy normally falls down the group as atoms get larger, but fluorine is an exception because of lone-pair repulsion in its small atoms. So the order is Cl₂ > Br₂ > F₂ > I₂.', wrong = '{"0": "F₂ (156 kJ mol⁻¹) is weaker than Br₂ (193 kJ mol⁻¹), so F₂ cannot come before Br₂.", "1": "F₂ (156 kJ mol⁻¹) is weaker than both Cl₂ and Br₂, so it cannot be first.", "3": "This reverses the order. I₂ (151 kJ mol⁻¹) has the weakest bond of the four."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0559' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When sodium burns in chlorine, each Na atom gives one electron to a chlorine atom: 2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl. Chlorine gains electrons, going from 0 to −1, so it is reduced. A substance that is reduced while taking electrons from another is an oxidizing agent.', wrong = '{"1": "NaCl is an ionic compound made of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions. Electrons are transferred, not shared.", "2": "Sodium loses electrons and is oxidized. Chlorine gains them and is reduced.", "3": "Chlorine''s oxidation number changes from 0 in Cl₂ to −1 in NaCl, so it is reduced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0560' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Oxidizing power falls down the group: Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂. A halogen can oxidize and displace only the halide ions of halogens below it. Iodine is the weakest oxidizing agent here, so it cannot take electrons from Cl⁻, and I₂ + 2Cl⁻ does not react.', wrong = '{"0": "Chlorine is a stronger oxidizing agent than bromine, so it does oxidize Br⁻ to Br₂.", "1": "Bromine is a stronger oxidizing agent than iodine, so it does oxidize I⁻ to I₂.", "3": "Chlorine is a much stronger oxidizing agent than iodine, so it does oxidize I⁻ to I₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0561' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The reaction is Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂. Chlorine gains electrons, going from 0 to −1, so it is reduced. While doing so it oxidizes bromide ions to bromine. The species that is reduced is the oxidizing agent, so Cl₂ is the oxidizing agent.', wrong = '{"0": "K⁺ is a spectator ion. It does not gain or lose electrons in this reaction.", "1": "Br⁻ loses electrons and is oxidized to Br₂, so it is the reducing agent.", "3": "Br₂ is the product formed when Br⁻ is oxidized. It is not the oxidizing agent here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0562' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A standard reduction potential measures how readily a species gains electrons: the more positive E°, the stronger the oxidizing agent. E° falls from +2.87 V for F₂ to +0.54 V for I₂, so the halogens'' tendency to gain electrons, their oxidizing power, decreases from fluorine to iodine.', wrong = '{"1": "This is the reverse of the trend. Falling E° values mean oxidizing power decreases down the group.", "2": "Iodine has the least positive E° (+0.54 V), so it is the weakest oxidizing agent, not the strongest.", "3": "The values differ by more than 2 V, so the halogens clearly differ in oxidizing power."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0563' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The standard reduction potentials of the halogens are F₂ +2.87 V, Cl₂ +1.36 V, Br₂ +1.07 V and I₂ +0.54 V. The value +1.07 V belongs to bromine, which lies between chlorine and iodine in both position and oxidizing power.', wrong = '{"1": "Chlorine''s standard reduction potential is +1.36 V, higher than bromine''s.", "2": "Fluorine''s standard reduction potential is +2.87 V, the highest of all.", "3": "Iodine''s standard reduction potential is +0.54 V, the lowest of the four."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0564' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A halogen acts as an oxidizing agent by turning into X⁻(aq). This is easier when the X₂ molecule splits easily (low dissociation energy), when each atom readily accepts an electron (high electron affinity), and when the X⁻ ion is strongly hydrated (high hydration energy). All three together give high oxidizing power.', wrong = '{"0": "A high dissociation energy, low electron affinity and low hydration energy all make forming X⁻(aq) harder, so oxidizing power would be low.", "1": "The low dissociation energy helps, but low electron affinity and low hydration energy both reduce oxidizing power.", "2": "The high electron affinity helps, but high dissociation energy and low hydration energy both work against it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0565' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chlorine and hydrogen react readily to form hydrogen chloride when exposed to UV light (or sunlight) or a spark: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The light breaks Cl₂ into free radicals, which start a fast chain reaction that can be explosive in bright light.', wrong = '{"0": "Heating with a catalyst in a slow, reversible reaction describes iodine, the least reactive halogen.", "1": "Reacting even in the dark at very low temperature describes fluorine, which reacts explosively.", "3": "Chlorine does react readily with hydrogen once UV light or a spark is supplied."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0566' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Reactivity with hydrogen falls down the group. Iodine is the least reactive: H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI needs a high temperature and a catalyst, goes slowly, and is reversible because HI easily breaks down again. The weak H–I bond is the reason the reverse reaction occurs.', wrong = '{"0": "Fluorine reacts explosively with hydrogen even in the cold and dark.", "1": "Chlorine reacts readily with hydrogen when exposed to UV light or a spark.", "2": "Bromine reacts with hydrogen on heating, but it is iodine whose reaction is slow, reversible and needs a catalyst."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0567' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The hydrogen halides, HF, HCl, HBr and HI, are covalent molecules that are colourless gases at room temperature. They are very soluble in water, where they ionize to form acidic solutions called hydrohalic acids, such as hydrochloric acid from HCl.', wrong = '{"1": "Hydrogen halides are covalent molecules and are gases at room temperature, not ionic solids.", "2": "Hydrogen halides are colourless and very soluble in water.", "3": "Hydrogen halides are gases at room temperature and dissolve readily in water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0568' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Thermal stability depends on how strong the H–X bond is. The bond dissociation energies fall from H–F (569) to H–Cl (431), H–Br (366) and H–I (299 kJ mol⁻¹). The stronger the bond, the harder it is to break on heating, so stability falls as HF > HCl > HBr > HI.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse order. HI has the weakest bond, so it is the least stable, not the most.", "1": "HF has the strongest H–X bond, so it is the most stable, not the least.", "2": "HF has a stronger bond (569 kJ mol⁻¹) than HCl (431 kJ mol⁻¹), so it is more stable than HCl."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0569' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The iodine atom is very large, so its outer orbital overlaps poorly with hydrogen''s small 1s orbital. This makes the H–I bond the longest and weakest of the hydrogen halides (299 kJ mol⁻¹). It breaks most easily on heating, so HI is the least thermally stable.', wrong = '{"0": "Heating breaks the covalent H–I bond. HI does not form significant hydrogen bonds.", "1": "Iodine is the least electronegative of these halogens, not the most.", "3": "Because the iodine atom is so large, the H–I bond is the longest of the hydrogen halides, not the shortest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0570' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The H–F bond dissociation energy is 569 kJ mol⁻¹ and the H–I value is 299 kJ mol⁻¹. The fall is 569 − 299 = 270 kJ mol⁻¹. This large drop shows how much weaker the H–X bond becomes as the halogen atom gets larger.', wrong = '{"1": "203 kJ mol⁻¹ is the fall from H–F to H–Br (569 − 366), not to H–I.", "2": "138 kJ mol⁻¹ is the fall from H–F to H–Cl (569 − 431), not to H–I.", "3": "868 kJ mol⁻¹ is the sum of the two values (569 + 299), not their difference."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0571' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'ΔH = energy to break bonds − energy released forming bonds. Breaking one H–H and one Cl–Cl bond needs 436 + 243 = 679 kJ. Forming two H–Cl bonds releases 2 × 431 = 862 kJ. So ΔH = 679 − 862 = −183 kJ mol⁻¹, an exothermic reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "+183 has the sign reversed. More energy is released forming two H–Cl bonds than is used breaking H–H and Cl–Cl.", "1": "−103 kJ mol⁻¹ is the estimate for H₂ + Br₂ → 2HBr: (436 + 193) − 2(366).", "3": "+248 kJ mol⁻¹ counts only one H–Cl bond formed (679 − 431). The equation forms two H–Cl bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0572' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A reducing agent works by giving away electrons. Fluorine is the most electronegative element, and F⁻ is the smallest halide ion, so its charge is packed into a small volume (high charge density). Its extra electron is held very tightly and is hard to remove, so F⁻ is the weakest reducing agent.', wrong = '{"1": "Fluorine is the most electronegative halogen, not the least.", "2": "F⁻ is the smallest halide ion, with the highest charge density, not the largest.", "3": "F⁻ has the fewest electrons of the halide ions, only 10."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0573' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Iodine has the lowest electronegativity of the common halogens, and I⁻ is a large ion with a low charge density. Its outer electrons are far from the nucleus and well shielded, so one is easily given away. This makes I⁻ a strong reducing agent, easily oxidized to I₂.', wrong = '{"0": "Small size and high charge density describe F⁻, the weakest reducing agent.", "1": "I⁻ readily loses an electron to form iodine. That is exactly why it is a good reducing agent.", "2": "Iodine is much less electronegative than chlorine, not more."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0574' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Aqueous silver nitrate is used to test for halide ions. Chloride, bromide and iodide give insoluble silver halide precipitates, but silver fluoride is soluble in water. So adding AgNO₃ to sodium fluoride solution gives no precipitate.', wrong = '{"0": "A white precipitate is silver chloride, given by chloride ions, not fluoride.", "1": "A cream precipitate is silver bromide, given by bromide ions.", "2": "A yellow precipitate is silver iodide, given by iodide ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0575' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Silver iodide is a pale yellow precipitate and is the least soluble silver halide. It does not dissolve in dilute or concentrated aqueous ammonia. So a pale yellow precipitate that resists both shows that the solution contains iodide ions.', wrong = '{"0": "Fluoride gives no precipitate with silver nitrate, because silver fluoride is soluble.", "1": "Silver chloride is white, not pale yellow, and it dissolves in dilute ammonia.", "2": "Silver bromide is cream and dissolves in concentrated ammonia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0576' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ammonia molecules bond to Ag⁺ through their lone pairs, forming a soluble complex ion: AgCl(s) + 2NH₃(aq) → [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq). This removes Ag⁺ from solution, so the white precipitate dissolves to give a colourless solution of the diamminesilver(I) ion.', wrong = '{"0": "No new solid forms. The precipitate dissolves to give a soluble complex ion.", "1": "No silver oxide forms. The white precipitate dissolves into a colourless solution.", "3": "Silver is not reduced to the metal. It stays in the +1 state in the complex ion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0577' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both KCl and KBr give precipitates with silver nitrate: white AgCl and cream AgBr. Adding dilute aqueous ammonia then dissolves AgCl but not AgBr. So only the chloride''s precipitate disappears, which clearly tells the two apart.', wrong = '{"1": "Both chloride and bromide give a precipitate with silver nitrate, so this alone would not separate them.", "2": "Concentrated ammonia dissolves both AgCl and AgBr, so both precipitates would disappear.", "3": "Ammonia alone gives no precipitate with either potassium halide, so it shows no difference."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0578' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Concentrated sulphuric acid first acts as an acid: NaF + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HF. HF is a very weak reducing agent, because F⁻ holds its electrons so tightly. It cannot reduce H₂SO₄, so no further reaction occurs and only steamy fumes of HF are seen.', wrong = '{"1": "Sodium fluoride does react, giving NaHSO₄ and fumes of HF.", "2": "HF is not acting as an oxidizing agent here. The point is that it is too weak a reducing agent to reduce the acid.", "3": "SO₂ forms only when the hydrogen halide is a stronger reducing agent, as with HBr. HF cannot reduce H₂SO₄."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0579' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First the acid gives steamy fumes of HBr: NaBr + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HBr. HBr is a strong enough reducing agent to reduce some H₂SO₄ to SO₂, and is itself oxidized to brown Br₂: 2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O.', wrong = '{"0": "HBr alone would form only if HBr were too weak a reducing agent, as with HF and HCl. HBr goes on to reduce the acid.", "1": "H₂S is the reduction product formed with iodide, which is a stronger reducing agent than bromide.", "3": "No oxygen gas is produced. Sulphuric acid is reduced to SO₂, and HBr forms along with Br₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0580' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bromine''s oxidation number rises from −1 in HBr to 0 in Br₂, so HBr is oxidized. At the same time sulphur falls from +6 in H₂SO₄ to +4 in SO₂, so the acid is reduced. A substance that is oxidized while reducing another is a reducing agent.', wrong = '{"0": "H₂SO₄ is the oxidizing agent here. HBr is the one being oxidized.", "2": "HBr is used up in the reaction, so it is not a catalyst.", "3": "HBr does not remove water from anything. It is oxidized to bromine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0581' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'HI is a strong reducing agent. It reduces sulphur in H₂SO₄, which is +6, all the way to H₂S, where sulphur is −2: 8HI + H₂SO₄ → 4I₂ + H₂S + 4H₂O. So the change in sulphur''s oxidation state is from +6 to −2.', wrong = '{"0": "+6 to +4 is the change when H₂SO₄ is reduced to SO₂, as happens with bromide.", "2": "Sulphur in H₂SO₄ is +6, not +4, so the starting value is wrong.", "3": "Sulphur in H₂S is −2, not 0. An oxidation state of 0 would mean elemental sulphur."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0582' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Iodide is the strongest reducing agent among the halides. Concentrated sulphuric acid oxidizes it to iodine, which forms purple fumes (or a dark solid) on warming. HI fumes and the rotten-egg smell of H₂S are also produced.', wrong = '{"0": "Sodium fluoride gives only steamy fumes of HF, with no coloured fumes.", "1": "Sodium chloride gives only steamy fumes of HCl, with no coloured fumes.", "2": "Sodium bromide gives brown fumes of bromine, along with HBr and SO₂, not purple fumes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0583' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Disproportionation is a reaction in which a single element is both oxidized and reduced at the same time. Part of the element''s atoms go to a higher oxidation state and part go to a lower one, as when Cl₂ (0) forms Cl⁻ (−1) and ClO⁻ (+1).', wrong = '{"0": "Disproportionation involves one element, part of which is oxidized and part reduced. It is not two elements both being oxidized.", "2": "A compound breaking down on heating is thermal decomposition, which need not involve any redox change.", "3": "An acid and a base forming a salt and water is neutralization, not disproportionation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0584' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With cold, dilute sodium hydroxide, chlorine disproportionates: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O. Chlorine is reduced to −1 in NaCl and oxidized to +1 in NaClO, sodium chlorate(I), the active ingredient of household bleach.', wrong = '{"0": "NaClO₃, sodium chlorate(V), forms when chlorine reacts with hot sodium hydroxide, not cold.", "2": "NaClO₄, sodium chlorate(VII), is not formed by chlorine with either cold or hot NaOH.", "3": "NaOCl₂ is not a real compound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0585' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With hot, concentrated NaOH: 3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O. In NaClO₃, Na is +1 and each O is −2, so Cl + 1 − 6 = 0 and Cl = +5. Chlorine is oxidized from 0 to +5, while the rest is reduced to −1 in NaCl.', wrong = '{"0": "NaClO, with chlorine at +1, is the oxidized product with cold alkali, not hot.", "2": "NaClO₄, with chlorine at +7, is not formed in this reaction.", "3": "Going from 0 to −1 in NaCl is reduction, not oxidation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0586' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Under the modern naming system, the oxidation state of chlorine is shown in Roman numerals. NaClO has chlorine in the +1 state, so it is sodium chlorate(I). Its older name is sodium hypochlorite, which is still common on bleach labels.', wrong = '{"0": "Sodium chlorate was the older name for NaClO₃, now called sodium chlorate(V).", "2": "Sodium perchlorate is NaClO₄, now called sodium chlorate(VII).", "3": "Sodium chlorite is NaClO₂, now called sodium chlorate(III)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0587' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Cl₂ chlorine is 0. In HCl, H is +1, so Cl is −1 (reduced). In HClO, H is +1 and O is −2, so Cl is +1 (oxidized). Since the same element is both oxidized and reduced in one reaction, chlorine disproportionates.', wrong = '{"0": "The values are swapped. Chlorine is −1 in HCl and +1 in HClO.", "2": "Chlorine in HClO is +1, not −1, because H is +1 and O is −2.", "3": "Chlorine in HCl is −1, not 0, so chlorine is also reduced, not only oxidized."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0588' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When chlorine is added to water it reacts to form HCl and HOCl (chloric(I) acid). HOCl is a weak acid and partly ionizes to H⁺ and OCl⁻. These two species, HOCl and OCl⁻, are the active disinfectants that kill microorganisms.', wrong = '{"1": "Cl₂ is quickly hydrolysed in water, and chloride ions have no disinfecting action.", "2": "HCl is formed, but it fully ionizes to H⁺ and Cl⁻, which do not disinfect.", "3": "Chlorate(V) and chlorate(VII) ions are not formed when water is chlorinated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0589' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'HOCl is a neutral molecule, while OCl⁻ carries a negative charge. The cell walls of microorganisms let neutral molecules through much more easily. HOCl therefore enters the cells readily and oxidizes proteins, lipids and enzymes, making it the more effective disinfectant.', wrong = '{"0": "HOCl is a weak acid, while HCl is a strong acid. Acid strength is not the reason anyway.", "2": "OCl⁻ can also oxidize cell components, but its negative charge makes it harder for it to get into cells.", "3": "HOCl and OCl⁻ each contain one chlorine atom."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0590' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'HOCl ⇌ H⁺ + OCl⁻ depends on pH. Between pH 6 and 7.5, HOCl is the main species. Above pH 7.5, there are few H⁺ ions, so the equilibrium shifts right and OCl⁻ predominates. OCl⁻ still disinfects, but less effectively than HOCl.', wrong = '{"0": "HOCl predominates at pH 6 to 7.5. Above 7.5 the balance shifts towards OCl⁻.", "2": "OCl⁻ is still present above pH 7.5 and still acts as a disinfectant.", "3": "Chlorine reacts with water to form HOCl and OCl⁻, so Cl₂ is not the main species."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0591' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Swimming pools are used by many people, so the water is likely to contain a higher concentration of bacteria than drinking water. A slightly higher chlorine concentration is used to make sure these microorganisms are killed.', wrong = '{"0": "Chlorine is equally poisonous wherever it is used. The higher dose is for the extra bacteria.", "2": "pH does affect disinfection, but the reason for the higher dose is the larger number of bacteria.", "3": "Chlorine does not warm the water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0592' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Three main factors affect how well chlorine disinfects water: the pH, which decides whether HOCl or OCl⁻ predominates; the chlorine dose; and the contact time. The colour of the water is not one of these factors.', wrong = '{"0": "pH is a factor. It decides whether the more effective HOCl or the less effective OCl⁻ predominates.", "1": "Chlorine dose is a factor. A larger dose gives more HOCl and OCl⁻ to kill microorganisms.", "2": "Contact time is a factor. The disinfectants need enough time to kill bacteria, viruses and protozoa."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0593' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dry air is mostly nitrogen, about 78%, and oxygen, about 21%. Together these two gases make up roughly 99% of the atmosphere, so they are its major components. Everything else, such as argon and carbon dioxide, is present in much smaller amounts.', wrong = '{"1": "Oxygen is a major component, but argon (about 0.93%) is only a minor one.", "2": "Nitrogen is a major component, but carbon dioxide (about 0.04%) is only a minor one.", "3": "Argon (0.93%) and carbon dioxide (0.04%) are both minor components, not major ones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0594' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The gases of the atmosphere are grouped by amount. Nitrogen and oxygen are the major components, argon (0.93%) and carbon dioxide (0.04%) are the minor components, and gases such as methane, neon, helium, krypton and xenon are trace components. So argon is a minor component.', wrong = '{"0": "Only nitrogen (about 78%) and oxygen (about 21%) are major components. Argon is far less abundant.", "1": "Argon is a natural, unreactive noble gas in air. It is not a pollutant, primary or secondary.", "2": "Trace components are present in much smaller amounts, like methane, neon, helium, krypton and xenon. Argon''s 0.93% makes it minor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0595' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The atmosphere is divided into four layers: troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere and thermosphere. The boundaries are set by how the temperature changes as altitude increases. In some layers temperature falls with height and in others it rises.', wrong = '{"0": "Atmospheric layers are not defined by colour.", "1": "The Moon plays no part in defining the layers of Earth''s atmosphere.", "2": "Nitrogen is a major gas throughout the lower atmosphere, so it cannot mark the boundaries between layers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0596' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The troposphere is the lowest and densest layer, reaching up to about 12 km. It contains most of the air''s mass and nearly all its water vapour, so clouds form here. Weather events such as rain, lightning and hurricanes all happen in the troposphere.', wrong = '{"0": "The thermosphere (about 80 to 600 km) is very thin and is heated by UV and X-rays. Weather does not form there.", "1": "The stratosphere (about 12 to 50 km) contains the ozone layer and is largely free of weather.", "2": "The mesosphere (about 50 to 80 km) is the coldest layer, but it does not hold weather systems."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0597' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the stratosphere, about 12 to 50 km up, temperature rises with height from about −58 °C to −2 °C. This warming comes from ozone: its formation from O₂ and O atoms releases heat, and ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation from the Sun.', wrong = '{"0": "In the troposphere temperature falls with height, from about 17 °C to −58 °C.", "1": "The thermosphere also warms with height, but to hundreds or thousands of degrees, not from −58 °C to −2 °C.", "2": "In the mesosphere temperature falls with height, from about −2 °C to −93 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0598' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the mesosphere, about 50 to 80 km up, temperature falls with height to around −93 °C. There is very little ozone to absorb UV and the air is too thin to hold much heat. This makes the top of the mesosphere the coldest part of the atmosphere.', wrong = '{"0": "The troposphere cools only to about −58 °C at its top, not as cold as the mesosphere.", "1": "The stratosphere warms with height, from about −58 °C to −2 °C.", "2": "The thermosphere is the hottest layer, reaching 500 to 2000 °C or more."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0599' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The thermosphere, about 80 to 600 km up, is the outermost layer that meets the Sun''s radiation first. Its few gas particles absorb high-energy ultraviolet radiation and X-rays, which heats them strongly. So temperature rises with altitude, reaching 500 to 2000 °C or more.', wrong = '{"0": "Exothermic ozone formation warms the stratosphere, not the thermosphere.", "1": "The thermosphere has very thin air. The troposphere is the densest layer.", "3": "Almost all water vapour is in the troposphere, not the thermosphere."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0600' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the upper atmosphere, strong UV splits O₂ into oxygen atoms. In the middle stratosphere, where less UV gets through, these single atoms combine with O₂ molecules to form ozone: O₂ + O → O₃. Forming a new bond releases energy, so this reaction is exothermic.', wrong = '{"0": "Ozone in the ozone layer forms in the stratosphere, by O₂ + O → O₃, not in the troposphere.", "1": "O₃ → O₂ + O is the breakdown of ozone by UV, not its formation.", "2": "Splitting O₂ into atoms breaks a bond, which needs energy, so it is endothermic, not exothermic. It also does not form ozone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0601' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ozone forms when O atoms combine with O₂, and O atoms come from O₂ split by UV radiation. By the time sunlight reaches the lower stratosphere, most of the UV has already been absorbed above. Without UV there are no free oxygen atoms, so ozone cannot form there.', wrong = '{"0": "The reason is the lack of UV radiation and O atoms, not a high temperature.", "1": "CFCs damage ozone, but the reason ozone does not form in the lower stratosphere is the lack of UV and O atoms.", "3": "O₂ is present in the lower stratosphere. What is missing is single oxygen atoms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0602' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A primary pollutant is released directly into the air from its source. Carbon monoxide comes straight out of a vehicle''s exhaust pipe, so it is primary. Secondary pollutants form later in the air when primary pollutants react with each other or with natural substances.', wrong = '{"0": "PAN forms in the air from NOx and hydrocarbons in sunlight, so it is a secondary pollutant.", "1": "Ozone in photochemical smog forms by reactions of NOx and hydrocarbons in sunlight, so it is secondary.", "2": "Sulphuric acid forms in the air from SO₂ via SO₃ and water, so it is a secondary pollutant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0603' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Algae living on the ocean surface release hydrogen sulphide, H₂S, as they break down sulphur compounds. Other natural sources each give a different pollutant: swamps and peat bogs release methane, lightning makes nitrogen oxides, and wind erosion lifts dust as particulate matter.', wrong = '{"0": "Wind erosion adds particulate matter (dust) to the air, not nitrogen oxides.", "1": "Swamps and peat bogs release methane from decaying plant matter, not sulphur dioxide.", "3": "Lightning produces nitrogen oxides, not methane."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0604' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Man-made sources of air pollution are divided into mobile and point sources. Point sources are fixed in one place, such as factories and power plants. Mobile sources move around, such as cars, trucks, aeroplanes and ships. A power plant is therefore a point source.', wrong = '{"0": "A car moves from place to place, so it is a mobile source.", "1": "An aeroplane moves from place to place, so it is a mobile source.", "2": "A marine engine moves with its ship, so it is a mobile source."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0605' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a carbon fuel burns with plenty of oxygen it forms CO₂. When the oxygen supply is limited, combustion is incomplete and carbon monoxide forms instead: 2C + O₂ → 2CO. Vehicle engines and poorly ventilated stoves are major sources.', wrong = '{"0": "Complete combustion, with enough oxygen, produces CO₂, not CO.", "1": "Photosynthesis takes in CO₂ and releases O₂. It does not produce CO.", "3": "Algae on the ocean surface release hydrogen sulphide, not carbon monoxide."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0606' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin in red blood cells much more strongly than oxygen does. This blocks haemoglobin from carrying oxygen, so body tissues are starved of oxygen. Treatment uses oxygen at high pressure to push the CO off the haemoglobin.', wrong = '{"0": "Ozone layer depletion is caused mainly by CFCs, not carbon monoxide.", "1": "CO is a reducing agent, not an oxidizing agent. Its toxicity comes from binding haemoglobin.", "3": "CO is a neutral oxide. It does not form an acid with water in the lungs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0607' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Air is mostly N₂ and O₂, which normally do not react because the N≡N bond is very strong. At the high temperatures inside engines and furnaces they combine: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO. The NO is then oxidized further in the air to NO₂.', wrong = '{"0": "N₂ does not react with water to form NO and H₂. Nitric oxide comes from N₂ and O₂ in air.", "1": "2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂ converts NO that already exists into NO₂. It does not make NO from air.", "2": "This is not how NO forms in engines, and as written it is not balanced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0608' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Coal and oil contain sulphur compounds as impurities. When these fuels burn, the sulphur combines with oxygen to form sulphur dioxide, S + O₂ → SO₂, and some SO₃. Power plants and factories burning coal and oil are the main sources of SOx.', wrong = '{"0": "Photosynthesis releases oxygen, not sulphur oxides.", "1": "Lightning produces nitrogen oxides, not sulphur oxides.", "3": "Old refrigerators were a source of CFCs, not sulphur oxides."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0609' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Methane, CH₄, is the most common hydrocarbon air pollutant. It is released by natural sources such as swamps as well as by human activities. Among man-made sources, vehicles are the biggest emitters of hydrocarbons as unburnt fuel.', wrong = '{"0": "Naphthalene is a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAH), not the most common hydrocarbon pollutant.", "1": "Ethane is a hydrocarbon pollutant, but it is much less common than methane.", "2": "Benzene is a volatile organic compound (VOC), not the most common hydrocarbon pollutant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0610' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'CFCs are very stable in the lower atmosphere and slowly drift up to the stratosphere. There, strong UV radiation breaks a C–Cl bond, for example CFCl₃ → CFCl₂• + Cl•. The chlorine free radical released is very reactive and attacks ozone.', wrong = '{"1": "The C–F bond is much stronger and is not the one broken. Fluorine gas is not released.", "2": "The bond breaks evenly (homolytically), so a neutral chlorine atom with an unpaired electron forms, not a chloride ion.", "3": "Carbon dioxide is not produced when UV breaks down CFCs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0611' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the first step Cl• destroys an ozone molecule and becomes ClO•. In the second step ClO• reacts with an oxygen atom and gives back Cl•. Since the chlorine radical is regenerated, it acts like a catalyst, repeating the cycle again and again. One chlorine atom can destroy up to about 100 000 ozone molecules.', wrong = '{"0": "O₂ is a product of the cycle. It does not attack ozone.", "1": "Nitrogen plays no part in these two steps.", "3": "Each cycle uses one Cl• and returns one Cl•. There is no increase in the number of radicals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0612' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) are made of two or more benzene rings fused together. Naphthalene has two fused rings, while anthracene and phenanthrene each have three. So all three are PAHs.', wrong = '{"0": "CFCs are compounds of carbon, chlorine and fluorine. They contain no aromatic rings.", "1": "VOCs are substances that evaporate easily at room temperature, such as benzene, toluene, formaldehyde and acetone.", "2": "POPs are long-lasting toxic chemicals such as PCBs and DDT, not these simple fused-ring hydrocarbons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0613' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PAHs form mainly when carbon-based fuels burn incompletely, without enough oxygen. Sources include burning coal, oil, petrol and wood, vehicle exhaust, some industrial processes, and even grilled or charred food.', wrong = '{"1": "UV light acting on CFCs releases chlorine free radicals, not PAHs.", "2": "Bacterial denitrification in soil releases nitrogen gas and nitrogen oxides, not PAHs.", "3": "Evaporation of liquid fuels at room temperature releases VOCs, not PAHs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0614' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Persistent organic pollutants are not easily broken down by chemical reactions, living organisms or sunlight (photolysis). So they remain in the environment for a long time and build up in food chains. Wind and water carry them far, so POPs released in one country can harm people and wildlife elsewhere.', wrong = '{"0": "POPs resist breakdown by sunlight. That is part of why they are called persistent.", "2": "POPs are carried by wind and water far from where they were released.", "3": "POPs are toxic and harmful to health. Their danger has nothing to do with smell."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0615' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'DDT, dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane, was widely used as an insecticide in agriculture and to control mosquitoes. It is a persistent organic pollutant because it breaks down very slowly and builds up in food chains.', wrong = '{"1": "CFCs, not DDT, were used as coolants in refrigerators.", "2": "PCBs, not DDT, were used as insulating fluids in electrical equipment.", "3": "DDT was used as an insecticide, not as a fuel additive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0616' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Volatile organic compounds (VOCs) are carbon compounds that evaporate easily at room temperature. Examples include benzene, toluene, xylene, ethanol, acetone and formaldehyde. Formaldehyde, given off by some paints, glues and furniture, is a VOC.', wrong = '{"0": "Anthracene is a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAH) with three fused rings. It is a solid that does not evaporate easily.", "2": "DDT is a persistent organic pollutant (POP), not a VOC.", "3": "Lead is a heavy metal, an element, not an organic compound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0617' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Particulate matter is a mixture of tiny solid particles and liquid droplets suspended in the air. The size range of concern is roughly 2.5 to 10 micrometres across. PM comes from both natural sources, such as volcanoes, fires and dust storms, and human activities.', wrong = '{"1": "PM includes liquid droplets as well as solid particles.", "2": "PM also has natural sources such as volcanoes, forest fires, dust storms and sea salt.", "3": "PM is made of particles and droplets, not a gas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0618' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heavy metals such as lead, mercury and cadmium enter the air mainly from human activities. The major sources are metallurgy (extracting and processing metals), waste from batteries, and incineration (burning) of waste.', wrong = '{"0": "Photosynthesis and respiration exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide, not heavy metals.", "2": "Lightning produces nitrogen oxides and ocean algae release hydrogen sulphide, not heavy metals.", "3": "Swamps and peat bogs release methane, not heavy metals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0619' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Industrial smog, also called classical or London smog, forms in cold, damp weather when large amounts of sulphur oxides and smoke from burning coal mix with fog. SOx are the main cause. It is a reducing smog, unlike the oxidizing photochemical smog.', wrong = '{"0": "CFCs damage the ozone layer high in the stratosphere. They do not cause London smog.", "2": "PAN is a secondary pollutant found in photochemical smog, not industrial smog.", "3": "Ground-level ozone is a secondary pollutant of photochemical smog, not industrial smog."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0620' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Photochemical smog forms when nitrogen oxides, volatile organic compounds and unburned hydrocarbons, mostly from vehicle exhaust, react together in sunlight. The reactions produce secondary pollutants such as ozone and PAN, giving a brownish, oxidizing haze.', wrong = '{"1": "SOx and fog cause industrial (London) smog. Photochemical smog needs sunlight, not night.", "2": "CFCs and UV in the stratosphere cause ozone depletion, not smog at ground level.", "3": "Heavy metals and rain play no part in forming photochemical smog."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0621' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Industrial smog is caused mainly by SO₂ and smoke from burning coal. SO₂ is a reducing agent, so this type of smog is called reducing smog. It is also known as classical smog or London smog, after the deadly London smog of 1952.', wrong = '{"1": "Oxidizing smog is photochemical smog, which contains oxidants such as ozone and PAN.", "2": "Los Angeles smog is another name for photochemical smog, not industrial smog.", "3": "Photochemical smog is the other main type, formed from NOx and hydrocarbons in sunlight."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0622' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Even clean rain is slightly acidic, because dissolved CO₂ forms weak carbonic acid and gives a pH of about 5.6. Rain is called acid rain only when its pH falls below 5.6, usually because of dissolved SOx and NOx forming sulphuric and nitric acids.', wrong = '{"0": "pH 7 is neutral, but normal rain is already slightly acidic, so 7 is not the threshold.", "2": "6.5 is not the threshold. Normal rain has a pH of about 5.6.", "3": "Rain at pH 4.0 is acid rain, but the threshold is 5.6, not 4.0."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0623' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sulphur dioxide from burning fossil fuels is first oxidized in the air to sulphur trioxide: 2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃. The SO₃ then reacts with water droplets to form sulphuric acid: SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄. This acid falls to the ground as acid rain.', wrong = '{"0": "SO₂ + H₂O gives sulphurous acid, H₂SO₃, not H₂SO₄. As written, the oxygen atoms do not balance.", "1": "NO contains no hydrogen, so SO₃ + NO cannot form H₂SO₄. Water is needed.", "3": "Breaking SO₃ down to SO₂ is the reverse of what happens. SO₂ must be oxidized to SO₃ first."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0624' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In HNO₃, +1 + N + 3(−2) = 0, so N = +5. In NO, N + (−2) = 0, so N = +2. So nitrogen goes from +4 in NO₂ up to +5 in HNO₃ and down to +2 in NO. Nitrogen is both oxidized and reduced, so this is a disproportionation.', wrong = '{"0": "+3 is nitrogen''s oxidation state in nitrous acid, HNO₂, not nitric acid.", "2": "In NO, nitrogen is +2, not +4.", "3": "Nitrogen has five valence electrons, so it cannot go above +5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0625' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sunlight reaching Earth is mostly short-wavelength radiation, which passes through CO₂ and water vapour and warms the ground. The warm Earth then gives off longer-wavelength infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases absorb this infrared and send much of it back, so heat is trapped near the surface.', wrong = '{"1": "Short-wavelength sunlight passes through greenhouse gases and reaches the ground. It is not blocked.", "2": "The greenhouse effect comes from absorbing infrared radiation, not from reactions with ozone.", "3": "If all infrared were reflected into space, heat would escape and there would be no warming."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0626' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Global warming is the long-term rise in Earth''s average surface temperature. It is caused mainly by human activities, especially burning fossil fuels, which release greenhouse gases such as CO₂ that trap extra heat in the atmosphere.', wrong = '{"1": "Summer warming is a regular seasonal change, not a long-term rise in average temperature.", "2": "Heating of the thermosphere by X-rays is a natural feature of that layer, not global warming.", "3": "Warming of the stratosphere from ozone formation is natural and is not what global warming means."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0627' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Air Quality Index (AQI) turns pollutant levels into a single number with a colour band. Values from 301 to 500 are classed as Hazardous and shown in maroon. At this level everyone may suffer serious health effects, so any value above 300 is hazardous.', wrong = '{"0": "Unhealthy covers AQI 151 to 200, shown in red, which is well below 300.", "2": "Very unhealthy covers AQI 201 to 300, shown in purple. Values above 300 are in the next band.", "3": "Moderate covers AQI 51 to 100, shown in yellow, far below 300."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0628' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On the AQI scale, values from 101 to 150 are labelled unhealthy for sensitive groups, such as children, the elderly and people with asthma. This band is shown in orange. An AQI of 120 falls inside it.', wrong = '{"0": "Green is used for AQI 0 to 50, the good band.", "2": "Yellow is used for AQI 51 to 100, the moderate band.", "3": "Red is used for AQI 151 to 200, the unhealthy band."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0629' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Normally air near the ground is warmer and rises, carrying pollutants upward where they spread out. When a layer of warm air sits above cooler air near the ground, this rising is blocked. The pollutants are trapped close to the ground, so air quality becomes poor.', wrong = '{"0": "Acid rain depends on SOx and NOx dissolving in rain water, not on a warm air layer.", "1": "The warm layer does not create ozone. It only stops pollutants from rising and spreading out.", "3": "Nitrogen oxides come from combustion and lightning, not from a layer of warm air."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0630' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A nephelometer, also called a photometer, shines light through a sample of air. Particles such as dust, smoke, mist and fumes scatter some of the light. The instrument measures the total scattered light, which shows how much particulate matter is present.', wrong = '{"0": "A nephelometer does not measure the electric charge on particles. It works by light scattering.", "1": "Collecting particles on a filter and weighing them is a different method, not how a nephelometer works.", "3": "Particles are detected by the light they scatter, not by radioactivity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0631' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Satellites carry sensors that measure pollutants in the atmosphere from space. Because they view very large areas at once, this remote sensing gives data on air pollution over whole regions and the entire globe.', wrong = '{"1": "Continuous emission monitoring systems measure what comes out of one factory''s stack, not regional or global air.", "2": "A fixed monitoring station measures air at one location, such as a city or industrial zone.", "3": "A diesel particulate filter removes soot from exhaust. It does not measure air quality at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0632' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A catalytic converter uses metal catalysts to turn harmful exhaust gases into less harmful ones. Carbon monoxide is oxidized to CO₂, nitrogen oxides are reduced to N₂, and unburnt hydrocarbons are oxidized to CO₂ and H₂O.', wrong = '{"0": "Both directions are reversed. CO is oxidized to CO₂, and NOx is reduced to N₂.", "2": "Hydrocarbons are converted to CO₂ and water. Turning them into toxic CO would make the exhaust worse.", "3": "A car''s catalytic converter does not make sulphuric acid from SO₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0633' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diesel engines release fine soot particles in their exhaust. A diesel particulate filter traps and stores these soot particles, so far less particulate matter (PM) escapes into the air.', wrong = '{"0": "Converting NOx to N₂ is done by catalytic converters and selective catalytic reduction, not by the particulate filter.", "1": "Removing SO₂ with water is the job of scrubbers in factories, not of a diesel filter.", "3": "Measuring the AQI needs monitoring instruments such as nephelometers. A filter only removes soot."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0634' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Selective catalytic reduction (SCR) is used in diesel engines to reduce NOx to harmless N₂, and it also helps oxidize CO and hydrocarbons. The catalysts used for this include titanium dioxide (TiO₂) and zeolites.', wrong = '{"0": "Iron with molybdenum as a promoter is linked to the Haber process for ammonia, not SCR.", "2": "V₂O₅ is the contact process catalyst and platinum gauze is used to oxidize ammonia for nitric acid.", "3": "Nickel and palladium are linked to hydrogenation reactions, not SCR in diesel engines."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0635' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pakistan''s main environmental law is the Pakistan Environmental Protection Act (PEPA) of 1997. Related measures include the National Environmental Quality Standards and, at the provincial level, the Punjab Environmental Protection (Amendment) Act of 2012.', wrong = '{"0": "2012 is the year of the Punjab Environmental Protection (Amendment) Act, not PEPA.", "2": "1987 is not the year PEPA was passed.", "3": "2005 is not the year PEPA was passed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0636' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chemical analysis has two branches. Qualitative analysis detects or identifies which elements or substances are present in a sample. Quantitative analysis then finds how much of each is present.', wrong = '{"0": "Finding the relative amounts of elements is quantitative analysis, not qualitative.", "1": "Weighing a sample is a measurement step. It does not by itself identify what is present.", "3": "Calculating the yield of a reaction is quantitative work, since it deals with amounts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0637' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A complete quantitative determination has four major steps: obtaining a sample for analysis, separating the desired constituent, measuring and calculating the results, and drawing conclusions. Naming the compound by IUPAC rules is not one of these steps.', wrong = '{"0": "Obtaining a sample for analysis is the first step of a quantitative determination.", "1": "Separating the desired constituent is the second step.", "2": "Measurement and calculation of results is the third step."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0638' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In filtration, the liquid passes through the filter paper while undissolved solid is held back. The salt dissolves in water and passes through as the filtrate. The sand does not dissolve, so it stays on the filter paper, and this solid is called the residue.', wrong = '{"0": "The filtrate is the salt solution that passes through the paper, not the sand left on it.", "1": "A distillate is the liquid collected from the condenser in distillation, not a solid left in filtration.", "2": "The solute is the dissolved salt. Sand does not dissolve, so it is not a solute."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0639' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A solution has a solute, the substance that dissolves, and a solvent, the substance that does the dissolving and is present in larger amount. In salt solution, salt is dissolved in water and water is present in excess, so water is the solvent.', wrong = '{"0": "Salt is the substance that dissolves, so it is the solute, not the solvent.", "1": "Sand does not dissolve and is not part of the solution at all.", "3": "The filter paper is the filtering material. It is not part of the solution."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0640' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The filter paper should be chosen so that, at the end of filtration, it is about one-quarter to one-half full of precipitate. A paper this size holds the solid comfortably without overflowing, and the filtrate runs down without splashing or losing precipitate.', wrong = '{"0": "A completely full paper risks overflowing, so some precipitate could be lost over the edge.", "1": "The paper is folded into a cone and moistened for use, not left dry and flat.", "2": "The precipitate should stay on the paper. If it passes into the filtrate, the filtration has failed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0641' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fluted filter paper is folded like a fan into many ridges and hollows. This leaves gaps between the paper and the funnel, so the liquid can pass through a much larger area of paper at once. As a result, filtration is considerably faster than with a plain cone.', wrong = '{"0": "Fluted paper is still paper and would be attacked by concentrated HCl. Such liquids need a Gooch crucible with an asbestos mat.", "1": "Precipitates that must be ignited are filtered through a Gooch crucible, not fluted paper.", "3": "Fluted paper is placed in a conical funnel. It still needs the funnel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0642' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A Gooch crucible is a porcelain crucible with a perforated (holed) bottom. The holes are covered with paper pulp or a circle of filter paper, and the crucible is used with suction to speed up filtration. An asbestos mat can be used instead of paper when the liquid would attack paper.', wrong = '{"0": "A glass crucible with a porous glass disc sealed into it is a sintered glass crucible, not a Gooch crucible.", "1": "A desiccator is a closed container with a drying agent, used to dry crystals. It is not a filter.", "2": "A funnel with fluted filter paper is a separate filtering method for faster gravity filtration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0643' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Concentrated HCl and KMnO₄ solution attack and damage paper, so paper filters cannot be used. Instead, a Gooch crucible is used with its perforations covered by an asbestos mat. Asbestos is inorganic and is not attacked by these solutions.', wrong = '{"0": "Ordinary filter paper would be attacked and damaged by concentrated HCl or KMnO₄.", "1": "Fluted filter paper is still paper, so it would also be attacked.", "3": "Cotton wool is organic like paper and would also be attacked, so it is not suitable."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0644' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sintered glass crucible has a porous glass disc permanently sealed into its bottom, which acts as the filter. So it can be used straight away. A Gooch crucible, by contrast, needs a pad of paper or asbestos prepared over its perforations before each use.', wrong = '{"0": "Filter crucibles are normally used with suction, not only for gravity filtration.", "1": "The sintered crucible is made of glass. It is the Gooch crucible that is porcelain.", "3": "An asbestos mat is an option for the Gooch crucible. The sintered crucible uses a porous glass disc."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0645' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Crystallization uses the fact that most solids dissolve better in a hot solvent than in a cold one. The impure solid is dissolved in the minimum amount of hot solvent. On cooling, the solubility falls, so the excess solute comes out as pure crystals while impurities stay in solution.', wrong = '{"0": "A solute that is insoluble at all temperatures could not be dissolved, so it could not be recrystallized.", "1": "Adsorption onto a stationary phase is the basis of adsorption chromatography, not crystallization.", "2": "Separating by boiling point is the basis of distillation, not crystallization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0646' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A good crystallization solvent dissolves a lot of the substance when hot, near its boiling point, but very little at room temperature. This large difference in solubility means that most of the substance comes out as crystals on cooling, giving a good recovery.', wrong = '{"0": "The solvent must not react with the solute. A reaction would change the substance being purified.", "1": "If the solubility were the same at all temperatures, nothing would crystallize on cooling.", "3": "Impurities should stay in solution or not dissolve at all, so that they do not crystallize with the solute."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0647' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An inflammable solvent gives off vapour that can catch fire if it meets a naked flame. Heating the solution on a water bath keeps the flame away from the solvent and limits the temperature to about 100 °C, so the risk of fire is greatly reduced.', wrong = '{"0": "A Bunsen flame could ignite the inflammable solvent vapour, which is exactly what must be avoided.", "1": "A hot water funnel keeps a solution hot during filtration. It is not the way to heat the solution.", "2": "Heating a solvent in a sealed tube can build up dangerous pressure, so it is not used."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0648' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A hot saturated solution starts to deposit crystals as soon as it cools. If this happens during filtration, crystals form in the filter paper or the funnel stem and block them. A hot water funnel keeps the solution hot while it is filtered, preventing early crystallization.', wrong = '{"0": "Coloured impurities are removed by boiling with animal charcoal, not by using a hot water funnel.", "1": "The purpose is the opposite: to keep the solution hot during filtration, not to cool it.", "3": "Crystals are dried later, in air, an oven or a vacuum desiccator."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0649' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Finely powdered animal charcoal has a huge surface area that adsorbs coloured impurities. The crude product is dissolved and boiled with a little charcoal, and the hot solution is filtered to remove the charcoal along with the colour. The clear filtrate then gives colourless crystals on cooling.', wrong = '{"0": "Filtration alone removes only solid particles. Dissolved coloured impurities would pass straight through.", "1": "Concentrated sulphuric acid is very reactive and could damage or react with the product.", "2": "The rate of cooling affects the size of the crystals, not their colour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0650' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rate of cooling controls crystal size. Very rapid cooling gives tiny crystals that can trap impurities and are hard to filter, while very slow cooling gives large crystals. Cooling at a moderate rate gives medium-sized crystals, which are pure and easy to collect.', wrong = '{"0": "Cooling lowers the solubility, so solute comes out of solution rather than dissolving.", "1": "Boiling happens while heating to dissolve the solute, not while cooling.", "3": "Insoluble impurities were already removed by filtering the hot solution."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0651' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A vacuum desiccator dries crystals using a substance that strongly absorbs water. Suitable drying agents include anhydrous calcium chloride, silica gel and phosphorus pentoxide. Sodium chloride does not absorb water strongly, so it is not used as a drying agent.', wrong = '{"0": "Anhydrous calcium chloride absorbs moisture readily and is a common desiccator drying agent.", "1": "Silica gel absorbs water and is a common desiccator drying agent.", "2": "Phosphorus pentoxide is a very powerful drying agent used in desiccators."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0652' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An oven dries crystals at around 100 °C. This is safe only if the substance does not melt or decompose at that temperature. Substances that are not stable at 100 °C are dried instead in air, between filter papers, or in a vacuum desiccator.', wrong = '{"0": "The condition is that the substance stays stable at 100 °C. Removing water of crystallization would actually change the compound.", "1": "Colour has nothing to do with whether crystals can be dried in an oven.", "2": "Solubility in water is not the deciding condition. Stability on heating is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0653' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sea water is water containing many dissolved salts. In simple distillation the water is boiled off, the vapour is cooled in a condenser, and pure water is collected. The salts do not evaporate, so they stay behind in the flask, leaving drinkable water.', wrong = '{"0": "Filtration removes only undissolved solids. Dissolved salts pass straight through the filter.", "2": "Paper chromatography separates tiny samples for analysis. It cannot purify large volumes of water.", "3": "Crystallization would recover the salts, not the pure water needed for drinking."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0654' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a liquid is heated, it can suddenly boil violently in large bursts, called bumping. Boiling chips are small porous pieces that provide points where small bubbles can form steadily. This gives smooth, even boiling and prevents bumping.', wrong = '{"1": "Boiling chips do not change the boiling point of the liquid.", "2": "Boiling chips are inert and do not react with any impurities.", "3": "The vapour is condensed in the condenser, not by the boiling chips."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0655' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In simple distillation the vapour passes into the condenser, turns back into liquid and is collected in the receiving flask. This collected liquid is the distillate. The substances that do not evaporate and remain in the distillation flask are the residue.', wrong = '{"1": "Filtrate is the liquid that passes through a filter paper in filtration, not the product of distillation.", "2": "The terms are swapped. The collected liquid is the distillate and what remains is the residue.", "3": "Solute and solvent describe the parts of a solution, not the products of distillation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0656' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fractional distillation separates liquids that mix completely (are miscible) but have different boiling points. Ethanol boils at 78 °C and water at 100 °C, so ethanol vaporizes first and can be collected separately using a fractionating column.', wrong = '{"0": "Sand is removed from salt water by filtration, since sand does not dissolve.", "2": "A small mixture of dyes is separated for analysis by chromatography, not distillation.", "3": "Copper sulphate is recovered from its solution by crystallization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0657' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Glass beads in a fractionating column provide a large surface area. The rising vapour condenses and re-evaporates many times on these surfaces. Each cycle enriches the vapour in the lower-boiling liquid, so the separation of the liquids becomes much better.', wrong = '{"1": "Glass beads do not absorb water or any other liquid.", "2": "The separation is purely physical. Glass beads do not catalyse anything.", "3": "The vapour must pass up through the column to reach the condenser. The beads only slow and refine it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0658' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'While ethanol, which boils at 78 °C, is distilling, the thermometer reading stays steady at 78 °C. When nearly all the ethanol has distilled, the vapour reaching the thermometer changes and the temperature begins to rise. The receiving flask should then be changed so that the water, collected at 100 °C, is kept separate.', wrong = '{"0": "Water distils at 100 °C. When the temperature first starts rising it has not yet reached that value.", "2": "Nothing turns solid in the distillation flask during this process.", "3": "The rising temperature reflects a change in the vapour. It is not a sign that the condenser has failed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0659' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Petroleum is a mixture of many hydrocarbons whose boiling points are fairly close to each other. In a fractionating column, the mixture is separated into fractions that boil over different temperature ranges, such as petrol, kerosene, diesel and furnace oil. This is fractional distillation.', wrong = '{"0": "Filtration separates undissolved solids from liquids. It cannot separate liquids from each other.", "1": "Crystallization is used to purify solids, not to separate liquid fuels.", "3": "Paper chromatography is an analytical method for tiny samples, not an industrial separation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0660' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chromatography is classified by its stationary phase. When the stationary phase is a solid, substances in the mobile phase stick (are adsorbed) to its surface to different extents. This is adsorption chromatography, as in thin layer chromatography and column chromatography.', wrong = '{"0": "Partition chromatography has a liquid stationary phase, not a solid one.", "2": "Paper chromatography is a partition method, with water held in the paper as the stationary phase.", "3": "Gas–liquid chromatography is a partition method, with a liquid stationary phase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0661' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In partition chromatography the stationary phase is a liquid, and substances divide (partition) between it and the mobile phase. In paper chromatography the stationary liquid is water held in the paper, and in gas–liquid chromatography it is a liquid coated on a solid support. So both are partition methods.', wrong = '{"1": "Thin layer and column chromatography both use a solid stationary phase, so they are adsorption methods.", "2": "Column chromatography uses a solid stationary phase, so it is an adsorption method.", "3": "Thin layer chromatography uses a solid stationary phase, so it is an adsorption method."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0662' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Paper chromatography is a partition method. The cellulose fibres of the paper hold water tightly, and this trapped water is the stationary phase. The mobile phase is usually an organic solvent that does not mix with water, moving up the paper.', wrong = '{"0": "In paper chromatography the cellulose only holds the water. The trapped water, not the fibres, is the stationary phase.", "2": "The organic solvent moving up the paper is the mobile phase, not the stationary phase.", "3": "The air inside the tank plays no part in the separation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0663' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ascending paper chromatography the bottom edge of the paper dips into the solvent. The solvent is drawn upward through the tiny spaces between the paper fibres by capillary action, carrying the components of the sample with it.', wrong = '{"0": "Gravity pulls liquid downward. It is used in the descending method, not the ascending one.", "2": "No pump is used in paper chromatography.", "3": "Electric fields are used in electrophoresis, not in paper chromatography."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0664' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The starting line, drawn about 2.5 cm from the bottom edge, must stay fixed so distances can be measured from it. Pencil marks are graphite, which is insoluble in the solvent and stays in place. An ink line would dissolve and separate into coloured spots, confusing the results.', wrong = '{"1": "Cost is not the reason. Ink is unsuitable because it would dissolve and move with the solvent.", "2": "Pencil marks do not glow under UV light. That is not why pencil is used.", "3": "Locating agents such as ninhydrin are sprayed on after the run. Pencil plays no such role."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0665' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some substances, such as amino acids, are colourless, so their spots cannot be seen on the chromatogram. A locating agent is sprayed on after the run and reacts with them to form coloured spots. Ninhydrin is used this way for amino acids, giving purple spots.', wrong = '{"0": "Ninhydrin is sprayed on after the run. It is not the solvent (mobile phase).", "2": "The stationary phase in paper chromatography is the water held in the paper, not ninhydrin.", "3": "Drying agents are substances such as CaCl₂ or silica gel. Ninhydrin is used to show colourless spots."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0666' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The retardation factor is Rf = distance moved by the component ÷ distance moved by the solvent front, both measured from the starting line. Here Rf = 3.0 cm ÷ 7.5 cm = 0.40. Rf has no units and is always less than 1.', wrong = '{"1": "2.5 is 7.5 ÷ 3.0, the ratio upside down. Rf can never be greater than 1.", "2": "0.25 does not come from these distances. It would need a spot only about 1.9 cm from the start.", "3": "4.5 is the difference 7.5 − 3.0 in cm. Rf is a ratio of the two distances, not their difference."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0667' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Distance moved by a spot = Rf × distance moved by the solvent front. The first spot is 0.30 × 10.0 = 3.0 cm from the start and the second is 0.70 × 10.0 = 7.0 cm. So the spots are 7.0 − 3.0 = 4.0 cm apart.', wrong = '{"1": "1.0 cm would be the gap only if the solvent front had moved 2.5 cm, since 0.40 × 2.5 = 1.0.", "2": "3.0 cm is how far the first spot moved from the start, not the gap between the spots.", "3": "7.0 cm is how far the second spot moved from the start, not the gap between the spots."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0668' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pure substance gives a single spot on a chromatogram, since there is only one component to separate. Under the same conditions each substance has its own Rf value. A single spot with the same Rf as a pure reference sample therefore suggests the product is pure and probably the same substance.', wrong = '{"0": "An impure product containing two substances would usually give two separate spots.", "2": "Nothing in the result shows the product is an amino acid. It only matches the reference sample.", "3": "An insoluble sample would stay on the starting line instead of moving up to the reference''s Rf."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0669' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pure solid melts sharply at one fixed temperature; pure aspirin melts at about 136 °C. Impurities lower the melting point and make the solid melt gradually over a range. Melting slowly from 128 to 133 °C, below 136 °C, shows the aspirin is impure, for example containing unreacted salicylic acid.', wrong = '{"0": "Pure aspirin would melt sharply at about 136 °C, not gradually over 128 to 133 °C.", "2": "A melting range only shows the sample is impure. It does not identify the sample as salicylic acid.", "3": "Nothing in the result points to decomposition into acetic anhydride, a liquid at room temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0670' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a mixed melting point test, the product is mixed with a pure known sample. If the product is the same pure compound, the mixture is still one pure substance and melts sharply at the normal melting point. A different compound would act as an impurity and lower and broaden the melting range.', wrong = '{"1": "A different compound would act as an impurity, giving a lower and broader melting range, not a sharp one.", "2": "Salicylic acid present as an impurity would lower the melting point and make it melt over a range.", "3": "The mixed melting point test is a standard, valid way to check a product''s identity and purity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0671' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Aspirin is acetylsalicylic acid. It is made by acetylating salicylic acid, that is, adding an acetyl group to its –OH group. This is done with excess acetic anhydride and a few drops of acid catalyst, which also forms acetic acid as a by-product.', wrong = '{"1": "Ethanol does not add an acetyl group to salicylic acid, so it cannot form aspirin.", "2": "Sodium hydroxide would simply neutralize the acid to form a sodium salt.", "3": "Methanol with sulphuric acid esterifies the –COOH group, giving methyl salicylate (oil of wintergreen), not aspirin."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0672' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Many laboratory chemicals are toxic, corrosive or harmful to breathe. For safety, students must never taste or smell any compound or gas directly, and food and drink must not be brought into the laboratory.', wrong = '{"0": "Students should not work alone. They should work with the lab instructor present, so help is available.", "1": "Accidents and breakages must be reported to the person in charge immediately, not at the end of term.", "2": "Chemicals must not be poured down drains or into the sewer. They should be disposed of as instructed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0673' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using unfamiliar instruments or equipment incorrectly can cause injury and damage. If a student does not know how to handle an instrument properly, the safe step is to ask the instructor for help before using it.', wrong = '{"0": "Trying unfamiliar equipment without guidance risks injury to the student and damage to the equipment.", "1": "The instructor should be told about any difficulty. Silently skipping the experiment does not solve the problem.", "2": "A classmate may not know the safe procedure either. The instructor is the right person to ask."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0674' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Laboratory hazards are usually sorted by what causes the harm. Physical hazards come from the surroundings (wet floors, broken glass), chemical hazards come from the substances used, and biological hazards come from living things such as microbes and allergens. So the three groups are physical, chemical and biological.', wrong = '{"0": "Solid, liquid and gas are the three states of matter. They describe a substance''s physical form, not the kind of danger it poses.", "1": "Electrical and heat risks do appear in lab safety, mainly under fire, but these are not the three main hazard groups. Nuclear hazards are not part of a school lab grouping.", "2": "Acidic, basic and neutral describe the pH of solutions. They are properties of chemicals, not categories of hazard."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0675' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A physical hazard is a danger from the working environment rather than from a chemical or a microbe. The most common ones are slips and falls on a wet floor, so workers must take care to avoid slipping, pulling and falling accidents.', wrong = '{"0": "Corrosive acids harm the body through their chemical action, so they are chemical hazards, not physical ones.", "1": "Allergens and microbes come from living sources, so they are biological hazards.", "2": "Toxic gases cause harm by their chemical nature when breathed in, so they are chemical hazards."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0676' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Broken glass causes cuts and abrasions, which is a physical hazard. Cut-resistant gloves protect the hands while it is handled, and placing it in a special labelled container keeps cleaners and other workers from being injured later.', wrong = '{"0": "Picking up broken glass with bare hands, however quickly, risks cuts and skin damage. Protective gloves must be worn.", "1": "In an ordinary bin the sharp pieces are hidden and can injure whoever empties it. Broken glass goes into a special container.", "3": "Glass fragments do not belong in the sink. They can block drains and cut anyone cleaning the sink later."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0677' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Biological hazards come from living organisms or their products. The most common are allergens and microbes such as bacteria and viruses, which can pass to humans from animals, plants, water and air.', wrong = '{"0": "A wet floor is a physical hazard because it causes slips and falls.", "1": "A faulty socket can cause shocks or fire. That is a physical (electrical) hazard, not a biological one.", "2": "Concentrated sulphuric acid burns skin by chemical action, so it is a chemical hazard."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0678' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Under the rules for chemical waste, each container is labelled with the type of waste, the date and the place it came from. This tells the people who store and treat it what it is, how old it is and where to ask about it, so it can be handled safely.', wrong = '{"0": "Colour does not tell anyone what the waste is or how to treat it. Many different chemicals look alike.", "1": "A student''s name alone gives no information about the contents, date or origin of the waste.", "3": "Waste must be labelled before it is moved. Treatment sites need to know what is in each container to treat it safely."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0679' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chemical treatment changes waste into safer substances by neutralization, precipitation, ion exchange, oxidation or reduction. Pouring waste into the sewer is not a treatment at all. It is forbidden, because chemical waste must not go into bins or the sewer system.', wrong = '{"0": "Neutralization is a listed treatment. Acidic waste is reacted with a base, or basic waste with an acid, to make it less harmful.", "1": "Precipitation is a listed treatment. Harmful dissolved ions are turned into an insoluble solid that can be filtered off.", "2": "Ion exchange is a listed treatment. Harmful ions in the waste are swapped for harmless ones on an ion exchange resin."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0680' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Water first washes away most of the acid. A weakly basic solution, 1% sodium bicarbonate, then neutralizes any acid left in the eye. A mild base is chosen because a strong one would itself damage the eye.', wrong = '{"0": "1% boric acid is a weak acid. It is used after alkali gets into the eye, not acid.", "2": "2% ammonia solution is used to wash bromine burns on the skin. It is not used in the eye.", "3": "1% acetic acid is an acid. It is used on skin after alkali burns and would not neutralize acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0681' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Water first washes most of the alkali out of the eye. A weak acid, 1% boric acid solution, then neutralizes the remaining alkali. A very mild acid is used so the eye is not harmed further.', wrong = '{"0": "Ammonia solution is itself alkaline, so it would add to the injury. 2% ammonia is used for bromine burns on skin.", "1": "Sodium bicarbonate is a mild base. It is used to neutralize acids, not alkalis.", "2": "A drop of olive oil is used for a sore eye. It does not neutralize alkali."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0682' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An alkali burn is treated by washing freely with water, then with a weak acid, 1% acetic acid, to neutralize any alkali left on the skin. The skin is washed again with water, dried, and burnol is applied.', wrong = '{"0": "Saturated sodium bicarbonate solution is a mild base. It is used for acid burns, so it would not neutralize an alkali.", "1": "2% ammonia solution is alkaline and is used for bromine burns. It would not help an alkali burn.", "3": "Lime water is a solution of Ca(OH)₂, an alkali. It is used when acid has been swallowed, not on an alkali burn."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0683' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bromine burns are washed fully with 2% ammonia solution, which reacts with and removes the bromine. Glycerin is then applied to soothe the skin, wiped off after some time, and burnol is applied to the burn.', wrong = '{"0": "Burnol or mustard oil is the treatment for burns with dry heat, such as from a flame or hot object.", "1": "1% acetic acid is used for alkali burns, and olive oil is used for a sore eye. Neither is the bromine treatment.", "2": "Washing with saturated sodium bicarbonate (after ice cold water) is the treatment for acid burns."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0684' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A swallowed acid is diluted by drinking plenty of water and neutralized by a mild base. Lime water (Ca(OH)₂ solution) and milk of magnesia (Mg(OH)₂) are weak bases that react with the acid without harming the throat and stomach.', wrong = '{"0": "Lemon juice is acidic. A glass of lemon or orange juice is the treatment for swallowed caustic alkali, not acid.", "1": "Vinegar is dilute acetic acid. Adding more acid would make an acid poisoning worse.", "3": "Milk or egg white is the treatment for swallowed salts of heavy metals. It does not neutralize acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0685' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heavy metal ions bind to proteins. Milk or egg white supplies plenty of protein, which binds the metal ions in the stomach and reduces how much reaches the body''s own tissues. So milk or the white of an egg is the first aid.', wrong = '{"0": "Dilute HCl is an acid. It would irritate the stomach and does nothing to bind heavy metal ions.", "1": "Lime water is a mild base, used to neutralize swallowed acid.", "2": "Orange juice is mildly acidic. It is used for swallowed caustic alkali."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0686' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The main danger from a serious cut is blood loss. Firm pressure on the wound for about 10 minutes helps the blood clot and stops bleeding. A doctor should then be consulted, since the cut may need stitches or further care.', wrong = '{"0": "Burnol is a burn cream used for heat burns. It does not stop bleeding from a cut.", "1": "2% ammonia solution is used to treat bromine burns, not cuts.", "3": "Leaving a serious cut open lets it keep bleeding and risks infection. Pressure and medical help are needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0687' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen. Covering the beaker with a duster or damp cloth cuts off the supply of oxygen, so the burning liquid goes out without being disturbed or spilled.', wrong = '{"0": "Most inflammable liquids float on water. Water can splash the burning liquid and spread the fire.", "2": "Blowing on the flame brings in more air and can spread burning liquid. It does not reliably put the fire out.", "3": "Carrying a burning beaker risks spilling the liquid and spreading the fire, and can burn the person carrying it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0688' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Spirit and oil do not mix with water and are less dense, so they float. Water thrown on them breaks the burning liquid into droplets and carries it outward, which simply spreads the fire. A mixture of sand and sodium bicarbonate is used instead.', wrong = '{"0": "Water does not react explosively with oil. The danger is that it carries the floating, burning oil to a wider area.", "2": "Water does not change how easily oil burns. The oil keeps burning on top of the water.", "3": "Neither water nor oil contains chlorine, so no chlorine can be released."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0689' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Running makes air flow past the burning clothes and supplies more oxygen, so the fire grows. Wrapping in a blanket or dry cotton cloth and lying down on the floor cuts off oxygen and smothers the flames.', wrong = '{"0": "Running to a tap fans the flames with air, so the fire gets worse on the way.", "2": "Spirit is a fuel. Pouring it on flames would make the fire much bigger.", "3": "Standing with arms raised does not cut off oxygen, and flames rise upward towards the face."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0690' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With an electrical fire, the first step is to switch off the electric supply immediately, so the risk of shock is removed and the fire is not fed by current. Sand is then thrown on to smother it. Water must not be used because it conducts electricity.', wrong = '{"0": "Water conducts electricity and can give a deadly shock, so it is never used on electrical fires.", "2": "A damp cloth conducts electricity while the power is on. The supply must be switched off first.", "3": "Oil is a fuel and would feed the fire. Only sand is used after the supply is switched off."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0691' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An indicator is a substance that shows the completion of a reaction by changing colour. The moment at which the indicator changes colour during a titration is called the end point, and the burette reading is taken at that moment.', wrong = '{"0": "The equivalence point is when acid and base have reacted in exact stoichiometric amounts. It is a theoretical point, not the observed colour change.", "2": "The titre is the volume of solution added from the burette to reach the end point, not the moment of colour change.", "3": "Standard point is not a titration term. A standard solution is a solution of known concentration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0692' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pipette is rinsed first with distilled water to clean it, then with the NaOH solution itself. Any water left inside would dilute the NaOH, so the last rinse with NaOH makes sure the 10 cm³ delivered has exactly the stated concentration.', wrong = '{"0": "Drops of distilled water left in the pipette would dilute the NaOH, so fewer moles of base would be delivered.", "2": "HCl left in the pipette would react with the NaOH and lower the amount of base delivered.", "3": "Ethanol rinsing is not part of the procedure. It could also leave a residue in the pipette."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0693' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The moles of NaOH in the flask are fixed by the 10 cm³ measured with the pipette. Extra distilled water in the flask changes only the volume, not the moles, so the volume of acid needed stays the same. Rinsing with NaOH would leave extra base and give a wrong result.', wrong = '{"0": "The mass of the flask has nothing to do with a titration result. The issue is the amount of base inside it.", "1": "Dilute NaOH does not dissolve glass to any noticeable extent during a titration.", "2": "Distilled water is neutral and shows no colour change. Phenolphthalein is the indicator used here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0694' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The nozzle below the tap can hold an air bubble. If it is not removed, acid fills that space during the titration, and the burette reading drops without that acid reaching the flask, so the titre comes out too large. Running a little acid into a beaker clears the bubble before the initial reading.', wrong = '{"1": "Letting a little acid flow out does not change its temperature.", "2": "Running acid out of the burette does not add any water, so the concentration stays the same.", "3": "The indicator is in the conical flask, not in the burette, so this step cannot check it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0695' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Concordant readings are titres that agree closely with one another. In practice at least three readings that agree within 0.1 cm³ are taken, and their average is used in the calculation. This shows the result is reliable and not a one-off.', wrong = '{"1": "A school burette can only be read to about 0.05 cm³, so agreement to 0.001 cm³ is neither possible nor required.", "2": "Readings that differ by 1 cm³ or more show poor technique. They are the opposite of concordant.", "3": "The rough titration only gives an approximate end point. It is usually left out of the average."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0696' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Concordant readings agree within 0.1 cm³. 12.15, 12.20 and 12.10 all lie within 0.1 cm³ of one another, while 12.60 is about 0.4 cm³ away and is rejected. Average = (12.15 + 12.20 + 12.10) ÷ 3 = 36.45 ÷ 3 = 12.15 cm³.', wrong = '{"1": "12.26 cm³ is the average of all four titres, (12.60 + 12.15 + 12.20 + 12.10) ÷ 4 = 12.26. It wrongly includes the outlier 12.60.", "2": "12.60 cm³ is the outlier itself. It does not agree with the other readings and must be left out.", "3": "12.35 cm³ is the midpoint of the highest and lowest values, (12.60 + 12.10) ÷ 2. That is not an average of concordant readings."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0697' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O, so the mole ratio is 1 : 1 (n₁ = n₂ = 1). Using M₁V₁/n₁ = M₂V₂/n₂ with acid M₁ = 0.10 M, V₁ = 12.5 cm³ and base V₂ = 10.0 cm³: M₂ = (0.10 × 12.5) ÷ 10.0 = 0.125 M.', wrong = '{"1": "0.080 M comes from swapping the volumes: 0.10 × 10.0 ÷ 12.5. The acid volume must go with the acid molarity.", "2": "0.25 M is double the answer, which comes from wrongly using a 1 : 2 ratio. NaOH and HCl react 1 : 1.", "3": "1.25 M is ten times the correct value, a decimal-place slip in 1.25 ÷ 10."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0698' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O, so n₁ = 1 (acid) and n₂ = 2 (base). Rearranging M₁V₁/n₁ = M₂V₂/n₂ gives M₂ = M₁V₁n₂ ÷ (n₁V₂) = (0.050 × 20.0 × 2) ÷ (1 × 10.0) = 0.20 M.', wrong = '{"0": "0.050 M is simply the molarity of the acid. The base concentration must be calculated from the volumes and mole ratio.", "1": "0.10 M is (0.050 × 20.0) ÷ 10.0, which ignores that one H₂SO₄ needs two NaOH.", "3": "0.025 M comes from putting the mole ratio upside down, (0.050 × 20.0) ÷ (2 × 10.0). Each H₂SO₄ needs two NaOH, so the base needs more moles, not fewer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0699' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carbon dioxide from the air slowly dissolves in the solution and forms carbonic acid. This lowers the pH slightly, and phenolphthalein, which is pink only in basic solution, gradually turns colourless. So the faint end-point pink fades on standing.', wrong = '{"1": "Phenolphthalein does not evaporate from the solution in this short time. The colour change is caused by a change in pH.", "2": "NaCl is stable in light and is not decomposed. It also has no effect on the indicator colour.", "3": "A leaking burette would give a wrong volume reading. It would not make the colour in the flask fade."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0700' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The end point is judged by a faint colour change of the indicator. A white paper under the flask gives a plain background, so the first faint pink colour (or its loss) is easy to see, which helps stop at the right moment.', wrong = '{"1": "The paper is not there to absorb spills. Its job is to make the colour change easy to see.", "2": "A sheet of paper has no useful heating or warming effect on the flask.", "3": "An anti-parallax card is held behind the burette to read the liquid level. The paper under the flask serves a different purpose."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0701' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carbonates react with dilute acid and give off carbon dioxide with brisk effervescence: CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂. CO₂ turns lime water milky because insoluble calcium carbonate forms: Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O. So carbonate is present.', wrong = '{"0": "Chloride gives no gas with dilute HCl. It is tested with acidified silver nitrate, which gives a white precipitate.", "1": "Sulphate gives no gas with dilute HCl. It is tested with barium nitrate, which gives a white precipitate of BaSO₄.", "3": "Nitrate gives no CO₂ with dilute acid. It is tested by warming with NaOH and aluminium powder to give ammonia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0702' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carbonate ions also form a precipitate with Ag⁺, which could be mistaken for a silver halide. Adding dilute nitric acid first destroys any carbonate (as CO₂), so a precipitate that forms afterwards can only be a silver halide.', wrong = '{"1": "Silver halides do not dissolve in dilute nitric acid. The test depends on them staying as a precipitate.", "2": "Dilute nitric acid is colourless and adds no colour. The colours seen come from the silver halides.", "3": "Dilute nitric acid is not used here to oxidize halides. Its purpose is to remove carbonate ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0703' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Halide ions give coloured precipitates with acidified silver nitrate, and the shade identifies the halide. Iodide gives a bright yellow precipitate of AgI: Ag⁺ + I⁻ → AgI. So the salt contains I⁻.', wrong = '{"0": "Cl⁻ gives a thick white precipitate of AgCl, which dissolves in aqueous ammonia.", "1": "Br⁻ gives a thick cream-yellow precipitate of AgBr, paler than the bright yellow of AgI.", "3": "Sulphate is not tested with silver nitrate. It gives a white precipitate of BaSO₄ with barium nitrate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0704' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In alkaline solution, aluminium reduces nitrate ions (N in +5 state) to ammonia (N in −3 state): 3NO₃⁻ + 8Al + 5OH⁻ + 18H₂O → 3NH₃ + 8[Al(OH)₄]⁻. Ammonia has a sharp smell and is basic, so it turns moist red litmus blue.', wrong = '{"1": "Aluminium is a reducing agent, so it cannot oxidize nitrate. NO₂ is also an acidic gas and would not turn red litmus blue.", "2": "Hydrogen gas is neutral and has no effect on litmus. The gas detected here is ammonia.", "3": "Nitrogen gas is neutral and odourless, so it cannot turn red litmus blue."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0705' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sulphate ions form a heavy white precipitate with barium ions: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄. The solution is first acidified with dilute nitric acid to destroy any carbonate, which would also give a white barium precipitate. So barium nitrate is the reagent.', wrong = '{"0": "Silver nitrate is the reagent for halide ions (Cl⁻, Br⁻, I⁻), not sulphate.", "2": "Lime water detects carbon dioxide given off by carbonates. It does not test for sulphate.", "3": "Aluminium powder with NaOH is used to test for nitrate, which is reduced to ammonia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0706' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ammonium salts react with warm NaOH to release ammonia: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O. Ammonia has a distinct sharp smell and is basic, so it turns moist red litmus blue. A gas forming on warming with alkali is the key sign of NH₄⁺.', wrong = '{"0": "Ca²⁺ gives a white precipitate of Ca(OH)₂ with NaOH. No gas is released.", "2": "Zn²⁺ gives a white precipitate of Zn(OH)₂ that dissolves in excess NaOH. No gas is released.", "3": "Al³⁺ gives a white gelatinous precipitate of Al(OH)₃ with NaOH. No gas is released."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0707' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ca²⁺ gives a white Ca(OH)₂ precipitate with NaOH, and it does not dissolve in excess. Aqueous ammonia is a weak base that supplies only a low OH⁻ concentration, so with Ca²⁺ it gives only slight turbidity or no precipitate. This pair of results identifies calcium.', wrong = '{"0": "Zn²⁺ gives a white precipitate that dissolves in excess NaOH and also in excess aqueous ammonia.", "1": "Al³⁺ gives a clear white precipitate with aqueous ammonia, not just slight turbidity.", "3": "Cu²⁺ gives a light-blue precipitate with NaOH and a deep blue solution with aqueous ammonia, not white products."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0708' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cu²⁺ first forms a light-blue precipitate of Cu(OH)₂. With excess aqueous ammonia this dissolves, because ammonia molecules bind to Cu²⁺ and form a soluble complex ion, [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺, which is deep blue. So a deep blue solution results.', wrong = '{"1": "White precipitates are given by ions such as Al³⁺, Zn²⁺ and Ca²⁺, not by Cu²⁺.", "2": "A green precipitate is given by Cr³⁺ or Fe²⁺ hydroxides.", "3": "An orange-brown precipitate is Fe(OH)₃, given by Fe³⁺."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0709' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cr³⁺ gives a green precipitate of Cr(OH)₃. This hydroxide is amphoteric, so it dissolves in excess NaOH to give a green solution. Aqueous ammonia is too weak a base to dissolve it, so the green precipitate stays in excess ammonia. Only chromium fits all three observations.', wrong = '{"0": "Fe²⁺ gives a green precipitate with NaOH, but it does not dissolve in excess. It turns orange-brown on standing as it is oxidized in air.", "2": "Cu²⁺ gives a light-blue precipitate, and a deep blue solution with aqueous ammonia.", "3": "Fe³⁺ gives an orange-brown precipitate of Fe(OH)₃, not a green one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0710' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fe³⁺ reacts with hydroxide ions from either NaOH or aqueous ammonia: Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃. Iron(III) hydroxide is an orange-brown precipitate, and it does not dissolve in excess of either reagent.', wrong = '{"1": "Cu²⁺ gives a light-blue precipitate with NaOH and a deep blue solution with aqueous ammonia.", "2": "Zn²⁺ gives a white precipitate that dissolves in excess of both reagents.", "3": "Ca²⁺ gives a white precipitate with NaOH and little or no precipitate with ammonia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0711' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Zn²⁺ gives a white precipitate of Zn(OH)₂. It dissolves in excess NaOH because zinc hydroxide is amphoteric, and it dissolves in excess aqueous ammonia because zinc forms a soluble ammine complex. Dissolving in both reagents is the sign of zinc.', wrong = '{"0": "Al³⁺ gives a white precipitate that dissolves in excess NaOH, but not in excess aqueous ammonia.", "1": "Ca²⁺ gives a white precipitate that does not dissolve in excess NaOH.", "3": "Fe²⁺ gives a green precipitate, not a white one, with NaOH."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0712' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chemical properties depend mainly on the outer electrons. Every Group 2 atom has two electrons in its outermost s subshell (ns²). Each loses these two electrons to form an M²⁺ ion, so the elements react in similar ways.', wrong = '{"0": "Group 2 elements are s-block elements. Their chemistry is set by the two outer s electrons, not by a filled d subshell.", "2": "Seven valence electrons belong to the halogens of Group 17, not to Group 2.", "3": "Atomic radius is not the same. The metallic radius rises steadily from Be (112 pm) to Ba (222 pm)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0713' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Strontium has atomic number 38. The noble gas before it is krypton (36), so two electrons remain and they enter the 5s subshell. Its configuration is [Kr] 5s², which fits the Group 2 pattern of ns².', wrong = '{"1": "[Xe] 6s² has 54 + 2 = 56 electrons. That is barium, not strontium.", "2": "[Ar] 4s² has 18 + 2 = 20 electrons. That is calcium.", "3": "[Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5s² has 36 + 12 = 48 electrons, which is cadmium. Strontium has only 2 electrons beyond krypton."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0714' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Down Group 2 the atoms get larger and the inner electrons shield the outer ones more. The two outer electrons are held less tightly, so the ionization energy falls and the M²⁺ ion forms more easily. Reactivity therefore rises: Be < Mg < Ca < Sr < Ba.', wrong = '{"0": "Ionization energy decreases down the group. The first IE falls from 900 kJ mol⁻¹ for Be to 503 kJ mol⁻¹ for Ba.", "1": "Electronegativity decreases down the group, from 1.5 for Be to 0.9 for Ba.", "3": "Every Group 2 element has exactly two valence electrons, so that number does not change."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0715' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From the table: Be 1287 °C, Mg 650 °C, Ca 842 °C, Sr 777 °C and Ba 727 °C. Magnesium''s 650 °C is the lowest. Melting points do not fall smoothly down the group, because the packing of the metal atoms changes from one element to the next.', wrong = '{"0": "Beryllium has the highest melting point in the group, 1287 °C.", "1": "Calcium melts at 842 °C, which is higher than magnesium''s 650 °C.", "2": "Barium melts at 727 °C. It is low, but still higher than magnesium''s 650 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0716' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A more negative standard electrode potential means the metal gives up electrons more readily. Barium has the most negative value (−2.91 V), so it loses electrons most easily and is the strongest reducing agent among these metals.', wrong = '{"0": "The most negative E° means Ba²⁺ is the hardest of these ions to reduce back to the metal, not the easiest.", "1": "Mg (−2.34 V) is less negative than Ba (−2.91 V), so Mg loses electrons less readily than Ba.", "3": "More negative potentials down the group mean reactivity increases from Mg to Ba, not decreases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0717' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Second ionization energies in the table are Be 1757, Mg 1451, Ca 1145, Sr 1064 and Ba 965 kJ mol⁻¹. They fall down the group because the outer electron is further from the nucleus and better shielded, just as the first ionization energies do.', wrong = '{"1": "The second IE is always larger than the first. The second electron is pulled away from an ion that is already positive.", "2": "The values differ widely, from 1757 kJ mol⁻¹ for Be to 965 kJ mol⁻¹ for Ba.", "3": "The trend is downward from Be to Ba, not upward."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0718' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Beryllium reacts with oxygen only at high temperature, above about 600 °C, giving BeO. Normally a thin, strong layer of oxide covers its surface and stops oxygen reaching the metal, so it is less reactive than the other Group 2 metals.', wrong = '{"0": "Beryllium does have valence electrons: two in its 2s subshell ([He] 2s²).", "1": "Beryllium is a solid metal that melts at 1287 °C, the highest in Group 2.", "2": "Beryllium forms the normal oxide BeO, not a peroxide."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0719' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Calcium reacts with oxygen more vigorously than magnesium, forming calcium oxide, CaO. Calcium compounds and calcium itself colour a flame brick-red, so calcium burns with a brick-red flame. This colour is also used to identify calcium in flame tests.', wrong = '{"0": "A brilliant white light is given by burning magnesium, not calcium.", "1": "A green flame is linked with barium (apple-green) or copper, not calcium.", "3": "A lilac flame is characteristic of potassium, a Group 1 metal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0720' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Group 2 oxides are basic. The oxide ion, O²⁻, takes a proton from a water molecule, giving two hydroxide ions: O²⁻ + H₂O → 2OH⁻. These OH⁻ ions make the solution alkaline, and the basic character increases down the group.', wrong = '{"0": "Carbonates form only when the oxide or hydroxide reacts with CO₂. Water alone does not give a carbonate.", "1": "Releasing H⁺ would make the solution acidic, the opposite of what Group 2 oxides do.", "2": "No oxygen gas is released. The oxide ion reacts with water to form hydroxide ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0721' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Calcium reacts with cold water to form calcium hydroxide and hydrogen: Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂. Calcium hydroxide is only sparingly soluble, so once a little has dissolved the rest appears as a white precipitate.', wrong = '{"1": "With water, calcium gives the hydroxide, not the oxide. The white solid is Ca(OH)₂.", "2": "Neither calcium nor water contains carbon, so no calcium carbonate can form in this reaction.", "3": "Hydrogen is a colourless gas. It escapes as bubbles and cannot form a precipitate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0722' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Calcium and dilute sulphuric acid give calcium sulphate and hydrogen: Ca + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + H₂. Calcium sulphate is only sparingly soluble, so it settles on the metal as a coating. This layer keeps the acid away from the calcium and the reaction slows or stops.', wrong = '{"0": "Sulphuric acid is a strong acid. The reaction stops because of the insoluble product, not because the acid is weak.", "1": "Hydrogen gas does not react with calcium sulphate. It simply bubbles away.", "3": "Calcium is more reactive than magnesium, since reactivity increases down Group 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0723' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Group 2 metals must lose two electrons, which needs more energy than losing one, so they are less reactive than the Group 1 metal in the same period. They also have stronger metallic bonding, which makes them harder, denser and higher-melting than Group 1 metals.', wrong = '{"0": "It is the Group 1 metals that are very soft (they can be cut with a knife) and have low densities.", "1": "Group 1 metals are the more reactive ones, with lower melting and boiling points.", "2": "Group 1 and Group 2 metals differ clearly. Group 2 metals are harder and denser."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0724' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A Group 2 metal forms an M²⁺ ion and oxygen forms O²⁻. Equal and opposite charges combine in a 1 : 1 ratio, so the oxide formula is MO, for example MgO and CaO.', wrong = '{"0": "M₂O fits an M⁺ ion. It is the formula of Group 1 oxides, such as Na₂O.", "1": "M₂O₃ needs an M³⁺ ion, as in Al₂O₃ from Group 13.", "3": "MO₂ would need an M⁴⁺ ion, or a peroxide such as BaO₂. It is not the general oxide formula."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0725' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Thermal decomposition is the breaking down of one compound into simpler substances by heating. Bonds in the compound must be broken, which needs energy, so the reaction is usually endothermic. For Group 2 carbonates: MCO₃ → MO + CO₂.', wrong = '{"0": "The reaction absorbs heat rather than releasing it. Bond breaking needs more energy than is given out.", "1": "Thermal means caused by heat. The carbonate must be heated, often strongly, for it to break down.", "2": "The products are the metal oxide and carbon dioxide. The metal is not set free, so the metal is not reduced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0726' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The decomposition temperatures in the table are BeCO₃ 250 °C, MgCO₃ 540 °C, CaCO₃ 900 °C, SrCO₃ 1289 °C and BaCO₃ 1360 °C. The carbonate that breaks down at about 900 °C is CaCO₃, which matches the 900 to 1000 °C used in lime kilns.', wrong = '{"0": "MgCO₃ decomposes at a lower temperature, about 540 °C.", "1": "BaCO₃ is the most stable and needs about 1360 °C.", "3": "SrCO₃ needs about 1289 °C, well above 900 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0727' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mg²⁺ (65 pm) is smaller than Ca²⁺ (99 pm) but has the same 2+ charge, so it has a higher charge density and greater polarizing power. It distorts the electron cloud of the large CO₃²⁻ ion more, weakening its C–O bonds, so MgCO₃ breaks down at a lower temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "The temperature difference is explained by the cation polarizing the carbonate ion, not by when the oxide becomes stable.", "1": "This is reversed. Mg²⁺ is smaller than Ca²⁺ and has a higher polarizing power.", "2": "Mg²⁺ and Ca²⁺ both carry a 2+ charge. The difference lies in their size."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0728' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Group 2 nitrates decompose on heating to the metal oxide, nitrogen dioxide and oxygen. Of these, only nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) is coloured: it is a brown gas. So the brown fumes seen are NO₂.', wrong = '{"1": "Nitrogen gas is not a product here, and it is colourless anyway.", "2": "Oxygen is formed, but it is a colourless gas.", "3": "A nitrate contains no carbon, so carbon dioxide cannot form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0729' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Thermal stability of Group 2 nitrates increases down the group. Be²⁺ is the smallest ion, so it has the highest charge density and polarizes the NO₃⁻ ion the most, weakening its bonds. Be(NO₃)₂ therefore decomposes most easily and is the least stable.', wrong = '{"0": "Ca²⁺ is larger than Be²⁺ and Mg²⁺, so Ca(NO₃)₂ is more stable than both of them.", "1": "Mg(NO₃)₂ is less stable than calcium nitrate, but still more stable than Be(NO₃)₂, because Mg²⁺ is larger than Be²⁺.", "3": "Ba²⁺ is the largest ion with the lowest polarizing power, so Ba(NO₃)₂ is the most stable."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0730' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hydration enthalpy is the energy released when gaseous ions are surrounded by water molecules, so it is always exothermic (negative). Smaller ions attract water more strongly. Down the group the ions get larger, so the value becomes less negative.', wrong = '{"1": "The energy needed to break a crystal into gaseous ions is the lattice enthalpy, not the hydration enthalpy.", "2": "It is the other way round. Smaller ions at the top have the most negative values, so it becomes less negative down the group.", "3": "Hydration releases energy, so it is exothermic, not endothermic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0731' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lattice enthalpy is the energy needed to break apart one mole of a solid ionic compound into its separate gaseous ions. Energy must be supplied to pull the oppositely charged ions apart, so it is always endothermic. Stronger attractions in the crystal mean a higher lattice enthalpy.', wrong = '{"0": "The overall energy change when a solid dissolves in water is the enthalpy of solution.", "1": "The energy released when gaseous ions are surrounded by water is the hydration enthalpy.", "2": "The energy released when a substance burns in oxygen is the enthalpy of combustion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0732' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Down Group 2 both the hydration enthalpy of M²⁺ and the lattice enthalpy decrease. OH⁻ is a small ion, so as the cation grows the lattice enthalpy falls by more than the hydration enthalpy does. Dissolving becomes more favourable and the hydroxides become more soluble, from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂.', wrong = '{"1": "When hydration enthalpy falls more, solubility decreases. That is the explanation for the sulphates, not the hydroxides.", "2": "Hydration enthalpy becomes less negative down the group, because the ions get larger.", "3": "The anion is OH⁻ for every member, so its size does not change down the group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0733' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SO₄²⁻ is a large ion, so increasing the size of M²⁺ changes the lattice enthalpy only a little. The hydration enthalpy of M²⁺, however, falls a lot as the cation grows. Less energy is released on hydration, so dissolving becomes less favourable: MgSO₄ is soluble while BaSO₄ is insoluble.', wrong = '{"0": "If lattice enthalpy fell more, solubility would increase. That is the pattern for hydroxides, not sulphates.", "1": "The anion is the same SO₄²⁻ ion for every member, so it does not change size down the group.", "3": "Lattice enthalpy decreases slightly down the group as the cation grows. It does not rise sharply."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0734' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Divide the two solubilities: (1.5 × 10⁻²) ÷ (2 × 10⁻⁵) = (1.5 ÷ 2) × 10⁻²⁻⁽⁻⁵⁾ = 0.75 × 10³ = 750. So Ba(OH)₂ is about 750 times more soluble than Mg(OH)₂, showing how much hydroxide solubility rises down the group.', wrong = '{"0": "75 is ten times too small. It comes from a slip with the power of ten, using 10² instead of 10³.", "1": "3.0 is just the difference between the exponents, −2 − (−5) = 3. The ratio is 0.75 × 10³, not 3.", "2": "7500 is ten times too large. It comes from a slip with the power of ten, using 10⁴ instead of 10³."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0735' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a complex ion, ligands such as H₂O, NH₃, Cl⁻ and OH⁻ donate lone pairs of electrons to the central metal ion and form coordinate bonds. An electron pair acceptor is a Lewis acid, so the metal ion acts as a Lewis acid and the ligands as Lewis bases.', wrong = '{"0": "The ligands donate the electron pairs. They are the Lewis bases, not the metal ion.", "2": "Complex formation involves sharing electron pairs, not transferring protons, so the Brønsted idea does not apply.", "3": "Forming coordinate bonds does not change the metal''s oxidation state, so it is not acting as a reducing agent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0736' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The coordination number is the number of coordinate bonds from ligands to the central ion. In [Be(H₂O)₄]²⁺ four water molecules each donate one lone pair to Be²⁺, so the coordination number is 4. The tiny Be²⁺ ion has room for only four ligands, while the larger Mg²⁺ forms [Mg(H₂O)₆]²⁺ with six.', wrong = '{"1": "A coordination number of 2 would mean only two water molecules bonded to Be²⁺. The formula shows four.", "2": "Six is the coordination number of the larger Mg²⁺ ion in [Mg(H₂O)₆]²⁺, not of Be²⁺.", "3": "Eight would need eight ligands. Be²⁺ is far too small for that, and the formula shows four."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0737' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hard water contains Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions. EDTA can donate six electron pairs and wraps around one metal ion in a ring-like complex: Ca²⁺ + EDTA⁴⁻ → [Ca(EDTA)]²⁻. The ions are held tightly in solution and can no longer cause hardness.', wrong = '{"0": "Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ are not oxidized. They keep their 2+ charge inside the complex.", "2": "EDTA forms soluble complexes. It does not precipitate calcium as carbonate.", "3": "Softening by EDTA works by binding Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ in complexes, not by releasing Na⁺."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0738' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Seawater contains only a low concentration of Mg²⁺ ions. Adding calcium hydroxide (made from limestone or oyster shells) precipitates insoluble magnesium hydroxide: Mg²⁺ + Ca(OH)₂ → Mg(OH)₂ + Ca²⁺. The Mg(OH)₂ is then filtered off and used in the next steps.', wrong = '{"0": "MgCl₂ is made in a later step, when the filtered Mg(OH)₂ is treated with hydrochloric acid.", "1": "Carbon is not used. Magnesium metal is obtained at the end by electrolysis of molten MgCl₂.", "3": "CO₂ is not used in the Dow process. Magnesium is precipitated as the hydroxide."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0739' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In electrolysis, cations move to the negative cathode and are reduced, while anions move to the positive anode and are oxidized. So Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mg at the cathode, and 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻ at the anode.', wrong = '{"0": "The electrodes are swapped. Positive Mg²⁺ ions go to the cathode, and Cl⁻ ions go to the anode.", "1": "Electrolysis of molten MgCl₂ splits it into its elements. No oxygen or hydrogen is present to form MgO or HCl.", "2": "Molten MgCl₂ contains no water, so neither H₂ nor Mg(OH)₂ can form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0740' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'CaCO₃ ⇌ CaO + CO₂ is reversible in a closed container. By Le Chatelier''s principle, removing a product pulls the equilibrium to the right. Continuously removing CO₂ therefore keeps the limestone decomposing and gives a large yield of quicklime (CaO).', wrong = '{"0": "Removing the gas is done to shift the equilibrium. The kiln is kept at 900 to 1000 °C, not cooled.", "2": "Removing CO₂ drives the forward reaction on. It does not stop it.", "3": "CaO would turn back into CaCO₃ only if CO₂ were kept in, not removed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0741' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gypsum is calcium sulphate dihydrate, CaSO₄·2H₂O. When it is heated, it loses most of its water and forms the hemihydrate, CaSO₄·½H₂O, which is plaster of Paris. On mixing with water it sets hard again.', wrong = '{"0": "CaSO₄·2H₂O, with two waters, is gypsum, the starting material for plaster of Paris.", "2": "MgSO₄·7H₂O is Epsom salt, used as a laxative and in baths.", "3": "CaCO₃ is limestone, marble or chalk. It contains no sulphate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0742' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Barium sulphate is opaque to X-rays, so a barium meal outlines the digestive system on an X-ray image. Soluble barium compounds are poisonous, but BaSO₄ is so insoluble that almost no Ba²⁺ dissolves. It passes through the body without being absorbed, so it is safe.', wrong = '{"1": "If it dissolved and were absorbed, toxic Ba²⁺ ions would enter the blood. Its safety comes from being insoluble.", "2": "BaSO₄ is useful because it blocks X-rays (it is opaque). A transparent substance would not show up on the image.", "3": "BaSO₄ is a neutral salt and does not neutralize acid. Antacids include Mg(OH)₂ and CaCO₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0743' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Milk of magnesia is a suspension of magnesium hydroxide, Mg(OH)₂. As a weak base it neutralizes excess stomach acid, and it also works as a mild laxative. Its low solubility makes it safe, because it does not make the stomach too alkaline.', wrong = '{"0": "MgSO₄ is magnesium sulphate. As Epsom salt (MgSO₄·7H₂O) it is a laxative, but it is not milk of magnesia and is not basic.", "2": "MgO is magnesium oxide, used as a refractory lining for furnaces because of its high melting point.", "3": "MgCO₃ is magnesium carbonate. It is not the compound called milk of magnesia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0744' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Power plants that burn fuels containing sulphur release SO₂, an acidic gas that causes acid rain. CaO and CaCO₃ are basic, so in wet scrubbing they react with SO₂ and remove it from the flue gases before they reach the air. This is called flue gas desulphurization.', wrong = '{"0": "CaO and CaCO₃ do not produce oxygen. They are bases used to trap an acidic gas.", "2": "CO from car exhausts is removed by catalytic converters, not by flue gas desulphurization in power plants.", "3": "Their use here is on flue gases from power plants, removing SO₂. It is not a drinking-water treatment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0745' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Beryllium has very low atomic number and few electrons, so it absorbs very little X-radiation. Being transparent to X-rays, it is used as the window of X-ray tubes, letting the rays pass out while keeping the tube sealed.', wrong = '{"0": "An opaque window would block the X-rays from leaving the tube. Opaque substances like BaSO₄ are used for imaging, not windows.", "2": "Glowing red when heated is not why beryllium is used. Its value lies in letting X-rays pass through.", "3": "Solubility has nothing to do with a solid window in an X-ray tube."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0746' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Calcium hydroxide is a base. It neutralizes the acid in soil, which raises the pH to a level where crops grow better. Calcium carbonate is used in the same way, and both also supply calcium, an essential plant nutrient.', wrong = '{"1": "A base raises pH. Adding Ca(OH)₂ makes soil less acidic, not more.", "2": "Ca(OH)₂ contains no nitrogen. Nitrogen comes from fertilizers such as calcium nitrate or urea.", "3": "Liming is done to control soil pH, not to sterilize the soil. Soil bacteria are useful to crops."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0747' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A transition element must form at least one stable ion with a partly filled d subshell. Zinc is [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s², and its only ion, Zn²⁺, is [Ar] 3d¹⁰, a completely filled d subshell. So zinc is a d-block element but not a transition element.', wrong = '{"0": "Zinc does have d electrons: ten in its 3d subshell. The problem is that the subshell is full.", "1": "Zn²⁺ compounds are usually white, because a full 3d subshell cannot absorb visible light by d–d transitions.", "3": "Zinc forms only one ion, Zn²⁺. It does not show variable oxidation states."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0748' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Most first-row transition elements fill 4s before 3d as the Aufbau order predicts. Chromium is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ and copper is [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ instead, because a half-filled d⁵ or a completely filled d¹⁰ subshell is extra stable and more symmetrical.', wrong = '{"0": "Mn is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s² and Ni is [Ar] 3d⁸ 4s². Both follow the normal Aufbau order.", "1": "Fe is [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s² and Co is [Ar] 3d⁷ 4s². Both follow the normal Aufbau order.", "2": "Ti is [Ar] 3d² 4s² and V is [Ar] 3d³ 4s². Both follow the normal Aufbau order."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0749' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Iron is [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s². When a transition metal forms ions, the 4s electrons are lost first, then 3d electrons. Fe³⁺ loses both 4s electrons and one 3d electron, giving [Ar] 3d⁵, a stable half-filled d subshell.', wrong = '{"0": "[Ar] 3d⁶ has lost only the two 4s electrons. That is Fe²⁺, not Fe³⁺.", "1": "This removes three 3d electrons and keeps 4s². In reality the 4s electrons are removed first.", "3": "Fe³⁺ keeps no 4s electrons, because the 4s electrons go before any 3d electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0750' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'There are five d orbitals. By Hund''s rule the first five electrons occupy them singly, and the remaining two pair up in two of the orbitals. That leaves 5 − 2 = 3 orbitals with one unpaired electron each, so Co²⁺ (3d⁷) has 3 unpaired electrons.', wrong = '{"1": "One unpaired electron fits a d⁹ ion such as Cu²⁺, where four orbitals are full.", "2": "Five unpaired electrons fit a d⁵ ion such as Mn²⁺ or Fe³⁺, where every orbital holds one electron.", "3": "Only five d orbitals exist, so at most five electrons can be unpaired. Seven electrons must include two pairs."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0751' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Paramagnetic substances are weakly attracted into a magnetic field. This happens when atoms or ions have unpaired electrons, whose spins act like tiny magnets. Most first-row transition metals and their compounds have partly filled d subshells with unpaired electrons, so they are paramagnetic.', wrong = '{"0": "Density has no link with magnetism. Many dense metals are not paramagnetic.", "1": "A full d subshell has all its electrons paired, which gives diamagnetism, not paramagnetism.", "2": "Good heat conduction comes from mobile electrons in the metal. It does not explain attraction to a magnet."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0752' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From titanium to copper, each element adds one proton and one d electron. The growing nuclear charge pulls the electrons in, so the atoms shrink slightly while their mass increases. More mass packed into a smaller volume means the density generally increases across the row.', wrong = '{"0": "Atoms get smaller, not larger, across the row, from about 147 pm for Ti to about 124 pm for Ni.", "1": "Density does change. It rises from about 4.5 g cm⁻³ for titanium to about 8.9 g cm⁻³ for copper.", "3": "The rise in density is a general trend across the whole row, not limited to Cr and Cu."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0753' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First-row transition metals are generally silvery or grey lustrous solids. Copper is the clear exception: it has a reddish-brown colour, which is why it is easy to recognise in wires and coins.', wrong = '{"0": "Iron is a grey lustrous metal when clean. Its reddish-brown colour appears only as rust, Fe₂O₃.", "1": "Titanium is a silvery-grey lustrous metal, not reddish-brown.", "2": "Nickel is a silvery lustrous metal, used for plating because of its shine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0754' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Manganese is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s². It can use all seven of these electrons in bonding, the two 4s and five 3d, which gives the +7 state found in the permanganate ion, MnO₄⁻. This is the highest oxidation state in the first transition row.', wrong = '{"0": "Chromium ([Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹) has six outer electrons, so its highest state is +6, as in CrO₄²⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻.", "1": "Iron''s common states are +2 and +3, and its rare highest state is only +6.", "3": "Vanadium ([Ar] 3d³ 4s²) has five outer electrons, so its highest state is +5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0755' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In first-row transition metals the 4s electrons are lost first. Most of these atoms have two 4s electrons, so losing only these gives M²⁺, the common +2 oxidation state. Losing 3d electrons as well gives higher states such as +3.', wrong = '{"0": "+1 would need just one electron lost. That happens mainly for copper, which has a single 4s electron.", "1": "The highest states also need the 3d electrons to be lost, not just the 4s pair.", "2": "+3 needs one 3d electron to be lost after the two 4s electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0756' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In heterogeneous catalysis the catalyst is in a different phase from the reactants. In the Haber process, solid iron catalyses the reaction of gaseous N₂ and H₂: the gases adsorb on the iron surface, react and the NH₃ formed desorbs. So solid iron is a heterogeneous catalyst.', wrong = '{"0": "Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺ ions in the same solution as the reactants act as a homogeneous catalyst.", "1": "Nickel salts used to make acrylic acid from acetylene work through Ni(0)/Ni(II) changes, which is homogeneous catalysis.", "3": "EDTA complexes of copper are not catalysts. EDTA is a ligand that binds metal ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0757' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A transition metal ion can switch easily between stable oxidation states, such as Fe²⁺ ⇌ Fe³⁺. It reacts with a reactant to form an unstable intermediate, which then gives the product and returns the ion to its original state. This new route has a lower activation energy.', wrong = '{"0": "A catalyst does not work by precipitating the products. It is regenerated unchanged at the end.", "1": "Density has nothing to do with catalytic activity. What matters is the ability to change oxidation state.", "2": "The key is moving between oxidation states. An ion stuck in its highest state could not cycle back and forth."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0758' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A ligand is a molecule or ion with at least one lone pair of electrons. It donates that pair to the central metal atom or ion, forming a coordinate (dative) covalent bond. Because it donates an electron pair, a ligand acts as a Lewis base.', wrong = '{"0": "This is backwards. The metal ion accepts the lone pair, and the ligand donates it.", "1": "Ligands can be neutral, such as H₂O, NH₃ and CO, and a few are even positive, such as NO⁺.", "3": "Ligands are electron-pair donors such as H₂O, NH₃ and Cl⁻. The metal is the central atom, not a ligand."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0759' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neutral ligands usually keep their ordinary names, but a few have special names in complexes: H₂O is aqua, NH₃ is ammine (with a double m), and CO is carbonyl. So NH₃ is called ammine, as in [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, hexaamminecobalt(III).', wrong = '{"0": "Amine (single m) is an organic functional group with an –NH₂ group on carbon. It is not the ligand name for NH₃.", "1": "Aqua is the name used for a water ligand, H₂O.", "2": "Amido is the name for the NH₂⁻ ion, not the neutral NH₃ molecule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0760' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A bidentate ligand donates two lone pairs to the same metal ion. In 1,2-diaminoethane, H₂NCH₂CH₂NH₂ (en), each of the two nitrogen atoms donates one lone pair, so it forms two dative bonds and is bidentate.', wrong = '{"0": "NH₃ has only one nitrogen lone pair to donate, so it is monodentate.", "1": "Cl⁻ forms just one dative bond to the metal, so it is monodentate.", "3": "EDTA uses six donor atoms, so it is hexadentate, not bidentate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0761' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'EDTA has two nitrogen atoms, each with a lone pair, and four carboxylate groups, each of which donates through one oxygen atom. So its six donor atoms are four oxygens and two nitrogens, which let it wrap around a single metal ion.', wrong = '{"0": "Not all six donors are oxygen. Two of them are the nitrogen atoms of the amine groups.", "1": "EDTA contains only two nitrogen atoms, so it cannot donate through four.", "3": "EDTA has only two nitrogens. Four of its donor atoms are carboxylate oxygens."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0762' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The charge of a complex equals the metal''s charge plus the total ligand charge. Each cyanide is CN⁻, so with x as iron''s oxidation state: x + 6(−1) = −4, giving x = +2. This complex is hexacyanoferrate(II).', wrong = '{"0": "With iron at +3 the charge would be +3 − 6 = −3, giving [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻, not the 4− ion.", "2": "With iron at +4 the charge would be +4 − 6 = −2, not −4.", "3": "With iron at +6 the charge would be +6 − 6 = 0, a neutral complex."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0763' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The complex charge equals the metal charge plus the ligand charges. Water is neutral, so the charge is (+3) + 6 × (0) = +3. Writing the metal first and then the six ligands inside square brackets gives [Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺.', wrong = '{"0": "This has only four water ligands. The stem gives six, matching a coordination number of 6.", "1": "Neutral ligands add no charge, so they cannot turn the metal''s +3 into a negative charge.", "2": "The Cr³⁺ charge is not cancelled by neutral water ligands, so the complex is not neutral."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0764' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A complex is neutral when the metal charge and ligand charges add to zero. In [Ni(CO)₄], nickel is in the 0 oxidation state and each carbonyl ligand (CO) is neutral, so the charge is 0 + 4 × 0 = 0.', wrong = '{"0": "[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ has Co³⁺ and neutral NH₃, so its charge is +3. It is a cationic complex.", "1": "[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ has Fe²⁺ and six CN⁻, so its charge is +2 − 6 = −4. It is an anionic complex.", "3": "[CuCl₄]²⁻ has Cu²⁺ and four Cl⁻, so its charge is +2 − 4 = −2. It is anionic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0765' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ has two ligands around the silver ion. Two ligands repel each other least when they sit on opposite sides of the metal, so the complex is linear with a bond angle of 180°. K[Ag(CN)₂] is another linear example.', wrong = '{"1": "Square planar geometry needs four ligands in one plane at 90°.", "2": "Tetrahedral geometry needs four ligands at 109.5°.", "3": "Octahedral geometry needs six ligands along the x, y and z axes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0766' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Square planar complexes have four ligands at the corners of a square, at 90° to each other, all in one plane with the metal. This shape is typical of d⁸ ions such as Ni²⁺ and Pt²⁺ with strong ligands like CN⁻ and NH₃, for example [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ and [Pt(NH₃)₄]²⁺.', wrong = '{"0": "Four-coordinate d¹⁰ ions such as Zn²⁺ usually form tetrahedral complexes.", "2": "d⁰ ions such as Ti⁴⁺ are not known for square planar complexes.", "3": "Four-coordinate d⁵ ions such as Mn²⁺ form tetrahedral complexes, for example [MnCl₄]²⁻."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0767' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Most transition metal complexes have six ligands arranged along the x, y and z axes. Four lie in a square plane at 90°, and the other two sit above and below it. This octahedral shape is the most common, for example [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ and [Co(en)₃]³⁺.', wrong = '{"0": "Linear complexes have only two ligands, as in [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺. They are relatively uncommon.", "1": "Square planar shapes are found mainly with d⁸ ions such as Ni²⁺ and Pt²⁺.", "2": "Tetrahedral complexes, such as [CoCl₄]²⁻, occur for some ions but are less common than octahedral ones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0768' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an octahedral complex the six ligands approach along the x, y and z axes. The dx²−y² and dz² orbitals point straight at the ligands, so they are repelled most and rise in energy. dxy, dxz and dyz point between the axes and stay lower. So the split is three lower and two higher.', wrong = '{"1": "Two lower and three higher is the tetrahedral pattern, which is the inverse of the octahedral one.", "2": "Five orbitals of equal energy (degenerate) is the situation in an isolated ion, before ligands approach.", "3": "The d orbitals split into a set of three and a set of two, not one and four."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0769' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a tetrahedral complex the four ligands approach between the axes, so the pattern flips: dxy, dxz and dyz rise to higher energy and dx²−y² and dz² are lower. Fewer ligands, none pointing straight at any orbital, also give a smaller splitting than in octahedral complexes.', wrong = '{"1": "The pattern is not the same. It is inverted, with three orbitals higher, and the splitting is smaller, not larger.", "2": "The pattern is inverted, but the splitting is smaller than octahedral, not larger. Only four ligands create the field.", "3": "The d orbitals do split in a tetrahedral field. The gap is just smaller than in an octahedral complex."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0770' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A complex looks the colour of the light it does not absorb. On the colour wheel, the colour seen is the complementary colour, directly opposite the one absorbed. Yellow and violet are opposite each other, so a complex that absorbs yellow light appears violet.', wrong = '{"0": "Yellow is the colour being absorbed and removed from white light, so it is not the colour seen.", "2": "Green is seen when red light is absorbed, since red and green are complementary.", "3": "Red is seen when green light is absorbed, since green and red are complementary."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0771' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ligands can be ranked by how strongly they split the d orbitals: CN⁻ > en > NH₃ > H₂O > OH⁻ > Cl⁻ > Br⁻ > I⁻. CN⁻ is above H₂O, so swapping H₂O for CN⁻ increases ΔE. Since ΔE = hν = hc/λ, a bigger gap means light of higher frequency and shorter wavelength is absorbed.', wrong = '{"1": "This is the reverse. CN⁻ is a stronger-field ligand than H₂O, so ΔE goes up, not down.", "2": "Changing the ligand changes the size of the splitting, so ΔE and the colour both change.", "3": "Ligands cause the splitting in the first place. CN⁻ makes it larger, it does not remove it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0772' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ligands arranged from largest to smallest splitting are: CN⁻ > en > NH₃ > H₂O > OH⁻ > Cl⁻ > Br⁻ > I⁻. Iodide is at the end of this series, so it gives the smallest ΔE of the ligands listed.', wrong = '{"0": "CN⁻ is at the top of the series. It causes the largest splitting, not the smallest.", "1": "NH₃ is near the top of the series and splits the d orbitals more than water does.", "2": "H₂O is in the middle of the series. Halide ions such as I⁻ cause smaller splitting."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0773' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Blue copper(II) sulphate solution contains [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺. With concentrated HCl, chloride ions replace the water ligands: [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ → [CuCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O. The new ligands change ΔE, so a different colour is absorbed and the solution turns yellow(-green).', wrong = '{"0": "Copper stays in the +2 state in [CuCl₄]²⁻. Ligand exchange does not change the oxidation state.", "2": "Copper(II) hydroxide precipitates when OH⁻ is added, for example from NaOH. HCl is an acid and supplies Cl⁻, not OH⁻.", "3": "There is no ammonia in copper sulphate solution, so no ammonia ligands can be lost."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0774' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'NaOH replaces two water ligands, giving the light blue precipitate [Cu(H₂O)₄(OH)₂]. Concentrated ammonia then replaces the hydroxide and some water ligands, forming the soluble complex [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺, so the precipitate dissolves to give a dark blue solution.', wrong = '{"0": "The copper–ammine complex absorbs strongly in the visible region, so it is dark blue, not colourless.", "2": "[CuCl₄]²⁻ forms when concentrated HCl supplies Cl⁻ ions. Ammonia gives an ammine complex instead.", "3": "Pink is the colour of aqueous cobalt(II), [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺, not of any copper ammine complex."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0775' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pink aqueous cobalt(II) is [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺. Concentrated HCl supplies many Cl⁻ ions, which replace the water: [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ → [CoCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O. Chloride ions are large, so only four fit around Co²⁺, giving a blue tetrahedral complex.', wrong = '{"0": "[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is the pink octahedral starting complex, not the blue product.", "2": "[Co(NH₃)₆]²⁺ forms when ammonia is added, and its solution is yellow. No ammonia is present here.", "3": "Chloride ions are too large for six to fit around Co²⁺. The product has four Cl⁻ ligands in a tetrahedral shape."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0776' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kstab is the equilibrium constant for forming the complex: products over reactants, each concentration raised to its coefficient. Water is the solvent, so its concentration is effectively constant and left out. This gives Kstab = [CuCl₄²⁻] / ([Cu(H₂O)₆²⁺][Cl⁻]⁴).', wrong = '{"0": "This includes [H₂O]⁶. Water is the solvent, so its concentration stays constant and is left out of the expression.", "2": "This is upside down. It is the reciprocal of Kstab, the constant for the complex breaking apart.", "3": "Four chloride ions appear in the equation, so [Cl⁻] must be raised to the power 4, not 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0777' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A larger log Kstab means a more stable complex. [Cu(EDTA)]²⁻ has 18.8 against 5.6 for [CuCl₄]²⁻, so it is far more stable, and EDTA can displace chloride from copper. Polydentate ligands like EDTA form especially stable complexes: this is the chelate effect.', wrong = '{"0": "[CuCl₄]²⁻ has the lower log Kstab, 5.6, so it is the less stable complex.", "2": "Chloride forms the less stable complex, so it cannot displace EDTA from copper under normal conditions.", "3": "The values differ by 18.8 − 5.6 = 13.2 log units, a factor of about 10¹³ in Kstab, so they are not equally stable."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0778' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cisplatin, cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂], is an important anticancer drug. Inside cells its chloride ligands are replaced by water, and the platinum then binds to DNA, preventing cancer cells from copying their DNA. Its trans isomer, transplatin, does not have this activity.', wrong = '{"1": "Antacids are bases such as Mg(OH)₂ and CaCO₃. Cisplatin is not used to neutralize stomach acid.", "2": "Water is softened with reagents such as EDTA, not with platinum complexes.", "3": "Cisplatin is a medicine. Fertilizers supply nutrients such as nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0779' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both isomers are square planar [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]. In transplatin, the two Cl ligands are at 180° to each other and so are the two NH₃ ligands, so the bond dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel. In cisplatin, the identical ligands are at 90°, so a net dipole remains and it is polar.', wrong = '{"0": "Both isomers have the same formula, with two Cl and two NH₃ ligands. They differ only in arrangement.", "2": "Cisplatin is square planar, not tetrahedral. Both isomers share the same square planar shape.", "3": "Transplatin has only four ligands, so it is square planar, not octahedral."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0780' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Optical isomers are non-superimposable mirror images. Octahedral complexes with bidentate ligands, such as [Ni(en)₃]²⁺ with three 1,2-diaminoethane ligands, form a propeller-like shape that has no plane of symmetry. Its two mirror-image forms cannot be superimposed, so it shows optical isomerism.', wrong = '{"1": "A complex with six identical monodentate ligands has planes of symmetry, so its mirror image is the same and it is not chiral.", "2": "A linear complex lies along a single line and has planes of symmetry, so it cannot be chiral.", "3": "A square planar complex has its own plane of symmetry. The common optical isomers are octahedral."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0781' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Feasibility of a redox reaction is judged from E°cell = E°(reduction) − E°(oxidation). If E°cell is positive, the reaction can take place as written. The half-reaction with the more positive E° goes as a reduction, and the other goes as an oxidation.', wrong = '{"1": "A negative E°cell means the reaction is not feasible as written. The reverse reaction would be feasible instead.", "2": "What matters is the difference between the two E° values. Both can be negative and still give a positive E°cell.", "3": "The oxidizing agent is the species that is reduced, and that is the one with the higher (more positive) E°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0782' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The couple with the more positive E° is reduced. MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺ (+1.52 V) is higher than Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ (+0.77 V), so MnO₄⁻ is reduced to Mn²⁺ and Fe²⁺ is oxidized to Fe³⁺. E°cell = 1.52 − 0.77 = +0.75 V, which is positive, so the reaction is feasible.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse reaction. It would have E°cell = 0.77 − 1.52 = −0.75 V, so it is not feasible.", "2": "MnO₄⁻ gains electrons and is reduced, so it is the oxidizing agent. Fe²⁺ is the reducing agent.", "3": "E°cell is +0.75 V, a positive value, so the reaction is feasible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0783' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From E° values alone, I₂/I⁻ (+0.54 V) is higher than Cu²⁺/Cu⁺ (+0.15 V), so E°cell for the reaction is 0.15 − 0.54 = −0.39 V. But Cu⁺ forms CuI, a white precipitate, and leaves the solution. By Le Chatelier''s principle, removing this product pulls the equilibrium to the right, so I₂ forms.', wrong = '{"1": "Iodine is the product, formed when iodide ions are oxidized. Iodide, not iodine, is the reducing agent here.", "2": "Copper(II) is reduced only to copper(I), which appears in the white CuI precipitate. No copper metal forms.", "3": "K⁺ ions are spectator ions. They take no part in the reaction and do not catalyse it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0784' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Catenation (from the Latin catena, meaning chain) is the ability of atoms of the same element to link to each other. Carbon forms very strong, stable C–C bonds, so it can build long chains, branches and rings. This is why there are millions of organic compounds.', wrong = '{"1": "Breaking C–C bonds by heat is cracking (thermal cleavage), the opposite of linking atoms together.", "2": "Catenation means atoms of the same element bonding to each other. C–H bonds are not self-linkage.", "3": "Carbon forms covalent bonds when it catenates. C–C bonds are shared electron pairs, not ionic bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0785' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Aliphatic comes from the Greek word aleiphatos, meaning fat. Fats contain long open chains of carbon atoms, so open-chain (acyclic) hydrocarbons such as alkanes, alkenes and alkynes are called aliphatic.', wrong = '{"0": "Aliphatic compounds are defined by open chains. Ring compounds are called cyclic.", "1": "Chain is the meaning of the Latin word catena, which gives the term catenation.", "2": "Fragrant is linked to the word aromatic, used for benzene-type compounds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0786' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cyclohexane, C₆H₁₂, is a ring of six carbon atoms joined by single bonds. Alicyclic hydrocarbons are cyclic compounds that behave like aliphatic ones, such as cycloalkanes and cycloalkenes. Cyclohexane reacts much like hexane, so it is alicyclic.', wrong = '{"0": "Aromatic hydrocarbons contain a benzene ring or a similar structure. Cyclohexane has no such ring of delocalized electrons.", "1": "A heterocyclic ring contains at least one atom other than carbon, such as N or O. Cyclohexane''s ring is all carbon.", "2": "Cyclohexane is a closed ring with only single bonds. It is neither open-chain nor an alkene."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0787' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alcohols contain the hydroxyl (–OH) functional group. The H on oxygen can form hydrogen bonds with neighbouring alcohol molecules, which take extra energy to overcome. This raises melting and boiling points, so methanol and ethanol are liquids, while methane and ethane have no such group and are gases.', wrong = '{"0": "Methanol and ethanol contain only single bonds. They have no double bond.", "1": "Methane (16 g mol⁻¹) is lighter than methanol (32 g mol⁻¹), not heavier. The key factor is hydrogen bonding.", "2": "Alcohols are covalent molecules, not ionic compounds. Their higher boiling points come from hydrogen bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0788' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A functional group is an atom, group of atoms, or a double or triple bond that gives a compound its typical reactions. Alkanes and cycloalkanes contain only C–C and C–H single bonds, with no charges, polar groups or multiple bonds, so they have no functional group.', wrong = '{"0": "The C=C double bond is the functional group of alkenes.", "1": "Carboxylic acids contain the –COOH (carboxyl) functional group.", "3": "Alcohols contain the –OH (hydroxyl) functional group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0789' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An IUPAC name has a stem (number of carbons), a primary suffix, a secondary suffix and a prefix. The primary suffix (suffix of the stem) tells whether the parent chain is saturated or unsaturated: -an for single bonds only, -en for a C=C, -yn for a C≡C.', wrong = '{"0": "The family name, such as -ol or -al, comes from the secondary suffix, the suffix of the functional group.", "1": "The number of carbon atoms in the parent chain is given by the stem or root, such as meth-, eth- or prop-.", "2": "Side chains and substituents are named in the prefix, together with their positions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0790' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CHO, the longest chain containing the CHO carbon has four carbons, so the parent is butanal. Numbering starts from the CHO carbon (C-1): CH₂ is C-2 and the CH carrying the extra methyl is C-3. So the name is 3-methylbutanal.', wrong = '{"0": "2-Methylbutanal is CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CHO, with the methyl next to the CHO carbon. Here the methyl is on C-3.", "1": "The C=O is at the end of the chain with an H attached, so this is an aldehyde (-al), not a ketone (-one).", "3": "Pentanal is the unbranched CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CHO. This compound has five carbons but a branched chain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0791' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Esters are named alkyl alkanoate. The alkyl group joined to the single-bonded oxygen comes first: here –CH₂CH₃, ethyl. The acid part, CH₃COO–, comes from ethanoic acid and is called ethanoate. So CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ is ethyl ethanoate.', wrong = '{"0": "Propyl methanoate is HCOOCH₂CH₂CH₃, made from methanoic acid and propanol. It is an isomer, not this compound.", "1": "Methyl propanoate is CH₃CH₂COOCH₃, made from propanoic acid and methanol. It is an isomer, not this compound.", "2": "Ethoxyethanone is not a valid IUPAC name for an ester. Esters are named as alkyl alkanoates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0792' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an ether, R–O–R′, the smaller group together with the oxygen is named as an alkoxy prefix, and the larger group is the parent alkane. So ethers are named alkoxyalkanes, for example CH₃OCH₂CH₃ is methoxyethane.', wrong = '{"0": "Alkanol (ending -ol) is the IUPAC name pattern for alcohols.", "1": "Alkanal (ending -al) is the IUPAC name pattern for aldehydes.", "3": "Alkanone (ending -one) is the IUPAC name pattern for ketones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0793' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'CH₃CH₂CH₂NHCH₃ has two carbon groups on nitrogen, so it is a secondary amine. The longer group, propyl, gives the parent propan-1-amine, with N on C-1. The methyl on nitrogen is shown with the locant N, giving N-methylpropan-1-amine.', wrong = '{"0": "The parent must be the longer carbon chain, propyl, not methyl. So the name is built on propanamine, not methanamine.", "1": "Butan-1-amine is CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂, a primary amine with all four carbons in one chain.", "2": "Propan-2-amine is (CH₃)₂CHNH₂, a primary amine with NH₂ on the middle carbon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0794' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alkyl cyanides are named as alkanenitriles, and the carbon of the –C≡N group is counted in the chain. CH₃–C≡N has two carbons, so its parent is ethane and the IUPAC name is ethanenitrile. Its common name is methyl cyanide.', wrong = '{"0": "Methyl cyanide is the common name for CH₃CN. The question asks for the IUPAC name, ethanenitrile.", "1": "Ethanamine is CH₃CH₂NH₂, an amine with an –NH₂ group, not a nitrile.", "3": "Methanenitrile would have only one carbon, HCN. The nitrile carbon is counted, so CH₃CN has two carbons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0795' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH, and methoxymethane, CH₃OCH₃, each contain 2 C, 6 H and 1 O, so both have the molecular formula C₂H₆O. A molecular formula shows only how many atoms of each kind are present, not how they are joined, so different compounds (isomers) can share it.', wrong = '{"0": "C₂H₄O has two fewer hydrogens. It is the formula of ethanal, CH₃CHO.", "1": "C₂H₆O₂ has two oxygens. It is the formula of ethane-1,2-diol, a diol.", "2": "CH₄O has only one carbon. It is methanol, CH₃OH."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0796' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The empirical formula shows the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms. Butane is C₄H₁₀, and both 4 and 10 can be divided by 2, giving C₂H₅. Since 2 and 5 share no further common factor, C₂H₅ is the empirical formula.', wrong = '{"0": "CH₃ is the empirical formula of ethane, C₂H₆, not butane.", "1": "CH₂ is the empirical formula of alkenes and cycloalkanes, whose formulae are CₙH₂ₙ.", "3": "C₄H₁₀ is the molecular formula. It can still be divided by 2, so it is not the simplest ratio."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0797' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A skeletal formula draws the carbon chain as zigzag lines. Each line end or junction is a carbon atom, and the C and H symbols and C–H bonds are left out, since each carbon is assumed to have enough H atoms to make four bonds. Double bonds, triple bonds and functional groups are still drawn.', wrong = '{"0": "Writing out every atom and bond is a displayed formula, not a skeletal formula.", "1": "The carbon skeleton is drawn with lines. Functional groups are shown on that skeleton, not on their own.", "2": "Double and triple bonds are always shown in skeletal formulae, as double or triple lines."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0798' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In homolytic fission (homolysis), a covalent bond breaks evenly and each atom keeps one electron of the shared pair. This gives two species with unpaired electrons, called free radicals, for example Cl₂ → 2Cl·. It happens when the breaking bond is non-polar, usually with heat or light.', wrong = '{"0": "When one atom keeps both electrons and ions form, the fission is heterolytic, not homolytic.", "1": "Homolysis happens with non-polar bonds. Polar bonds tend to break heterolytically.", "3": "Carbocations and carbanions are ions, which come from heterolytic fission."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0799' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a bond breaks heterolytically, the more electronegative atom keeps the electron pair. If carbon is bonded to a less electronegative metal such as Li or Mg, carbon keeps the pair and becomes negatively charged. A negatively charged carbon species is a carbanion.', wrong = '{"0": "With a more electronegative partner like O or N, the partner takes the electron pair, leaving carbon positive. That gives a carbocation.", "2": "Homolysis splits the pair evenly, giving free radicals, not charged carbanions.", "3": "Chlorine in sunlight splits by homolysis into chlorine radicals, not carbanions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0800' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electrophile means electron lover. An electrophile is electron-poor and accepts a pair of electrons from another species to form a new covalent bond. Electrophiles are positive ions such as H⁺ and NO₂⁺, or neutral molecules with an empty orbital, such as BF₃ and SO₃.', wrong = '{"0": "A species that donates an electron pair to form a bond is a nucleophile, the opposite of an electrophile.", "2": "A species with an unpaired electron is a free radical, which reacts by sharing single electrons.", "3": "Electrophiles are positive or neutral, because they need electrons. Negative ions are usually nucleophiles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0801' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A nucleophile (nucleus lover) donates a pair of electrons to form a new bond. NH₃ has a lone pair on its nitrogen atom, which it can donate to an electron-poor atom, so ammonia is a nucleophile.', wrong = '{"0": "BF₃ has an empty orbital on boron and accepts an electron pair, so it is an electrophile.", "2": "NO₂⁺, the nitronium ion, is positively charged and accepts electrons, so it is an electrophile.", "3": "CH₃⁺ is a carbocation with only six outer electrons on carbon, so it is an electrophile."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0802' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hydrogen adds across the C=C double bond: CH₂=CH₂ + H₂ → CH₃CH₃, with nickel as catalyst. Two reactants combine to give one product, so this is an addition reaction. Carbon gains hydrogen, so it is also a reduction (hydrogenation).', wrong = '{"0": "In substitution one atom or group replaces another. Here nothing is replaced: H₂ simply adds to the double bond.", "2": "Elimination removes atoms to form a double bond. Here a double bond is used up instead.", "3": "Hydrolysis is breaking a compound with water. No water is involved in this reaction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0803' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With alcoholic KOH, a hydrogen atom and the bromine atom are removed from neighbouring carbon atoms of bromoethane, and a C=C double bond forms: CH₃CH₂Br + KOH → CH₂=CH₂ + KBr + H₂O. Removing atoms to make a multiple bond is elimination, the reverse of addition.', wrong = '{"0": "Nucleophilic substitution happens with aqueous KOH, where OH⁻ replaces Br⁻ and ethanol forms instead of ethene.", "1": "Electrophilic addition uses up a double bond. Here a double bond is formed, so it is the opposite process.", "3": "Condensation joins two molecules into a larger one. Here one molecule loses HBr and becomes smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0804' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Propanone and hydroxylamine join to form one larger molecule, propanone oxime, and a small molecule, water, is lost: (CH₃)₂C=O + H₂NOH → (CH₃)₂C=NOH + H₂O. Two molecules combining with loss of a small molecule is a condensation reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "Hydrolysis uses water to split a compound. Here water is formed, not used.", "2": "Water is eliminated, but two separate molecules also join into one. That overall change is condensation, not just elimination.", "3": "Free radical substitution involves radicals, as in methane with chlorine in light. No radicals take part here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0805' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In diffused sunlight, Cl₂ splits homolytically into chlorine radicals (initiation). A Cl· takes an H from methane, and the CH₃· formed reacts with Cl₂ to give CH₃Cl and a new Cl· (propagation). Radicals finally combine (termination). This is a free radical substitution mechanism.', wrong = '{"0": "Nucleophilic addition happens at a polar C=O group, as in aldehydes and ketones. Methane has no such group.", "2": "The attacking species here are chlorine radicals, not nucleophiles with electron pairs.", "3": "Methane has only single bonds, so nothing can add to it. Addition needs a double or triple bond."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0806' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The C=O bond is polar, so the carbonyl carbon is δ+. The cyanide ion, a nucleophile, attacks this carbon and the C=O opens; the O⁻ then picks up H⁺. The two parts of HCN add across the C=O, so this is nucleophilic addition: CH₃CHO + HCN → CH₃CH(OH)CN.', wrong = '{"0": "Nothing is replaced in ethanal. HCN adds across the C=O bond, so it is addition, not substitution.", "1": "Electrophilic addition happens at electron-rich C=C bonds. Here the first attack is by the CN⁻ nucleophile on a δ+ carbon.", "2": "No free radicals are involved. The attacking species is the cyanide ion with its lone pair."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0807' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The C=C double bond is rich in electrons. The δ+ hydrogen of HBr acts as an electrophile and attacks it, forming a carbocation and Br⁻. Br⁻ then bonds to the carbocation, giving CH₃CH₂Br. The first step is attack by an electrophile, so this is electrophilic addition.', wrong = '{"1": "Nucleophilic addition happens at polar C=O groups. Alkenes are attacked first by electrophiles.", "2": "Two reactants combine into one product without anything being replaced, so this is addition, not substitution.", "3": "Elimination forms a double bond. Here the C=C double bond is used up."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0808' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Isomers are compounds with the same molecular formula but a different arrangement of atoms. Because their structures differ, they have different physical and often different chemical properties. Ethanol and methoxymethane, both C₂H₆O, are one example.', wrong = '{"0": "Isomers must have the same molecular formula. Compounds with different formulae are simply different compounds.", "2": "Compounds with the same structure and properties are the same compound, not isomers.", "3": "Sharing an empirical formula is not enough. C₂H₄ and C₄H₈ are both CH₂ but have different molecular formulae."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0809' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A chiral carbon (asymmetric carbon) is bonded to four different atoms or groups. Such a carbon has no plane of symmetry, so the molecule and its mirror image cannot be superimposed. This gives rise to a pair of optical isomers.', wrong = '{"0": "A carbon in a double bond is joined to only three groups, so it cannot have four different groups.", "2": "If two groups are the same, the carbon has a plane of symmetry and its mirror image is identical.", "3": "A benzene ring carbon is bonded to only three atoms and lies in a flat ring, so it is not chiral."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0810' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In lactic acid, CH₃CH(OH)COOH, the middle carbon is joined to a methyl group (CH₃), a hydrogen atom (H), a hydroxyl group (OH) and a carboxyl group (COOH). All four are different, so this carbon is chiral and lactic acid exists as two enantiomers.', wrong = '{"1": "The middle carbon has only one OH group. Its fourth group is H.", "2": "The middle carbon carries one H and one CH₃, not two hydrogens.", "3": "There is only one CH₃ group on the chiral carbon. The other group is COOH."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0811' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A racemic mixture contains equal amounts (50 : 50) of the two enantiomers. The (+) form rotates plane-polarized light one way and the (−) form rotates it equally the other way, so the effects cancel and the mixture is optically inactive. It is shown with the symbol (±).', wrong = '{"1": "A single enantiomer rotates plane-polarized light, so it is optically active. A racemic mixture has both forms.", "2": "A meso compound is a single compound that is inactive because of an internal plane of symmetry, not a mixture.", "3": "Cis and trans forms are geometrical isomers. A racemic mixture contains optical isomers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0812' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Meso-tartaric acid has two chiral carbons, but the molecule has an internal plane of symmetry. Its two halves are mirror images, so one half rotates plane-polarized light one way and the other half rotates it equally the other way. The rotations cancel within the molecule (internal compensation).', wrong = '{"1": "A racemic mixture is a mixture of two different molecules. Meso-tartaric acid is a single compound.", "2": "Each of its two central carbons does carry four different groups, so they are chiral centres.", "3": "Solubility does not decide optical activity. Meso-tartaric acid is inactive because of its symmetry."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0813' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each chiral carbon can have one of two arrangements. With n chiral carbons and no symmetry, the maximum number of optical isomers is 2ⁿ. Here n = 3, so 2³ = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8.', wrong = '{"1": "6 comes from 2 × 3, multiplying instead of raising 2 to the power 3.", "2": "3 is just the number of chiral carbons. Each one doubles the number of possible isomers.", "3": "9 comes from 3², swapping the base and the power. The formula is 2ⁿ, not n²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0814' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A C=C double bond has a σ bond plus a π bond made by side-by-side overlap of p orbitals. Rotation would twist the p orbitals apart and break the π bond, which needs about 263 kJ mol⁻¹. Ordinary room-temperature energy is far too small, so rotation is restricted.', wrong = '{"0": "The carbons of a C=C bond are sp² hybridized, not sp³. sp³ carbons have only single bonds and rotate freely.", "1": "The σ bond is strong and on its own allows rotation. It is the π bond that prevents rotation.", "3": "Alkenes are covalent molecules, not ionic compounds. Ionic character has nothing to do with rotation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0815' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cis-trans isomerism needs each carbon of the C=C bond to carry two different groups. In 2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene, (CH₃)₂C=C(CH₃)₂, each double-bond carbon has two identical CH₃ groups, so swapping them gives the same molecule. It cannot show cis-trans isomerism.', wrong = '{"1": "In pent-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃, one double-bond carbon has H and CH₃ and the other has H and C₂H₅, so cis and trans forms exist.", "2": "In but-2-ene each double-bond carbon carries H and CH₃, so it has cis and trans forms.", "3": "In 1,2-dichloroethene each double-bond carbon carries H and Cl, so it has cis and trans forms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0816' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a cis isomer, the larger groups are on the same side of the double bond and crowd each other. In the trans isomer they are on opposite sides, with less repulsion. So trans isomers are generally more stable than the corresponding cis isomers.', wrong = '{"1": "Rotating plane-polarized light in opposite directions is a property of enantiomers, not of cis-trans isomers.", "2": "Cis and trans isomers have different physical properties, such as melting point, boiling point, density and dipole moment.", "3": "They cannot interconvert at room temperature, because the π bond would have to break."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0817' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Butane''s C2–C3 bond is a single σ bond, so the two ends can rotate freely. Rotation produces different shapes, called conformations. Because they turn into one another so easily, these conformational isomers cannot be separated at room temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "Cis-trans isomers need restricted rotation, usually about a C=C double bond. Butane''s C2–C3 bond is single and rotates freely.", "1": "Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images that do not interconvert by simple rotation about a single bond.", "3": "Structural isomers have atoms joined in a different order. In these forms the order of bonding is the same."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0818' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The (+) isomer of thalidomide was a safe sedative, while the (−) isomer harmed the developing fetus. However, pure (+) thalidomide racemises quickly once it enters the bloodstream, with over 50% conversion within about 10 minutes. The body makes the harmful (−) form anyway, so selling only the (+) isomer would not help.', wrong = '{"0": "The (+) isomer was the effective, safe sedative. The (−) isomer caused the birth defects.", "2": "The enantiomers can be separated. The problem is that they re-mix (racemise) inside the body.", "3": "Thalidomide is chiral. It has a chiral centre and exists as (+) and (−) enantiomers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0819' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'X-ray studies show benzene is a flat, regular hexagon of six sp² hybridized carbon atoms. The three sp² orbitals on each carbon lie in one plane, spread evenly, so every C–C–C and H–C–C bond angle is 120°.', wrong = '{"0": "109.5° is the tetrahedral angle around an sp³ carbon, as in methane. Benzene''s carbons are sp².", "2": "180° is the linear angle around an sp carbon, as in ethyne.", "3": "90° does not occur in the benzene ring. A regular hexagon has interior angles of 120°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0820' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A C–C single bond is about 154 pm and a C=C double bond about 133 pm. In benzene the π electrons are delocalized over the whole ring, so all six C–C bonds are identical, with a bond order between one and two. Their length, 139 pm, lies between 154 and 133 pm.', wrong = '{"0": "139 pm is longer than both 109 pm and 133 pm, so it cannot lie between them.", "1": "139 pm is longer than a C=C bond (133 pm), so it is not between a double and a triple bond.", "2": "Benzene contains no triple bonds. Its C–C bonds are intermediate between single and double."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0821' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1865 the German chemist August Kekulé proposed that benzene is a six-membered ring of carbon atoms with alternate single and double bonds. This Kekulé structure explained the C₆H₆ formula, though not all of benzene''s properties, which were later explained by delocalization.', wrong = '{"0": "Michael Faraday discovered benzene in 1825, but he did not propose its ring structure.", "2": "Linus Pauling worked on resonance and bonding in the 20th century, long after 1865.", "3": "Friedrich Wöhler is known for making urea from an inorganic compound in 1828, not for benzene''s structure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0822' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hydrogenating one C=C bond (cyclohexene) releases 120 kJ mol⁻¹, so a ring with three fixed C=C bonds should release 3 × 120 = 360 kJ mol⁻¹. Benzene releases only 208 kJ mol⁻¹. The difference, 360 − 208 = 152 kJ mol⁻¹, is the extra stability from delocalization, the resonance energy.', wrong = '{"1": "120 kJ mol⁻¹ is the heat released by hydrogenating cyclohexene, which has just one C=C bond.", "2": "208 kJ mol⁻¹ is benzene''s actual heat of hydrogenation, not the difference from the expected value.", "3": "568 kJ mol⁻¹ is 360 + 208, the sum. Resonance energy is the difference between the two."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0823' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each benzene carbon is sp² hybridized and uses its three sp² orbitals for σ bonds to two carbons and one hydrogen. The one unhybridized 2p orbital left on each carbon stands perpendicular to the ring. These six p orbitals overlap sideways, forming a π cloud above and below the ring in which six electrons are shared by all six carbons.', wrong = '{"0": "Benzene''s carbons are sp², not sp³, and end-on overlap forms σ bonds, not π bonds.", "2": "Overlap of sp² orbitals with hydrogen 1s orbitals forms the C–H σ bonds, not the π system.", "3": "The π electrons are not held in three separate bonds. They are spread evenly over all six carbons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0824' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'C₆H₅NH₂ is benzene with one amino (–NH₂) group. Its traditional name, aniline, is accepted by IUPAC as a base name, and its systematic name is benzenamine.', wrong = '{"0": "Phenol is C₆H₅OH, benzene with a hydroxyl group.", "1": "Anisole (methoxybenzene) is C₆H₅OCH₃, benzene with a methoxy group.", "2": "Toluene (methylbenzene) is C₆H₅CH₃, benzene with a methyl group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0825' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a ring carries two different groups, the higher-priority group names the parent and its carbon is C-1. –COOH ranks above –OH, so the parent is benzoic acid. Counting round the ring, the –OH is on C-3 and becomes a 3-hydroxy prefix: 3-hydroxybenzoic acid.', wrong = '{"1": "This uses phenol as the parent, but –COOH outranks –OH, so the parent must be benzoic acid.", "2": "The COOH carbon is C-1 by definition in benzoic acid, so it gets no number, and this ordering breaks the naming rules.", "3": "This is not a systematic name. The accepted name uses benzoic acid as the parent with a 3-hydroxy prefix."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0826' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For two groups on a benzene ring, ortho (o-) means neighbouring carbons, 1,2. Meta (m-) means one carbon is skipped, 1,3. Para (p-) means opposite carbons, 1,4. So meta corresponds to positions 1,3.', wrong = '{"1": "1,2 means the groups are on neighbouring carbons. That is ortho.", "2": "1,4 means the groups are on opposite carbons of the ring. That is para.", "3": "Carbons 1 and 6 are neighbours in a ring, so 1,6 is the same as 1,2 (ortho)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0827' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Benzene''s delocalized π system gives it great extra stability. In substitution, an H is replaced by Br and the delocalized ring is restored when H⁺ is lost, so the product keeps its aromatic stability. Addition would permanently destroy the delocalized system, which costs too much energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Benzene has six π electrons, delocalized over the ring. That is why it reacts with electrophiles.", "2": "Addition products are not aromatic. Adding across the ring destroys the delocalized π system.", "3": "Bromine, once polarized by a Lewis acid such as FeBr₃, is the electrophile in the bromination of benzene."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0828' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In benzene the π electrons are delocalized, which makes the ring very stable. As a result benzene is less reactive than alkenes, needs catalysts to react with electrophiles, and prefers electrophilic substitution so that its stable ring is kept.', wrong = '{"0": "Alkenes are more reactive than benzene and readily undergo electrophilic addition. Benzene does not.", "1": "Benzene does react with electrophiles, for example in nitration and halogenation, when suitable catalysts are used.", "3": "Free radical substitution is typical of alkanes. Benzene''s typical reactions are electrophilic substitutions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0829' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cl₂ and Br₂ are non-polar and too weak as electrophiles to attack stable benzene. A Lewis acid such as FeBr₃ or AlCl₃ accepts a lone pair from one halogen atom, which polarizes the X–X bond. The other halogen atom becomes strongly δ+ (behaving like X⁺) and can attack the ring.', wrong = '{"0": "The Lewis acid does not reduce the halogen. It only polarizes the X–X bond.", "1": "The ring stays intact in substitution. The Lewis acid acts on the halogen, not on the ring.", "2": "A Lewis acid accepts an electron pair, so it is an electrophile, not a nucleophile."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0830' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the electrophile bonds to one ring carbon, that carbon becomes sp³ and the ring loses its full delocalization. The remaining four π electrons are spread over the other five carbons, which share a positive charge. This positively charged intermediate is the sigma complex, or arenium ion.', wrong = '{"0": "A carbanion is negatively charged. The intermediate here carries a positive charge.", "1": "A free radical has an unpaired electron. In this mechanism the electrons move in pairs.", "3": "The acylium ion, RCO⁺, is the electrophile in Friedel–Crafts acylation. It attacks the ring; it is not the intermediate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0831' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Concentrated sulphuric acid reacts with concentrated nitric acid to form the nitronium ion: HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + HSO₄⁻ + H₂O. NO₂⁺ is a strong electrophile that attacks the benzene ring and replaces an H, giving nitrobenzene.', wrong = '{"0": "NO₂⁻, the nitrite ion, is negatively charged. It is electron-rich and cannot act as an electrophile here.", "1": "HNO₃ is only the source of the electrophile. It must first be converted to NO₂⁺ by sulphuric acid.", "2": "NO₃⁻, the nitrate ion, is negatively charged and cannot attack an electron-rich ring."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0832' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitration of benzene is carried out at about 50 °C or below. At higher temperatures the reaction goes further and more than one nitro group is substituted, mainly giving 1,3-dinitrobenzene, because the first –NO₂ group directs the second to the meta position.', wrong = '{"0": "A higher temperature speeds up nitration. The problem is too much reaction, not none.", "1": "Benzene still undergoes electrophilic substitution at higher temperatures, not addition.", "3": "Nitrobenzene does not lose its nitro group and turn back into benzene when heated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0833' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is Friedel–Crafts alkylation. AlCl₃ pulls Cl from chloroethane, producing an ethyl carbocation (CH₃CH₂⁺) that attacks the ring. An ethyl group replaces a hydrogen: C₆H₆ + CH₃CH₂Cl → C₆H₅CH₂CH₃ + HCl. The product is ethylbenzene.', wrong = '{"0": "Chlorobenzene is formed when benzene reacts with Cl₂ in the presence of a Lewis acid, not with chloroethane.", "1": "Benzoic acid is made by oxidizing the side chain of an alkylbenzene, for example with hot KMnO₄.", "2": "Phenylethanone is made by Friedel–Crafts acylation with ethanoyl chloride, CH₃COCl, not chloroethane."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0834' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Friedel–Crafts acylation, AlCl₃ removes a chloride ion from the acyl chloride: RCOCl + AlCl₃ → RC≡O⁺ + AlCl₄⁻. The acylium ion, RCO⁺, is a strong electrophile that attacks the benzene ring, giving an aryl ketone.', wrong = '{"0": "A carbanion is negatively charged and acts as a nucleophile, not an electrophile.", "1": "The nitronium ion, NO₂⁺, is the electrophile in nitration with HNO₃ and H₂SO₄.", "3": "The chloride ion is removed by AlCl₃ to form AlCl₄⁻. Being negative, it cannot be the electrophile."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0835' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'AlCl₃ takes Cl⁻ from the halide to make the electrophile and forms AlCl₄⁻. At the end, the intermediate loses H⁺, which reacts with AlCl₄⁻ to give HCl and AlCl₃ again. Since AlCl₃ is regenerated, it acts as a catalyst.', wrong = '{"0": "AlCl₃ is not used up. It is regenerated when AlCl₄⁻ reacts with the H⁺ released from the ring.", "1": "AlCl₃ accepts an electron pair, so it is a Lewis acid, not a nucleophile.", "2": "AlCl₃ contains no carbon, so it cannot become benzene. It is only a catalyst."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0836' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –OH group has lone pairs on oxygen that it donates into the ring through delocalization. This raises the electron density of the ring, especially at the 2 and 4 positions, so it activates the ring and directs incoming electrophiles to the ortho and para positions.', wrong = '{"0": "–NO₂ withdraws electrons from the ring, so it deactivates it and directs to the meta position.", "1": "–CN withdraws electrons from the ring, so it is a deactivating meta director.", "3": "–CHO has an electron-withdrawing C=O group, so it is a deactivating meta director."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0837' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With AlCl₃ and no UV light, chlorine reacts by electrophilic substitution on the ring. The methyl group donates electrons and directs the incoming Cl to the 2 and 4 positions, so the main products are 2-chloromethylbenzene and 4-chloromethylbenzene.', wrong = '{"0": "–CH₃ is an ortho-para director, so the meta product, 3-chloromethylbenzene, forms only in small amounts.", "1": "C₆H₅CH₂Cl comes from free radical substitution on the methyl group, which needs UV light, not AlCl₃.", "3": "Hexachlorocyclohexane comes from adding chlorine to benzene in UV light without a catalyst."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0838' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –NO₂ group strongly withdraws electrons from the ring, so it deactivates it, especially at the 2 and 4 positions. The 3 position is less affected, so the electrophile attacks there. Chlorination of nitrobenzene therefore gives mainly 3-chloronitrobenzene.', wrong = '{"0": "–NO₂ is a meta director, so the 2-chloro (ortho) product is not the main one.", "1": "The nitro group is not replaced. Chlorine substitutes a hydrogen, not the –NO₂ group.", "2": "–NO₂ is a meta director, so the 4-chloro (para) product is not the main one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0839' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The order of steps decides where the second group goes. Nitrating first gives nitrobenzene, and its –NO₂ group directs the incoming Cl to the meta position, giving 3-chloronitrobenzene. If chlorination came first, –Cl would direct the nitro group to the 2 and 4 positions instead.', wrong = '{"1": "Chlorine is an ortho-para director, so nitrating chlorobenzene gives mainly 2- and 4-chloronitrobenzene, not the 3-isomer.", "2": "HCl is not a chlorinating agent for benzene rings. Ring chlorination needs Cl₂ with a Lewis acid.", "3": "Chlorine with benzene in UV light gives addition (hexachlorocyclohexane), not substitution on the ring."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0840' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Complete combustion gives CO₂ and H₂O. Check 2C₆H₆ + 15O₂ → 12CO₂ + 6H₂O: carbon 12 = 12, hydrogen 12 = 12, oxygen 30 = 24 + 6. Benzene''s high carbon content makes it burn with a smoky (sooty) flame, which is a test for aromatic compounds.', wrong = '{"1": "Complete combustion produces water, not hydrogen gas. Hydrogen would itself burn in the oxygen.", "2": "This equation balances, but it gives carbon monoxide. That is incomplete combustion, not complete combustion.", "3": "This shows soot (carbon) forming, which is incomplete combustion, and its oxygen does not balance (6 on the left, 3 on the right)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0841' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hot alkaline KMnO₄ oxidizes the alkyl side chain of an alkylbenzene down to a –COOH group, leaving the stable benzene ring untouched. Methylbenzene gives the benzoate ion, which on acidification becomes benzoic acid, C₆H₅COOH.', wrong = '{"0": "The side chain is oxidized, not removed. The carbon attached to the ring stays as –COOH.", "2": "Benzyl alcohol is only a partial oxidation product. Hot KMnO₄ oxidizes the side chain all the way to the acid.", "3": "Phenol has –OH on the ring itself. Here the methyl carbon is turned into –COOH."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0842' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Side-chain oxidation needs at least one hydrogen on the benzylic carbon (the carbon attached to the ring). In tert-butylbenzene, C₆H₅C(CH₃)₃, that carbon carries three methyl groups and no hydrogen. So it is not oxidized and does not decolourize hot KMnO₄.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethylbenzene''s benzylic carbon carries two hydrogens, so it is oxidized to benzoic acid.", "1": "Isopropylbenzene''s benzylic carbon carries one hydrogen, so it is oxidized to benzoic acid.", "3": "Methylbenzene''s benzylic carbon carries three hydrogens, so it is readily oxidized to benzoic acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0843' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hot alkaline KMnO₄ attacks the side chain at the benzylic carbon. Any alkyl group with a benzylic hydrogen, whatever its length, is cut back to a single –COOH group on the ring; the rest of the chain is oxidized away. Propylbenzene therefore gives benzoic acid.', wrong = '{"1": "The whole three-carbon chain is not kept. Oxidation cuts it back to one carboxyl carbon attached to the ring.", "2": "The chain is not kept at two carbons. Only the carbon attached to the ring remains, as –COOH.", "3": "The benzene ring is not lost. The product is an aromatic acid, benzoic acid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0844' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Complete hydrogenation adds three molecules of H₂ to benzene, one for each double bond in the Kekulé picture: C₆H₆ + 3H₂ → C₆H₁₂. It is done over finely divided nickel at about 150 °C and higher pressure. The ring is kept and fully saturated, giving cyclohexane.', wrong = '{"0": "Cyclohexene, C₆H₁₀, would form if only one H₂ were added. Complete hydrogenation adds three.", "2": "Hexane is an open chain. Hydrogenation saturates the ring but does not break it open.", "3": "Cyclohexadiene, C₆H₈, would form if only one H₂ were added to benzene. It still has two double bonds, so it is not fully hydrogenated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0845' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Without a Lewis acid catalyst, but with UV light and heat, chlorine adds to benzene instead of substituting. Three Cl₂ molecules add across the ring, one Cl to each carbon: C₆H₆ + 3Cl₂ → C₆H₆Cl₆, which is 1,2,3,4,5,6-hexachlorocyclohexane.', wrong = '{"1": "Chlorobenzene forms by electrophilic substitution, which needs a Lewis acid catalyst such as AlCl₃ or FeCl₃.", "2": "Dichlorobenzenes form by further substitution in the presence of a Lewis acid, not by addition in UV light.", "3": "Hexachlorobenzene, C₆Cl₆, would need all six hydrogens replaced. In the addition product each carbon keeps its H."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0846' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Forming six new C–H bonds releases more energy than is used, so the reaction is exothermic overall. But to start, the stable delocalized π system must be broken, which gives a high activation energy. Finely divided nickel at about 150 °C and a higher pressure are used to overcome this barrier.', wrong = '{"0": "The reaction is exothermic overall, because the many new C–H bonds are strong.", "2": "Hydrogen does react readily with alkenes over nickel. The difficulty here is benzene''s stable ring.", "3": "Cyclohexane is a stable saturated compound, widely used as a solvent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0847' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Naphthalene is made of two benzene rings fused together, sharing one side (two carbon atoms). It has 10 carbon atoms, and only 8 of them carry a hydrogen because the two shared carbons have none. So its formula is C₁₀H₈.', wrong = '{"0": "C₆H₆ is benzene, which has a single six-carbon ring, not two fused rings.", "2": "C₁₄H₁₀ is the formula of anthracene and phenanthrene, which have three fused rings.", "3": "C₁₀H₁₀ has two hydrogens too many for two fused aromatic rings."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0848' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Halogenoalkanes are classified by the carbon that carries the halogen. In (CH₃)₃CCl, the carbon bonded to Cl is also bonded to three other carbon atoms (the three methyl groups). A carbon joined to three other carbons is tertiary, so this is a tertiary halogenoalkane.', wrong = '{"0": "In a primary halogenoalkane, the C–X carbon is bonded to one or no other carbon atoms, as in chloroethane.", "1": "In a secondary halogenoalkane, the C–X carbon is bonded to two other carbons, as in 2-chloropropane.", "3": "A halogenoarene has the halogen joined directly to a benzene ring. There is no ring here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0849' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A secondary halogenoalkane has the halogen on a carbon that is bonded to two other carbon atoms. In 2-chlorobutane, CH₃CHClCH₂CH₃, the carbon carrying Cl (C-2) is bonded to C-1 and C-3. So it is secondary.', wrong = '{"0": "In 1-chloropropane the C–Cl carbon is at the end of the chain, bonded to only one other carbon, so it is primary.", "1": "In chloromethane the C–Cl carbon is bonded to no other carbon. It is counted as primary.", "3": "In 2-chloro-2-methylpropane the C–Cl carbon is bonded to three other carbons, so it is tertiary."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0850' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With thionyl chloride, an alcohol gives a chloroalkane: ROH + SOCl₂ → RCl + SO₂ + HCl. Both by-products, SO₂ and HCl, are gases that escape from the mixture. This leaves the chloroalkane fairly pure and easy to collect.', wrong = '{"0": "No alkene is formed. The products are the chloroalkane and the gases SO₂ and HCl.", "1": "A real reaction takes place, in which Cl replaces the –OH group of the alcohol.", "3": "Phosphorous acid, H₃PO₃, is a by-product of the phosphorus trihalides such as PBr₃, not of SOCl₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0851' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Phosphorus tribromide replaces the –OH groups of three alcohol molecules with Br: 3CH₃CH₂OH + PBr₃ → 3CH₃CH₂Br + H₃PO₃. The phosphorus ends up in phosphorous acid, H₃PO₃.', wrong = '{"0": "H₃PO₄ is phosphoric acid, with phosphorus in the +5 state. PBr₃ has phosphorus in the +3 state and gives H₃PO₃.", "1": "The phosphorus must go somewhere. It ends up in H₃PO₃, not as HBr alone.", "2": "An oxohalide such as POCl₃ forms with the pentahalide PCl₅. PBr₃ gives H₃PO₃."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0852' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alcohols react with hydrogen halides to give halogenoalkanes. For chloroalkanes the reaction with HCl is slow, so anhydrous zinc chloride is added as a catalyst: CH₃CH₂OH + HCl → CH₃CH₂Cl + H₂O. ZnCl₂ speeds up the reaction without being used up.', wrong = '{"0": "The chlorine in chloroethane comes from HCl. ZnCl₂ only catalyses the reaction.", "1": "No oxidation takes place. The –OH group is simply replaced by –Cl.", "3": "ZnCl₂ is added in small amounts to speed up the reaction. It is not the solvent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0853' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Iodide ions are easily oxidized. Concentrated sulphuric acid is an oxidizing agent and would turn I⁻ into I₂, wasting the iodide. Phosphoric acid is only a weak oxidizing agent, so it gives HI from KI without oxidizing it, and the HI then converts the alcohol to an iodoalkane.', wrong = '{"0": "The iodine comes from KI. Phosphoric acid only supplies the H⁺ ions to make HI.", "1": "Phosphoric acid is a weaker acid than sulphuric acid. It is chosen because it is a weaker oxidizing agent.", "2": "Sulphuric acid does react with KI. The problem is that it then oxidizes the iodide to iodine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0854' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Reactions of halogenoalkanes involve breaking the C–X bond, so the bond strength mainly decides reactivity. The C–F bond is the strongest, 467 kJ mol⁻¹, so it is very hard to break, and fluoroalkanes hardly react under ordinary conditions.', wrong = '{"0": "Fluoroalkanes are covalent molecules, not ionic compounds.", "1": "Fluorine has the highest electronegativity of all elements, not a low one.", "3": "Fluorine is the smallest halogen atom. Its small size helps make the C–F bond short and strong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0855' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The extra energy needed is the difference between the two bond energies: C–Cl minus C–I = 346 − 228 = 118 kJ mol⁻¹. The weaker C–I bond is why iodoalkanes are the most reactive halogenoalkanes.', wrong = '{"1": "56 kJ mol⁻¹ is the difference between C–Cl and C–Br (346 − 290), not C–Cl and C–I.", "2": "121 kJ mol⁻¹ is the difference between C–F and C–Cl (467 − 346).", "3": "574 kJ mol⁻¹ is the sum 346 + 228. The question asks for the difference."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0856' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In SN2 (substitution, nucleophilic, bimolecular) the nucleophile attacks the δ+ carbon from the side opposite the halogen. The new bond forms as the C–X bond breaks, all in a single step through one transition state. This back-side attack also inverts the arrangement at that carbon.', wrong = '{"0": "The nucleophile cannot attack from the halogen''s side, which is blocked by the leaving group and its electron density. Back-side attack is the mark of SN2.", "1": "The nucleophile is attracted to the δ+ carbon. It does not attack the δ− halogen atom.", "2": "A carbocation forming first is the SN1 mechanism. SN2 has no intermediate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0857' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SN2 takes place in one step involving both reactants, so its rate equation is rate = k[CH₃Br][OH⁻]. The reaction is first order in OH⁻. With [CH₃Br] constant, doubling [OH⁻] doubles the rate.', wrong = '{"0": "This fits SN1, where the rate depends only on the alkyl halide. In SN2 the nucleophile appears in the rate equation.", "1": "Raising a reactant''s concentration in the rate equation cannot slow the reaction down.", "3": "Quadrupling would need the rate to be second order in OH⁻, or both concentrations to be doubled."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0858' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SN1 takes place in two steps. First, the C–X bond breaks slowly to give a carbocation and a halide ion; this slow step controls the rate. Then the nucleophile combines quickly with the carbocation. Only the alkyl halide is in the slow step, so rate = k[alkyl halide].', wrong = '{"0": "The attack of the nucleophile on the carbocation is the fast second step, not the slow one.", "2": "A single step involving both reactants describes the SN2 mechanism.", "3": "Removing a β-hydrogen happens in elimination reactions, not in SN1 substitution."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0859' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A tertiary halogenoalkane ionizes to a tertiary carbocation, which is stabilized by the electron-pushing (+I) effect of three alkyl groups. The same three bulky groups crowd the back of the carbon, blocking the back-side attack needed for SN2. So these halides react by SN1.', wrong = '{"0": "SN1 depends on ionization. Tertiary halogenoalkanes ionize most easily of all.", "1": "Tertiary halogenoalkanes do have β-hydrogens on their methyl groups. That is why they can also undergo elimination.", "2": "The C–X bond strength depends mainly on which halogen is present, not on whether the carbon is primary or tertiary."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0860' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alkyl groups push electrons towards a positive carbon (+I effect) and spread out its charge. The more alkyl groups attached, the more stable the carbocation: tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl. (CH₃)₃C⁺ has three methyl groups, so it is the most stable.', wrong = '{"0": "CH₃⁺ has no alkyl groups to stabilize its charge, so it is the least stable carbocation.", "1": "CH₃CH₂⁺ is primary, with only one alkyl group to help stabilize the charge.", "2": "(CH₃)₂CH⁺ is secondary, with two alkyl groups. It is less stable than the tertiary ion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0861' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The carbon carrying the halogen is the α-carbon, and its neighbour is the β-carbon. In β-elimination a base removes a hydrogen from the β-carbon while the halogen leaves from the α-carbon. The electrons left behind form a C=C double bond between these two carbons.', wrong = '{"0": "Only one halogen is removed, together with one hydrogen. Removing two halogens from one carbon is not β-elimination.", "2": "The hydrogen comes from the β-carbon, next to the C–X carbon, not from the α-carbon.", "3": "The halogenoalkane loses H and X, which together form HX. No oxygen is removed from it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0862' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In E1, 2-bromo-2-methylpropane first loses Br⁻ to form the tertiary carbocation (CH₃)₃C⁺. A base then removes H⁺ from one of the neighbouring methyl groups, forming a C=C bond. The product is (CH₃)₂C=CH₂, which is 2-methylpropene.', wrong = '{"0": "But-1-ene, CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃, has an unbranched chain. The starting compound is branched, and elimination keeps the carbon skeleton.", "2": "Propene has only three carbons. The starting compound has four, and none are lost.", "3": "But-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃, has an unbranched chain, so it cannot come from this branched compound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0863' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In aqueous NaOH, OH⁻ acts mainly as a nucleophile and replaces Br, giving ethanol: CH₃CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂OH + Br⁻. In ethanol as solvent, OH⁻ acts mainly as a base and removes HBr, giving ethene: CH₃CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O + Br⁻.', wrong = '{"0": "The products are swapped. Aqueous NaOH favours substitution (ethanol), and ethanolic NaOH favours elimination (ethene).", "2": "Ethane is not formed in either reaction. That would need reduction, not substitution or elimination.", "3": "Ethanolic NaOH removes HBr, which gives ethene with a double bond, not ethane."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0864' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'CN⁻ has a lone pair on carbon that readily attacks the δ+ carbon of a halogenoalkane, so it is a strong nucleophile. It is only a weak base, so it rarely removes a β-hydrogen. Substitution therefore wins: CH₃CH₂Br + CN⁻ → CH₃CH₂CN + Br⁻, giving propanenitrile.', wrong = '{"1": "CN⁻ is only a weak base. A strong base would favour elimination to an alkene.", "2": "The reaction is carried out in ethanolic solution, so KCN does dissolve enough to react.", "3": "CN⁻ replaces Br⁻, so this is a nucleophilic substitution, not an elimination."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0865' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The cyanide ion brings a new carbon atom with it. Bromoethane (2 carbons) becomes propanenitrile, CH₃CH₂CN (3 carbons). Lengthening the chain by one carbon is very useful in synthesis, and the nitrile can then be turned into an acid or an amine.', wrong = '{"0": "A carbon atom is added by the CN group, so the chain gets longer, not shorter.", "2": "The product contains a –C≡N group, so it is a nitrile, not an alkane.", "3": "The halogen is not just removed. It is replaced by a CN group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0866' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ammonia replaces Br to give ethanamine, CH₃CH₂NH₂. But ethanamine also has a lone pair on nitrogen, so it is a nucleophile too and attacks more bromoethane, giving diethylamine, then triethylamine, and finally a quaternary ammonium salt. So a mixture of products forms.', wrong = '{"1": "Ammonia is a weak base and a nucleophile, not a strong acid.", "2": "The mixture described is of amines, formed by substitution. Elimination to ethene is not the cause.", "3": "No oxidation takes place. Each step is a nucleophilic substitution."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0867' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each amine formed still has a lone pair on nitrogen and attacks more bromoethane. With a large excess of bromoethane this continues until nitrogen carries four ethyl groups. The final product is the quaternary ammonium salt, tetraethylammonium bromide, (C₂H₅)₄N⁺Br⁻.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanamine is the first product. It is the main product only when ammonia, not bromoethane, is in excess.", "1": "N-ethylethanamine (diethylamine) still has a lone pair and reacts with more bromoethane.", "2": "N,N-diethylethanamine (triethylamine) still has a lone pair and reacts with more bromoethane to give the quaternary salt."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0868' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Halogenoalkanes do not mix well with water, but silver nitrate is used as an aqueous solution. Ethanol dissolves both, so they come into contact. Water then acts as the nucleophile and releases halide ions, which form a silver halide precipitate with Ag⁺.', wrong = '{"1": "Water, not ethanol, acts as the nucleophile that frees the halide ion. Ethanol is the common solvent.", "2": "The precipitate is the silver halide, such as AgCl or AgBr. Ethanol does not form it.", "3": "No oxidation takes place. The halide is released by nucleophilic substitution."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0869' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Water in the ethanolic solution slowly frees the halide ion, which then forms a silver halide precipitate. The colour identifies the halogen: AgCl is white, AgBr is cream-yellow and AgI is bright yellow. A cream-yellow precipitate means the halogen is bromine.', wrong = '{"1": "Chlorine gives a white precipitate of AgCl, and it forms most slowly because the C–Cl bond is strongest of the three.", "2": "Iodine gives a bright yellow precipitate of AgI, and it forms fastest because the C–I bond is weakest.", "3": "AgF is soluble in water, so no precipitate forms. The very strong C–F bond also hardly breaks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0870' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chlorobenzene is made by electrophilic substitution: C₆H₆ + Cl₂ → C₆H₅Cl + HCl. Chlorine alone is too weak an electrophile, so anhydrous FeCl₃ (a Lewis acid, also called a halogen carrier) is used to polarize Cl₂. The reaction runs at room temperature.', wrong = '{"1": "UV light makes chlorine add to benzene, giving hexachlorocyclohexane, not chlorobenzene.", "2": "Aqueous NaOH has no chlorine to give and does not react with benzene.", "3": "Ethanolic KCN is used to convert halogenoalkanes into nitriles. It does not chlorinate benzene."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0871' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In chlorobenzene, one lone pair on chlorine overlaps with the ring''s π system and is partly delocalized into it. This gives the C–Cl bond some double-bond character, making it shorter and stronger than in chloroethane. A nucleophile therefore finds it much harder to replace the chlorine.', wrong = '{"0": "The C–Cl bond in chlorobenzene is covalent, not ionic. An ionic bond would actually break more easily in solution.", "2": "Chlorine has three lone pairs in chlorobenzene. One of them is the pair that is delocalized into the ring.", "3": "It is the reverse. The C–Cl bond in chlorobenzene is the stronger one, which is why it reacts less."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0872' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Halothane, CF₃CHBrCl, is a halogenated hydrocarbon. It is a volatile liquid whose vapour is breathed in to produce general anaesthesia, so it is used as a powerful inhalation anaesthetic.', wrong = '{"0": "Refrigerants were mainly CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons) such as CCl₂F₂, not halothane.", "2": "The classic halogenated pesticide is DDT, not halothane.", "3": "Halogen-containing polymers include PVC and PTFE. Halothane is a small molecule, not a polymer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0873' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alcohols are classified by the number of –OH groups. Those with one –OH are monohydric, and those with more than one are polyhydric. Glycerol, CH₂(OH)CH(OH)CH₂(OH), has three –OH groups, so it is a polyhydric (trihydric) alcohol.', wrong = '{"0": "A monohydric alcohol has only one –OH group, such as ethanol. Glycerol has three.", "1": "Glycerol has two primary –OH groups and one secondary –OH group, and none that is tertiary.", "2": "A phenol has –OH directly on a benzene ring. Glycerol has no ring."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0874' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An alcohol is classified by how many alkyl groups are on the carbon carrying the –OH. In R₂CHOH that carbon carries two alkyl groups (R and R) and one H. So it is a secondary (2°) alcohol, like propan-2-ol.', wrong = '{"0": "A primary alcohol, RCH₂OH, has only one alkyl group on the C–OH carbon.", "1": "A tertiary alcohol, R₃COH, has three alkyl groups and no H on the C–OH carbon.", "3": "A phenol has –OH bonded directly to an aromatic ring, not to an alkyl carbon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0875' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acid strength depends on how stable the anion is after H⁺ is lost. The ethyl group pushes electron density towards oxygen (+I effect), so the negative charge on ethoxide, CH₃CH₂O⁻, is concentrated and it grabs H⁺ back easily. Hydroxide is less destabilized, so ethanol ionizes less than water and is the weaker acid.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanol is not more polar than water. The difference comes from the alkyl group''s effect on the anion.", "1": "Ethanol does have an O–H bond. It is the H of this bond that can be lost as H⁺.", "2": "Ethoxide has no ring for delocalization. It is phenoxide that is stabilized by delocalization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0876' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Phenol is the strongest of the three because the negative charge of phenoxide is spread into the benzene ring. Water comes next. Ethanol is the weakest, because the ethyl group pushes electrons onto oxygen and destabilizes ethoxide. So the order is phenol > water > ethanol.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the exact reverse. Ethanol is the weakest acid of the three, not the strongest.", "1": "Phenol is more acidic than water, because its anion is stabilized by delocalization.", "3": "Phenol is the most acidic of the three, not the least."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0877' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When phenol loses H⁺, a lone pair on the oxygen of phenoxide overlaps with the ring''s π system. The negative charge is spread over the ring instead of sitting only on oxygen, which stabilizes the ion. Phenoxide therefore takes back H⁺ less readily than ethoxide, so phenol is more acidic.', wrong = '{"0": "Electrons move the other way: the oxygen lone pair is delocalized into the ring, which lowers the charge on oxygen.", "1": "The number of hydrogen atoms does not set acidity. Only the O–H hydrogen ionizes in either compound.", "2": "Both phenol and ethanol have an –OH group and both form hydrogen bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0878' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Industrially, ethanol is made by hydration of ethene: CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O(g) → CH₃CH₂OH. Steam adds across the double bond in an electrophilic addition, with concentrated phosphoric acid (H₃PO₄) as the catalyst, at high temperature and pressure.', wrong = '{"0": "Aqueous NaOH converts halogenoalkanes into alcohols by substitution. It does not add water to ethene.", "1": "Nickel catalyses the addition of hydrogen (hydrogenation), not water. Ethene with H₂ over nickel gives ethane.", "3": "Cold dilute KMnO₄ adds two –OH groups to ethene, giving ethane-1,2-diol, not ethanol."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0879' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cold, dilute KMnO₄ is a mild oxidizing agent. It adds an –OH group to each carbon of the double bond, so ethene becomes HOCH₂CH₂OH, ethane-1,2-diol (glycol). The purple colour of the permanganate disappears as it reacts.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanol has only one –OH. It is made by adding steam to ethene with phosphoric acid catalyst.", "2": "Ethanoic acid would need stronger oxidation, breaking more bonds. Cold dilute KMnO₄ stops at the diol.", "3": "Ethanal is made by oxidizing ethanol, for example with acidified dichromate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0880' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'NaBH₄ and LiAlH₄ supply hydride that adds hydrogen across the C=O bond. Ketones are reduced to secondary alcohols. Propanone gives propan-2-ol: CH₃COCH₃ + 2[H] → CH₃CH(OH)CH₃.', wrong = '{"0": "Propan-1-ol is a primary alcohol. It comes from reducing the aldehyde propanal, not a ketone.", "2": "Propanal is an aldehyde, an isomer of propanone. Reduction adds hydrogen, so it cannot give another carbonyl compound.", "3": "These reagents reduce C=O to CH–OH and stop there. They do not remove the oxygen to give propane."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0881' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carboxylic acids are hard to reduce and need a powerful reducing agent. Lithium aluminium hydride, LiAlH₄ (in dry ether), reduces them to primary alcohols: CH₃COOH + 4[H] → CH₃CH₂OH + H₂O.', wrong = '{"0": "NaBH₄ is a milder reducing agent. It reduces aldehydes and ketones but not carboxylic acids.", "2": "Acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ is an oxidizing agent. It would not reduce an acid.", "3": "Dilute HCl is just an acid and has no reducing power."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0882' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Complete combustion gives CO₂ and H₂O. In C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O, carbon is 2 = 2, hydrogen 6 = 6, and oxygen 1 + 6 = 7 on the left against 4 + 3 = 7 on the right. About 1367 kJ mol⁻¹ is released, which makes ethanol a useful fuel.', wrong = '{"0": "This is not balanced. Oxygen atoms on the left are 2 + 14 = 16, but on the right only 8 + 6 = 14.", "1": "Combustion produces water, not hydrogen gas. Hydrogen would itself burn in the oxygen.", "2": "This gives CO, so it is incomplete combustion, and oxygen does not balance (3 on the left, 5 on the right)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0883' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sodium replaces the hydrogen of the –OH group: 2CH₃CH₂OH + 2Na → 2CH₃CH₂ONa + H₂. The products are sodium ethoxide and hydrogen gas. Ethanol is a weaker acid than water, so the reaction is gentler than sodium with water.', wrong = '{"0": "The gas released is hydrogen from the O–H bond, not oxygen.", "1": "No alkene forms. Sodium replaces the hydroxyl hydrogen and the carbon chain is unchanged.", "3": "Sodium does react with ethanol, and the hydrogen bubbles are used as a test for an –OH group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0884' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The dichromate(VI) ion, Cr₂O₇²⁻, is orange. When it oxidizes a primary or secondary alcohol, it is reduced to chromium(III) ions, Cr³⁺, which are green. So the colour changes from orange to green.', wrong = '{"0": "The colours are the wrong way round. Orange dichromate is reduced to green Cr³⁺.", "2": "Purple to colourless is the change seen when acidified KMnO₄ (manganate(VII)) is reduced.", "3": "This is not the dichromate change. Dichromate starts orange, not colourless."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0885' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Oxidation of an alcohol removes the hydrogen from the carbon carrying the –OH. 2-Methylpropan-2-ol, (CH₃)₃COH, is a tertiary alcohol: that carbon has no hydrogen. So it is not oxidized, and the dichromate stays orange.', wrong = '{"0": "Propan-1-ol is a primary alcohol. It is oxidized to propanal and then propanoic acid, turning the solution green.", "1": "Propan-2-ol is a secondary alcohol. It is oxidized to propanone, turning the solution green.", "3": "Ethanol is a primary alcohol. It is oxidized to ethanal and then ethanoic acid, turning the solution green."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0886' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Propan-2-ol is a secondary alcohol, so its C–OH carbon has only one hydrogen. Oxidation removes that H and the O–H hydrogen, forming a C=O group: CH₃CH(OH)CH₃ + [O] → CH₃COCH₃ + H₂O. The product is the ketone propanone, and oxidation stops there.', wrong = '{"0": "Propanal is an aldehyde, formed by oxidizing the primary alcohol propan-1-ol.", "2": "Propanoic acid comes from further oxidation of propanal. A ketone cannot be easily oxidized further.", "3": "Propene is made by dehydrating propan-2-ol, not by oxidizing it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0887' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hot aluminium oxide is a dehydrating agent. It removes an –OH from one carbon and an H from the next, which leave as water, and a C=C bond forms: CH₃CH₂OH → CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O. This dehydration is an elimination reaction giving ethene.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanoic acid would need oxidation. Al₂O₃ removes water and does not oxidize.", "1": "Ethanal would also need an oxidizing agent. Al₂O₃ is a dehydrating agent.", "2": "Over hot Al₂O₃ the main product is the alkene, ethene. Ethoxyethane forms under milder conditions with excess ethanol."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0888' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When dichromate oxidizes an alcohol, it is itself reduced, and its half-equation needs hydrogen ions: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. The acid, usually dilute sulphuric acid, supplies these H⁺ ions so the reduction can happen.', wrong = '{"0": "The acid does not make dichromate green. The green colour comes from Cr³⁺, formed only when dichromate is reduced.", "1": "The dilute acid on its own cannot oxidize alcohols. Dichromate is the oxidizing agent.", "3": "Small alcohols such as ethanol already mix with water. The acid is needed for H⁺ ions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0889' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Phenol, C₆H₅OH, has slightly acidic properties, and in the past it was called carbolic acid. It is a strong antiseptic and was used in carbolic soap and as one of the first antiseptics in surgery.', wrong = '{"0": "Glycol is the common name for ethane-1,2-diol, HOCH₂CH₂OH.", "1": "Wood spirit is an old name for methanol, which was once made by heating wood.", "2": "Grain alcohol is ethanol, made by fermenting sugars from grains."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0890' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Phenol is a stronger acid than water, so it reacts with NaOH: C₆H₅OH + NaOH → C₆H₅ONa + H₂O. The sodium phenoxide formed is soluble, so phenol dissolves. Ethanol is a weaker acid than water, so it does not react with NaOH.', wrong = '{"1": "Phenol acts as an acid here, giving its H⁺ to OH⁻. It is not a base.", "2": "Ethanol mixes completely with water. The difference is about acidity, not solubility.", "3": "This is an acid–base reaction. NaOH is not an oxidizing agent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0891' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrous acid, HNO₂, is too unstable to store, so it is made in situ by reacting sodium nitrite with dilute hydrochloric acid, kept below 10 °C with ice. It converts phenylamine into a diazonium salt, which gives phenol, N₂ and HCl on warming with water.', wrong = '{"1": "Concentrated HNO₃ with H₂SO₄ makes the nitronium ion for nitration, not nitrous acid.", "2": "Sodium nitrate with NaOH gives no acid at all. Nitrous acid needs a nitrite and an acid.", "3": "HCl with Br₂ gives no nitrous acid. Nitrous acid needs a nitrite source."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0892' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'NaOH turns phenol into the phenoxide ion, C₆H₅O⁻, which is a much better nucleophile. Phenoxide attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon of ethanoyl chloride, and Cl⁻ is lost: C₆H₅OH + CH₃COCl → CH₃COOC₆H₅ + HCl. The product, phenyl ethanoate, is an ester.', wrong = '{"1": "Methoxybenzene, C₆H₅OCH₃, is an ether with no C=O. The product here keeps the C=O of the ethanoyl group, so it is an ester.", "2": "Benzoic acid would need a –COOH on the ring. Neither reactant can supply that.", "3": "Ethanoyl chloride gives an ethanoyl group to oxygen. It does not chlorinate the ring."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0893' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –OH group donates electrons into the ring, so phenol is much more reactive than benzene and reacts even with dilute nitric acid at room temperature. –OH directs to the 2, 4 and 6 positions, so a mixture of 2-nitrophenol and 4-nitrophenol forms.', wrong = '{"1": "–OH is an ortho-para director, so the meta product, 3-nitrophenol, is not formed in useful amounts.", "2": "2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (picric acid) forms with concentrated nitric acid, not dilute.", "3": "Nitrobenzene has no –OH group. The –OH stays on the ring during nitration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0894' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In phenol, a lone pair on the oxygen of –OH is delocalized into the benzene ring. This raises the electron density of the ring, especially at the 2, 4 and 6 positions, so it attracts electrophiles much more strongly than benzene does. Milder conditions, such as dilute HNO₃ at room temperature, are then enough.', wrong = '{"1": "Phenol is more reactive than benzene towards electrophiles, because the –OH group activates the ring.", "2": "Phenol keeps the aromatic π system of benzene, and the oxygen lone pair adds to its electron density.", "3": "Dilute HNO₃ contains less NO₂⁺, not more. The much more reactive ring makes up for the weaker electrophile."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0895' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1-naphthol the –OH group behaves as it does in phenol: it donates electrons into the rings and directs electrophiles to the positions ortho and para to it. In naphthalene numbering these are positions 2 and 4. Positions 9 and 10 are the shared ring-fusion carbons and carry no hydrogen.', wrong = '{"1": "Carbons 9 and 10 are where the two rings join. They have no hydrogen, so no substitution can happen there.", "2": "Positions 5 and 8 are on the other ring, away from the –OH group, so they are not the activated positions.", "3": "Position 3 is meta to the –OH group, so it is not activated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0896' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –OH group strongly activates phenol''s ring, so it reacts with bromine water at room temperature without any catalyst, decolourizing it and giving a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol. Benzene, being much less reactive, needs pure bromine and a Lewis acid catalyst.', wrong = '{"0": "It is benzene that needs a Lewis acid catalyst such as AlBr₃ or FeBr₃. Phenol does not.", "2": "UV light is used to make halogens add to benzene. It is not needed for phenol''s substitution.", "3": "No heating is needed. Phenol reacts with bromine water readily at room temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0897' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acidified dichromate oxidizes ethanol first to ethanal and then on to ethanoic acid. Ethanal has a low boiling point (about 21 °C), so it evaporates as soon as it forms. Distilling it off straight away removes it from the oxidizing agent before it can be oxidized further to ethanoic acid.', wrong = '{"1": "Ethanol has the higher boiling point (78 °C), so it stays in the flask to keep reacting. Distillation is not used to remove it.", "2": "Distillation protects the product from over-oxidation. It does not speed up the reduction of dichromate.", "3": "No hydrate is formed or wanted. The aim is to collect pure ethanal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0898' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ethanal boils at about 21 °C, lower than ethanol (78 °C). Keeping the temperature above ethanal''s boiling point but below ethanol''s lets ethanal distil off as soon as it forms, while ethanol stays in the flask to be oxidized.', wrong = '{"0": "Below ethanal''s boiling point, ethanal would stay in the flask and be oxidized further to ethanoic acid.", "1": "Above ethanol''s boiling point, the unreacted ethanol would also boil away before it could be oxidized.", "3": "At ethanoic acid''s boiling point (118 °C), everything would boil off, and the acid is the product to be avoided."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0899' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Methanal, HCHO, is a gas at room temperature, so it is usually supplied as formalin, a 40% solution of methanal in water. Formalin kills microbes and hardens tissue proteins, so it is used to preserve biological specimens.', wrong = '{"0": "Propanone (acetone) solution is used as a solvent, for example in nail polish remover. It is not formalin.", "2": "Formalin contains methanal, the aldehyde, not methanol, the alcohol.", "3": "Ethanal, CH₃CHO, is a different aldehyde. Formalin is a water solution of methanal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0900' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon, so the C=O bond is polar: oxygen is δ− and carbon is δ+. The δ+ carbonyl carbon is electron-poor, so nucleophiles attack it. This is why carbonyl compounds typically undergo nucleophilic addition.', wrong = '{"0": "The carbonyl carbon is sp² hybridized, with a flat trigonal planar shape, not sp³ and tetrahedral.", "1": "Oxygen pulls electrons towards itself, so it is δ−, not δ+.", "2": "Carbon and oxygen do not share electrons equally. Oxygen''s higher electronegativity makes the bond polar."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0901' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alkyl groups push electrons towards the carbonyl carbon (+I effect), reducing its δ+ charge, and they also crowd it, blocking the nucleophile. Methanal has no alkyl groups, other aldehydes have one, and ketones have two. So reactivity is HCHO > RCHO > R₂CO.', wrong = '{"0": "Ketones, with two alkyl groups, are the least reactive, so they cannot come first.", "1": "This is the reverse order. Ketones are least reactive and methanal is most reactive.", "3": "Methanal has no alkyl group at all, so it is more reactive than an aldehyde with one alkyl group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0902' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A ketone has two alkyl groups on the carbonyl carbon, an aldehyde only one. The two groups push electrons towards the carbon (+I effect), lowering its δ+ charge, and their bulk gets in the way of the incoming nucleophile (steric hindrance). Both effects make ketones less reactive.', wrong = '{"0": "Hydrogen bonding is not the reason, and ketones are not more polar in a way that lowers reactivity.", "1": "Alkyl groups release electrons, so they have a +I effect, not a −I effect. Molar mass is not the reason.", "2": "In both aldehydes and ketones the carbonyl carbon is sp². Neither explanation is correct."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0903' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'HCN adds across the C=O of ethanal: CN⁻ bonds to the carbonyl carbon and H goes to the oxygen. The product is CH₃CH(OH)CN. Its longest chain, counting the nitrile carbon, has three carbons, with OH on C-2, so it is 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, a cyanohydrin with one more carbon than ethanal.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanenitrile, CH₃CN, has only two carbons and no –OH group. HCN adds a carbon and an –OH forms.", "2": "2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile, (CH₃)₂C(OH)CN, is the product from propanone, not ethanal.", "3": "Propanoic acid is not formed directly. An acid would only come from hydrolysing the nitrile group later."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0904' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The reaction needs free CN⁻ ions to attack the carbonyl carbon. HCN is a very weak acid that hardly ionizes, and the HCN molecule has no lone pair on carbon to act as a nucleophile itself. A slight excess of KCN supplies free CN⁻, which starts the reaction and keeps it going at a reasonable rate.', wrong = '{"0": "KCN is not a dehydrating agent. It is added to supply cyanide ions.", "1": "Aldehydes and ketones are not acids, so there is nothing for KCN to neutralize.", "3": "HCN is a poor nucleophile, not a strong one. That is why free CN⁻ is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0905' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The cyanide ion, CN⁻, is the nucleophile. In the first step it uses its lone pair to attack the δ+ carbonyl carbon, the C=O π bond breaks and an alkoxide ion (O⁻) forms. In the second step this O⁻ takes H⁺ from HCN (or water), giving the cyanohydrin.', wrong = '{"0": "H⁺ is added in the second step, to the negative oxygen of the alkoxide, not to the carbon.", "1": "Nothing is eliminated in this reaction. It is a nucleophilic addition.", "2": "The carbonyl oxygen is δ−, so a negatively charged nucleophile is repelled by it, not attracted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0906' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'NaBH₄ adds hydrogen across the C=O bond. Ketones are reduced to secondary alcohols, so propanone, CH₃COCH₃, gives CH₃CH(OH)CH₃, which is propan-2-ol. The carbonyl carbon keeps its two methyl groups and gains an H.', wrong = '{"0": "Propan-1-ol is a primary alcohol. It would come from reducing the aldehyde propanal.", "2": "Propanal is an aldehyde, an isomer of propanone. Reduction gives an alcohol, not another carbonyl compound.", "3": "Propanoic acid would need oxidation, not reduction. NaBH₄ is a reducing agent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0907' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'NaBH₄ acts as a source of the hydride ion, H⁻, a hydrogen with a pair of electrons. H⁻ attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon as a nucleophile, giving an alkoxide ion. The alkoxide then takes H⁺ from water to form the alcohol. The symbol [H] in equations stands for this, not for free hydrogen atoms.', wrong = '{"0": "[H] in the equation does not mean atomic hydrogen. The real attacking species is the hydride ion.", "2": "H⁺ has no electrons to donate, so it cannot be a nucleophile. It is added to oxygen later.", "3": "OH⁻ is not the attacking species. It is formed when the alkoxide takes H⁺ from water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0908' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '2,4-Dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNPH) gives an orange crystalline precipitate with aldehydes and ketones, so it identifies the carbonyl group in these compounds. Carboxylic acids, esters, acyl chlorides and amides also contain C=O but do not give the test. Ethanal reacts, while ethyl ethanoate, an ester, does not.', wrong = '{"0": "Propanone would react, but ethanamide is an amide and does not give the 2,4-DNPH test.", "1": "Ethanal would react, but ethanoic acid is a carboxylic acid and does not give the test.", "3": "Acyl chlorides such as ethanoyl chloride do not give an orange precipitate with 2,4-DNPH."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0909' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –NH₂ group of 2,4-DNPH adds to the C=O of the aldehyde or ketone, and then a molecule of water is lost to form a C=N bond. Addition followed by loss of a small molecule is an addition–elimination, also called a condensation reaction. The product is a 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone.', wrong = '{"0": "No free radicals are formed. The –NH₂ group attacks using its lone pair of electrons.", "1": "The carbonyl compound is not oxidized or reduced. It joins the reagent and water is lost.", "2": "The attacking group is a nucleophile (–NH₂), not an electrophile, and water is then eliminated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0910' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Tollens'' reagent is ammoniacal silver nitrate, containing [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺. An aldehyde is oxidized to a carboxylic acid (as its salt) and reduces Ag⁺ to silver metal. The silver deposits on the clean glass and forms a shiny silver mirror.', wrong = '{"0": "An orange precipitate is given by aldehydes and ketones with 2,4-DNPH, not with Tollens'' reagent.", "2": "A brick-red precipitate of Cu₂O is given by aldehydes with Fehling''s solution.", "3": "A yellow precipitate of iodoform, CHI₃, is given in the iodoform test with I₂/NaOH."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0911' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fehling''s solution contains blue Cu²⁺ ions held in a tartrate complex in alkali. An aldehyde reduces Cu²⁺ to Cu⁺, while the aldehyde is oxidized. In alkaline solution the Cu⁺ forms copper(I) oxide, Cu₂O, which is a brick-red precipitate.', wrong = '{"0": "Cu(OH)₂ is a blue solid containing Cu²⁺. The brick-red solid contains copper(I).", "1": "CuO is black and contains copper(II). Moreover, copper is reduced in this test, not oxidized.", "3": "Copper is reduced only to the +1 state, giving Cu₂O, not all the way to metallic copper."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0912' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An aldehyde has a hydrogen on its carbonyl carbon, which is easily oxidized to –COOH by mild oxidizing agents. A ketone has two carbon groups there, so it can only be oxidized by breaking a strong C–C bond. Mild reagents such as Tollens'' and Fehling''s cannot do this, so ketones give negative tests.', wrong = '{"0": "Ketones do contain a C=O carbonyl group. They differ from aldehydes in having no H on that carbon.", "1": "Small ketones such as propanone mix completely with water, so solubility is not the reason.", "2": "Tollens'' and Fehling''s reagents are oxidizing agents. They do not reduce ketones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0913' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The iodoform test is positive for compounds with a CH₃CO– group, or a CH₃CH(OH)– group that I₂/NaOH can oxidize to CH₃CO–. Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH, contains CH₃CH(OH)– and is oxidized to ethanal, giving yellow CHI₃. Methanol has no CH₃ next to the C–OH carbon, so it gives no precipitate.', wrong = '{"0": "Boiling point plays no part in the test. What matters is the CH₃CH(OH)– unit.", "2": "Acidity is not the reason. The test depends on having a methyl group next to the C–OH carbon.", "3": "Ethanol is a primary alcohol. Only one carbon is attached to its C–OH carbon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0914' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'No silver mirror with Tollens'' reagent means the compound is not an aldehyde. A yellow iodoform precipitate shows a CH₃CO– group. Of the C₃H₆O compounds, only propanone, CH₃COCH₃, is a methyl ketone, so it fits both tests.', wrong = '{"0": "Prop-2-en-1-ol, CH₂=CHCH₂OH, has no CH₃CO– or CH₃CH(OH)– group, so it gives no iodoform.", "1": "Propanal is an aldehyde and would give a silver mirror. It also lacks a CH₃CO– group.", "3": "Propan-1-ol is C₃H₈O, not C₃H₆O, and it gives neither test."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0915' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pure ethanoic acid melts at about 17 °C, close to room temperature. On cold days it freezes into colourless, ice-like crystals that look like a glacier. So pure (water-free) ethanoic acid is called glacial acetic acid.', wrong = '{"0": "The name describes its ice-like appearance when frozen. It is not obtained from glaciers.", "1": "Glacial acetic acid is pure, almost water-free acid, not a dilute solution. Vinegar is the dilute form.", "2": "Ethanoic acid does boil at 118 °C, but that has nothing to do with the name glacial."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0916' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –COOH group can form hydrogen bonds. Two ethanoic acid molecules pair up through two O–H···O=C hydrogen bonds to form a dimer, which behaves like a molecule of double the mass. Extra energy is needed to separate them, so the boiling point (118 °C) is high for its molar mass.', wrong = '{"0": "Dipole–dipole forces alone are much weaker. The high boiling point comes mainly from hydrogen bonding.", "2": "Ethanoic acid is a covalent molecule. Pure liquid ethanoic acid contains almost no ions.", "3": "Ethanoic acid has only two carbon atoms. Long-chain fatty acids have many more."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0917' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a nitrile is boiled with aqueous NaOH, the –C≡N group is hydrolysed. In alkali the acid formed is neutralized to its salt and the nitrogen is released as ammonia: CH₃CN + NaOH + H₂O → CH₃COONa + NH₃. Acidifying afterwards gives free ethanoic acid.', wrong = '{"0": "Hydrolysis turns –C≡N into a carboxyl group. It does not give an alcohol or release HCN.", "1": "Ethanoic acid and NH₄Cl are the products of acid hydrolysis with dilute HCl, not of alkaline hydrolysis.", "3": "Ethanamine is formed by reducing a nitrile, for example with LiAlH₄. Hydrolysis gives an acid or its salt."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0918' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'KMnO₄ oxidizes the side chain of an alkylbenzene to a –COOH group while leaving the stable ring untouched. Methylbenzene''s –CH₃ becomes –COOH, so the product is benzoic acid, C₆H₅COOH.', wrong = '{"0": "Benzene has no side chain to oxidize, and its ring resists KMnO₄. No benzoic acid forms.", "1": "Chlorobenzene does not react with water. Its C–Cl bond is strengthened by the ring.", "2": "Phenol with NaOH gives sodium phenoxide in an acid–base reaction. No carboxyl group forms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0919' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Thionyl chloride replaces the –OH of the carboxyl group with Cl: CH₃COOH + SOCl₂ → CH₃COCl + HCl + SO₂. Both by-products, hydrogen chloride and sulphur dioxide, are gases that escape, which makes the acyl chloride easy to obtain pure.', wrong = '{"0": "No water is formed. The H and O from the –OH leave as part of HCl and SO₂.", "2": "POCl₃ (with HCl) is produced when phosphorus pentachloride, PCl₅, is used instead of SOCl₂.", "3": "SO₃ is not formed. Sulphur leaves as SO₂, and HCl is also produced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0920' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The carboxyl group is hard to reduce. Only a powerful reducing agent, lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH₄) in dry ether, reduces ethanoic acid to the primary alcohol: CH₃COOH + 4[H] → CH₃CH₂OH + H₂O. Milder reagents such as NaBH₄ and H₂/Ni do not reduce carboxylic acids.', wrong = '{"0": "Tollens'' reagent is a mild oxidizing agent used to detect aldehydes. It cannot reduce anything.", "1": "NaBH₄ is too mild. It reduces aldehydes and ketones but not carboxylic acids.", "3": "Hydrogen over nickel does not reduce the carboxyl group of carboxylic acids."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0921' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Methanoic acid, HCOOH, has a hydrogen attached directly to its carbonyl carbon, like an aldehyde. This part is easily oxidized (to CO₂ and water), so methanoic acid acts as a reducing agent and gives a silver mirror with Tollens'' reagent. Ethanoic acid has a CH₃ there instead and gives no mirror.', wrong = '{"0": "Methanoic acid is a carboxylic acid, so it reacts with NaHCO₃ and gives off CO₂, just like ethanoic acid.", "1": "The H of its –COOH group is acidic, so methanoic acid ionizes in water like other carboxylic acids.", "2": "Methanoic acid forms esters with alcohols, such as methyl methanoate, like other carboxylic acids."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0922' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A lower pKa means a stronger acid. Typical values are about 5 for RCOOH, 10 for phenols (ArOH), 14 for water and 16 for alcohols (ROH). So the order of acid strength is RCOOH > ArOH > H₂O > ROH.', wrong = '{"0": "Carboxylic acids (pKa about 5) are stronger than phenols (pKa about 10), and alcohols are weaker than water.", "2": "This is the reverse order. Alcohols have the highest pKa and are the weakest acids here.", "3": "Water (pKa about 14) is weaker than both carboxylic acids and phenols."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0923' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a carboxylic acid loses H⁺, the negative charge of RCOO⁻ is shared equally by its two oxygen atoms through delocalization. This spreading of the charge stabilizes the carboxylate ion, so it is less likely to take back H⁺. In an alkoxide ion, RO⁻, the charge sits on one oxygen only.', wrong = '{"0": "Volatility is about how easily a liquid evaporates. It has nothing to do with acid strength.", "1": "Only the O–H hydrogen ionizes. The total number of hydrogen atoms does not set acid strength.", "3": "The alkoxide ion has no resonance. Its charge stays on one oxygen, which is why alcohols are weak acids."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0924' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An electron-donating group pushes more negative charge onto the carboxylate ion and destabilizes it. In ethanoic acid the CH₃ group has a +I effect, so ethanoate holds H⁺ more strongly. Methanoic acid has only H on the carboxyl carbon, so methanoate is more stable and the acid is about ten times stronger.', wrong = '{"0": "Both methanoate and ethanoate ions have their charge delocalized over two oxygens.", "1": "Both acids have two oxygen atoms in the –COOH group, so this cannot explain the difference.", "2": "Ethanoic acid does form hydrogen bonds, for example as dimers. That is not why it is weaker."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0925' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chlorine is electronegative and pulls electron density away from the carboxylate group (−I effect), spreading its negative charge and stabilizing the anion. Each extra Cl adds to this. So pKa falls with more Cl: CH₃COOH about 4.75, ClCH₂COOH 2.86, Cl₂CHCOOH 1.26, Cl₃CCOOH 0.64. Trichloroethanoic acid has the lowest pKa.', wrong = '{"1": "Dichloroethanoic acid has two Cl atoms and a pKa of 1.26, higher than trichloroethanoic acid''s 0.64.", "2": "Chloroethanoic acid has only one Cl atom and a pKa of 2.86.", "3": "Ethanoic acid has no Cl atoms. Its pKa of about 4.75 makes it the weakest of the four."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0926' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ethanol and ethanoic acid both have O–H groups and hold their molecules together by hydrogen bonds. Ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃, has no O–H group, so its molecules cannot hydrogen bond with each other. Weaker intermolecular forces mean it boils lower, at 77 °C.', wrong = '{"1": "Esters are covalent molecules. Ionic compounds have very high boiling points.", "2": "Ethyl ethanoate (88 g mol⁻¹) is heavier than ethanoic acid (60 g mol⁻¹) and ethanol (46 g mol⁻¹), so mass would predict a higher boiling point.", "3": "Simple esters like ethyl ethanoate are small molecules, not polymers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0927' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acyl chlorides are far more reactive than carboxylic acids. Ethanoyl chloride reacts vigorously with ethanol at room temperature: CH₃COCl + CH₃CH₂OH → CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + HCl. HCl escapes as a gas and the reaction goes to completion, unlike the reversible Fischer esterification.', wrong = '{"1": "An equilibrium mixture is a feature of the reversible acid + alcohol (Fischer) esterification.", "2": "Concentrated H₂SO₄ and reflux are needed for the slow acid + alcohol reaction, not for an acyl chloride.", "3": "The by-product here is hydrogen chloride. Water is the by-product of the acid + alcohol method."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0928' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ester''s name has two parts: the alkyl group from the alcohol, then the -oate name from the acid. Ethanol gives ethyl, and propanoic acid gives propanoate. So the ester is ethyl propanoate, CH₃CH₂COOCH₂CH₃.', wrong = '{"1": "Propyl ethanoate would be made from ethanoic acid and propan-1-ol.", "2": "Ethyl ethanoate would be made from ethanoic acid and ethanol.", "3": "Propyl propanoate would be made from propanoic acid and propan-1-ol."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0929' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ethanoyl chloride reacts with water at room temperature within minutes, because its carbonyl carbon is very δ+. Chloroethane needs prolonged heating with water. Chlorobenzene does not react with water at all, because its C–Cl bond is strengthened by the ring. So the order is ethanoyl chloride > chloroethane > chlorobenzene.', wrong = '{"1": "This is the reverse order. Chlorobenzene is the least reactive, not the most.", "2": "Ethanoyl chloride reacts fastest of the three, faster than chloroethane.", "3": "Their rates are very different, from minutes at room temperature to no reaction at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0930' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In chlorobenzene, a lone pair on chlorine overlaps with the π electrons of the ring and is partly delocalized. This gives the C–Cl bond some double-bond character, making it shorter and stronger. Water cannot break such a strong bond, so chlorobenzene is not hydrolysed.', wrong = '{"1": "Chlorine is more electronegative than carbon, not less.", "2": "A large δ+ would make the carbon more open to attack. In fact delocalization reduces the δ+ on this carbon.", "3": "Chlorobenzene is a liquid at room temperature (boiling point about 132 °C), not a gas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0931' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The carbonyl carbon of ethanoyl chloride is bonded to two electronegative atoms, oxygen and chlorine. Both pull electrons away, leaving a large δ+ charge, and the flat sp² carbon is easy to reach. Water attacks it quickly. In chloroethane only Cl pulls electrons, so its carbon is much less δ+.', wrong = '{"1": "The carbonyl carbon is sp² hybridized and flat, which makes it easier to attack, not harder.", "2": "The carbonyl carbon carries a large δ+ charge because both O and Cl withdraw electrons.", "3": "The single methyl group does not block attack on the flat carbonyl carbon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0932' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Amines are derived from ammonia by replacing its hydrogen atoms with alkyl or aryl groups. The class depends on how many such groups are bonded to nitrogen: one gives a primary amine, two a secondary amine, three a tertiary amine. This differs from alcohols, which are classified at the carbon atom.', wrong = '{"0": "Chain length does not decide the class. Butan-1-amine and methanamine are both primary.", "1": "Counting carbons on the carbon carrying the group is how alcohols and halogenoalkanes are classified, not amines.", "3": "Simple amines have only one nitrogen. The class depends on the groups attached to it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0933' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In CH₃–NH–CH₂CH₃, the nitrogen carries two carbon groups, methyl and ethyl, and one hydrogen. An amine with two alkyl groups on nitrogen is a secondary amine.', wrong = '{"0": "A primary amine has just one alkyl group on nitrogen, like ethanamine, CH₃CH₂NH₂.", "2": "A tertiary amine has three alkyl groups and no hydrogen on nitrogen, like (CH₃)₃N.", "3": "A quaternary ammonium salt has four groups on a positively charged nitrogen. This molecule is neutral with only two."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0934' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In (CH₃)₄N⁺Cl⁻, all four bonds of nitrogen are to methyl groups, so nitrogen carries a positive charge, balanced by Cl⁻. This is a quaternary ammonium salt, named by listing the groups on the ammonium ion and then the anion: tetramethylammonium chloride.', wrong = '{"0": "Trimethylamine is (CH₃)₃N, a neutral tertiary amine with only three methyl groups.", "1": "N,N-Dimethylmethanamine is another name for (CH₃)₃N, a tertiary amine, not a quaternary salt.", "2": "Methanamine hydrochloride is CH₃NH₃⁺Cl⁻, with only one methyl group on nitrogen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0935' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The ethanamine formed still has a lone pair on nitrogen, so it can react with more bromoethane to give secondary and tertiary amines. Using a large excess of ammonia makes it far more likely that bromoethane meets NH₃ than an amine, so ethanamine becomes the main product.', wrong = '{"0": "No nitrile is formed. Nitriles come from halogenoalkanes and cyanide ions, not ammonia.", "2": "A tertiary amine is favoured by an excess of the halogenoalkane, not by excess ammonia.", "3": "Ethanol is only the solvent. Ammonia is not used to neutralize it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0936' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Reduction adds hydrogen to the C≡N group, turning it into –CH₂NH₂, and keeps every carbon. Propanenitrile, CH₃CH₂CN, has three carbons, so it gives CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂, propan-1-amine.', wrong = '{"0": "Propanoic acid is formed by hydrolysis of the nitrile, not by reduction.", "1": "Ethanamine has only two carbons. Reduction keeps all three carbons of propanenitrile.", "3": "Propanamide is a partial hydrolysis product of the nitrile, not a reduction product."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0937' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the Hofmann reaction, an amide is warmed with bromine and NaOH, and the carbonyl carbon is lost as carbonate. The amine formed has one carbon fewer than the amide: CH₃CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → CH₃NH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + 2NaBr + 2H₂O. Ethanamide gives methanamine.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanenitrile is made by dehydrating ethanamide (for example with P₄O₁₀), not by the Hofmann reaction.", "1": "Ethanamine keeps both carbons. It comes from reducing ethanamide with LiAlH₄, not from the Hofmann reaction.", "2": "Propanamine has three carbons. The Hofmann reaction removes a carbon; it does not add one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0938' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Methanamine, CH₃NH₂, has a lone pair on nitrogen and acts as a nucleophile. It replaces Br in bromoethane, so nitrogen gains an ethyl group: CH₃NH₂ + CH₃CH₂Br → CH₃NHCH₂CH₃ + HBr. The product, with methyl and ethyl on N, is the secondary amine N-methylethanamine.', wrong = '{"0": "Propan-1-amine is a primary amine with a three-carbon chain. Here two separate groups are joined through nitrogen.", "2": "Ethanamine would form if ammonia, not methanamine, reacted with bromoethane.", "3": "N,N-Dimethylethanamine would need two methyl groups on nitrogen. Only one comes from methanamine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0939' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrobenzene is reduced by heating with tin and concentrated hydrochloric acid, which together supply hydrogen ([H]). The –NO₂ group becomes –NH₂: C₆H₅NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅NH₂ + 2H₂O. Adding alkali afterwards frees phenylamine from its salt.', wrong = '{"0": "NaNO₂ with HCl makes nitrous acid, which turns phenylamine into a diazonium salt. It does not reduce nitrobenzene.", "1": "KMnO₄ with acid is an oxidizing agent. Reducing –NO₂ to –NH₂ needs a reducing agent.", "3": "Bromine water substitutes Br onto activated rings. It does not reduce the nitro group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0940' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The C–Cl bond in chlorobenzene is strengthened by delocalization of a chlorine lone pair into the ring, so it is very hard to replace. Ammonolysis therefore needs severe conditions: ammonia with a copper(I) oxide (Cu₂O) catalyst at about 250 °C and 50 atm pressure.', wrong = '{"0": "AlCl₃ is a Friedel–Crafts catalyst, and 0 °C is far too mild to break the strong aryl C–Cl bond.", "1": "Ethanolic ammonia at room temperature works only for halogenoalkanes. Chlorobenzene does not react under such mild conditions.", "2": "Aqueous NaOH at room temperature does not react with chlorobenzene and contains no nitrogen to form an amine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0941' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A smaller pKb means a stronger base. The values are ethanamine 3.29, ammonia 4.75 and phenylamine 9.38. So the order of basic strength is ethanamine > ammonia > phenylamine.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse order. Phenylamine has the largest pKb (9.38), so it is the weakest base.", "2": "Ethanamine (pKb 3.29) is a stronger base than ammonia (pKb 4.75).", "3": "Phenylamine is the weakest of the three, not stronger than ethanamine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0942' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A base accepts H⁺ using the lone pair on nitrogen. The ethyl group pushes electron density towards nitrogen (+I effect), which makes its lone pair more available to bond to H⁺. Ammonia has only H atoms on nitrogen, so ethanamine is the stronger base.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanamine''s lone pair is not delocalized. Delocalization lowers basicity, as in phenylamine.", "1": "The number of hydrogen atoms does not decide basicity. Ethanamine actually has fewer H on nitrogen than ammonia.", "3": "Base strength depends on how available the lone pair is, not on solubility. Ethanamine is very soluble in water anyway."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0943' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In phenylamine the lone pair on nitrogen overlaps with the π electrons of the benzene ring and is partly delocalized into it. This makes the lone pair much less available to accept a proton. So phenylamine (pKb 9.38) is a much weaker base than ammonia (pKb 4.75).', wrong = '{"0": "Phenylamine is a covalent molecule. Being ionic would not explain weak basicity anyway.", "1": "The phenyl ring draws electron density away from nitrogen by delocalization. It does not donate electrons to N.", "2": "Phenylamine does have a lone pair on nitrogen. It is weakly basic because that pair is delocalized."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0944' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The smell of fish comes mainly from trimethylamine, a volatile amine that is a weak base. Citric acid in lemon juice gives H⁺ to the amine, forming an ammonium salt: (CH₃)₃N + H⁺ → (CH₃)₃NH⁺. Salts are ionic and do not evaporate, so the smell is reduced.', wrong = '{"0": "Citric acid is not an oxidizing agent. It simply protonates the amine.", "2": "The smell comes from the amine, not from fat. Turning the amine into a salt removes the odour.", "3": "Amides are made from amines with acyl chlorides. Citric acid only forms a salt in an acid–base reaction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0945' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrous acid tells the three classes of aliphatic amines apart. Secondary amines react to form N-nitrosoamines, R₂N–N=O, which separate as yellow oily liquids. So a yellow oil means the amine is secondary.', wrong = '{"0": "Tertiary amines just form soluble nitrite salts, so no visible change is seen.", "1": "Primary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid and give off bubbles of nitrogen gas, not a yellow oil.", "3": "A quaternary ammonium salt has no lone pair or N–H on nitrogen, so it does not form a nitrosoamine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0946' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'N,N-Dimethylethanamine, (CH₃)₂NCH₂CH₃, is a tertiary amine: nitrogen carries three alkyl groups and no hydrogen. Tertiary amines only react as bases with nitrous acid, forming a soluble ammonium nitrite salt. Nothing visible happens.', wrong = '{"0": "No precipitate forms. The salt that forms stays dissolved.", "1": "Bubbles of nitrogen gas are seen with primary aliphatic amines, which have an –NH₂ group.", "2": "A yellow oil (an N-nitrosoamine) forms with secondary amines, which have one H on nitrogen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0947' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nitrous acid, HNO₂, decomposes easily and cannot be stored. It is therefore made in situ (in the reaction mixture) by adding sodium nitrite to hydrochloric acid: NaNO₂ + HCl → HNO₂ + NaCl. The mixture is kept cold with ice.', wrong = '{"0": "Nitrous acid is unstable and is not sold as a pure liquid. It must be made when needed.", "2": "Concentrated HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ form the nitrating mixture, which supplies NO₂⁺, not nitrous acid.", "3": "Ammonia and oxygen react in the Ostwald process on the way to nitric acid, not nitrous acid for diazotization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0948' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –NH₂ group''s lone pair is partly delocalized into the benzene ring, raising its electron density, especially at the 2, 4 and 6 positions. The ring becomes so reactive that bromine water substitutes all three positions at once without a catalyst, giving a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine.', wrong = '{"0": "Bromine water is a weaker electrophile than Br₂ with FeBr₃. The fast reaction is due to the activated ring.", "1": "–NH₂ is an activating, ortho-para directing group, not a meta-directing deactivating group.", "3": "Phenylamine is an aromatic amine. It has a benzene ring, not an alkene C=C bond."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0949' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Benzenediazonium chloride is unstable. Above about 10 °C it starts to decompose, losing nitrogen gas and reacting with water to give phenol. Keeping the mixture below 10 °C with ice preserves the diazonium salt so it can be used, for example in coupling reactions.', wrong = '{"0": "Cooling is not about the energy change of the reaction. It is needed to keep the unstable product from breaking down.", "1": "Nitrous acid does not freeze above 10 °C. It is kept cold because both it and the diazonium salt are unstable.", "2": "Phenylamine is a liquid that boils at about 184 °C, far above 10 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0950' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an aryl diazonium ion, ArN₂⁺, the positive charge can be spread into the π system of the benzene ring by delocalization. This stabilizes the ion, so it can be kept in cold solution. Alkyl diazonium ions have no such delocalization and break down almost at once, losing N₂.', wrong = '{"0": "The extra stability comes from delocalization into the ring, not from a +I effect.", "2": "Both aryl and alkyl diazonium ions contain the –N≡N⁺ group.", "3": "Aryl diazonium salts dissolve in water and are used as aqueous solutions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0951' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When an aqueous solution of benzenediazonium chloride is warmed, the diazonium group is replaced by –OH and nitrogen gas is released: C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + H₂O → C₆H₅OH + N₂ + HCl. The product is phenol.', wrong = '{"0": "With water as the reacting partner the –N₂⁺ group is replaced by –OH, so the main product is phenol, not chlorobenzene.", "1": "Phenylamine is the amine used to make the diazonium salt. Warming does not convert it back.", "3": "Azo compounds need a coupling partner such as phenol in mild alkali, and are made in the cold."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0952' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The diazonium ion, C₆H₅N₂⁺, is positively charged and electron-poor, so it acts as an electrophile. It attacks the ring of phenol, which is strongly activated by –OH, and substitutes mainly at the para position. The product is 4-hydroxyazobenzene, an azo dye.', wrong = '{"0": "The diazonium ion is not oxidized in coupling. It joins the phenol ring through the –N=N– group.", "1": "Phenol has no C=O group, and the positive diazonium ion is an electrophile, not a nucleophile.", "2": "No free radicals are formed. This is electrophilic substitution on the ring."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0953' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Coupling with phenol is done in mildly alkaline solution, such as dilute NaOH. The alkali turns phenol into phenoxide, whose ring is even more strongly activated for attack by the diazonium electrophile. Coupling with aromatic amines is instead done in mildly acidic conditions.', wrong = '{"0": "Concentrated sulphuric acid would not allow coupling with phenol. Phenol needs mild alkali.", "2": "At 100 °C the diazonium salt would decompose to phenol and N₂ before it could couple.", "3": "Coupling is carried out in cold aqueous solution, not in dry ether."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0954' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Azo dyes contain the azo group, –N=N–, linking two aromatic rings. This group, together with the rings it joins, absorbs visible light and is the chromophore that gives the colour. Changing the other groups on the rings shifts the colour, from yellow and orange to red, blue and green.', wrong = '{"0": "An –OH group can modify the colour, but it is not the chromophore. Phenol itself is colourless.", "1": "An –NH₂ group can shift the colour, but it is not the chromophore. Phenylamine is colourless when pure.", "3": "The –SO₃Na group mainly makes the dye soluble in water. It does not produce the colour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0955' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Butter yellow, p-dimethylaminoazobenzene, is an azo dye that was once used to colour food such as butter. It was found to be carcinogenic (it can cause cancer), so its use in food is now forbidden.', wrong = '{"0": "Butter yellow is a stable dye. Instability is not why it was banned.", "1": "It was used to colour fats such as butter, so it does mix with fat. Solubility is not the problem.", "2": "As its name says, butter yellow is a yellow dye, not colourless."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0956' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Methyl orange is an azo dye. Sulfanilic acid is diazotized to give a diazonium salt, which is then coupled with N,N-dimethylphenylamine (in ethanoic acid). The –N=N– link formed joins the two rings, giving methyl orange, which changes colour with pH.', wrong = '{"0": "Benzenediazonium chloride coupling with phenol gives 4-hydroxyazobenzene, not methyl orange.", "2": "Phenylamine with bromine water gives a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine.", "3": "Nitrobenzene with Sn and HCl is reduced to phenylamine, not an azo dye."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0957' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Boiling with dilute NaOH hydrolyses the –C≡N group. In alkali this gives the salt and ammonia: CH₃CN + NaOH + H₂O → CH₃COONa + NH₃. Adding dilute HCl then converts sodium ethanoate into ethanoic acid: CH₃COONa + HCl → CH₃COOH + NaCl.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanal is an aldehyde. Full hydrolysis of a nitrile gives a carboxylic acid, not an aldehyde.", "1": "Ethanamine is formed by reducing ethanenitrile, for example with LiAlH₄, not by hydrolysis.", "3": "Ethanol is not a hydrolysis product of a nitrile. The carbon of –C≡N ends up as –COOH."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0958' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The lone pair on nitrogen in ethanamine attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon of ethanoyl chloride, and Cl is lost. The product is CH₃CONHCH₂CH₃, an amide with an ethyl group on nitrogen (N-ethylethanamide), plus HCl. The reaction is fast at room temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanoic acid would come from reaction with water (hydrolysis), not with an amine.", "1": "An ester such as ethyl ethanoate needs an alcohol. An amine gives an amide.", "2": "No chloroethane forms. The chlorine leaves as HCl, and the amine''s nitrogen bonds to the carbonyl carbon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0959' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ammonia or an amine reacts with an acyl chloride, HCl is formed. HCl is an acid, and it reacts with more of the basic ammonia or amine to form an ammonium salt, for example NH₃ + HCl → NH₄Cl. Extra ammonia or amine is therefore needed so that enough is left to form the amide.', wrong = '{"1": "Ammonia is a reactant that becomes part of the amide. It is not a catalyst.", "2": "The reaction of acyl chlorides with ammonia goes to completion. The excess is needed because HCl uses some up.", "3": "Ammonia is not a reducing agent for amides. The excess only reacts with the HCl formed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0960' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an amide, R–CO–NH₂, the lone pair on nitrogen is delocalized towards the carbonyl oxygen, giving the C–N bond partial double-bond character. The lone pair is much less available to accept a proton, so amides are very weak bases and give almost neutral solutions in water.', wrong = '{"1": "Amides do have a lone pair on nitrogen. It is delocalized onto the C=O group, not missing.", "2": "The C=O group withdraws electrons from nitrogen. It does not donate electrons to it.", "3": "Amides are covalent molecules, not ionic compounds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0961' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an amide, the nitrogen lone pair overlaps with the π bond of C=O and is delocalized. This gives the C–N bond partial double-bond character. Double bonds are shorter than single bonds, so the amide C–N bond (about 132 pm) is shorter than the ordinary C–N single bond in an amine (about 147 pm).', wrong = '{"1": "Delocalization makes the amide nitrogen planar, close to sp², not sp³. sp³ nitrogen is found in amines.", "2": "Hydrogen bonding acts between molecules. It does not change the length of a covalent bond inside one molecule.", "3": "In the delocalized form, nitrogen carries a partial positive charge and oxygen a negative one, not the other way round."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0962' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A chiral carbon needs four different groups. In α-amino acids, H₂N–CHR–COOH, the α-carbon carries –NH₂, –COOH, H and R. In glycine R is H, so the α-carbon has two hydrogens and is not chiral. All other common α-amino acids have R ≠ H and are chiral.', wrong = '{"1": "In alanine R = CH₃, so the α-carbon carries four different groups and is chiral.", "2": "In lysine R is a long chain ending in –NH₂, so its α-carbon carries four different groups and is chiral.", "3": "In glutamic acid R is –CH₂CH₂COOH, so its α-carbon carries four different groups and is chiral."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0963' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a zwitterion, H₃N⁺–CHR–COO⁻, the carboxyl group has given its proton to the amino group. The –NH₃⁺ group can give a proton back, so it is the acidic group (pKa about 10). The –COO⁻ group can accept a proton, so it is the basic group (pKb about 12).', wrong = '{"1": "In the zwitterion these groups are not present as –COOH and –NH₂. They exist as –COO⁻ and –NH₃⁺.", "2": "–NH₂ is basic, not acidic, and in the zwitterion it is already protonated as –NH₃⁺.", "3": "This swaps the roles. –COO⁻ accepts protons (basic), while –NH₃⁺ donates them (acidic)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0964' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At low pH there are many H⁺ ions. They protonate the –COO⁻ group of the zwitterion, giving –COOH, while the amino group stays as –NH₃⁺. The amino acid therefore carries a net positive charge, existing mainly as the cation H₃N⁺–CHR–COOH.', wrong = '{"1": "The anion, H₂N–CHR–COO⁻, is the main form at high pH, where OH⁻ removes H⁺ from –NH₃⁺.", "2": "The zwitterion is the main form at the isoelectric point, not in strongly acidic solution.", "3": "The uncharged H₂N–CHR–COOH form is present only in tiny amounts. In acid, the cation dominates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0965' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At a pH below its pI an amino acid is positive and moves to the cathode (−). Above its pI it is negative and moves to the anode (+). At pH 6: lysine (pI 9.74) is positive and goes to the cathode, alanine (pI 6.02) has almost no net charge and stays near the origin, glutamic acid (pI 3.22) is negative and goes to the anode.', wrong = '{"1": "Glutamic acid has pI 3.22, below pH 6, so it is negative and moves to the anode, not the cathode.", "2": "The directions are reversed. Positive lysine goes to the negative cathode, and negative glutamic acid to the anode.", "3": "Only alanine has a pI close to 6. Lysine and glutamic acid are charged and move."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0966' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Most amino acids are colourless, so their spots cannot be seen after electrophoresis. The paper is sprayed with ninhydrin, a locating agent that reacts with amino acids to give a purple colour, showing where each one has moved.', wrong = '{"1": "Iodine solution gives a blue-black colour with starch. It is not used to show amino acids.", "2": "Tollens'' reagent is used to detect aldehydes, which give a silver mirror.", "3": "2,4-DNPH gives orange crystals with aldehydes and ketones, not amino acids."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0967' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A peptide bond (–CO–NH–) forms by condensation between –COOH of one amino acid and –NH₂ of the next, releasing one water molecule. A chain of three amino acids has two links between them. So a tripeptide has 2 peptide bonds and 2 water molecules are eliminated.', wrong = '{"1": "A straight chain of three units has only two links between them, not three.", "2": "Exactly one water molecule is released per peptide bond, so 2 bonds release 2 waters, not 3.", "3": "There are only two peptide bonds in a tripeptide, and the number of water molecules equals the number of bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0968' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every step in a synthesis loses some product, and the losses multiply. A good route starts from cheap, easily available materials and reaches the product in as few steps as possible, giving a high overall yield. This saves time, materials and money.', wrong = '{"1": "More steps mean more losses. Five steps at 80% each give only about 33% overall.", "2": "A low temperature is useless if the yield is poor. Overall yield and the number of steps matter most.", "3": "Most syntheses need several reagents. The aim is a short, high-yield route, not one reagent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0969' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Organic synthesis usually builds a larger target molecule from smaller, cheaper starting materials. Joining these pieces means forming new carbon–carbon bonds that create the carbon skeleton. So reactions such as Friedel–Crafts alkylation or adding a carbon with cyanide are central to synthesis.', wrong = '{"0": "Changing one functional group into another is functional group interconversion (FGI). It does not by itself build the carbon skeleton.", "2": "Many C–C bond-forming reactions need catalysts, for example AlCl₃ in Friedel–Crafts reactions.", "3": "C–C bond-forming reactions join carbon atoms together. They do not remove carbon atoms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0970' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –OH of an alcohol is a poor leaving group, so it is first replaced with Cl using thionyl chloride: R–CH₂OH + SOCl₂ → R–CH₂Cl + SO₂ + HCl. The halogenoalkane then reacts with excess ammonia by nucleophilic substitution to give the primary amine R–CH₂–NH₂.', wrong = '{"0": "H₂/Ni does not react with an alcohol, and HCl does not give an amine. Neither step introduces nitrogen.", "1": "NaBH₄ reduces aldehydes and ketones. It does not turn –OH into a group that ammonia can replace.", "3": "Acidified dichromate oxidizes the alcohol to an aldehyde or acid. Ammonia then does not give the required amine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0971' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Retrosynthesis is a planning method that works backwards. The chemist starts from the target molecule and imagines breaking it at suitable bonds (disconnections) into simpler pieces, again and again, until cheap, readily available starting materials are reached. The real synthesis then runs in the forward direction.', wrong = '{"0": "Retrosynthesis is a paper planning method. It is not the reverse reaction of an equilibrium.", "1": "No real reaction, such as hydrolysis, is carried out during retrosynthesis. The bonds are broken only on paper.", "2": "Breaking a molecule into ions in a mass spectrometer is fragmentation, an analysis method."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0972' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A synthon is an idealized fragment, often an ion such as NO₂⁺ or CH₃CO⁺, produced by an imaginary disconnection. Synthons are usually too unstable to handle. A synthetic equivalent is a real reagent that does the same job: for example, HNO₃ (with H₂SO₄) supplies NO₂⁺, and CH₃COCl (with AlCl₃) supplies CH₃CO⁺.', wrong = '{"0": "The imaginary breaking of a bond in the target is called a disconnection.", "2": "The target molecule is the compound being made. It is not the reagent used to supply a synthon.", "3": "Yield is the amount of product obtained. It has nothing to do with the term synthetic equivalent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0973' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a target molecule can be disconnected in several ways, all the possible disconnections and synthons are drawn as a branching diagram leading back to the starting materials. This diagram is called a retrosynthetic tree, and it helps the chemist choose the best route.', wrong = '{"0": "A flow sheet shows the units and flow of materials in an industrial plant, not disconnections.", "1": "A reaction profile is a graph of energy against the progress of a single reaction.", "3": "An energy level diagram shows energy levels, for example of orbitals or reactants and products."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0974' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The purpose of a disconnection is to break the target into simpler fragments. A good disconnection is chosen at a bond that a known, reliable reaction can form, and it should give a considerable structural simplification. Repeating this leads back to simple starting materials.', wrong = '{"0": "Disconnections break the target into smaller pieces. They do not add carbon atoms.", "1": "Synthons are usually imagined as ions (cations and anions), not radicals.", "2": "Disconnections are usually made at C–C or C–heteroatom bonds that known reactions can form, not at C–H bonds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0975' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heating a halogenoalkane with KOH dissolved in alcohol (ethanol) makes OH⁻ act mainly as a base. It removes a hydrogen from the carbon next to the C–X carbon, and the halide leaves, so HX is eliminated and an alkene forms.', wrong = '{"0": "Ammonia replaces the halogen with –NH₂, giving an amine, not an alkene.", "2": "Aqueous KOH acts mainly as a nucleophile, replacing the halogen with –OH to give an alcohol.", "3": "NaBH₄ reduces aldehydes and ketones. It does not convert halogenoalkanes into alkenes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0976' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Thionyl chloride replaces the –OH of a carboxylic acid with –Cl: CH₃COOH + SOCl₂ → CH₃COCl + SO₂ + HCl. This turns ethanoic acid into the much more reactive acyl chloride, ethanoyl chloride.', wrong = '{"0": "Aqueous KOH neutralizes ethanoic acid to potassium ethanoate, a salt, not an acyl chloride.", "1": "LiAlH₄ reduces ethanoic acid to ethanol. It does not add chlorine.", "3": "Ammonia is used later in the scheme, turning ethanoyl chloride into ethanamide."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0977' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ammonia''s lone pair attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon of ethanoyl chloride, and Cl is lost. –NH₂ replaces –Cl, giving the amide: CH₃COCl + 2NH₃ → CH₃CONH₂ + NH₄Cl. The product is ethanamide.', wrong = '{"0": "Ethanol reacts with ethanoyl chloride to give an ester, ethyl ethanoate, not an amide.", "1": "LiAlH₄ is a reducing agent. It would reduce the acyl chloride, not form an amide.", "2": "Water hydrolyses ethanoyl chloride to ethanoic acid and HCl, not to an amide."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0978' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First, chlorine in light reacts with the methyl side chain by free radical substitution, giving (chloromethyl)benzene, C₆H₅CH₂Cl. Then aqueous KOH replaces the Cl with –OH by nucleophilic substitution, giving benzyl alcohol, C₆H₅CH₂OH.', wrong = '{"0": "CH₃Cl with AlCl₃ is Friedel–Crafts alkylation. It would add another methyl group to the ring.", "2": "KMnO₄ with acid oxidizes the side chain all the way to –COOH, giving benzoic acid, not the alcohol.", "3": "Sn with HCl reduces nitro groups to amino groups. Methylbenzene has no nitro group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0979' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Benzenesulfonic acid, C₆H₅SO₃H, is fused with solid NaOH at high temperature, which replaces the –SO₃H group and gives sodium phenoxide. Acidifying with HCl then gives phenol, C₆H₅OH.', wrong = '{"0": "SO₃ (in concentrated H₂SO₄) is used to make benzenesulfonic acid from benzene, the step before this one.", "1": "Sn with HCl reduces nitrobenzene to phenylamine. It does not convert a sulfonic acid into phenol.", "3": "Nitration adds an –NO₂ group to the ring. It does not replace –SO₃H with –OH."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0980' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Disconnecting the C–O single bond of the ester CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ gives an acyl synthon, CH₃CO⁺, and an ethoxy synthon, CH₃CH₂O⁻. Neutralizing these gives the real reagents acetic acid and ethanol, which are heated together with concentrated H₂SO₄ to make ethyl acetate.', wrong = '{"0": "Benzene and nitric acid are the synthetic equivalents in the synthesis of nitrobenzene and aniline.", "1": "Acetyl chloride and benzene are used to make acetophenone by Friedel–Crafts acylation.", "2": "Ethanal and methanol do not combine to form an ester, and their carbon groups do not match ethyl acetate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0981' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'There is no simple way to put an –NH₂ group directly onto benzene. A nitro group, however, is easily added by electrophilic nitration with HNO₃ and H₂SO₄, and can then be reduced to –NH₂ with Sn and HCl. So in retrosynthesis, –NH₂ is first changed (FGI) to –NO₂, giving nitrobenzene.', wrong = '{"0": "–NH₂ is an ortho-para director, not a meta director, and directing effects are not the reason here.", "2": "Nitro compounds are not basic. The reason is that nitration is easy while direct amination is not.", "3": "Aniline itself is the target. The plan makes it by reducing nitrobenzene."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0982' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The synthon CH₃CO⁺ is the acylium ion. Its synthetic equivalent is ethanoyl chloride with AlCl₃: AlCl₃ removes Cl⁻ to generate CH₃CO⁺, which attacks benzene in Friedel–Crafts acylation to give acetophenone, C₆H₅COCH₃.', wrong = '{"0": "Methane has no carbonyl group and cannot supply an acylium ion.", "1": "Ethanol contains no carbonyl group and cannot supply CH₃CO⁺ to the ring.", "3": "NaBH₄ is a reducing agent, and ethanoic acid cannot acylate benzene this way."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0983' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Phenol is first nitrated with HNO₃/H₂SO₄ to give 4-nitrophenol. Sn/HCl then reduces the nitro group to an amino group, giving 4-aminophenol. Finally the –NH₂ reacts with ethanoyl chloride to form the amide group of paracetamol. The amine must exist before the acyl group can be attached to nitrogen.', wrong = '{"0": "Reacting phenol with CH₃COCl first would make an ester on the oxygen. The –NH₂ group for the amide is not yet present.", "1": "Carboxylation of phenol with CO₂ (Kolbe reaction) followed by acetylation is the route to aspirin, not paracetamol.", "2": "Phenol has no group that can be reduced, so reduction cannot be the first step."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0984' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A disconnection should be made at a bond that a known, reliable reaction can form. Amines react readily with acyl chlorides by nucleophilic addition–elimination: the –NH₂ adds to the C=O, then Cl⁻ is eliminated, forming an amide. So disconnecting the amide bond leads to 4-aminophenol and ethanoyl chloride.', wrong = '{"0": "4-Aminophenol is a nucleophile, because of the lone pair on nitrogen. Only the acyl chloride is the electrophile.", "2": "No free radicals are involved. The reaction goes through electron pairs.", "3": "Acyl chlorides react with amines readily at room temperature. High pressure is not needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0985' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Salicylic acid is phenol with a –COOH group on the carbon next to the –OH. In retrosynthesis, the C–C bond to the carboxyl group is disconnected, giving phenol and CO₂. In the forward direction, phenol (as sodium phenoxide) is heated with CO₂ under high pressure and then acidified to give salicylic acid.', wrong = '{"0": "KMnO₄ oxidizes alkyl side chains to –COOH. Phenol has no side chain to oxidize.", "1": "CH₃COCl with AlCl₃ adds an acyl group, giving a ketone, not a carboxylic acid group.", "3": "HNO₃ with H₂SO₄ adds nitro groups to the ring, not a carboxyl group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0986' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Aspirin is acetylsalicylic acid. The phenolic –OH of salicylic acid reacts with ethanoic anhydride, heated with an acid catalyst such as H₂SO₄ or H₃PO₄, forming an ester (–OCOCH₃) and releasing ethanoic acid. The –COOH group is left unchanged.', wrong = '{"0": "Ammonia would react with the acid group to form a salt or an amide, not the ester in aspirin.", "1": "Ethanol with H₂SO₄ would esterify the –COOH group, giving ethyl salicylate, not aspirin.", "2": "NaBH₄ is a reducing agent. It does not add an acetyl group."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0987' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In paracetamol the acetyl group, CH₃CO–, is attached to nitrogen as –NHCOCH₃, which is an amide group. In aspirin, the CH₃CO– is attached to oxygen as –OCOCH₃, an ester group. Both come from phenol, but the atom bonded to the acetyl group differs.', wrong = '{"1": "In a ketone, the C=O carbon is bonded to two carbons. In paracetamol, the acetyl carbon is bonded to nitrogen.", "2": "An ether has a C–O–C link with no C=O. Paracetamol''s acetyl group is joined through N and keeps its C=O.", "3": "An aldehyde needs a –CHO group. Paracetamol has no hydrogen on its acetyl carbonyl carbon."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0988' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Researchers at MIT trained a machine-learning model to recognise chemical features that make molecules effective at killing E. coli bacteria. The model picked out halicin, an antibacterial molecule with a structure different from existing antibiotics, which later killed many drug-resistant bacteria.', wrong = '{"1": "The study searched for antibacterial molecules. Blood pressure drugs were not part of it.", "2": "Halicin was found as an antibacterial agent against bacteria such as E. coli, not as an antiviral.", "3": "Dissolving kidney stones was not the aim of the study."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0989' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After finding halicin, the researchers used their computer model to screen more than 100 million molecules from a database. This took only about three days and picked out 23 more promising candidates, showing how much faster AI can search than laboratory testing.', wrong = '{"1": "Three years would be far too slow. The point of the study was the speed of computer screening.", "2": "Ten years refers to the long collaboration on the total synthesis of vitamin B₁₂.", "3": "Three months is still far longer than the actual time. The screen took about three days."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0990' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The total synthesis of vitamin B₁₂ was completed in 1972 by R. B. Woodward and A. Eschenmoser, after more than ten years of collaboration. It took over 100 steps and the work of 91 postdoctoral researchers and 12 PhD students.', wrong = '{"1": "Robert Robinson is remembered for the synthesis of cholesterol in the early 1950s, not vitamin B₁₂.", "2": "Kekulé proposed benzene''s ring structure and Faraday discovered benzene. Neither worked on vitamin B₁₂.", "3": "The MIT team used machine learning to find the antibiotic halicin. They did not synthesize vitamin B₁₂."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0991' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In addition polymerization, the π bond of a C=C (or C≡C) bond opens, and the freed electrons are used to link monomers end to end. Without a multiple bond there is nothing to open, so the monomer must contain one, as in ethene, propene or chloroethene.', wrong = '{"1": "Two –OH groups make a diol, which forms condensation polymers (polyesters) with dicarboxylic acids.", "2": "An –NH₂ and a –COOH group suit condensation polymerization, forming polyamides with loss of water.", "3": "A benzene ring alone does not allow addition polymerization. Styrene polymerizes because of its C=C side chain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0992' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In addition polymerization, monomers with C=C bonds join one after another in a chain reaction, often started by a free-radical initiator. No small molecule is removed at any step, so every atom of the monomers ends up in the polymer. The polymer has the same empirical formula as the monomer.', wrong = '{"0": "Addition polymerization needs a C=C bond. Two functional groups per monomer are needed for condensation polymerization.", "1": "Losing a small molecule such as water at each step is the mark of condensation polymerization.", "3": "The chain is started by an initiator (or catalyst), such as a free-radical source, not by HCl."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0993' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'LDPE is made at high pressure and its chains have many side branches. The branches stop the chains from packing closely, so there is more space between them and weaker forces, making LDPE soft and less dense. HDPE has mostly straight chains that pack tightly, giving a denser, stiffer plastic.', wrong = '{"0": "Both LDPE and HDPE are addition polymers of ethene. Neither is a condensation polymer.", "1": "Both forms contain only carbon and hydrogen. Chlorine is present in PVC, not poly(ethene).", "2": "Cross-links between chains would make a polymer harder and more rigid, not softer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0994' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'HDPE is made by polymerizing ethene with a Ziegler–Natta catalyst at low pressure and low temperature. This catalyst, discovered by Karl Ziegler in 1953, makes the chains grow without branching, so the linear chains pack closely into dense, tough poly(ethene).', wrong = '{"0": "Very high pressure with a free-radical initiator gives branched LDPE, not linear HDPE.", "2": "Concentrated H₂SO₄ is not used to polymerize ethene into HDPE. The process needs a Ziegler–Natta catalyst.", "3": "Chlorine in UV light would substitute or add chlorine, not join ethene molecules into a polymer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0995' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Karl Ziegler''s catalyst made linear poly(ethene). Giulio Natta extended this family of catalysts to produce stereoregular polymers, in which the side groups are arranged in a regular pattern along the chain. His best-known example is crystalline poly(propene).', wrong = '{"0": "PVC is made by addition polymerization of chloroethene with a free-radical initiator. It is not the example linked with Natta.", "1": "Nylon-6,6 is a condensation polymer made from a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid, not with Ziegler–Natta catalysts.", "3": "Kevlar is an aromatic polyamide made by condensation, not by Ziegler–Natta catalysis."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0996' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PVC stands for polyvinyl chloride, systematic name poly(chloroethene). Its monomer is chloroethene, CH₂=CHCl. The C=C bonds open and the monomers join end to end by addition polymerization: n CH₂=CHCl → –[CH₂–CHCl]ₙ–.', wrong = '{"0": "Propene, CH₂=CHCH₃, is the monomer of poly(propene), used for ropes and clotheslines.", "1": "Ethene, CH₂=CH₂, is the monomer of poly(ethene), used for bags and bottles.", "2": "Tetrafluoroethene, CF₂=CF₂, is the monomer of PTFE (Teflon), not PVC."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0997' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PVC has a chlorine atom on every second carbon of its chain. Because of these chlorine atoms, rigid PVC naturally resists burning and puts itself out (self-extinguishing) when the flame is taken away.', wrong = '{"0": "Rigid PVC contains no plasticizers. Plasticizers make PVC soft and flexible; they do not make it fire resistant.", "2": "PVC is an addition polymer with a carbon-only backbone, so it has no ester links.", "3": "PVC has no benzene rings. Its chain is –CH₂–CHCl– repeated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0998' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PVC is chemically inert: it does not react with most acids, bases or oils, so it does not corrode. It also resists weathering and lasts a long time. These properties make it the usual choice for water supply, drainage and sewage pipes and fittings.', wrong = '{"0": "PVC does not dissolve in water. A pipe that dissolved would be useless.", "1": "PVC is a good electrical insulator, which is why it is used to coat wires. It does not conduct.", "3": "PVC is not readily biodegradable, which causes disposal problems. Its long life is the reason it suits pipes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-0999' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In condensation polymerization, each monomer must be able to bond at both ends. So it needs at least two reactive functional groups, such as –OH, –COOH or –NH₂. Each time two groups join, a small molecule such as water is lost, and the chain grows step by step.', wrong = '{"0": "A chlorine atom is not required. Many condensation monomers, such as diols and diacids, contain no chlorine.", "1": "A C=C double bond is what a monomer needs for addition polymerization, not condensation.", "2": "A free-radical initiator starts addition polymerization. It is not part of the monomer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1000' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Terylene (PET) is a polyester. Its monomers are ethane-1,2-diol, HOCH₂CH₂OH, and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (terephthalic acid). Each –OH of the diol reacts with a –COOH of the acid to form an ester link, and a water molecule is lost each time.', wrong = '{"0": "Hexane-1,6-diamine and hexanedioic acid form nylon-6,6, a polyamide, not PET.", "2": "Caprolactam forms nylon-6 by ring opening, not a polyester.", "3": "Chloroethene forms PVC by addition polymerization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1001' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Polyester fibres have high tensile strength. They are cheap to produce and the fabric resists creasing, so polyester is used as an alternative to cotton, often blended with it in clothing.', wrong = '{"0": "Polyester is a synthetic polymer made from a diol and a dicarboxylic acid. Cotton is the natural one.", "1": "Polyester absorbs very little water. Its advantages are low cost and crease resistance.", "3": "Polyester melts at a high temperature, far above body temperature, so it is safe to wear."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1002' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The –NH₂ group of the diamine reacts with the –COOH group of the dicarboxylic acid, forming an amide link, –CONH–. The H from nitrogen and the OH from the carboxyl group leave together as water. So one molecule of H₂O is eliminated for every amide link formed.', wrong = '{"0": "CO₂ is not formed. The carboxyl carbon stays in the chain as part of the amide link.", "1": "HCl is eliminated when a dioyl chloride (–COCl) is used instead of a dicarboxylic acid.", "2": "Ammonia is not eliminated. The nitrogen stays in the chain as part of the amide link."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1003' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acyl chlorides are much more reactive than carboxylic acids, because the carbonyl carbon is attached to both O and Cl and carries a large δ+ charge. A dioyl chloride therefore reacts with a diamine quickly, even at room temperature, and HCl is eliminated.', wrong = '{"0": "With a diamine, a dioyl chloride forms amide links, not ester links. Ester links need a diol.", "2": "The by-product with a dioyl chloride is HCl, not water.", "3": "The reaction is still condensation polymerization, since HCl is lost at each step."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1004' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Proteins are made of amino acids joined by peptide bonds, –CO–NH–. Each bond forms when –NH₂ of one amino acid reacts with –COOH of another and water is lost. A peptide bond is an amide link, so proteins are natural polyamides formed by condensation.', wrong = '{"0": "Proteins form by condensation, with water lost at each link. Addition polymers need C=C bonds.", "1": "Proteins have amide (–CONH–) links, not ester (–COO–) links in their backbone.", "3": "Poly(alkene)s are hydrocarbon chains from alkenes. Proteins contain N and O in their chains."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1005' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nylon-6,6 is a polyamide made by condensation of hexamethylenediamine (1,6-diaminohexane) with adipic acid (hexanedioic acid). Each monomer has six carbon atoms, which gives the name 6,6. Water is lost as each amide link forms.', wrong = '{"0": "Caprolactam is the single monomer of nylon-6, not nylon-6,6.", "1": "Ethane-1,2-diol reacts with dicarboxylic acids to form polyesters such as PET, not nylon.", "2": "Terephthalic acid has a benzene ring and is used for PET and aromatic polyamides, not nylon-6,6, whose diacid has six carbons in a chain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1006' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nylon-6 forms when the ring of caprolactam opens and the units join, so no small molecule is lost. It is still classed as a condensation polymer because its chain is held together by amide (–CONH–) links, the same linkage formed in condensation polyamides such as nylon-6,6.', wrong = '{"0": "Caprolactam has no C=C bond that opens. The ring opens at its amide group.", "2": "Nylon-6 is made from one monomer, caprolactam, not two.", "3": "A free-radical chain reaction is typical of addition polymers made from alkenes, not nylon-6."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1007' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Caprolactam is a cyclic amide that contains six carbon atoms. The polymer made from it is named nylon-6 after this six-carbon monomer. In nylon-6,6 the two numbers instead give the carbon counts of its two different monomers.', wrong = '{"0": "The name does not count amide links. A nylon chain contains thousands of amide links.", "1": "Caprolactam contains only one nitrogen atom, in its amide group.", "3": "The number has nothing to do with melting point. Nylon melts at a high temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1008' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nylon fibres are very strong when pulled (high tensile strength), stretchy, and resist wear even when wet. Ropes, fishing nets and lines must take heavy loads in water without breaking, so nylon suits them well.', wrong = '{"0": "Nylon has a high melting point, not a low one, which helps it in machine parts too.", "1": "Nylon does not dissolve in water. A rope that dissolved would be useless.", "2": "Nylon is an electrical insulator. Conductivity is not needed for ropes or nets."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1009' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Addition polymers are made by joining alkenes through their C=C bonds, so their backbone is a chain of carbon atoms only, –C–C–C–. Condensation polymers have O or N in the backbone, as ester (–COO–) or amide (–CONH–) links. A carbon-only backbone therefore indicates an addition polymer.', wrong = '{"0": "A polyester has –COO– ester links, so oxygen atoms appear in its backbone.", "2": "A polyamide has –CONH– amide links, so nitrogen atoms appear in its backbone.", "3": "Polymers of amino acids (polypeptides) have –CONH– links, so nitrogen appears in the chain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1010' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A polyamide has amide links, –CONH–, in its main chain. Nylon-6,6 is made from a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid, and Kevlar from an aromatic diamine and an aromatic diacid. Both chains are joined by amide links, so both are polyamides.', wrong = '{"0": "PVC and PTFE are both addition polymers with carbon-only backbones. They have no amide links.", "1": "PET and PLA (polylactic acid) are both polyesters, with ester links in their chains.", "3": "Nylon-6,6 is a polyamide, but Terylene is a polyester, so this pair is mixed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'CHE-1011' AND answer = 2;
